@misc{meta-F1-wall-vs-cost,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The dispatcher names a WALL where the honest finding is COST, a true reading, a non-command, or a strongest-setting gate — and the error is directional, always toward routing around something that works.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher. FOUR INSTANCES IN ONE SESSION, 2026-08-24, each caught only because a dispatched agent measured instead of accepting the brief. ONE, sppf_db --height was called a blocker gating the 7 largest ingestion units. It is not a refusal, it is 14 minutes — it returned exit 0 on the 4.59M core, and there are zero drained cores on live, so the refusal branch is unreached. TWO, nine toolmodes rows were called attribution defects whose documentation would write falsehoods. Every sampled row is a genuine argv compare in the tool's own file. THREE, mint-baselines was listed as one command and highest leverage. It is not a command at all — the baseline mints on the commit path and forcing it is the no-verify shape. FOUR, five ratchet baselines were reported ABSENT and vacuous-green when they are EMPTY, which the census legend calls the STRONGEST setting, so five zero-tolerance gates were inverted to broken. THE TELL IS AN ABSENCE-WORD IN A BRIEF WRITTEN FOR SOMEONE ELSE, where the cost of being wrong is that they route around a working thing or build machinery for a case the tree does not contain. Same family as report-presence-never-absence, but the dispatcher-to-agent direction is what makes it expensive: an unchecked wall in a brief propagates as established fact. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I name a WALL where the honest finding is COST, a TRUE READING, a NON-COMMAND, or a STRONGEST setting. Four instances in one session, all the same direction: --height called blocked when it was 14 minutes; 9 attribution defects called falsehoods when they were genuine argv compares; mint-baselines called a one-command step when it mints only on commit; five EMPTY zero-tolerance baselines reported as ABSENT vacuous-green. Each surfaced only because a dispatched agent measured instead of accepting. THE TELL is an absence-word in a brief I am writing for someone else, where the cost of the error is that they route around a thing that works.}
}

@misc{gate-F-fixture-category,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A deliberately-failing test fixture is a third kind in the divergence census, not undocumented debt — and its declaration must live in the tool that owns the fixture, never in a roster on the census side.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=mode-consolidation-r2. WITNESS: five selftest cases drive it — a marked+dispatched flag is FIXTURE, a sibling flag on the same tool is not, a mark naming an undispatched flag declares nothing, an empty source declares nothing, and divergence_census emits the FIXTURE kind rather than dropping the row. The gate excludes FIXTURE from the ratchet set and still prints it with its own count. Live consumer sppf_db --selftest-demo-failure remains honest UNDOCUMENTED debt until its owner adds the toolmodes:fixture mark — cross-agent dependency OWED to the sppf-ingestion agent. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓜ10 ⟡fixture-category LANDED. The divergence ratchet now has a third kind, FIXTURE, alongside UNDOCUMENTED and UNBACKED. A fixture is a dispatched flag that is not a capability — it exists to FAIL, as a positive control proving a selftest harness can report failure at all. Counted as UNDOCUMENTED it offered exactly one paydown (write a usage line), and a usage line advertising a broken flag as a capability is worse debt than the row it clears. DESIGN: the declaration lives in the OWNING TOOL, not in a roster in toolmodes — a tool marks its dispatch site with the comment token 'toolmodes:fixture' and the census reads it out of that tool's source. A list on the census side would let any tool's debt be cleared by editing the census, with no witness in the tool. The mark is checked against the tool's dispatched flags, so it cannot mint an exemption for a flag that is not there, and an unreadable source declares NOTHING (a read error must never grant an exemption). The gate EXCLUDES fixtures from the ratchet set but still PRINTS them with their own count — an exemption that vanishes from the output is how a ratchet rots. Four selftest cases drive the predicate and one drives the census kind.}
}

