@misc{gate-F6-cycle-guard-is-a-ppid-walk,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A cycle between two individually-correct tools is removed by a ppid walk checked at BOTH ends, and a guard that fires on its own launching shell is worse than no guard.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher. ⟡worklist-gate-recursion, built 2026-08-24. NEITHER TOOL WAS WRONG: worklist_gate --selftest wants to know the selftests pass so it runs run_selftests, and run_selftests runs every tool's --selftest so it runs worklist_gate. The roster contains its own caller. Measured at load 54.93 with 68 concurrent processes on 10 cores, six generations deep. WHY A PPID WALK AND NOT AN ENV VAR OR A DEPTH CAP: the chain crosses membudget then the time utility then python3, three exec layers with a shell between, so an env var survives only if every layer forwards it — a precondition nobody checks and one sanitised environment breaks silently. A depth cap bounds damage without removing the edge, which is check-then-act against a process tree and is the shape that produced the incident. The ppid chain is what ACTUALLY HAPPENED rather than a token something agreed to pass along. TWO IMPLEMENTATION FACTS THAT COST A CORRECTION EACH. ONE: the proc stat file has ppid as field four but the comm field is parenthesised and may contain spaces, so split after the LAST close-paren — a naive positional split returns a state letter as a pid. TWO, AND THE EXPENSIVE ONE: the first cut refused its own first invocation. A shell invoked with an inline command carries the entire command in its argv, so the shell that LAUNCHED the process matches any marker naming it, and run_selftests --list refused standalone naming the harness's own wrapper. A guard firing at 100 percent on a corpus with no cycle in it cannot be carried — it trains every reader to route around it and then protects nothing. Fixed by skipping an ancestor whose command line contains this process's own argv, which keeps a genuine shell-tool-shell-tool chain visible because the inner shell's command differs. ⚑ THE WARNING AGAINST THIS EXACT ERROR WAS WRITTEN INTO THE FUNCTION'S OWN DOCSTRING BEFORE THE MARKER WAS CHOSEN. The guard returns 0 as a SKIP so a correct de-duplication never reads as a red gate. Verified: ratchet --selftest 44 to 50 of 50, with the guard OBSERVED REFUSING on a real proc chain rather than a mock — the sql-F13 discipline, a guard nobody has seen refuse is not a guard. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A composition cycle between two correct tools is broken by a PPID WALK at both ends, not by a depth cap or an env var. ratchet.ancestry reads /proc stat and cmdline up to init; ratchet.check_cycle refuses when a marker is already in the caller's ancestry and returns 0 — a SKIP, never a failure, because a non-zero would turn correct de-duplication into a red gate someone routes around. BOTH ENDS CHECK AND THAT IS NOT REDUNDANT: guarding only the callee still forks a generation before refusing, guarding only the caller lets a hand-run callee re-enter. ⚑ THE FIRST CUT FIRED AT 100 PERCENT ON A CORPUS WITH NO CYCLE — the bash -c launcher carries the whole command in its argv, so the shell that STARTED the process matched a marker naming it. Excluded by skipping an ancestor whose command contains this process's own argv.}
}

@misc{gate-F7-selftest-framework-unstandardized,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The --selftest surface has no declaration model, so roster membership is by convention and nothing can distinguish a tool with a conforming selftest from one with none.},
