  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher. UNWITNESSED — no command today tells a conforming selftest from an absent one, which is itself the finding. Operator, 2026-08-24: our --selftest framework needs to be standardized, preferably using the same approach as farm.py. THREE MEASUREMENTS THIS SESSION MAKE THE CASE. ONE, run_selftests discovers 96 suites by convention and worklist_gate is a MEMBER that also INVOKES the roster — membership by discovery is what admitted the cycle, and a declared roster with a kind field could have refused it statically instead of at runtime by ppid walk. TWO, fix_notinscope.py and check_mode_contracts.py have NO --selftest at all, so the standing instruction run the selftest after you change a tool was UNSATISFIABLE for both, and two agents reported it as a gap rather than a failure. That is the non-command shape of meta-F1, in the verification layer. THREE, sppf_tree and sppf_drain likewise have none and a third agent verified them by running read modes by hand. THE MODEL ALREADY EXISTS ONE LAYER OVER: scripts/climode.py holds the farm.py-inspired Mode record with fields a discriminator can CHECK, adopted by 7 rosters and gated by check_mode_contracts at 0 disagreements. A Selftest record is the same move — declared not discovered, so that has-none, has-one, and is-a-fixture are three distinguishable states rather than one silence. Note the FIXTURE precedent already landed for the divergence census, which is the same distinction arriving in the adjacent surface. CLOSES WHEN such a record exists and a gate reads it. Until then this is exit 2 by construction, because a green here would be a fact about the census. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The --selftest surface has no declaration model, and no command distinguishes 'this tool has a conforming selftest' from 'this tool has none'. run_selftests discovers 96 suites by convention; membership is what let a member invoke the roster. Two tools changed under gate instructions this session have NO --selftest at all (fix_notinscope.py, check_mode_contracts.py), so 'run the selftest after you change it' was unsatisfiable — a non-command, the meta-F1 family. The fix named by the operator is the farm.py model already standardized for MODES in scripts/climode.py: a declared Selftest record whose fields a discriminator can CHECK. Closes when such a record exists and a gate reads it.}
}

@misc{gate-F8-cycle-exclusion-was-a-substring-test,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {check_cycle's launcher exclusion was a SUBSTRING test over sys.argv while its own comment claimed a process-KIND test, so it silently stops excluding a launcher whose copy of the command differs by one argument, re-arming the 100 percent false-positive it was patched for.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=mode-consolidation-r3. Measured on a genuine two-generation re-entry chain. The guard refused correctly at gen 1 while the launching shell still appeared in the hit list, because argv differed by the depth argument. Replaced by ratchet.is_interpreter_wrapper, an argv[0] basename test against a named wrapper set. ratchet selftest 50/50 to 57/57. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): check_cycle's exclusion said 'the test is process-kind, not string distance' in its own comment while comparing ' '.join(sys.argv) against each ancestor's cmdline — a SUBSTRING test wearing a kind test's description. MEASURED 2026-08-24 on a genuine two-generation chain (the nested-roster run gate-F6 says was never tested): the guard CORRECTLY refused at generation 1, but at that generation the process's argv was '... cyc_inner.py 1' while the launching shell held '... cyc_inner.py 0', so the shell was NOT excluded and appeared in the hit list beside the real ancestor. The exclusion had already stopped working, one argument apart, while the refusal it guards still looked right — so the 100%-false-positive it was patched for was re-armed SILENTLY and would return on any spelling change (python3 x.py vs /usr/bin/python3 /abs/x.py, a quoted arg, an inserted flag). Replaced by ratchet.is_interpreter_wrapper, a kind test over argv[0] basename against a named wrapper set, which does not depend on the command at all. The old positive-control case passed ONLY because the substring form let shells through: it drew its marker from the FIRST ancestor, which under kind-exclusion is a skipped wrapper, so it had to be re-pointed at the first NON-wrapper ancestor. ratchet 50/50 -> 57/57; genuine re-entry still refused at gen 1 with the launcher no longer in the hit list. gate-F6's stated uncertainty ('may be fooled by a genuine re-entry whose argv happens to be a substring') named the WRONG DIRECTION: the realized defect was the opposite — argv failing to match a launcher that should have been excluded.}
}

@misc{gate-F10-selftest-record-built,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {climode.Selftest makes has-none / has-one / IS-A-FIXTURE three declared states rather than one silence, and its runs_roster field lets the roster refuse a self-including member STATICALLY from source, which the ppid walk can only do at runtime after a generation has forked.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=mode-consolidation-r3. Closes gate-F7. Same farm.py Arm lineage as climode.Mode, not a fork. Verified live: run_selftests --declarations names scripts/worklist_gate.py with no process tree. Both halves ship — a declaration governs only declared tools, so an undeclared roster call is caught only by check_cycle. climode 36/36 to 63/63, run_selftests 55/55 to 66/66. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ⟡selftest-record BUILT (closes gate-F7). climode.Selftest is the climode.Mode move one layer over — same farm.py Arm lineage, not a fork: every declared field is one a discriminator can CHECK. SELFTEST_STATES makes has-none / has-one / IS-A-FIXTURE three states rather than one silence, which is the distinction run_selftests.discover() structurally cannot draw (it answers 'is this file in the roster', so a tool with no suite, a deliberate fixture, and a tool nobody looked at are the same absence from a 96-row list). Fields: state, why (REQUIRED for none — otherwise the state is a cheaper way to be silent than having no declaration), key, requires, tenant, runs_roster. contract() holds a declaration to its own fields, selftest_rows() holds DECLARED intent against DISCOVERED membership as two independent readings, and undeclared is REPORTED not omitted (a row-set of disagreements alone cannot distinguish 'all consistent' from 'nothing declared'). ⚑ THE ROSTER CAN NOW REFUSE A SELF-INCLUDING MEMBER STATICALLY: runs_roster is the field the fork bomb needed and nobody could write, and climode.self_including names scripts/worklist_gate.py from SOURCE at commit time — verified live via run_selftests --declarations, no process tree involved. It does NOT replace the ppid walk and both ship: a declaration governs only the tools that made one, so an undeclared tool whose suite grows a roster call tomorrow is invisible to it and caught only by check_cycle. --declarations is a READ with no baseline DELIBERATELY: a ratchet over a 5-of-98 seed would mint over an unassessed census, the ABSENT-baseline free pass. climode 36/36 -> 63/63, run_selftests 55/55 -> 66/66.}
}

