@misc{meta-F2-tell-refuted-by-check-claims,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The meta-F1 tell (an absence-word in a brief about a thing that works) is not a usable predicate -- check_claims.py already measured ABSENT at ~0/11 and rejected it, and an independent re-measurement over 48 plan documents fires at 9.8 percent with zero true positives in a seeded 30-hit sample.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=notation-agent. Measured 2026-08-24 over corpus ~/.claude/plans, 48 files, 26309 sentence-like units. An absence-word regex fires 2583 times (9.8 percent). A seeded sample of 30 contains 0 instances of the meta-F1 defect -- all are negated flag names, measured absences carrying witnesses, quoted error strings, policy statements or verified negatives. The prior art is scripts/check_claims.py lines 27-34, which records ABSENT at ~0/11 useful and the sibling ZERO predicate at 0/3 as DELIBERATELY NOT PREDICATES, chosen by running an over-firing census and reading every hit. Structural reason the tuning cannot be fixed -- absence-words appear in both the defect and its correction, so vocabulary cannot separate them. Conclusion is that no honest mechanical check exists on this tell, and no gate was built. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE meta-F1 TELL IS REFUTED, AND THE REFUTATION WAS ALREADY ON DISK BEFORE I WAS DISPATCHED. The brief's hypothesis -- 'the tell is an absence-word in a brief about a thing that works' -- was tested and rejected as a predicate by scripts/check_claims.py in July, and its docstring records the measurement: ABSENT ('does not exist') scored ~0/11 useful, nine of eleven hits being MATHEMATICS (Cantor's diagonal at Lawvere.agda:79) rather than tree claims; the sibling ZERO predicate ('no consumers') scored 0/3. check_claims ships TWO predicates, SHIM (16/16) and COUNT (3/4), both chosen by running an over-firing census (scratch/claim_census.py) and READING every hit -- the exact discipline the brief asked for, already performed, with absence-words the named casualty. I re-measured independently on the only durable brief-shaped corpus, the 48 plan documents in ~/.claude/plans: 26,309 sentence-like units, an absence-word regex (no/none/cannot/impossible/irreducible/missing/absent/blocked/blocker/unreachable/refuses/swallows/mis-reports/not built/would need/the only) fires on 2,583 = 9.8%. A seeded 30-hit sample contains ZERO instances of the meta-F1 defect. Every sampled hit is legitimate: a negated FLAG NAME (--no-verify, no-globs, no-tofu, font-embed/no-tofu), a correctly-measured absence carrying its witness ('No entry for the spelling ... the oracle verified'), a quoted error string (ModuleNotFoundError: No module named pikepdf), a policy statement ('no glob, no 3,478-file pass, no host risk'), or a genuine verified negative ('NO UNIT, AND NO LATTICE WAS EVER AVAILABLE'). Precision on this corpus is indistinguishable from zero at a 9.8% firing rate -- gate-F6's carried-guard criterion ('a guard firing at 100% on a corpus with no cycle in it cannot be carried') fails here by a wide margin. AND THE DEEPER REASON IS STRUCTURAL, NOT TUNING: the absence-word is present in BOTH the defect and its CORRECTION -- every one of the seventeen instances was WRITTEN UP using absence-words ('does NOT refuse', 'ZERO drained cores', 'never absent', 'was never an SCC'), so a vocabulary predicate cannot separate the error from the finding that catches it. It is a fact about vocabulary, not about correctness, which is census-green-is-a-fact-about-the-census in prose form. NO HONEST MECHANICAL CHECK EXISTS ON THIS TELL.}
}

