  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {meta-F1 is three findings and one counterexample, not one -- tool-capability-guess (8 of 17, defended by a bare invocation), stale-or-misread-fact (5 of 17, already gated by check_claims and cite-a-symbol-never-a-line), unchecked-inference-from-a-symptom (3 of 17), plus tmi-F16 which is the OPPOSITE direction and falsifies the finding's own claim that the error is always directional.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=notation-agent. Adjudicated 2026-08-24 by reading each cited instance finding rather than the brief's summaries. Class A tool-capability-guess is agda-F13, agda-F12, gate-F9, sql-F14, sql-F10, sql-F18, sql-F6, gate-F4 -- a claim about a tool's behaviour asserted without invoking the tool, and all eight would have been caught by one bare run, which struct-tools already makes the universal entry move. Class B stale-or-misread-fact is the EMPTY-vs-ABSENT inversion, sql-F18's moved line cites, the Bb5 SCC figure (indeg>0 unions each SCC with its downstream cone, so 1242 overstates 94 modules and 20 back-edges by 13x), and gate-F8's comment claiming a process-kind test over a substring test. Class C unchecked-inference is the census-poller machine-vs-database misattribution, tmi-F15's adjacency join, and the dispatcher's own unremeasured box-is-clear claim at dispatch time. The outlier tmi-F16 is inverted caution -- a cue warned as at-least-as-dangerous as a 48-percent-firing cue measured at 1.2 percent because a positional anchor bounds firing rate by construction. Its presence in the instance list refutes the word ALWAYS in the standing finding. The honest common structure is not a direction but a missing step -- a claim about an artefact's behaviour asserted at the confidence of a checked claim, which produces over-caution and under-caution alike. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): meta-F1 IS NOT ONE FINDING. Seventeen instances under one label, and they partition by MECHANISM into three classes with different costs, different detectability and different fixes -- merging them is why the brief's single tell could not be made to work, because a vocabulary predicate is the only thing all three share. (A) TOOL-CAPABILITY-GUESS -- 8 of 17, the dominant class and the only mechanically addressable one: agda-F13 (--literal 'swallows' a flag-shaped needle; the POSIX -- terminator had answered it since 2026-08-18), agda-F12 (--declare-modes 'mis-reports'; two tools reading two authorities, both correct), gate-F9 (fix_notinscope 'has no --selftest'; 250 lines, 56 cases, already RED at 55/56), sql-F14 (Ss5 slice-ht0 'BUILD IT'; built and default-on at sppf_db.py:637-693), sql-F10 (--verify listed as a mode to document; does not dispatch), sql-F18 (Ss9 read-scoping listed as open; already paid down by a3-route-pack-only, and rewriting it would destroy the --equiv reference arm), sql-F6 (--height 'refuses on drained cores'; exit 0 in 14 min and zero drained cores exist), gate-F4 ('mint the baselines' as one command; baselines mint only on the commit path). THE MECHANISM IS UNIFORM AND NARROW: a claim about a TOOL'S BEHAVIOUR asserted without invoking the tool. IT HAS A CHEAP DEFENCE THAT IS NOT A GATE -- run the tool bare, which this repo has already made universal ('running a tool bare lists its modes', struct-tools). Every one of the eight would have been caught by one bare invocation before the brief was written. (B) STALE-OR-MISREAD-FACT -- 5 of 17: gate-F1/the EMPTY-vs-ABSENT inversion (read a legend backwards; the STRONGEST setting reported as broken), sql-F10 again on the recorded-removal note, sql-F18's line-number cites having moved, Bb5 ('a 1,242-module SCC' where gen_build_makefiles reports indeg>0, which unions each SCC with its downstream cone -- real answer 94 modules and 20 back-edges, a 13x overstatement), gate-F8 (a comment claiming a process-kind test over a substring test). MECHANISM: a figure or a legend quoted correctly ONCE and re-quoted after its referent moved, or read against the wrong operator. THIS CLASS IS ALREADY GATED, and by the closest available analogue: check_claims.py is precisely a stale-prose-claim ratchet, and cite-a-symbol-never-a-line is already always-loaded in CLAUDE.md. (C) UNCHECKED-INFERENCE-FROM-A-SYMPTOM -- 3 of 17: Ss14 census-poller (a database-shaped cause given to a machine-shaped symptom; it was a selftest fork bomb at load 54.93), tmi-F15 (the prior-turn join called 'the one place the rejected adjacency join is RIGHT'; not adjacency at all, filler envelopes and tool_results sit between, correct join is nearest preceding human turn), and the session interruption itself ('the box was clear at load ~30 and falling' -- a claim made and not re-measured at dispatch time, which is the same shape and which the dispatcher correctly flagged as an UNESTABLISHED cause rather than asserting one). MECHANISM: a cause supplied in the vocabulary of the domain currently in context rather than the domain of the symptom. AND (D) IS A SINGLE OUTLIER THAT REFUTES THE LABEL'S DIRECTIONALITY: tmi-F16, where the CAUTION was the error -- a concession cue warned as 'at least as dangerous' as a 48%-firing cue measured at 1.2%, the least frequent of four, because a positional anchor bounds firing rate by construction. THE STANDING FINDING SAYS THE ERROR IS DIRECTIONAL, 'always toward routing around something that works'. tmi-F16 IS THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION and is in the instance list, so 'always' is falsified by the evidence the finding itself cites. The honest characterisation is not a direction but a MISSING STEP: a claim about an artefact's behaviour is being asserted at the confidence of a claim that was checked. Over-caution and under-caution are both what that produces.}
}

@misc{meta-F4-bare-run-is-not-the-defence,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Invoke-the-tool-bare is dangerous advice in this repo and is not the class-A defence — read the DECLARATION instead, because every class-A instance is a source fact.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher of=meta-F3. CORRECTS THE ONE ACTIONABLE RECOMMENDATION OF meta-F3, and the correction came from the operator within minutes of my relaying it. meta-F3 established that class A (a claim about a tool's behaviour asserted without invoking the tool) is 8 of 17 instances, and said its defence is a bare run, citing struct-tools' running-a-tool-bare-lists-its-modes. THAT ADVICE WOULD HAVE CAUSED THIS SESSION'S LARGEST INCIDENT. Measured today. run_selftests.py bare is the fork bomb that reached load average 54.93 with 68 concurrent processes on 10 cores over six generations. worklist_gate.py bare fans out per item and is recorded as agda-F1, the finding whose text is a-bare-run-melts-the-box. scope_fix WRITES BY DEFAULT, so a bare run mutates the Agda tree. agda_imports, split_lemmas and split_upstream raise a bare IndexError with no usage. ⚑ THE SAFE FORM READS THE DECLARATION AND NEVER THE RUNTIME. Use toolmodes --find, pycodemod --declare-modes, or the source. Ⓣ14 toolmodes-static was built for exactly this reason — launching every tool to enumerate its modes WAS the hazard, which makes the bare-run advice a proposal to undo a landed fix. ⚑⚑ AND THE DECLARATION READ LOSES NOTHING, because all eight class-A instances are SOURCE facts. sppf_db --verify does not dispatch (one site, role=doc). --declare-modes and --divergence read different authorities and both are correct. slice-ht0 is default-on in pack_core_sql's visible signature. fix_notinscope has a 250-line 56-case suite. Launching was never required to catch any of them, so the safe reading is not a weaker instrument — it is the same instrument without the hazard. ⚑⚑⚑ THIS IS CLASS A ABOUT THE CLASS-A DEFENCE. An agent asserted what a bare run does, I relayed it, and neither of us checked it against agda-F1, which sits in the same ledger. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The class-A defence 'invoke the tool bare before claiming what it does' is DANGEROUS ADVICE in this repo and would have caused this session's largest incident. run_selftests bare is the fork bomb that reached load 54.93 with 68 processes; worklist_gate bare fans out per item (agda-F1); scope_fix WRITES BY DEFAULT so a bare run mutates the tree; agda_imports, split_lemmas and split_upstream raise a bare IndexError. The safe form reads the DECLARATION, never the runtime: toolmodes --find, pycodemod --declare-modes, or the source. Ⓣ14 toolmodes-static exists precisely because launching every tool to enumerate its modes was the hazard. ⚑ ALL EIGHT class-A instances are SOURCE facts: --verify does not dispatch, --declare-modes and --divergence read different authorities, slice-ht0 is default-on in a visible signature, fix_notinscope has a 250-line suite. Launching was never required to catch any of them.}
}

@misc{gate-F12-tenant-mechanism-has-zero-adopters,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Zero of 98 discovered selftest suites declare a store tenant, so the runner's whole TENANT mechanism has no adopters -- including schema.py, which calls store.tenant and is the tool the mechanism was built for.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r2. Witness -- run_selftests --pragmas over 98 suites reports 1 requires and 0 tenant, and pycodemod --calls tenant reports 1 def at store.py:303 with 1 call at schema.py:187. Fix is in jea/metalanguage, owned by sppf-ingestion. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): run_selftests --pragmas (built this round) censuses the two pragmas the runner ACTS on. Over 98 discovered suites: 1 declares a dependency (jea_circuit.py numpy), and ZERO declare a tenant. The entire TENANT mechanism -- regex, _tenant(), the append in BOTH _cmd branches, a documented incident -- has no adopters: the rewire_only shape (1 def, 0 consumers) in pragma form. Load-bearing, not cosmetic: schema.py is a discovered suite that CALLS store.tenant (pycodemod --calls: 1 def store.py:303, 1 call schema.py:187) and declares no tenant -- the measured 2026-08-21 incident where schema.py --selftest resolved LIVE and ran reclaim(dry_run=True) over ~128M obs rows for 35+ min on the pre-commit path. Fix belongs to jea/metalanguage (sppf-ingestion), so REPORTED not edited. ⚑ The census cannot say the other 97 are clean -- it reports what a suite SAYS and cannot distinguish 'needs nothing' from 'never asked'; the mode prints that boundary rather than a defect count.}
