@misc{gate-F16-wrapper-set-fails-closed-and-should,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {An unlisted wrapper is not excluded, so the cycle guard fails CLOSED toward a redundant skip rather than open toward a missed cycle -- the right direction, because check_cycle returns 0 and a false positive can never redden a gate.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r2. Supersedes the mis-named key gate-F16-wrapper-set-fails-open-correctly, kept as residue. Witness -- under the Claude harness, ratchet --selftest resolved its first non-wrapper ancestor to the harness binary itself, which is not in the 16-name _WRAPPER_ARGV0, so check_cycle printed a SKIP and the suite still passed 57/57 because the case asserts agreement with the real chain by construction. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓧ2 ANSWERED. ⚑ SUPERSEDES THE KEY gate-F16-wrapper-set-fails-open-correctly, which I registered one command earlier with a name that CONTRADICTS its own body -- the direction is CLOSED, not open. Recorded rather than silently replaced. THE ANSWER: _WRAPPER_ARGV0 is a 16-name frozenset. A wrapper NOT in it is NOT excluded, so it stays in the ancestry chain and can match a marker -- the guard fails CLOSED, toward a false REFUSAL, never toward missing a cycle. That is the right direction here, and the reason is that check_cycle returns 0 (a SKIP), not non-zero: a false positive costs a redundant skip, never a red gate, so the route-around pressure that killed the substring form does not re-arm. Fail-open would silently lose a cycle, which is what the 68-process incident cost. ⚑ MEASURED LIVE THIS ROUND: running ratchet --selftest under the Claude harness, the positive control's first non-wrapper ancestor resolved to the harness binary itself (.../native-binary/claude --output-format ...), which carries a command and is not in the set, so it was admitted as a real ancestor and check_cycle printed a SKIP. The suite still passed 57/57 because the case asserts the guard AGREES with the real chain by construction rather than expecting a fixed answer. The staleness is real, observed, and absorbed. RECOMMENDATION: leave the set alone -- adding 'claude' fits the predicate to one harness, and the construction-not-expectation case shape is what makes an unlisted wrapper a skip rather than a failure.}
}

@misc{gate-F16-wrapper-set-fails-open-correctly,
