@misc{gate-F17-declarations-baseline-condition-not-threshold,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {--declarations earns a baseline on a CONDITION, not a count: when every declaration it holds is one selftest_rows could report False for -- which today is 5 of 5, so more rows would not help and the real precondition is adopting the falsifiable tenant pragma first.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r2. Witness -- the falsifiable states are none, fixture, runs_roster, and any key/requires/tenant, all already declared for the 5. run_selftests --pragmas reports 1 requires and 0 tenant over 98 suites, so tenant is the field with teeth and gate-F12 is the paydown that must precede a mint. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): WHEN DOES --declarations EARN A BASELINE? NOT AT A COUNT -- the question presupposes a threshold, and a threshold is the wrong shape here. A ratchet is honest when its census can DISAGREE with the tree, and for this mode the disagreeable population is not the 93. Per gate-F14, a bare suite declaration on a discovered file cannot fail, so a baseline minted at 50 or 93 declared rows would be a baseline over rows that are green by construction -- more of them does not make it mean more. THE CONDITION, not a number: --declarations earns a baseline when EVERY declaration it holds is one selftest_rows could report False for. Concretely that is the union of the three falsifiable states -- state='none' (checkable: discovery must NOT reach it), state='fixture' (checkable: must not be a roster member), and runs_roster=True (checkable: self_including must name it) -- plus any suite carrying key/requires/tenant, each of which contract() can contradict. Today that set is 5 of 5, which is why the seed is already at 100 percent of what a gate could bite on and the baseline would still be vacuous: there is nothing it could ever refuse. ⚑ THE REAL PRECONDITION IS THEREFORE A DIFFERENT MODE, AND IT NOW EXISTS: --pragmas, built this round, reads the two pragmas the runner ACTS on and found 1 requires / 0 tenant over 98 suites (gate-F12). Tenant is the falsifiable field -- a suite that reaches a store and declares no tenant is a disagreement a gate CAN name. So the sequencing is: adopt tenant pragmas on the store-reaching suites first, THEN a --declarations baseline has a population with teeth. Minting before that is the ABSENT free pass of gate-F1 with extra steps.}
}

@misc{meta-F4-cycle-guard-is-not-a-fanout-bound,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {ratchet.check_cycle bounds RE-ENTRY and not fan-out WIDTH, so a roster-invoking suite is not made safe by it, and runs_roster=True is the declaration-read form of that hazard.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize. AMENDS meta-F4. One legitimate non-recursive worklist_gate --selftest pass fanned to 40 workers, load 6 to 29 in 90s, and killing the launcher orphaned all 40 to init. The ppid guard behaved correctly throughout. climode.Selftest(runs_roster=True) on run_selftests and worklist_gate makes the property readable at commit time via self_including, which is the gateable form. Confirmed twice more this session by the amending agent running run_selftests and findings.py bare. pgrep -cf selftest reads 46 due to the harness bash wrapper quoting the query, so pgrep -af is the honest form. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): AMENDS meta-F4, which is INCOMPLETE IN A WAY I MEASURED. meta-F4 says the safe form reads the DECLARATION and never the runtime, and names run_selftests bare as the fork bomb. What it does NOT say is that the cycle guard is not the defence against fan-out WIDTH. ratchet.check_cycle is a ppid walk: it bounds RE-ENTRY (a roster containing its own caller) and nothing else. One LEGITIMATE non-recursive pass of worklist_gate --selftest fanned to 40 workers, load 6 to 29 in 90 seconds, and killing the launcher orphaned all 40 to init. The guard did its job correctly and the box still went down. So 'the cycle guard refuses this' is not a reason a roster-invoking suite is safe to launch. AND runs_roster=True NOW SAYS SO FROM SOURCE -- climode.Selftest(runs_roster=True) is declared on run_selftests and worklist_gate, and self_including reads it at commit time, so the roster-invoking property is a DECLARATION-READ fact and needs no launch to establish. That declaration is the gateable form of the hazard the ppid walk only ever caught after a generation had forked. CONFIRMED AGAIN THIS SESSION, TWICE, BY ME: I ran run_selftests bare expecting a mode list (it started the roster; killed at the 120s timeout) and then findings.py bare (the scratch/ homonym, which walks the Agda tree on import). Both were the class-A defence meta-F4 forbids, committed by the agent amending meta-F4. The bare-run reflex survives reading the finding, which is why the amendment is a declaration to READ rather than a caution to remember. Instrument note: pgrep -cf selftest read 46 because the harness's bash wrapper quotes the query into its own cmdline; pgrep -af shows the wrapper as the only match, so the COUNT is an artifact and the LISTING is the honest form.}
}

@misc{gate-F18-import-reach-is-not-store-access,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Store reach for the selftest roster is 12 of 99 by MODULE-LEVEL import closure, not 54 by ast.walk, because a function-local import is a different edge, and reach is necessary but not sufficient for the strict-tenant abort.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize. Built run_selftests --tenants as a 2x2 of reaches-a-store against declares-a-tenant. 12 OWED, 0 declared, 0 STRAY, 87 clear. The first cut said 54, inflated by _pycodemod_sql._pg_probe_con, a lazy cached exception-swallowed import store inside a function only the EXPLAIN modes call, reached by ten-hop chains from an Agda lexer and a text gate. Live controls: schema.py exits 2 with no record, catalog_query 7/8 with a record, while catalog_db, set1_ratchet_cores and query_defs pass despite being OWED. Known blind spot printed with the count, per sql-F23: a store-reacher with no import edge is invisible, so this ships as a READ and not a gate. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): V5 tenant-pragma MEASURED, and the honest count is 12 of 99, not 54. Built run_selftests --tenants: REACHES-A-STORE (import closure over 11 seed modules) against DECLARES-A-TENANT (the pragma), reported as a 2x2 (OWED / declared / STRAY / reach-no-store). Result 2026-08-24: 12 OWED, 0 declared, 0 STRAY, 87 reach no store. MY FIRST CUT REPORTED 54 OF 99 AND IT WAS A FALSE POSITIVE AT SCALE, caught by reading the ROUTES rather than the count. agda_lex (an Agda lexer) and check_sorted_order (a text gate) both 'reached' store via a ten-hop chain ending pycodemod->_pycodemod_sql->store. That edge is _pycodemod_sql._pg_probe_con: a lazy, cached, exception-swallowed 'import store' INSIDE a function only the EXPLAIN-probing modes call, which also calls connect()/sandbox_connect() directly and never store.tenant. Importing pycodemod does not execute it. Fix: walk the MODULE BODY (descending into module-scope if/try/for, skipping FunctionDef/ClassDef) instead of ast.walk. 54 to 12. A DEFERRED IMPORT IS A DIFFERENT EDGE FROM A MODULE-LEVEL ONE, and collapsing them made the census report the import GRAPH where the question was what RUNS. AND EVEN 12 OVERSTATES THE ABORT POPULATION -- reach is necessary, not sufficient. Live checks: schema.py exits 2 with no record (aborts), catalog_query 7/8 with a record (a real defect, pre-existing), while catalog_db 12/12, set1_ratchet_cores 12/12 and query_defs 19/19 all PASS despite being OWED, because their tenant() calls sit under mode guards that --selftest does not take. So OWED reads 'must declare before it can safely reach the resolver', never 'is currently broken'. KNOWN BLIND SPOT, printed with the count: a store-reacher with no import edge is invisible here, which sql-F23 measured on query_defs (unsayable tenant, missed by --calls tenant, --guarded and --literal alike). This is why --tenants ships as a READ and not a gate.}
}

@misc{gate-F19-store-argv-was-the-last-reader,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {ratchet.store_argv was the last reader of SUBSTRATE_EXPLICIT_TENANT after store.tenant deleted it, so the enforcement half stayed permanently advisory while its docstring promised the two halves flip together.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize. pycodemod --literal found 9 sites, all 9 in ratchet.py: the live gate plus five cases pinning the advisory branch. store.py mentioned it only in prose saying it was gone. A coordination promise in prose is not a coupling -- the halves shared a VARIABLE, and when one stopped reading it the promise became false silently. Repaired to refuse unconditionally with store.tenant's require= remedy line. The advisory case was deleted rather than migrated, per sql-F24, and replaced by env-absent and env-inert cases so a reintroduced read fails. ratchet 57/57 to 58/58. Exactly one production caller passes needs_store=True (set1_ratchet_cores, 12/12 unchanged). ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE TWO HALVES DID NOT FLIP TOGETHER, AND ONLY THE DOCSTRING SAID THEY HAD. ratchet.store_argv's text promised it was 'ADVISORY until SUBSTRATE_EXPLICIT_TENANT=1, matching store.tenant's own gate so the two halves flip together'. store.tenant flipped on 2026-08-24 (sql-F22) and DELETED the variable, stating in its own docstring that setting it is inert and unsetting it restores nothing. store_argv kept reading it. Measured with pycodemod --literal: 9 sites remained and ALL NINE were in ratchet.py -- the live gate at :319 plus five selftest sites pinning the advisory branch -- while store.py mentioned it only in prose saying it was gone. So the ENFORCEMENT half sat permanently advisory, keyed on a switch nothing in the tree could set, and its docstring was the only evidence anyone would have that it had not flipped. A COORDINATION PROMISE IN PROSE IS NOT A COUPLING: the halves were never wired to each other, they were wired to a shared VARIABLE, and when one side stopped reading it the promise became false silently. REPAIRED: store_argv now refuses unconditionally, prints the same require= remedy line store.tenant does, and its cases drive it with no environment at all. The old 'advisory does not refuse' case was DELETED rather than migrated -- its subject no longer exists -- which is the same move sql-F24 records for schema_selftest's two flagless cases. Two cases replaced it: one with the variable absent, one with it set to 0, both asserting refusal, so a reintroduced read of it fails a case instead of quietly restoring configurability. ratchet 57/57 to 58/58. Scope note: exactly one production caller passes needs_store=True (set1_ratchet_cores, 12/12 before and after), the other three sites being ratchet's own cases, so the blast radius of the flip was one tool.}
}

@misc{gate-F20-refused-is-a-sixth-verdict-kind,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A suite a guard refuses at the door (no record, exit 2) is a different verdict kind from one that ran and printed nothing, and run_selftests collapsed both into 'silent' until a sixth kind 'refused' was added.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize. The run_selftests contract half of sql-F19. Measured: schema.py --selftest exits 2 mid-run with no record under tenant-mandatory, typed 'silent', which reads as a missing emitter and sends the reader into the suite for an upstream cause. Exit 2 is what every guard here uses for a bad invocation. The discriminator is the ABSENCE of a record and not the code -- a suite reporting 7/8 then exiting 2 is judged by its numbers, pinned by a case -- and a kill signal still outranks the code so 137 stays 'unfit'. All three reader projections updated together: the --last _WHY table, the ledger kind column, and the in-run failure printer. run_selftests 73/73 to 86/86. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): S17 strict-tenant-abort ANSWERED on the run_selftests side. sql-F19 named the repair as spanning store.py, schema_selftest.py and the run_selftests CONTRACT. sppf-ingestion did their two files (sql-F24: the two flagless cases were RETIRED, not migrated, and their coverage relocated onto _tenant_named, schema 221 to 224/224). The contract half was a MISSING VERDICT KIND. run_selftests typed a suite by (did it emit a counted record, was it killed by a signal), which collapsed two opposite situations into 'silent': a suite that ran to completion printing nothing (a REPORTING defect in the suite) and a suite a GUARD refused at the door (not the suite's defect at all, and it assessed nothing). MEASURED under tenant-mandatory: schema.py --selftest exits 2 having run part of its cases with no record emitted, which typed 'silent' and reads as a missing emitter -- sending a reader into the suite to hunt a bug that is upstream. ADDED the kind 'refused' = no counted record AND exit 2, the code every guard in this toolkit uses for a bad INVOCATION (check_argv unknown flag, mutating_argv unstated intent, store.tenant unstated tenant). THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE ABSENCE OF A RECORD, NOT THE CODE: a suite that reported 7/8 and then exited 2 is judged by its numbers, pinned by a case, or a complete run would be erased into 'refused'. A signal still outranks the code, so 137 stays 'unfit' and a guard is not blamed for an OOM. All THREE reader projections were updated together (--last's _WHY table, the ledger's kind column via verdict_kind, and the in-run failure printer), because this file records the derived-view defect four times and the in-run printer is the projection an operator actually reads. run_selftests 73/73 to 86/86.}
}

@misc{gate-F21-tenant-baseline-honest-but-not-yet,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A tenant-scoped baseline IS honest where a membership-scoped one was not, because STRAY makes the field falsifiable, but it must not be minted until reach is measured by something the import graph cannot hide from.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize. Answers gate-F17 now its condition is partly met. gate-F14's bare rows cannot fail (selftest_rows checks membership, true by construction). A tenant declaration can be wrong in BOTH directions and --tenants reports both: OWED and STRAY, the latter having no analogue among membership rows. But store_reach reads import edges and sql-F23 measured a store-reacher with none, so a ratchet on it would refuse what it sees and certify the rest. The sequencing is three steps not two: adopt pragmas on the 12 OWED, then measure reach non-structurally, then the baseline has teeth. gate-F17 collapsed the middle step because --tenants did not exist to expose its own boundary. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): GATE-F17'S CONDITION IS NOW PARTLY MET AND THE ANSWER IS A QUALIFIED YES -- a tenant-scoped baseline WOULD be honest where a membership-scoped one was not, but it must not be minted today, and the blocker is not the count. WHY IT IS HONEST WHERE gate-F14 WAS NOT. gate-F14 established that a bare Selftest() row on a discovered file CANNOT FAIL: selftest_rows checks 'tool in disc', true by construction for a discovered file, so 93 such rows convert an UNDECLARED boundary into a green census that assessed nothing. A tenant declaration is structurally different because it can be WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, and --tenants reports both cells: OWED (reaches a store, declares nothing -- can abort) and STRAY (declares a tenant, reaches no store -- appends a tenant flag to a tool that opens none, which check_argv's own note calls a flag that reads as meaningful and does nothing). STRAY is the cell that makes the field falsifiable, and no bare membership row has an analogue of it. So gate-F17's stated condition -- every declaration is one the census could report False for -- is SATISFIED by the tenant field in a way it is not by membership. WHY IT STILL MUST NOT BE A GATE TODAY, and this is the part gate-F17 did not anticipate. The census that computes 'reaches' has a KNOWN blind spot: store_reach reads IMPORT EDGES, and sql-F23 measured a store-reacher with no such edge (query_defs's inlined sandbox_connect-else-connect, invisible to --calls tenant, --guarded and --literal alike). A ratchet minted on this refuses the population it can see and CERTIFIES the rest -- census-green-is-a-fact-about-the-census with an exit code, which is exactly the failure gate-F17 was written to avoid one layer up. THE SEQUENCING IS THEREFORE THREE STEPS, NOT TWO: (1) adopt tenant pragmas on the 12 OWED, (2) measure reach by something the import graph cannot hide from, THEN (3) a --declarations baseline has a population with teeth. gate-F17 collapsed (1) and (2) into 'adopt tenant pragmas first' because --tenants did not exist yet to expose its own boundary. My own round-2 framing was right that tenant was the only falsifiable candidate and wrong that adoption alone was the precondition.}
}

@misc{gate-F22-tenant-pragma-adopted,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {All twelve store-reaching suites now declare a sandbox tenant, which completes step one of three and does NOT license a baseline.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher. MEASURED 2026-08-24 after ⟡tenant-mandatory. run_selftests --tenants read 12 OWED / 0 declared before, 0 OWED / 12 declared / 0 STRAY after, over 99 discovered suites with 87 reaching no store. Every declaration is sandbox, and that was VERIFIED not assumed — pycodemod --literal -- --live over the three largest found only usage strings and docstrings, no selftest path hardcoding live. Two suites got more than a one-line pragma because their history demanded it. set1_ratchet_cores is the recorded incident where --quiet, a gate that only COUNTS Set1 debt, reached write_events with prune=True, ran about 90 minutes, died on DeadlockDetected and destroyed a live --equiv comparison window (3 cores comparable to 0) — for that suite sandbox is not a default but the only admissible value. catalog_query carries a note that it is separately RED at 7 of 8 WITH a record, a real pre-existing defect surfaced by the census and not caused by it, so a green there would be the thing to distrust. ⚑ THE REMAINING TWO STEPS STAND AND THIS ENTRY EXISTS TO STOP THEM BEING SKIPPED: measure reach non-structurally, THEN baseline. sql-F23 measured a store-reacher import closure cannot see — query_defs held an inlined sandbox_connect-else-connect and declared no --live at all, so its tenant was UNSAYABLE and invisible to --calls tenant. A ratchet on the present reach signal would refuse what it sees and CERTIFY THE REST, which is gate-F21's stated hazard and gate-F14's shape one layer over. ⚑⚑ ADOPTION DID NOT MAKE THE CENSUS CLEAN. 86 of 99 suites declare NEITHER field, and --pragmas prints that it cannot distinguish needs-nothing from never-asked. Twelve declarations bought a falsifiable field over the population that has one, not a green tree. Verified: run_selftests 86 of 86, schema 224 of 224 under --sandbox, query_defs 19 of 19, sppf_db 243 of 243. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): STEP ONE OF THREE COMPLETE. All 12 store-reaching suites now declare a tenant: run_selftests --tenants reads 0 OWED, 12 declared, 0 STRAY, 87 reach no store, of 99 discovered. Every one is sandbox, verified before choosing — no selftest path in any of them carries a --live literal, only usage strings. THE REMAINING TWO STEPS STAND: measure reach non-structurally, THEN baseline. Do not mint on this. sql-F23 measured a store-reacher that import-closure cannot see (query_defs' inlined sandbox_connect-else-connect made its tenant UNSAYABLE), so a ratchet on the current reach signal would refuse what it sees and CERTIFY THE REST — gate-F21's stated hazard. ⚑ AND THE ADOPTION DID NOT MAKE THE CENSUS CLEAN: 86 of 99 suites declare NEITHER a tenant nor a dependency, and --pragmas prints that it cannot tell needs-nothing from never-asked. Twelve declarations bought a falsifiable field over the population that has one, not a green tree.}
}

@misc{gate-F23-two-lenses-disagree-on-two-suites,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A second call-site lens over the same suites disagrees with import-reach on exactly two, both real, and a third tenant mechanism turns up in the disagreement.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher. STEP TWO OF THREE for the tenant baseline, measured 2026-08-24. run_selftests --tenants now runs TWO lenses over the same 99 suites and prints only where they disagree. store_reach walks module-level IMPORT EDGES and answers what importing this executes. The new _connect_sites walks CALL SITES at any depth, including inside function bodies, and answers whether the suite can open a store at all. THE TWO ARE WRONG IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS BY CONSTRUCTION, which is the correlated-instruments-agreeing-is-not-coverage discipline: coverage is the UNION of blind spots, so a reader needs both readings and needs to see the gap. RESULT: 2 of 99 disagree, both genuine, ZERO false positives from the connect-is-a-common-verb risk the mode's own docstring warns about. ONE, store.py DEFINES connect and sandbox_connect, so it cannot import a seed to reach itself — a structural blind spot of the import lens rather than a defect in any suite. TWO, scratch/scope_store.py is the real miss: a function-local import store followed by _store.sandbox_connect(reset=False), which _local_imports correctly excludes because a deferred import is a different edge from a module-level one. That is sql-F23's shape reproduced independently on a second file. ⚑ AND THE ADJUDICATION FOUND MORE THAN THE MISS. scope_store selects its tenant by ENV VAR — SUBSTRATE_SANDBOX at _connect_catalog — a THIRD mechanism that neither the --sandbox/--live flags nor the selftest-tenant pragma can express, and its own selftest sets the variable at two sites. So it is sandbox-safe today by a route the census structurally cannot report, and adding a pragma there would have papered over the finding rather than recording it. ⚑⚑ THE MODE PRINTS CANDIDATES, NEVER VERDICTS, because the call names are unresolved — resolving which connect is store.connect would require importing the module, which is exactly what the subprocess design exists to avoid. Same stance as --label-families. Verified: run_selftests 86 of 86 before and after. STANDING until reach is expressible for the env-var mechanism too, at which point step three can mint against the union. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): STEP TWO OF THREE. run_selftests --tenants now carries a SECOND, DECORRELATED lens: store_reach walks IMPORT EDGES, _connect_sites walks CALL SITES at any depth, and only the DISAGREEMENTS print. Measured 2026-08-24: 2 of 99 suites reach no seed by import yet name a store-opening call, and BOTH are real with zero false positives from the connect-is-a-common-verb risk. store.py DEFINES connect and sandbox_connect so it cannot import a seed to reach itself — a structural blind spot of the import lens. scratch/scope_store.py is the genuine miss: a function-local import store then _store.sandbox_connect, which _local_imports correctly excludes because a deferred import is a different edge. ⚑ AND scope_store SELECTS ITS TENANT BY ENV VAR (SUBSTRATE_SANDBOX), a THIRD mechanism neither the CLI flags nor the pragma covers, and its own selftest sets it — so it is sandbox-safe today by a route the census cannot express. A pragma there would paper over the finding. THE POINT IS NOT THAT ONE LENS IS RIGHT: import-reach answers what importing this executes, the call lens answers whether it can open a store at all. Coverage is the UNION.}
}

@misc{gate-F24-no-value-may-mean-i-opt-out,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A declaration vocabulary may gain a value naming a new tenant, but never a value meaning I-opt-out-of-naming-one — the second makes a bypass declarable and therefore permanent.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher. BUILT AND BACKED OUT 2026-08-24, and the reason is worth more than the code was. gate-F23 surfaced scratch/scope_store.py as a store-reacher the import lens cannot see, and adjudicating it found the suite selects its tenant from the SUBSTRATE_SANDBOX env var rather than a CLI flag. Its docstring defends that as a design choice: an env var here, not a parameter, because this is a leaf — scope_fix's entry points are CLI-driven and many, and threading a flag through every one of them to reach a verification-only path would put rehearsal plumbing in the production signature. I ACCEPTED THAT RATIONALE AND EXTENDED THE PRAGMA to describe it, adding a third value self plus a separate reader and a fourth census bucket. THE OPERATOR REFUSED IT IN ONE SENTENCE: this is exactly why we have --dry versus --apply and --live versus --sandbox, and why those are REQUIRED parameters for any command that needs either. ⚑ STATING THE TENANT IS THE PRODUCTION SIGNATURE, NOT PLUMBING AROUND IT. The parallel settles it — nobody argues that threading MUTATION INTENT through a leaf is test scaffolding. A tool that mutates says so and a tool that picks a store says which one, for the same reason and at the same cost. Verification-only-therefore-exempt is exemption-for-the-uncovered-case, which CLAUDE.md's standing rule names as the loophole that swallows the rule. ⚑⚑ THE ASYMMETRY IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART. A value meaning I-set-my-own does not merely fail to fix the bypass, it makes the bypass DECLARABLE — permanent, blessed, and invisible to the very census built to find it. That is gate-F14's lesson with the sign flipped: a declaration that cannot fail is worse than none, and a declaration that MISDESCRIBES is worse still because it reads as assessed. A THIRD TENANT is a different proposal and is admissible — fixture, named on the CLI, mandatory like the other two (operator, same exchange: if we need a third tenant, --fixture, I could see that). EXTENDING WHAT YOU CAN NAME IS NOT EXTENDING WHAT YOU NEED NOT NAME. Backed out in full: regex, reader, bucket and count. scope_store stays UNDECLARED and visible as a call-site-lens disagreement in --tenants, which is the honest state until its env var becomes a stated tenant. Verified run_selftests 86 of 86 before and after. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I BUILT A PRAGMA VALUE MEANING 'I SET MY OWN TENANT' AND THE OPERATOR REFUSED IT. scope_store.py picks its store from SUBSTRATE_SANDBOX rather than a flag, defending it as 'an env var here, not a parameter, because this is a leaf — threading a flag through every entry point to reach a verification-only path would put rehearsal plumbing in the production signature.' I read that as a reasoned choice the vocabulary should accommodate and added a third value to DESCRIBE it. Operator: 'this is EXACTLY why we have --dry vs --apply, --live vs --sandbox, and why those are REQUIRED parameters for any command that needs either.' STATING THE TENANT IS THE PRODUCTION SIGNATURE. Nobody argues that threading MUTATION INTENT through a leaf is rehearsal plumbing. Verification-only-therefore-exempt is exemption-for-the-uncovered-case, the loophole that swallows the rule. ⚑ THE ASYMMETRY IS THE FINDING: a value meaning 'I opt out of stating one' does not describe a bypass, it makes the bypass DECLARABLE and therefore permanent and therefore invisible to the census built to find it. A THIRD TENANT is admissible and different — fixture, named on the CLI, mandatory like the other two. Extending WHAT YOU CAN NAME is not extending WHAT YOU NEED NOT NAME. Backed out; scope_store stays undeclared and visible as a call-site-lens disagreement.}
}

@misc{gate-F25-a-deferred-import-truncates-the-chain,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A deferred import does not merely hide its own module from a reach census — it truncates every chain through it, and hoisting one surfaced four suites at once.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher. MEASURED 2026-08-24, closing the last false row of the tenant census. scope_store.py imported store INSIDE _connect_catalog, and TWO READERS INCLUDING ME READ THAT AS DELIBERATE LAZINESS. It was a path-setup artifact: the module-scope sys.path insert at the top of that file reaches only scratch/, while store lives in jea/metalanguage/, so the import had to follow a second insert performed inside the function. THE FIX WAS THE PATH, NOT THE IMPORT — both directories inserted once at module scope, then a plain top-level import store. ⚑ THE PROPAGATION IS THE FINDING. run_selftests --tenants read 0 OWED / 13 declared / 1 STRAY before the hoist and 3 OWED / 13 declared / 0 STRAY immediately after: scope_fix, drop_dead_renames and rewire_to_leaves had all been reading as reaching no store while every one of them genuinely does, via scope_fix to scope_store to store. Import-closure reach stopped one hop short and everything behind that hop was invisible. ⚑⚑ AND THE THREE THAT SURFACED ARE THE WORST POSSIBLE ONES TO HAVE MISSED. All three REWRITE AGDA SOURCE. A suite that rehearsed a tree-wide import rewrite against the live corpus is the wrong-tenant failure with the widest blast radius in this toolkit, and rewire_to_leaves errs WIDE by design. ⚑ THE STRAY ROW WAS THE TELL AND IT WAS WORTH KEEPING RATHER THAN SUPPRESSING. After scope_store declared a tenant but before the hoist, the census labelled it STRAY — declares a tenant, reaches no store — which was FALSE, and the call-site lens contradicted it two lines below in the same output. A census that prints its own boundary is what made the next step obvious. Final state: 0 OWED, 16 declared, 0 STRAY, 83 reach no store, of 99. The call-site disagreement fell from 2 to 1, the survivor being store.py itself, which cannot import a seed to reach itself — structural, not a gap. Verified run_selftests 86 of 86 throughout and scope_store unchanged at its pre-existing vacuous 0/0 (sandbox ref is empty, its own recorded state). STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): HOISTING ONE DEFERRED IMPORT SURFACED FOUR SUITES. scope_store imported store INSIDE _connect_catalog — not deliberate laziness but a path-setup artifact: the module-scope sys.path insert reaches only scratch/, and store lives in jea/metalanguage/, so the import had to follow a second insert done in the function. Two readers, me included, read that placement as intent. THE FIX IS THE PATH, NOT THE IMPORT: both directories inserted once at module scope, import store at the top. ⚑ A DEFERRED IMPORT DOES NOT MERELY HIDE ITS OWN MODULE, IT TRUNCATES EVERY CHAIN THROUGH IT. run_selftests --tenants went 0 OWED / 13 declared / 1 STRAY to 3 OWED / 13 declared / 0 STRAY the instant the import moved — scope_fix, drop_dead_renames and rewire_to_leaves had been reading as reaching no store while all three genuinely do, via scope_fix to scope_store to store. All three REWRITE AGDA SOURCE, so the wrong tenant there has the widest blast radius in the toolkit. Now 0 OWED / 16 declared / 0 STRAY, and the call-site lens disagreement fell 2 to 1 — the survivor being store.py, which cannot import a seed to reach itself and is structural rather than a gap.}
}

@misc{gate-F26-the-reach-graph-is-cut-in-23-places,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The import-closure reach signal is cut in 23 places, not one — gate-F25 was an instance of a class I read as an incident, and a baseline on that signal would certify a graph it cannot traverse.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher of=gate-F25. MEASURED 2026-08-24 by a THIRD LENS added to run_selftests --tenants: for every local module, which store SEEDS does it import from inside a function body. That is the precise shape gate-F25 recorded, made queryable instead of anecdotal. IT READS 23. schema_rows, schema_decl, schema_diag, schema_queue, query_builders, query_defs, query_registry, sppf_db, sppf_tree, sppf_drain, sppf_vocab, sppf_decode, sql_lift, sql_census, pipeflow, numpy_builders, ddl_lifecycle, dec_drain, tier_readset, reuse_tui, _pycodemod_sql, schema_selftest, and scope_store. ⚑ THE SCAN DELIBERATELY COVERS EVERY LOCAL MODULE AND NOT ONLY THE 99 DISCOVERED SUITES. The import that hid four suites happened to live in one, but nothing makes that necessary: a deferred seed import anywhere on a chain truncates every suite behind it, so scoping this scan to suites would have reproduced the very defect it exists to find, one level out — a census blind exactly where the previous one was. ⚑⚑ AND scope_store IS STILL ON THE LIST. I hoisted its store import last turn and it continues to defer catalog_db, query_builders and schema, so my conclusion that the last known-false row was gone was too clean by three seeds. That is the same over-tidy reading that made gate-F25 look like a one-file repair. THE STANDING CONSEQUENCE FOR STEP THREE: a ratchet minted on import-closure reach would certify a graph cut in 23 places, which is gate-F21's refuse-what-it-sees-and-certify-the-rest with a measured denominator. ⚑ NOT A DEFECT LIST. A deferred import can be exactly right — an optional backend, a heavy module a single mode needs, or a genuine cycle break. What it ALWAYS is, is a place the reach census stops. The rows are candidates for adjudication (deliberate, or a sys.path ordering artifact as scope_store's was) and the mode prints them as such, never as a repair queue. Verified run_selftests 86 of 86 before and after. STANDING until the cuts are adjudicated or reach is measured by something that does not stop at them. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-F25 WAS AN INSTANCE OF A CLASS AND I READ IT AS AN INCIDENT. A third lens now scans every local module for store seeds imported from inside a function — the exact shape that hid four suites — and it reads 23 CUTS, not one: schema_rows, schema_decl, schema_diag, schema_queue, query_builders, query_defs, query_registry, sppf_db, sppf_tree, sppf_drain, sppf_vocab, sppf_decode, sql_lift, sql_census, pipeflow, numpy_builders, ddl_lifecycle, dec_drain, tier_readset, reuse_tui, _pycodemod_sql, schema_selftest, and scope_store. ⚑ THE SCAN COVERS ALL LOCAL MODULES, NOT ONLY THE 99 SUITES, because a cut in a non-suite module truncates every chain behind it just the same — restricting it to suites would have reproduced the defect one level out. ⚑⚑ AND scope_store STILL APPEARS: I hoisted its store import and it still defers catalog_db, query_builders and schema, so my last-turn claim that the last known-false row was gone was too clean. THE STANDING CONSEQUENCE: import-closure reach is cut in 23 places, so a baseline minted on it would certify a graph it cannot traverse. A deferral is not a defect — an optional backend or a heavy one-mode import can be right — but it is always a place the census stops, and the rows are candidates for adjudication rather than a repair list.}
}

@misc{gate-F27-the-cuts-are-load-bearing,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The deferred store imports are load-bearing — they are what makes the code reachable from a selftest — so the 23-row list is a reading list and never a repair queue.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher of=gate-F26. ADJUDICATED 2026-08-24 and the answer was written at the site by its author. schema_rows._cli_connect carries: IMPORTED HERE, LIKE EVERY SIBLING. Module-scope store is bound inside the __main__ block, so a module-level reference works at dispatch and RAISES NameError FROM THE SELFTEST — which is precisely why this resolver had no case until now, the suite could not reach it. THE FUNCTION-LOCAL IMPORT IS WHAT MAKES THE CODE TESTABLE, and it is the sibling pattern across jea/metalanguage rather than one file's habit. Hoisting it would undo a fix and re-break the case it enabled. ⚑ SO scope_store WAS THE EXCEPTION, NOT THE TEMPLATE. Its deferral was a sys.path ordering artifact — the module-scope insert reached only scratch/ while store lives in jea/metalanguage — and hoisting it was right there and wrong here. gate-F26's list is a set of places the reach census stops, which is a fact about the census, and each row needs the same reading before anything is moved. ⚑⚑ AND I REACHED THIS ONLY AFTER THREE FALSE NEGATIVES OF MY OWN, WHICH IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART. pycodemod --literal with the needles import-store, from-store and store all reported schema_rows as not importing store at all. pycodemod --imports reported store present, correctly. I had used a TEXT census to check a STRUCTURAL claim, collected three confident zeros, and briefly concluded that the lens I had just shipped was reporting a cut that did not exist. The lens was right and my probes were the wrong instrument — agda-F13's family exactly, where the wrong tool returns a plausible ANSWER rather than an error, and the plausibility is what makes it expensive. ⚑ FIXED IN PASSING: the same site said store.tenant refuses (advisory today), a FOURTH place describing behaviour the tree dropped at tenant-mandatory, after gate-F19 found ratchet.store_argv and the run_selftests TENANT block saying the same. A parenthetical hedge costs exactly this when the hedge expires. Verified run_selftests 86 of 86 and schema 224 of 224 under --sandbox. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE 23 DEFERRED-SEED IMPORTS ARE NOT A REPAIR QUEUE, and the first one adjudicated says so at the site. schema_rows._cli_connect: 'IMPORTED HERE, LIKE EVERY SIBLING. Module-scope store is bound inside the __main__ block, so a module-level reference works at dispatch and RAISES NameError FROM THE SELFTEST — which is precisely why this resolver had no case until now: the suite could not reach it.' THE FUNCTION-LOCAL IMPORT IS WHAT MAKES THE CODE TESTABLE. Hoisting it would undo a fix, and it is the sibling pattern across jea/metalanguage. So gate-F26's list is a READING LIST, and scope_store was the exception rather than the template — its deferral was a sys.path ordering artifact, these are deliberate. ⚑⚑ AND I ESTABLISHED THIS ONLY AFTER THREE FALSE NEGATIVES OF MY OWN. pycodemod --literal 'import store', 'from store' and 'store' all reported schema_rows as not importing store; --imports reported it correctly. I used a TEXT census to check a STRUCTURAL claim, got confident zeros, and briefly concluded my own new lens was buggy. The lens was right. That is agda-F13's family: the wrong instrument returns a plausible answer rather than an error. ⚑ Also fixed in passing: the site said store.tenant 'refuses (advisory today)', a fourth place describing behaviour the tree dropped at tenant-mandatory — after gate-F19 found two others.}
}

@misc{gate-F28-two-reach-questions-not-one,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Store reach is two questions — what importing executes, and what could run — and a boolean answering both re-admits the false-positive population the strict walk was narrowed to exclude.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher of=gate-F27. BUILT 2026-08-24. store_reach gained through_cuts. False, the default, follows only module-level imports and answers what does IMPORTING this execute — the question OWED is computed from. True additionally follows the deferred seed edges gate-F27 established are load-bearing and permanent, marking each such hop with a tilde in the route, and answers what could this reach IF THE CODE RUNS. --tenants asks both and prints them as separate blocks: 0 OWED strictly, 7 if-it-runs. ⚑ I MADE THE TRAVERSAL UNCONDITIONAL FIRST AND THE SUITE REFUSED IT IN ONE RUN: a FUNCTION-LOCAL store import does NOT reach, got True want False. That case carries the comment THE CASE THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT MY 54 — it pins the narrowing that repaired a 54-of-99 false positive in which an Agda lexer reached a store down a ten-hop chain ending in a lazy, cached, exception-swallowed import that only the EXPLAIN-probing modes call. Following cuts by default re-admits precisely that population, and the pinned case is the only reason the regression cost one run rather than a census nobody could act on. A CASE WRITTEN AGAINST A PAST MISTAKE CAUGHT ITS REPETITION BY A DIFFERENT AUTHOR, which is what those comments are for. ⚑⚑ THE MARK IS WHAT MAKES ADJUDICATION POSSIBLE, and the two ends of the list prove it: query_builders reaching tilde-query_defs is plainly real, while check_mypy_ratchet arriving at tilde-store via pycodemod then _pycodemod_sql is the same EXPLAIN-probe chain the narrowing excluded. Same mechanism, opposite verdicts, and only the ROUTE distinguishes them — a count of 7 would have been unactionable in exactly the way the original 54 was. ⚑ AND THE GENERAL SHAPE: a predicate that answers two questions is the collapse this file already records against its own verdict column. The honest move is a parameter and two printed readings, not a smarter boolean. Verified run_selftests 86 of 86 after the revert-and-parameterise. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): REACH IS TWO QUESTIONS AND I TRIED TO MAKE ONE BOOLEAN ANSWER BOTH. store_reach now takes through_cuts: False asks what does IMPORTING this execute, True also follows deferred seed edges and marks each such hop with a tilde in the route, asking what could this reach IF THE CODE RUNS. --tenants prints both, neither standing in for the other: 0 OWED strictly, 7 if-it-runs. ⚑ I MADE THE TRAVERSAL UNCONDITIONAL FIRST AND THE SUITE CAUGHT IT — 'a FUNCTION-LOCAL store import does NOT reach: got True want False'. That case carries the comment 'THE CASE THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT MY 54' and pins the narrowing that fixed a 54-of-99 false positive, where an Agda lexer reached a store down a ten-hop chain ending in a lazy exception-swallowed import only the EXPLAIN modes call. Following cuts by default re-admits exactly that population, and the pinned case is the only reason I found out in one step rather than after a census nobody could act on. ⚑⚑ THE ROUTES MAKE THE ADJUDICATION READABLE, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE MARK: query_builders reaching tilde-query_defs is plainly real, while check_mypy_ratchet arriving at tilde-store via pycodemod then _pycodemod_sql is the same ten-hop EXPLAIN-probe chain the narrowing excluded. Same mechanism, opposite verdicts, and only the route distinguishes them.}
}

@misc{gate-F29-pycodemod-opens-a-store-unstatably,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {pycodemod is the ONE genuine store reacher of the seven if-it-runs rows, and its tenant is UNSTATABLE because its selftest calls _pg_probe_con() unconditionally, which is store.connect(mode=rw) bypassing store.tenant and swallowing its exception, so a --sandbox pragma would be a false declaration.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r4. The witness is _pycodemod_selftest.py line 1815, where an unconditional _pg_probe_con() call guards the postgres-dialect case and prints SKIP UNMEASURED when None, read against _pycodemod_sql.py lines 852-868 where _pg_probe_con is store.connect(mode=rw, back=postgres) inside a try/except Exception returning None. Six sibling rows adjudicated not-owed or runs-not-owed by reading each suite path. schema_decl deferred store reaches only store.dialect(), pure compilation, so the tilde-schema_decl edge cannot trip the tenant refusal at all. Rulings recorded in run_selftests.IF_IT_RUNS_RULINGS with three total-ness cases. Suite moved 86/86 to 89/89. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): V15 adjudicate-seven CLOSED, and the deciding predicate was NOT the one the brief named. The brief asked, per row, whether the suite selftest path EXECUTES the deferred edge. Three of the seven do execute it and STILL owe nothing, because what sits behind the cut is not a connection: schema_decl deferred import store reaches only store.dialect(), which returns a SQLAlchemy postgresql dialect object and opens NOTHING, so the tilde-schema_decl edge cannot trip store.tenant no matter who runs it. ddl_lifecycle EXECUTES it via read to emitted to D.ddl(postgres) and the site itself says why that is safe: IMPORTED FOR THE NAMESPACE AUTHORITY, NOT FOR A CONNECTION. A seed-module edge is a PROXY for store access and here the proxy and the thing part company. The honest predicate is one hop further: does the executed path reach store.tenant or a live connect/sandbox_connect. THE SEVEN. query_builders not-owed: never runs the tilde-query_defs hop; its own function-local import store as _store reads backend(), an os.environ read with no connection. sql_census not-owed: builder objects only. sql_lift runs/not-owed: selftest DOES execute from query_defs import q_reach and calls it, but that builds a Core statement object and query_defs own store import is itself deferred and untouched. ddl_lifecycle runs/not-owed. tier_readset not-owed: suite runs entirely on _fixtures(). check_mypy_ratchet not-owed, the ten-hop EXPLAIN chain, CONFIRMED rather than assumed, because its suite drives pycodemod.type_errors, forwards and census, all of which live in _pycodemod_census and _pycodemod_query, never _pycodemod_sql, so _pg_probe_con is not on any path it takes. AND ONE IS REAL. pycodemod is OWED-UNSTATABLE: _pycodemod_selftest.py calls _pg_probe_con() UNCONDITIONALLY to guard the postgres-dialect case, printing SKIP/UNMEASURED when it is None, and _pg_probe_con is store.connect(mode=rw, back=postgres) -- a LIVE READ-WRITE connection that BYPASSES store.tenant entirely and swallows its own exception. So the selftest roster opens a live rw connection to the main store today, and a --sandbox pragma on pycodemod would be a FALSE DECLARATION: the site does not consult argv, so declaring a tenant would not change which store it opens. This is sql-F23 shape at a second site -- a store reacher the tenant contract cannot bind -- and it is why the seventh row is filed rather than declared away. THE BRIEF FRAMING INVERTED BOTH ENDS: it called query_builders plainly real and check_mypy_ratchet plainly excluded; both are not-owed, while the row nobody flagged, pycodemod, one hop SHORTER on the same chain, is the only genuine reacher. Rulings now live in run_selftests.IF_IT_RUNS_RULINGS with three total-ness cases: every row carries a ruling, no ruling outlives its row, and the verdict vocabulary is closed. 86/86 to 89/89.}
}

@misc{gate-F30-baseline-still-blocked-and-the-blocker-moved,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A --declarations tenant baseline must NOT be minted: gate-F21 step 2 is unmet, and pycodemod is the witness -- its store access is unstatable by any declaration and exception-swallowed, so the census reads identically whether it connected or not. The missing instrument is a RUNTIME lens at store.tenant/connect/sandbox_connect, decorrelated from both static source censuses.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r4. The witness is gate-F29 plus the observation that both offered readings fail for opposite reasons. Strict-only would have certified away pycodemod, the one real reacher. Union admits check_mypy_ratchet, which source reading establishes is not a reacher, and still cannot see a seedless opener. Neither lens is decorrelated from the other because both are source censuses sharing the sql-F23 blind spot. Baselines also mint only on the commit path per gate-F4, so this is a decision not to prepare one, not a failed attempt. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): V10 baseline-mint DECIDED: DO NOT MINT, and the reason is now MEASURED rather than anticipated. gate-F21 set the sequencing as three steps -- (1) adopt tenant pragmas on the OWED, (2) measure reach by something the import graph cannot hide from, (3) THEN a --declarations baseline has a population with teeth. Step 1 is done, 16 declared, 0 OWED, 0 STRAY. STEP 2 IS NOT, AND ADJUDICATING THE SEVEN IS WHAT PROVED IT RATHER THAN CLOSING IT. gate-F29 found pycodemod opening a LIVE read-write connection to the main store from inside the selftest roster, through _pg_probe_con, which calls store.connect directly and never store.tenant. Two properties make this fatal to a baseline today. FIRST, the site does not consult argv, so the tenant field cannot be made TRUE for it by declaring anything -- a --sandbox pragma there would be a declaration the code cannot honour, which is exactly gate-F14 unfalsifiability with the polarity flipped: not a row that cannot fail, but a row that cannot succeed. SECOND, and worse for the ratchet specifically, the connection is EXCEPTION-SWALLOWED, so on a machine with no store the whole path degrades to None and the census reads identical to a machine where it connected. A ratchet cannot distinguish those two states, which means the field is not observable in the realizability sense on the population that matters most. SO NEITHER READING QUALIFIES, AND THE BRIEF OFFERED EXACTLY THOSE TWO. Strict-only certifies the seven away and would have certified pycodemod away with them -- the very row that turned out to be the real one. Union counts check_mypy_ratchet, which reading establishes is NOT a reacher, so union buys a false positive to catch a true one and still cannot see a site that opens a store without importing a seed. THE THIRD THING, which is what my round-2 self would say and what I say now: the missing instrument is a RUNTIME lens, not a third static one. store.tenant is already a single chokepoint that exits 2, and store.connect/sandbox_connect are the only two openers. A counter or an emitted marker at those three functions, read back after a suite runs, answers did this suite open a store and which tenant did it name with no import graph involved -- and it would have found pycodemod on the first run instead of on the seventh careful read. That is the step-2 instrument. It is also decorrelated from both static lenses in the correlated-instruments-agreeing-is-not-coverage sense: import-reach and the call-site lens are both source censuses and share the blind spot that sql-F23 measured and gate-F29 re-measured. Until that lens exists and its population is reconciled with the static one, a --declarations baseline would be census-green-is-a-fact-about-the-census with an exit code. Filed as the successor the refusal names.}
}

@misc{gate-F31-divergence-moved-under-me,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A corpus-wide statistic cannot carry a before/after in a concurrent-write swarm: divergence moved 46 to 51 and discovered suites 99 to 100 during this round, neither from my edits. The attributable instrument is the per-file census.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r4. The witness is that scripts/run_selftests.py appears nowhere in the toolmodes --divergence listing, its dispatched roster is unchanged at 8 modes per pycodemod --declare-modes, and toolmodes --find run_selftests returns no undeclared row -- while the corpus total rose and the tool count went 89 to 90. Same shape as the sql-relalg census drift where two honest readings minutes apart disagreed because the tree moved between them. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The divergence census read 46 in the round-4 brief and reads 51 today (41 undocumented plus 10 unbacked over 90 tools, up from 89). MY EDITS ARE NOT IN IT: run_selftests.py does not appear in the --divergence listing, its dispatched roster is unchanged at 8 modes, and toolmodes --find run_selftests returns no undeclared row. Discovered suites also moved 99 to 100 between the brief being written and my first census run. Both deltas are concurrent sibling WRITE agents landing in the same tree during this round. Recorded because a divergence figure quoted from a brief is a MEASUREMENT WITH A TIMESTAMP, not a constant, and the sql-relalg census drift incident is the same shape one layer over: two honest readings minutes apart, the tree moving between them. A before/after on a shared corpus-wide statistic is only attributable when nobody else is writing, which in a three-agent swarm is never -- so the attributable statistic is the PER-FILE one (toolmodes --find on the file I touched), and that is what I report.}
}

@misc{gate-F32-my-sqlite-census-was-keyed-on-one-spelling,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {My sqlite census was keyed on the dotted sqlite3.connect spelling and was blind to the bare connect() form after import sqlite3, which is how both real path-valued opens are written -- so I reported a fact about the method as a fact about the substrate, in a report about that error class.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r4. The witness is sppf_query.py:26 and reuse_tui.py:80, both opening catalog/catalog.db with a bare connect(), neither returned by pycodemod --literal sqlite3.connect, which returns only 5 fixture-string and docstring sites. Found by the sql-oracle on a dispatch I made because I had correctly flagged those three files as UNREAD. The saving move was reporting the unread population as unread rather than concluding over it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): MY OWN CENSUS WAS KEYED ON ONE SPELLING, AND THE BRIEF INHERITED IT. I read the sqlite population as three sites, all :memory: fixtures in _pycodemod_sql and pycodemod suite, and reported it that way. The sql-oracle re-derived it: pycodemod --literal sqlite3.connect returns 5 sites in 3 files, ALL of them fixture strings and docstrings, and is BLIND to the bare connect(...) spelling after import sqlite3 -- which is how BOTH real path-valued opens are written. sppf_query.py:26 opens catalog/catalog.db via sqlite3.connect(DB), and reuse_tui.py:80 opens it via sqlite3.connect(CATALOG_DB). A 334MB sqlite file exists on disk dated the day before the 2026-08-11 refusal. This is census-keyed-on-one-spelling, which is ALREADY IN THE ALWAYS-LOADED MEMORY SET, firing on the exact tool whose own header documents the scope-directional twin of this failure. The dotted-vs-bare call spelling is the same two-spelling relation as def q_x versus call QB.run(x). I reported a fact about the METHOD as a fact about the substrate, in a report whose whole subject is that error class. ⚑ AND THE CROSS-CHECK IS WHAT SAVED IT, not the census: I had flagged reuse_catalog, reuse_tui and sppf_query as UNREAD and dispatched a reader rather than concluding over them. Reporting the unread population as unread is what made the correction possible -- the finding is not that the census was wrong but that a single-spelling census plus a named blind spot is a workable instrument, while a single-spelling census reported as complete is not.}
}

@misc{gate-F32-size-and-commentary-are-ungated,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {pycodemod's 1200-line size threshold and its commentary_lost set-difference are BOTH ungated -- neither is reachable from any declared pre-commit gate, so growing an already-over-cap Python file refuses nothing.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. Witness: build_census.py --gates enumerates the declared roster across 2 phases and names no size or commentary gate. gate_status.py --list prints every wired command line and none invokes pycodemod. toolmodes --find size reports that --size is declared ONLY on scratch/pycodemod.py, and separately that a '--size' named in check_mode_contracts.py's own output is declared by NO tool. pycodemod --calls module_size_all resolves to 1 def with calls confined to scratch/pycodemod.py, scratch/_pycodemod_census.py and scratch/_pycodemod_selftest.py. pycodemod --calls commentary_lost resolves to the same three-file closure. WHAT A GREEN ASSERTS: nothing whatever about Python file size or comment retention. BLIND SPOT: the size census has no gated authority, so the phrase in pycodemod --split's help, 'the LINE bound, counted the way a size gate counts', cites a MECHANISM that does not exist -- a comment-is-a-hypothesis instance where the hypothesis invents an enforcer rather than merely staling a number. SECOND CONTRACT: commentary_lost is direction-asymmetric by construction, computing lost and gained as a set difference against a git ref, so an ADD-only edit yields an empty lost set and cannot fire. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): pycodemod --size and commentary_lost are ungated: no pre-commit gate consults either, so an over-cap Python file commits freely}
}

@misc{gate-F33-mode-census-is-a-swarm-contended-artifact,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A generated-census gate assumes one writer per generation, and a three-agent WRITE swarm violates that silently: toolmodes --sync regenerates from the live tree, so paying down my own two modes would commit two sibling agents' in-flight rosters as if I had declared them.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r4. The witness is the toolmodes --check diff, which spans run_selftests 4 to 6 modes (mine), procstruct.py at 9 modes (process-hygiene agent), tmi_subject.py at 5 (transcript-indexer), scratch/findings.py at 3, and query_defs gaining --live -- 20 documented modes and 3 tools across three owners in one block. Deliberately NOT synced. Also records the gate-oracle finding that no size gate exists at all, so pycodemod --split's help citing a 1200-line size gate is an invented mechanism rather than a stale number. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): toolmodes --check is RED (Phase A, blocking) and the diff spans ALL THREE concurrent WRITE agents, which makes --sync a coordination hazard rather than a paydown. The stale block undercounts by 20 documented modes and 3 tools: my run_selftests 4 to 6 (--pragmas, --tenants), the process-hygiene agent's whole procstruct.py at 9 modes, the transcript-indexer's tmi_subject.py at 5, plus scratch/findings.py at 3 and query_defs gaining --live. WHOEVER RUNS --sync COMMITS THE OTHER TWO AGENTS' ROSTERS as a side effect of paying down their own. That is not a merge conflict, which git would surface, but a SILENT ADOPTION: the census regenerates from the live tree, so it captures whatever half-landed state the siblings are in at that instant, and the resulting block reads as a deliberate declaration of their modes by me. THE GATE IS CORRECT AND THE TIMING IS THE PROBLEM. A generated-census gate assumes ONE writer per generation, and the swarm violates that assumption without any mechanism noticing -- the same shape as the sql-relalg census reading STALE then FRESH minutes apart, except here the two readings would be attributed to different authors. Deliberately NOT synced. The last agent to finish should run it, or the controller should run it once after all three land. Recorded so the next reader does not read the RED as a defect in the tool or as my omission. ⚑ Related and separate: the gate-oracle established that pycodemod --split's help text asserts a SIZE GATE that does not exist -- an invented mechanism rather than a stale number, one class worse than the MEM_CAP drift, since there the authority existed and only the copy was wrong. run_selftests.py at 1394 lines and _pycodemod_selftest.py at 1704 are over pycodemod's own 1200 default and trip nothing.}
}

@misc{proc-F4-before-or-after-the-run-is-the-missing-axis,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A roster tool's own flags need a BEFORE-OR-AFTER-THE-RUN axis, not runs_roster, because runs_roster marks a roster MEMBER that re-enters while run_selftests --times is the tool's own post-run reporting modifier and has no roster to carry any field.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=mode-consolidation-r4. P10 adjudicated. Measured 2026-08-24 that pycodemod --literal -- MODES scripts/run_selftests.py returns 0 sites, so no climode roster exists to carry the field. KNOWN_FLAGS moved from line 201 as recorded by proc-F2 to two sites inside _selftest around line 2397 during this round, so the file is being restructured by the concurrent selftest-standardize agent. REPORTED NOT EDITED. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓟ10 ⟡roster-tool-declaration ADJUDICATED, and the answer is NOT to extend runs_roster. proc-F2 established that run_selftests has no climode roster and that --times dispatches inside main() as a post-run reporting modifier while --list --last --declarations --pragmas --tenants return before the run. The brief (and my own first reading) proposed marking --times via climode.Selftest.runs_roster. That is the wrong field and extending it would blur the one distinction it makes cleanly: runs_roster marks a roster MEMBER that re-enters the roster, and self_including refuses it STATICALLY on that basis. --times is not a member and does not re-enter; it is the roster TOOL's own flag, and what makes it dangerous is neither writing nor re-entry but DISPATCH POSITION -- it is reached only after the roster has already run to completion, so its cost is the whole suite and its output is a byproduct. The honest axis is therefore BEFORE-OR-AFTER-THE-RUN, a two-valued property of every flag on a roster tool, and it is decidable statically from where the branch sits relative to the run call -- the same kind of source-anchored test contract_rows already performs. A Mode field spelled something like returns_before_run=True on the five genuine reads, defaulting False, would make 'is this flag a read' answerable without invoking it, which is exactly the question that cost the dispatcher 19 launchers and 27 workers. CONFIRMED STILL UNBUILT AND STILL NOT MINE 2026-08-24 round 4: pycodemod --literal -- 'MODES' scripts/run_selftests.py returns 0 sites, so no roster exists to carry the field. ⚑ AND THE FILE IS MOVING UNDER ME: proc-F2 recorded KNOWN_FLAGS at :201, and --literal now finds only two sites, both inside _selftest() at :2397-2398, so the concurrent selftest-standardize agent has restructured the flag surface since that finding was written. A line number in a finding about a contended file is stale within the round. REPORTED NOT EDITED -- scripts/run_selftests.py and scripts/climode.py belong to that agent.}
}

@misc{gate-F34-the-size-gate-phrase-is-a-simile-not-a-citation,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {pycodemod --split's help does not cite a nonexistent 1200-line size gate — 1200 is the live default of the real --max flag and 'counted the way a size gate counts' is a simile naming a counting convention, though the absence of any size gate is real and was already filed as gate-F32.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=mode-consolidation-r4. V17 invented-size-gate half refuted. Measured 2026-08-24 that pycodemod.py:2611 reads '--max the LINE bound, counted the way a size gate counts (default 1200)' and that gate_status --list wires 35 gates none of which counts lines. Minimal honest correction is to name the owning function _pycodemod_split._size rather than describe the behaviour. REPORTED NOT EDITED. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓥ17 ⟡invented-size-gate is HALF REFUTED, and the half that survives was already filed. THE BRIEF SAYS pycodemod --split's help 'cites a 1200-line size gate that does not exist' and grades that one class worse than the MEM_CAP drift because an invented MECHANISM sends a reader hunting for a gate never built. MEASURED 2026-08-24. pycodemod --literal -- '1200' scratch/pycodemod.py returns exactly two sites. The one in question is pycodemod.py:2611 and it reads '--max the LINE bound, counted the way a size gate counts (default 1200)'. So 1200 is NOT a figure attributed to a gate — it is the live DEFAULT of a real flag, --max, on a real mode, and _pycodemod_split._size is the function that computes it, glossed in toolmodes --find as 'the line count a SIZE GATE sees'. The sentence does not assert that a gate exists. 'Counted the way a size gate counts' is a SIMILE naming a COUNTING CONVENTION — it tells the reader that blank and comment lines are treated the way such a gate would treat them, which is the one thing a bare line number is ambiguous about. Read as a citation it is an invented mechanism. Read as written it is a description of the arithmetic. ⚑ THE LOAD-BEARING HALF IS TRUE AND IS NOT NEW: NO SIZE GATE EXISTS. gate_status --list reports 35 wired gates, 29 unconditional, and none of them counts lines. gate-F32-size-and-commentary-are-ungated ALREADY RECORDS THIS in the same words — 'pycodemod --size and commentary_lost are ungated: no pre-commit gate consults either'. So the honest finding is not a false citation in --split's help but the ABSENCE the help's simile quietly presumes a reader knows about, and that absence was filed before this round. ⚑ THE CORRECTION IS A COMMA, NOT A MECHANISM. If the phrase is to be repaired at all, the minimal honest edit is to make the simile's hypothetical mood explicit — 'counted the way a size gate WOULD count' — or to name the function instead, '_pycodemod_split._size'. Naming the function is strictly better because it is a SYMBOL rather than a description, which is the cite-a-symbol-never-a-line discipline applied one level up: the current phrasing describes a behaviour that has an owner, and the owner is nameable. ⚑ MY BRIEF WAS WRONG IN THE meta-F1 DIRECTION AGAIN and this is the third round running. It used an absence-word about a thing that works — 'does not exist' about a default that does exist, on a flag that does dispatch — and had I acted on it as briefed I would have deleted or rewritten a correct default's documentation in a file I do not own. REPORTED NOT EDITED: scratch/pycodemod.py belongs to the concurrent live-store-in-precommit agent.}
}

@misc{gate-F26-pycodemod-split-cites-a-nonexistent-size-gate,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {pycodemod --split's help cites a 1200-line size gate that has no referent in the gate roster, and an invented authority never decays the way a stale figure does},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r5. Witness — no gate in the declared roster measures python file length and no 1200 threshold is declared anywhere. Report-only, since scratch/pycodemod.py is owned by the concurrent live-store-in-precommit agent this round and was not edited. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): REPORT-ONLY for the owner of scratch/pycodemod.py (⟡live-store-in-precommit holds that file this round; not edited here). pycodemod --split's help text cites a '1200-line size gate' that DOES NOT EXIST — no gate in the pre-commit roster measures python file length, and no threshold of 1200 is declared anywhere in the gate machinery. The correction the owner should apply: DELETE the citation rather than reword it, and if a size discipline is wanted, state it as what it actually is — a JUDGEMENT the author is invited to apply, with no enforcement behind it — using the word 'guideline' and naming no number that no file owns. ⚑ WHY THIS IS ONE CLASS WORSE THAN THE MEM_CAP DRIFT, which is the part worth preserving: MEM_CAP was a real gate quoted at a stale VALUE — a reader who acts on it does the right kind of thing to the wrong degree, and the enforcing gate eventually corrects them. This is a citation to a gate that has no referent at all, so nothing will ever correct it: the reader restructures a file to satisfy an authority that cannot fire, and every downstream reader inherits the invented number as though a gate owned it. A wrong figure decays when its authority moves; an invented authority never does. It is also exactly meta-F3 class (A) inside the toolkit's own help text — a claim about the gate surface's behaviour asserted without invoking the gate surface.}
