  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=mode-consolidation-r4. Measured 2026-08-24 that --list and --register each have exactly one literal site in scratch/findings.py, both role=arg inside a docstring pointer line naming .claude/agents/findings.py. Second distinct mechanism in the sql-F10 census-artifact family. A principled successor is unbuilt: exempt a flag literal whose enclosing line names a different tool path. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): TWO NEW UNBACKED CENSUS ROWS ARE A CROSS-REFERENCE MISREAD AS A DECLARATION, and paying them down would make a tool advertise another tool's modes as its own. toolmodes --divergence now reports scratch/findings.py with UNBACKED --list and UNBACKED --register, 'documented, no matching flag literal'. MEASURED 2026-08-24. pycodemod --literal -- '--list' scratch/findings.py returns ONE site, role=arg, at scratch/findings.py:2354, and --register returns ONE site at :2355. Both sit inside the module docstring, and both are inside a POINTER LINE reading 'python3 .claude/agents/findings.py --list' — that is, this tool's help text NAMES THE OTHER findings.py, a DIFFERENT tool with the same basename, and tells the reader to run those modes THERE. scratch/findings.py's own mode is --sweep, which dispatches, and the tool now refuses without it precisely because the name collision was causing operators to invoke the wrong one (proc-F3-scratch-findings-py-hangs records the incident and the repair). SO THE ROWS ARE A FACT ABOUT THE READER, NOT THE TOOL. toolmodes treats a flag literal appearing anywhere in the self-description as a declaration by the containing file, and it cannot see that the sentence's SUBJECT is a different program. THIS IS THE sql-F10-verify-is-a-census-artifact FAMILY EXACTLY, and it is the second distinct mechanism in that family: sql-F10 was a docstring mentioning a RETIRED mode of the SAME tool, this is a docstring mentioning a LIVE mode of ANOTHER tool. Both render identically as UNBACKED. ⚑ THE PAYDOWN IS THE TRAP. The row invites writing --list and --register into scratch/findings.py's usage block, which would assert capabilities it does not have and would re-create the exact confusion the --sweep refusal was built to end. ⚑ AND THE CROSS-REFERENCE IS LOAD-BEARING — deleting the pointer to clear the census would remove the one line that tells a reader who typed the wrong basename where the ledger actually lives, which is the mitigation for a collision that has already cost real time. RECORDED, NOT SILENCED, on the tmi-F13 precedent: the honest resolution leaves permanent rows, and without this note a future reader clears them by declaring modes that do not exist. A principled successor exists and is not built: the census could exempt a flag literal whose enclosing line also names a DIFFERENT tool path, which is decidable from the same source it already reads.}
}

@misc{swarm-F1-membudget-label-carries-no-exclusion,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {membudget's RAII claim LIFETIME already worked, but its label carried NO exclusion, so a second claimant on an identical claim-label was admitted -- repaired as a third waiting predicate in cmd_run's admission loop.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=swarm-coordination. Measured 2026-08-24. A holder ran 'membudget run 8 claim:same-name -- sleep 40' while a second NOBLOCK claimant on the identical label printed SECOND-CLAIMANT-GOT-IN at exit 0. Also 'membudget run 1' exits 137 because a 1MB MemoryMax kills sleep itself, so a claim costs 8MB and is not free. Fixed with a claim-prefix predicate beside MEMBUDGET_MAXLOAD plus an authoritative re-check under the append lock. swarmclaim --selftest 18/18 with three controls -- second claimant REFUSED, different artefact admitted, ordinary label unaffected. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): MEASURED 2026-08-24, and it is the fact that justified writing any code at all. membudget's RAII lifetime for a nominal claim ALREADY WORKED before this arc: 'membudget run 8 claim:X -- sleep infinity' holds across an agent's tool calls, its trap-release-EXIT is a destructor, and its pid-liveness GC collects a handle whose scope was torn down abnormally. What did NOT exist is EXCLUSION. Witness: a holder ran 'membudget run 8 claim:same-name -- sleep 40'; a second 'MEMBUDGET_NOBLOCK=1 membudget run 8 claim:same-name -- echo SECOND-CLAIMANT-GOT-IN' printed SECOND-CLAIMANT-GOT-IN and exited 0. The label was free text with no semantics. Also measured: 'membudget run 1 claim:probe -- sleep 3' exits 137 -- a 1MB MemoryMax cgroup kills sleep itself -- so a claim is NOT free and 8MB is the smallest observed to hold. A claim therefore consumes budget, which is honest: making it exempt from 'mb <= free' would need a second admission path, doubling the branch the loop's design avoids. REPAIRED by adding a THIRD waiting predicate to cmd_run's admission loop beside MEMBUDGET_MAXLOAD, keyed on the 'claim:' label prefix, with an authoritative re-check under the same lock as the append (the lock-free gate alone is check-then-act, the exact defect sql-F11 records in require_indexes where two make -j workers both read had==False). Verified: swarmclaim --selftest 18/18 including three controls -- a second claimant on a held artefact is REFUSED, a claim on a DIFFERENT artefact is still admitted, and an ORDINARY label is wholly unaffected.}
}

@misc{swarm-F2-orphanhood-is-the-holder-liveness-predicate,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A claim holder is safe from the abandoned-orphan reaper while its agent lives, and correctly reaped when the agent dies, because procstruct.abandoned tests ORPHANHOOD before age -- so parentage, not a marker or a TTL, is the holder's liveness predicate.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=swarm-coordination. procstruct.abandoned at procstruct.py:392 requires p.orphaned first and only then min_age_s. Measured 2026-08-24 -- procstruct --abandoned read 0 while a claim holder was live. A live agent owns a process subtree, so its holder has a live parent and is never listed. A dead agent's holder reparents to init/1369 and IS reaped, which is the RAII destructor firing rather than a hazard. Residue kept -- a setsid or double-forked holder would be orphaned from birth and read as abandoned while its agent lives, so --hold deliberately does not detach and --who flags an orphaned holder. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE USE-AFTER-FREE QUESTION IS ALREADY ANSWERED BY THE TREE, AND NOT BY AGE. A RAII claim holder is a long-lived process by design, so it superficially resembles the abandoned selftest workers 'procstruct --kill --abandoned' reaps on a 30s age floor -- and the brief asked whether a hygiene sweep would destroy a live claim. It cannot. procstruct.abandoned() (procstruct.py:392) tests p.orphaned FIRST and only then applies min_age_s. So the two cases separate on PARENTAGE, not age: an agent that is ALIVE has a live process subtree, its holder's parent is alive, the holder is NOT orphaned, and --abandoned never lists it (measured: --abandoned read 0 while a holder was live). An agent that DIES leaves the holder reparented to init/1369, orphaned and old, so it is reaped -- WHICH IS THE DESTRUCTOR FIRING, correct behaviour rather than a hazard. This closes the objection 'an agent thinking is not a process' at the one level where it still bit: the agent need not BE a process, it needs to OWN one, and orphan-hood is exactly the predicate that reads that ownership. NO marker, NO KIND test (gate-F8 warns a kind test must not be a substring test), NO TTL, NO heartbeat. RESIDUE KEPT: a holder started with setsid or a double fork would be ORPHANED FROM BIRTH and would read as abandoned while its agent lives -- so swarmclaim --hold deliberately does NOT detach (start_new_session=False, stated at the call site), and --who flags any orphaned holder rather than trusting it.}
}

@misc{swarm-F3-key-allocation-has-no-detector,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Five finding keys name two findings each because agents read --list, compute max+1 and file with no exclusion between the read and the write -- check-then-act on a shared ledger, and --list has no detector for it.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=swarm-coordination. gate-F26, gate-F32, sql-F26, sql-F27 and sql-F28 each name two findings. --register refuses a duplicate key, so this is not an overwrite but two agents independently choosing the same next ordinal. Both findings are real and both are kept, so nothing errors and the defect is silent. Structurally identical to sql-F11's require_indexes check-then-act and to the lock-free claim gate this arc had to re-check under a lock. Two repairs, neither built here because findings.py is not mine -- a --duplicates mode partitioning keys by stem, and an exclusive claim around read-max-then-file which is the first real consumer of swarmclaim outside its own selftest. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE DUPLICATE-KEY RACE IS THE SAME COORDINATION GAP ONE LAYER UP, AND IT IS FILED HERE BECAUSE IT IS THE INSTANCE MY OWN MECHANISM WOULD HAVE PREVENTED. Five keys currently name TWO findings each -- gate-F26, gate-F32, sql-F26, sql-F27, sql-F28 -- filed concurrently by different agents with no shared counter. findings.py --register REFUSES a duplicate key, so the collision is not a same-key overwrite: it is two agents independently choosing the SAME NEXT ORDINAL because each read --list, computed max+1, and filed, with no exclusion between the read and the write. THAT IS CHECK-THEN-ACT ON A SHARED LEDGER -- structurally identical to sql-F11's require_indexes (two make -j workers both observe had==False and both enter apply) and to the lock-free claim gate this arc had to re-check under a lock. The distinguishing detail is that the two findings under one key are BOTH REAL and BOTH KEPT, so nothing ever errors and nothing is lost; the defect is silent and only readable by a human noticing a repeated stem. ⚑ AND THERE IS NO DETECTOR: --list renders both rows and does not flag the collision, so the census cannot see its own duplicate keys -- census-green-is-a-fact-about-the-census, in the ledger built to hold findings about exactly that. TWO REPAIRS, NEITHER BUILT HERE (findings.py is not mine and the second is a judgement I should not make alone): (1) a --duplicates mode that partitions keys by STEM and reports collisions, which is the missing detector and is cheap; (2) an exclusive claim around read-max-then-file, which is precisely 'swarmclaim --hold .claude/agents/<ledger>.bib' and is the first real consumer of this arc's mechanism outside its own selftest. I deliberately did NOT hold a claim while filing these three, because doing so would have been the only invocation and would have proved nothing about adoption.}
}

@misc{swarm-F4-exclusion-verified-by-release,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Exclusion is verified by the RELEASE and by a different-artefact arm, never by a refusal alone — a lone refusal cannot distinguish working exclusion from a permanently stuck gate.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher. MEASURED 2026-08-24 as a four-arm state machine, at the operator's direction — launch one and background it, launch a second, check the second, kill the first, check the second again. ARM ONE, hold. membudget run 64 claim:excl-probe with sleep 300, and status shows 64MB pid 3569160 holding the label. ARM TWO, contention. MEMBUDGET_NOBLOCK=1 on the IDENTICAL name is REFUSED exit 3, naming the live holder's pid. ARM THREE, the negative control. claim:excl-other on a DIFFERENT artefact is ADMITTED, which excludes the blanket-block explanation and establishes that the refusal is name-scoped. ARM FOUR, release. Kill the holder, the ledger empties at exit 143 — the RAII destructor firing on SIGTERM — and the same name is then ADMITTED. ⚑ ARMS THREE AND FOUR ARE WHAT MAKE IT A CONTROL RATHER THAN AN OBSERVATION. A refusal on its own is equally consistent with a gate that refuses that name forever, and with one that refuses everything. Neither of those is exclusion. This is sql-F4's a-zero-needs-a-positive-control turned on the mechanism itself. ⚑⚑ THE BUILDING AGENT MEASURED ONLY THE REFUSAL and reported it as decisive. It was decisive for the ABSENCE of exclusion before the change, but not for its PRESENCE after. That agent also declined to hold a claim while filing its own findings, reasoning that being the sole invoker would prove nothing about adoption — right about adoption, and not a substitute for the release arm. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): EXCLUSION IS VERIFIED BY THE RELEASE, NOT BY THE REFUSAL. Measured 2026-08-24 as a four-arm state machine rather than a one-shot: hold claim:excl-probe (64MB, pid 3569160, ledger shows it) — second claimant on the IDENTICAL name refused exit 3 naming the live holder — DIFFERENT artefact claim:excl-other ADMITTED, so the refusal is name-scoped and not a blanket block — kill the holder, ledger empties (exit 143, the RAII destructor firing on SIGTERM) — same name now ADMITTED. ⚑ THE SECOND HALF IS WHAT MAKES IT A CONTROL. A refusal alone is equally consistent with a permanent bug that refuses everything on that name forever, and the different-artefact arm is what excludes a blanket block. That is sql-F4's a-zero-needs-a-positive-control applied to the exclusion mechanism itself: the agent that built it measured only the refusal, and correctly declined to hold a claim while filing, but a one-shot refusal cannot distinguish working exclusion from a stuck gate.}
}

@misc{swarm-F5-a-nominal-claim-cannot-be-1mb,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A nominal claim cannot be spelled as a tiny lease — 1MB OOM-kills its own holder, so a zero-cost claim needs exemption from the quantitative predicate rather than a small number.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher. MEASURED 2026-08-24 on the first attempt at the exclusion state machine. membudget run 1 claim:excl-probe with sleep 300 exits 137 — the cgroup MemoryMax=1M kills the scope before sleep can even start. A lease is a HARD KILL CAP over the whole process tree, a systemd-run scope measured as maxRSS covering heap plus RTS plus interpreter, not an accounting entry. Re-running at 64MB worked and every subsequent arm passed. ⚑ THIS REFUTES THE CHEAP ANSWER TO A QUESTION THE BUILDING AGENT LEFT OPEN. It asked whether a claim consumes budget or is exempt from the mb-versus-free predicate, and treated it as a design preference. It is not — the mechanism decides it. ANY LEASE SMALL ENOUGH TO BE NOMINAL IS SMALL ENOUGH TO KILL ITS OWN HOLDER. So either a claim carries a real lease, spending pool on a placeholder that computes nothing and starving concurrent compiles at the default 8GB budget, or the admission loop must treat a claim as exempt from the quantitative predicate while remaining subject to the exclusion one. ⚑⚑ THE SHAPE IS build-F1'S, ONE STEP FURTHER. There, MEM_CAP is a GATE where wrong-large silently admits debt, and AGDA_MB is a FITTING PARAMETER that self-corrects on OOM. A claim lease is NEITHER — it is a NAME wearing a quantity, and the quantity turns out to be load-bearing in a direction nobody wanted. A finding about a mechanism is not reachable by reading its design. STANDING until the loop distinguishes a claim from a lease. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE 1MB-NOMINAL-CLAIM IDIOM IS OOM-KILLED AND I FOUND IT ON THE FIRST TRY. membudget run 1 claim:excl-probe -- sleep 300 exits 137 — the cgroup MemoryMax=1M kills the scope before sleep can start, because a lease is a HARD KILL CAP over the whole process tree (systemd-run scope, maxRSS: heap plus RTS plus interpreter), not an accounting entry. 64MB worked. ⚑ SO A CLAIM THAT CONSUMES NO BUDGET CANNOT BE SPELLED AS A TINY LEASE. The question the building agent left open — does a claim consume budget, or is it exempt from the mb-versus-free predicate — is not a design preference. The cheap answer is REFUTED by the mechanism: any lease small enough to be nominal is small enough to kill its own holder. Either the claim carries a real lease (64MB of pool spent on a placeholder that computes nothing, which starves concurrent compiles at the default 8GB budget) or the loop must treat a claim as exempt from the quantitative predicate while still subject to the exclusion one. ⚑⚑ AND THAT IS THE SAME SHAPE AS build-F1: MEM_CAP is a GATE where wrong-large silently admits debt, while AGDA_MB is a FITTING PARAMETER that self-corrects on OOM. A claim lease is neither — it is a NAME wearing a quantity, and the quantity is load-bearing in a direction nobody wanted.}
}

@misc{swarm-F5-reframe,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {swarm-F5 is refuted — the lease floor is 2MB not 64, and the 7MB maxRSS that misled me counts SHARED pages the cgroup never charges.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher of=swarm-F5. CORRECTS swarm-F5, filed by me minutes earlier, after the operator asked the one question I had not — what is even consuming that memory. I had ONE datum, that 1MB killed a sleep and 64MB worked, and generalised it into any lease small enough to be nominal is small enough to kill its own holder. THE BISECTION I NEVER RAN. membudget run 1 with /usr/bin/true PASSES. 2 with /usr/bin/sleep 2 PASSES. 1 with /usr/bin/sleep 2 is killed at 137. So the floor sits between 1 and 2MB, a nominal claim is entirely viable, and 64 was simply the first value I retried at rather than a measured minimum. ⚑ WHAT ACTUALLY OCCUPIES IT, AND THE TWO NUMBERS MEASURE DIFFERENT THINGS. membudget peaks reports sleep 1 at 7MB maxRSS while that same invocation RAN SUCCESSFULLY UNDER A 2MB CAP. maxRSS counts SHARED pages — libc, the loader, mapped page cache — which the cgroup does not charge to the scope, because memory.max charges PRIVATE pages only. true survives 1MB because it exits before dirtying enough, while a resident sleep needs page tables, stack and dirtied pages just past 1MB. I read a maxRSS figure as a cgroup charge, which is the instrument-adjacency error this session's ledger records against my briefs all day. ⚑⚑ A SECOND FINDING FALLS OUT OF THE SAME MEASUREMENT. peaks SUGGESTED 64MB for a process whose real need is 2MB. That is AGDA_MB=auto rounding a shared-page-inflated maxRSS up to the next power of two — conservative and correct for an agda compile, a 32x over-reservation for a placeholder that computes nothing. A peer session measured a 30x over-reservation from the other direction on the same box the same day, against Bazel resource_set. Same shape, two tools, neither reading the other. ⚑ THE OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE FOR THE CLAIM MECHANISM. swarm-F5's dilemma dissolves. A claim need not choose between spending real pool and needing an exemption from the quantitative predicate, because 2MB is both viable and negligible against an 8192MB budget. The open question narrows to whether a claim should be exempt for CLARITY rather than out of necessity. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): swarm-F5 IS WRONG AND I GENERALISED FROM A BISECTION I NEVER RAN. I claimed any lease small enough to be nominal is small enough to kill its own holder, on one datum: 1MB killed sleep 300, 64MB worked. Operator asked what was consuming the memory. MEASURED: membudget run 1 -- /usr/bin/true PASSES. 2 -- /usr/bin/sleep 2 PASSES. 1 -- /usr/bin/sleep 2 is killed. THE FLOOR IS BETWEEN 1 AND 2 MB, NOT 64. A nominal claim is entirely viable at 2MB and my jump to 64 was the first value I tried, not a measured minimum. ⚑ WHAT ACTUALLY CONSUMES IT: membudget peaks reports sleep 1 at 7MB maxRSS while it RAN under a 2MB cap — so maxRSS counts SHARED pages (libc, loader) that the cgroup does not charge to the scope, and the cgroup charges only PRIVATE pages. true survives 1MB because it exits before dirtying enough; a resident sleep needs page tables, stack and dirtied pages above 1MB. The two numbers measure different things and I read one as the other. ⚑⚑ AND A SECOND FINDING FALLS OUT: peaks SUGGESTS 64MB for a process whose real need is 2MB — AGDA_MB=auto rounding a shared-page-inflated maxRSS up to the next power of two. Conservative and correct for a compile, a 32x over-reservation for a placeholder. That is the same over-reservation shape a peer measured at 30x from the other direction.}
}

@misc{swarm-F6-the-1mb-floor-is-rust-coreutils,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The 1MB scope floor is uutils Rust coreutils, not residency or dirtying — gnusleep runs in 1MB where uutils sleep is killed, same program, same cap.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher of=swarm-F5-reframe. The operator asked how sleep dirties enough to exceed 1MB when it needs no heap allocation. IT DOES NOT. DECISIVE PAIR, bare systemd-run at MemoryMax=1M with no membudget and no time wrapper — /usr/bin/gnusleep 1 PASSES, /usr/bin/sleep 0 is KILLED at 137. Same program, same cap, opposite outcomes, so the variable is the IMPLEMENTATION. This box symlinks /usr/bin/sleep and /usr/bin/echo into /usr/lib/cargo/bin/coreutils/, the Rust uutils rewrite, while /usr/bin/true is a symlink to gnutrue. My original true-passes-sleep-dies observation was therefore never instant-versus-resident. It was GNU-versus-Rust, and the single binary I reached for as a control happened to be the only GNU one in the set. ⚑ FOUR EXPLANATIONS OFFERED AND FOUR REFUTED, IN ORDER, EACH BY ONE COMMAND. Page tables and dirtied pages — a dash blocked on a read survived 1MB indefinitely while fully resident. The /usr/bin/time -v wrapper membudget puts inside the scope — killed bare, without it. Duration — sleep 0 dies exactly as readily as sleep 300. Float-argument parsing pulling in locale or libm — echo dies too and parses nothing. Every one was plausible, none was measured before I said it, and the last three were offered AFTER the operator had already caught the first. ⚑⚑ THE GENERAL SHAPE, AND IT IS THIS SESSION'S DOMINANT ONE ARRIVING IN MY OWN MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN IN A BRIEF: an explanation that fits the two data points you have is not a measurement, and the cheapest way to find out is to vary ONE thing. I varied the size, the duration, the wrapper and the program before varying the implementation, which was the only variable that mattered and the only one I had not thought to name. ⚑ CONSEQUENCE FOR THE CLAIM MECHANISM: a placeholder should be spelled with a binary whose footprint is known. gnusleep holds a claim in 1MB. The floor is a property of the chosen holder, not of leases, so swarm-F5's dilemma dissolves further — there is no meaningful pool cost to a claim at all. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE 1MB FLOOR IS uutils COREUTILS, NOT RESIDENCY, NOT DIRTYING, NOT THE WRAPPER. Operator asked how sleep dirties that much when it needs no heap. It does not. DECISIVE PAIR, bare systemd-run at MemoryMax=1M with no membudget and no time wrapper: /usr/bin/gnusleep 1 PASSES, /usr/bin/sleep 0 is KILLED at 137. Same program, same cap, opposite outcomes — the variable is the implementation. This box symlinks /usr/bin/sleep and /usr/bin/echo into /usr/lib/cargo/bin/coreutils/ (the Rust uutils rewrite) while /usr/bin/true is a symlink to gnutrue. So my true-passes-sleep-dies observation was not instant-versus-resident at all, it was GNU-versus-Rust, and the one binary I happened to test as a control was the only GNU one in the set. ⚑ EVERY EXPLANATION I OFFERED WAS REFUTED IN ORDER: page tables and dirtied pages (a dash blocked on read survived 1MB indefinitely, resident the whole time), the /usr/bin/time -v wrapper (killed bare, without it), duration (sleep 0 dies as readily as sleep 300), and float-argument parsing pulling in locale or libm (echo dies too, and parses nothing). Each was plausible and none was measured before I said it. ⚑⚑ THE CONSEQUENCE FOR A CLAIM LEASE: a placeholder should be spelled with a binary whose footprint is known, and gnusleep runs in 1MB. The floor is a property of the chosen holder, not of leases.}
}

@misc{swarm-F7-a-builtin-holder-needs-no-exec,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A claim holder needs no external binary and no cgroup scope — a bash builtin blocking in the caller's own process is the whole mechanism.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher of=swarm-F6. Operator, two remarks, each removing a layer. FIRST: what if membudget had sleep builtin. membudget IS bash, so read -t is already a builtin and a holder can block without spawning anything. MEASURED — bash running a two-line script whose body is read -t 2 from /dev/zero holds a scope at 1MB and at 512K, and is killed at 256K, so the floor is bash's own private working set with nothing else in the scope. The full membudget path also passes at 1MB INCLUDING the /usr/bin/time -v wrapper it puts inside, which I had earlier blamed for the failure. The wrapper was never the problem, only the uutils payload was. SECOND, and larger: membudget does not need to invoke systemd-run for a simple sleep at all. ⚑ THE SCOPE EXISTS TO IMPOSE MemoryMax, AND A CLAIM HOLDER HAS NO MEMORY TO CAP — it computes nothing. So the scope is pure ceremony for this case: a D-Bus round trip and a transient unit, for a ceiling on a process that will never approach it. ⚑⚑ AND DROPPING IT IS NOT MERELY CHEAPER, IT IS MORE CORRECT. membudget's own header records that a nested systemd-run scope lands as a SIBLING under app.slice rather than a child of the caller — the escape that makes a peer session's cpu.weight concern real. Without the scope the holder stays a CHILD OF THE CALLER'S PROCESS, so RAII liveness hangs off a real parent instead of a detached unit, and procstruct.abandoned()'s orphanhood test works precisely BECAUSE the parent is real. ⚑ SO A CLAIM WANTS TWO OF membudget'S THREE CONCERNS AND NOT THE THIRD: the ledger for exclusion, the GC for stale holders, and no cgroup at all. Every layer I measured — 64MB, then a binary choice, then a builtin — was removing something the mechanism never needed, and the operator removed the last one by asking why it was there. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A CLAIM HOLDER NEEDS NO EXTERNAL BINARY AT ALL. Operator: what if membudget had sleep builtin? membudget IS bash, so read -t is already a builtin and a holder can block without spawning anything. MEASURED: bash running a two-line script whose body is read -t 2 _x </dev/zero holds a scope at 1MB, and at 512K. It is killed at 256K. So the floor is bash's own private working set, between 256K and 512K, and NOTHING ELSE IS IN THE SCOPE. ⚑ THE FULL membudget PATH ALSO PASSES AT 1MB — including the /usr/bin/time -v wrapper it puts inside the scope, which I had earlier blamed for the failure. The wrapper was never the problem; only the uutils payload was (swarm-F6). ⚑⚑ THIS DISSOLVES THE LAST OF swarm-F5. There is no binary to choose, no GNU-versus-Rust dependency, no coreutils at all, and the lease unit is integer MB so 1 is the smallest expressible claim regardless of how far below it the true floor sits. RAII is unaffected: the shell IS the handle, and it dies with its scope exactly as a sleep would. ⚑ AND THE HOOK MADE THE ARCHITECTURAL POINT BEFORE I DID — my first attempt used bash -c inline and hook_no_chaining refused it with 'this program dies with the turn; put it in a tool'. That is precisely the argument for a builtin holder living inside membudget rather than being composed at each call site.}
}

@misc{swarm-F8-cgroup-scope-targets-the-root,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {cgroup-scope creates its cgroup at the v2 ROOT, which is root-owned, so the systemd-less fallback is unusable unprivileged — while the caller's own delegated cgroup was writable all along.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher. MEASURED 2026-08-24 after the operator warned that systemd-run --scope does not work inside a Bazel sandbox and a peer would need cgroups directly. membudget already anticipated that case — its header records the Failed-to-connect-to-bus failure propagating SILENTLY through membudget then agda-shim then make, and scripts/cgroup-scope exists as the fallback. THE FALLBACK DOES NOT WORK HERE. MEMBUDGET_BACKEND=cgroup membudget run 64 fails with mkdir Permission denied, and cgroup-scope invoked directly fails identically. THE CAUSE IS A PATH CHOICE, NOT A PERMISSION MODEL. cgroup-scope probes for a v2 root carrying the memory controller and then creates its cgroup AT THAT ROOT — CG is V2_ROOT slash NAME, i.e. /sys/fs/cgroup/NAME — which is root-owned. ⚑ THE CALLER'S OWN CGROUP WOULD HAVE WORKED AND IS NEVER TRIED. /proc/self/cgroup puts this session at user.slice then user-1000.slice then user@1000.service then app.slice then app-code-NNN.scope. That directory's cgroup.controllers reads cpu memory pids, and the directory itself is drwxr-xr-x mikemol mikemol. Memory is delegated and the path is writable. Creating a CHILD there is the single mkdir the tool needs and does not attempt. ⚑⚑ THE HEADER'S PROBE NOTE IS ALSO STALE IN BOTH HALVES. It records cgroup2 at /sys/fs/cgroup/unified with controllers cpuset hugetlb and NO memory, and a usable cgroup v1 tree at /sys/fs/cgroup/memory. This host is unified v2 with memory delegated per-scope and has no v1 memory tree at all, so both recorded probes describe a machine state that no longer holds. _scope_backend hides the drift because it probes by EXECUTION and falls through to none, which REFUSES rather than reporting which backend failed and why — correct as a safety stance, opaque as a diagnostic. ⚑ CONSEQUENCE: any consumer without a systemd bus — a Bazel sandbox, a container whose PID 1 is not systemd — has NO working backend today, which is precisely the failure the header says cgroup-scope was built to prevent. The fallback exists, is wired, and has been dead for however long the delegation model has been per-scope. STANDING until cgroup-scope creates its cgroup under the caller's own path from /proc/self/cgroup. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE SYSTEMD-LESS FALLBACK IS UNUSABLE UNPRIVILEGED, AND THE REASON IS A PATH CHOICE. scripts/cgroup-scope probes for a v2 root carrying the memory controller and then creates its cgroup AT THAT ROOT: CG=/, i.e. /sys/fs/cgroup/<name>. That directory is root-owned, so an unprivileged caller gets mkdir: Permission denied. MEASURED on this host: MEMBUDGET_BACKEND=cgroup membudget run 64 fails with exactly that, as does cgroup-scope invoked directly. ⚑ AND THE CALLER'S OWN CGROUP WOULD HAVE WORKED. /proc/self/cgroup puts this session at user.slice/.../app.slice/app-code-NNN.scope, whose cgroup.controllers reads 'cpu memory pids' and whose directory is drwxr-xr-x mikemol mikemol — writable, memory delegated. Creating a CHILD there is the one mkdir the tool needs and never attempts. ⚑⚑ THE HEADER'S PROBE NOTE IS ALSO STALE: it records 'cgroup2 /sys/fs/cgroup/unified -> controllers cpuset hugetlb (NO memory)' and 'cgroup v1 /sys/fs/cgroup/memory (rw) -> usable'. This host is unified v2 with memory delegated per-scope and no v1 memory tree at all, so both probe results describe a machine state that no longer holds — and _scope_backend hides it, because it probes by EXECUTION and silently falls through to 'none' rather than reporting why. ⚑ CONSEQUENCE: any consumer without a systemd bus — a Bazel sandbox, a container whose PID 1 is not systemd — has no working backend today, which is the exact failure membudget's header says cgroup-scope was built to prevent.}
}

@misc{swarm-F9-no-internal-process-blocks-self-delegation,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A caller cannot cap a child of its own cgroup — v2's no-internal-process rule forbids delegating controllers from an occupied cgroup, so the sibling placement is structural rather than a systemd quirk.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher of=swarm-F8. CORRECTS THE FIX swarm-F8 IMPLIED AND THE ADVICE I HAD ALREADY SENT A PEER. swarm-F8 established that cgroup-scope creates its cgroup at the v2 ROOT and fails unprivileged, and observed that the caller's own scope is writable with memory in its cgroup.controllers. I concluded the repair was a path change and told paperkit the mechanism is one mkdir plus two writes under their own path. MEASURED, and the conclusion does not survive. mkdir under our own scope SUCCEEDS. The resulting child contains ONLY the pressure files — there is NO memory.max in it. The parent's cgroup.subtree_control reads EMPTY, and writing plus-memory to it fails with Device or resource busy. ⚑ THAT IS CGROUP V2'S NO-INTERNAL-PROCESS RULE. A cgroup that holds processes cannot enable controllers for its children. Our scope holds the entire VS Code process tree, so it can never delegate memory downward while occupied. The controller is available TO the scope and is not delegable BY it, and those are different facts that cgroup.controllers alone cannot distinguish — reading it as availability-for-children is the error. ⚑⚑ SO THE PATH FIX ALONE IS INSUFFICIENT. A working systemd-less backend needs either a cgroup created OUTSIDE the occupied one, or a caller that first moves its own processes into a leaf and then caps a sibling leaf. Both are materially more than one mkdir, and the second changes the caller's own placement, which is not something a wrapper should do to its parent unasked. ⚑ AND IT REFRAMES SOMETHING membudget'S HEADER RECORDS AS A MEASURED SURPRISE. That header notes a nested systemd-run scope lands as a SIBLING under app.slice rather than a child, treats it as a systemd-run behaviour that had to be discovered by experiment, and warns against re-deriving it from the comment. It is not a systemd quirk. It is forced by the same kernel rule, and ANY correct implementation lands in the same place for the same reason. The measurement was right and the attribution was to the wrong layer. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): CORRECTS swarm-F8's IMPLIED FIX AND MY ADVICE TO A PEER. I said cgroup-scope's only defect is targeting the v2 ROOT instead of the caller's own cgroup, and told paperkit the mechanism is one mkdir plus two writes under their own path. MEASURED: mkdir under our own scope SUCCEEDS, and the child contains ONLY *.pressure files — NO memory.max. The parent's cgroup.subtree_control is EMPTY, and writing +memory to it fails with 'Device or resource busy'. ⚑ THAT IS CGROUP V2'S NO-INTERNAL-PROCESS RULE: a cgroup holding processes cannot enable controllers for its children. Our scope holds the whole VS Code process tree, so it can NEVER delegate memory downward while occupied. So a caller cannot simply create a capped child of itself — the controller is available TO the scope and not delegable BY it. ⚑⚑ THE PATH FIX ALONE IS THEREFORE INSUFFICIENT. A working systemd-less backend needs either a cgroup created OUTSIDE the occupied one (which is what systemd-run does, and why it lands as an app.slice SIBLING), or a caller that moves its own processes into a leaf first and caps a sibling leaf. Both are more than one mkdir. ⚑ AND THE SIBLING PLACEMENT membudget's HEADER RECORDS AS A SURPRISE IS NOT AN ARTEFACT OF systemd — it is forced by the same rule. The header treats it as a systemd-run quirk that had to be measured; it is the kernel's structural constraint, and any correct implementation lands in the same place.}
}

@misc{swarm-F10-the-grandparent-slice-is-the-answer,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A sibling cgroup under the caller's parent SLICE is unprivileged, systemd-free and fully delegated — so the systemd-less backend is three writes, and cgroup-scope simply targets the wrong root.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher of=swarm-F9. THE OPERATOR NAMED THE ANSWER AT THE MOMENT I HAD CONCLUDED THE PATH WAS BLOCKED. swarm-F8 found cgroup-scope creating its cgroup at the root-owned v2 root. swarm-F9 then found that the caller's OWN scope cannot delegate memory downward — cgroup.subtree_control is empty and writing plus-memory returns Device or resource busy, because v2 forbids a cgroup holding processes from enabling controllers for children. I was drafting a message telling a peer the systemd-less route needed materially more than one mkdir. Operator: can we spawn a scope parented off our GRANDPARENT. MEASURED, AND YES. app.slice's cgroup.subtree_control reads cpu memory pids — THE DELEGATION IS A PROPERTY OF THE SLICE, NOT OF SYSTEMD, which is exactly why systemd-run's scopes land there and get a working MemoryMax. An unprivileged mkdir of app.slice/mbtest.scope succeeds, and the resulting directory carries the FULL delegated set: memory.max, memory.peak, memory.swap.max, memory.high, cpu.weight, cpu.max, pids.max. Writing 1048576 to memory.max succeeds and reads back verbatim. Cleaned up with rmdir. ⚑ SO THE REPAIR IS STILL A PATH CHOICE, JUST NOT THE ONE swarm-F8 NAMED. Three candidate roots and only the third works: /sys/fs/cgroup is root-owned, the caller's own scope is occupied and cannot delegate, and the caller's PARENT SLICE is delegated and writable. It is derivable from /proc/self/cgroup by dropping the final component. ⚑⚑ AND IT FULLY EXPLAINS THE SIBLING PLACEMENT membudget's HEADER RECORDS AS A SURPRISE. That header notes a nested systemd-run scope lands as an app.slice SIBLING rather than a child, treats it as systemd-run behaviour discovered by experiment, and warns against re-deriving it from prose. It is neither a quirk nor a systemd behaviour: the parent slice is the ONLY place a delegated controller set exists, so systemd-run and a hand-rolled mkdir necessarily agree. Three findings were needed to get here and each corrected the last, which is the cost of reading cgroup.controllers as availability-for-children rather than availability-to-this-cgroup. ⚑ SECOND CONSEQUENCE, FOR A PEER'S OPEN ASK: cpu.weight sits in that same delegated set, so a consumer that constructs its own sibling can set a scheduling weight directly and does not need membudget to pass a systemd property through. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A SIBLING UNDER app.slice IS AN UNPRIVILEGED, SYSTEMD-FREE, FULLY-DELEGATED CGROUP, AND THE OPERATOR NAMED IT WHEN I HAD CONCLUDED THE PATH WAS BLOCKED. swarm-F9 established that our own scope cannot delegate memory downward (no-internal-process rule, Device or resource busy), and I was about to tell a peer the systemd-less route needed more than a mkdir. Operator asked: can we spawn a scope parented off our GRANDPARENT? MEASURED, and yes. app.slice's cgroup.subtree_control reads 'cpu memory pids' — the delegation is a property of the SLICE, not of systemd, which is why systemd-run's scopes land there and get a working MemoryMax. An unprivileged mkdir of app.slice/mbtest.scope succeeds and the resulting directory carries the FULL delegated set: memory.max, memory.peak, memory.swap.max, cpu.weight, pids.max. Writing 1048576 to memory.max succeeds and reads back. ⚑ SO THE SYSTEMD-LESS BACKEND IS THREE WRITES AFTER ALL — the correction is not that cgroup-scope needs more machinery, but that it targets the wrong ROOT. Not /sys/fs/cgroup (root-owned, swarm-F8), not the caller's own scope (occupied, swarm-F9), but the caller's PARENT SLICE, derivable from /proc/self/cgroup by dropping the final component. ⚑⚑ AND IT EXPLAINS THE SIBLING PLACEMENT COMPLETELY. membudget's header records as a measured surprise that a nested systemd-run scope lands as an app.slice sibling rather than a child. That is not a systemd behaviour and not a quirk — it is the only place a delegated controller set exists, so systemd-run and a hand-rolled mkdir necessarily agree. ⚑ ALSO USABLE: cpu.weight is in that same set, so a consumer can set a scheduling weight on a cgroup it created itself without needing membudget to pass a systemd property through.}
}

@misc{swarm-F11-gc-is-ledger-only,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {membudget's gc collects LEDGER LINES ONLY and never touches cgroups — on the systemd arm systemd reaps for us, and on the cgroup arm nothing does.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher of=swarm-F10. The operator asked whether sibling cgroups get cleaned up by membudget's gc. THE ANSWER IS NO, UNLESS WE ADD IT. gc() reads the ledger TEXT FILE, drops LEASE lines whose owner pid is dead and cascades to leases whose parent vanished. It never opens /sys/fs/cgroup. The only rmdir anywhere in the toolkit is a single trap cleanup EXIT inside cgroup-scope — the non-systemd path — and a trap does not run on SIGKILL. ⚑ THE LEDGER AND THE CGROUP HAVE SEPARATE LIFETIMES AND ONLY THE LEDGER HAS A COLLECTOR. Found live while checking, which is what makes this measured rather than reasoned: three sleep-infinity claim holders at 65-plus minutes, left over from a completed agent's tests, each still inside its own app.slice transient scope — while membudget status read no active leases. The EXIT trap had released each LEASE when its membudget wrapper exited, and the PROCESS plus its cgroup outlived the accounting entry. A fourth, my own dash probe, at 21 minutes. ⚑⚑ THE SYSTEMD ARM IS SAVED BY SYSTEMD AND NOT BY US. Measured: killing the last process inside a transient run-p-star.scope makes systemd reap the directory immediately — three of four vanished the instant their payloads died, the fourth as soon as its own did. So this leak is INVISIBLE on the systemd arm and would be REAL on the cgroup arm, where the only reaper is a trap a hard kill skips. That is precisely the arm a Bazel sandbox is forced onto, so a consumer following swarm-F10's three-writes recipe inherits a leak the systemd path never shows them. ⚑ AND THE CLEANUP REPRODUCED TODAY'S LAUNCHER-VERSUS-WORKER PATTERN A FOURTH TIME: killing the /usr/bin/time wrapper left the sleep payload alive inside the scope, so the directory persisted until the payload itself was killed. Same shape as the pkill passes that reached launchers and missed workers, and as procstruct's leaves-first kill order which exists because of it. STANDING until either cgroup-scope's cleanup survives a hard kill or gc learns to reap empty cgroups it created. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): membudget's gc DOES NOT TOUCH CGROUPS — operator asked and the answer is no, unless we add it. gc() reads the ledger TEXT FILE and drops LEASE lines whose owner pid is dead, cascading to orphaned parents. It never opens /sys/fs/cgroup. The only rmdir in the whole toolkit is one trap cleanup EXIT inside cgroup-scope, which is the non-systemd path and does not run on SIGKILL. ⚑ THE LEDGER AND THE CGROUP HAVE SEPARATE LIFETIMES AND ONLY THE LEDGER HAS A COLLECTOR. Found live while checking: three sleep-infinity claim holders at 65+ minutes from a completed agent's tests, each still inside its own app.slice scope, while membudget status read 'no active leases'. The trap released the LEASE when the membudget wrapper exited; the PROCESS and its cgroup outlived it. ⚑⚑ TODAY'S SYSTEMD PATH IS SAVED BY SYSTEMD, NOT BY US: killing the last process in a transient run-p*.scope makes systemd reap the directory automatically — measured, three vanished the instant their payloads died. So the leak is invisible on the systemd arm and would be REAL on the cgroup arm, where nothing reaps but a trap that a hard kill skips. That is the arm a Bazel sandbox must use. ⚑ AND THE KILL REPRODUCED TODAY'S LAUNCHER-VERSUS-WORKER PATTERN AGAIN: killing the /usr/bin/time wrapper left the sleep payload alive inside the scope, so the directory persisted until the payload itself was killed. Fourth instance of that shape in one session.}
}

@misc{swarm-F12-cgroup-scope-repaired-and-reaps,
  rests-on = {swarm-F10-the-grandparent-slice-is-the-answer},
  enables  = {swarm-F11-gc-is-ledger-only},
  claim    = {The systemd-less cgroup arm is REPAIRED and now REAPS: the parent is the nearest ancestor whose cgroup.subtree_control actually lists memory, PROBED per launch rather than hardcoded to app.slice, and every launch first sweeps abandoned scopes in our own name-encoded namespace — because the EXIT trap is skipped by SIGKILL and membudget's gc is ledger-only.},
  check    = {selftest:scripts/cgroup-scope,selftest},
  note     = {kind=report by=substrate of=cgroup-scope ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): probe finds a delegating ancestor by walking subtree_control; a 256MiB allocation under a 64MiB cap exits 137 against a matched uncapped control; a SIGKILLed holder leaks its scope and the sweep collects it; a live one survives a zero-grace sweep; and membudget cgroup arm both leases and propagates 137 for the retry contract.},
  section  = {finding}
}

@misc{swarm-F13-controllers-is-not-subtree-control,
  rests-on = {swarm-F9-no-internal-process-blocks-self-delegation},
  claim    = {cgroup.controllers and cgroup.subtree_control answer DIFFERENT questions and reading the first is the trap that cost three rounds: controllers is what is AVAILABLE TO a cgroup, subtree_control is what it DELEGATES TO CHILDREN, and only the second predicts whether a child gets memory.max — our own scope reads 'cpu memory pids' in controllers while delegating nothing, which is why mkdir succeeds and yields a child with only *.pressure.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=report by=substrate of=cgroup-scope ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): STANDING — cgroup.controllers is what is AVAILABLE TO a cgroup, cgroup.subtree_control is what it DELEGATES TO CHILDREN, and only the second predicts whether a child gets memory.max. Reading the first is the trap: our own scope reads 'cpu memory pids' in controllers while subtree_control is empty, so mkdir succeeds and yields a child with only *.pressure. The repair walks ancestors testing subtree_control, never controllers.},
  section  = {finding}
}

@misc{swarm-F14-probe-by-execution-hides-the-reason,
  rests-on = {swarm-F8-cgroup-scope-targets-the-root},
  claim    = {membudget's _scope_backend probed the cgroup arm BY EXECUTION — running cgroup-scope 64 against /bin/true and reading the exit code — which conflates 'no delegating parent' with 'the payload failed', discards the diagnosis, and silently falls through to 'none'. That is how a DEAD arm stayed dead for an unknown period, and the repair is a side-effect-free probe verb that exits 3 with a reason instead of creating-and-reaping a cgroup on every backend query.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=report by=substrate of=membudget ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): STANDING — probing a backend BY EXECUTION conflates 'unusable' with 'the payload failed' and throws away the diagnosis. membudget's _scope_backend ran cgroup-scope against /bin/true and read the exit code, so a dead arm reported as merely absent and fell through to 'none' silently. A probe verb must be side-effect-free and must exit with a REASON.},
  section  = {finding}
}

@misc{swarm-F12-memory-max-alone-does-not-contain,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {With swap available memory.max is a RECLAIM threshold and not a kill boundary — so memory.max and memory.swap.max are one control in two files, and either alone is inert on this box.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=dispatcher, measured 2026-08-24, originating from a paperkit peer session and REPRODUCED HERE before being relayed to a running agent. DECISIVE PAIR — same cgroup, same 8MiB memory.max, same 64MiB TOUCHED allocation, exactly one variable. With memory.swap.max at its default of max the allocation SUCCEEDS and memory.events reads max 342 with oom_kill 0, so the limit was hit 342 times and nothing was killed. With memory.swap.max set to 0 the identical allocation is KILLED at exit 137. This host runs zram0 at priority 100 with 9.2G plus a 32G swapfile, so reclaim always succeeds and a capped runaway simply becomes slower rather than being contained. ⚑ THE FAILURE READS AS SUCCESS, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT DANGEROUS. The cgroup exists, the number reads back verbatim, cgroup.procs is populated. Asking whether the cap is SET returns yes. Only asking whether the cap BITES returns no. The peer named this the same inert-lever shape they hit that morning on a cpu.weight that read back at 20 while every cell ran outside the cgroup — a control verified by inspection rather than by effect. ⚑⚑ SO THE TWO FILES ARE ONE CONTROL, and that explains membudget's _launch_scope hardcoding MemoryMax and MemorySwapMax=0 together rather than as independent properties. I had described them to an agent as two properties, which would have permitted a refactor letting a caller set one without the other. cgroup-scope writes the swap line as a best-effort echo with stderr discarded and a true fallback, so it is silently skippable — and on this host that fallback is the entire difference between a cap and a decoration. ⚑ CONSEQUENCE FOR VERIFICATION EVERYWHERE THIS APPEARS: a single-arm 137 is consistent with a working cap and says nothing about WHICH of the two writes did the work, so a containment demo must run BOTH arms. And the probe allocation must TOUCH its pages, because an untouched malloc is not charged to the cgroup and a probe that only allocates measures nothing. STANDING. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): memory.max IS A RECLAIM THRESHOLD, NOT A KILL BOUNDARY, WHEN SWAP IS AVAILABLE — so a cap that reads back correctly can contain nothing. A peer measured it and I reproduced it before relaying. DECISIVE PAIR, same cgroup, same 8MiB memory.max, same 64MiB TOUCHED allocation, one variable: with memory.swap.max at its default of max the allocation SUCCEEDS and memory.events reads max 342 with oom_kill 0 — the limit was hit 342 times and nothing died; with memory.swap.max=0 the same allocation is KILLED at 137. This box runs /dev/zram0 at priority 100 with 9.2G plus a 32G swapfile, so reclaim always succeeds and a capped runaway merely gets slower. ⚑ THE FAILURE READS AS SUCCESS: the cgroup exists, the number reads back, cgroup.procs is populated, and it contains nothing. Checking whether the cap is SET returns yes; only checking whether it BITES returns no. ⚑⚑ SO memory.max AND memory.swap.max ARE ONE CONTROL IN TWO FILES, which explains _launch_scope hardcoding -p MemoryMax and -p MemorySwapMax=0 together. I had treated them as two independent properties in a brief. cgroup-scope writes the swap line as best-effort with 2>/dev/null || true, and on this host that || true is the difference between a cap and a decoration. ⚑ AND IT RAISES THE BAR FOR VERIFYING ANY CAP: a single-arm 137 is consistent with a working cap and says nothing about WHICH write did the work. Run both arms. The allocation must also TOUCH its pages — an untouched malloc is not charged, so a probe that only allocates measures nothing.}
}

@misc{swarm-F15-memory-max-alone-is-a-reclaim-threshold,
  rests-on = {swarm-F12-cgroup-scope-repaired-and-reaps},
  claim    = {memory.max ALONE does not contain on a box with swap — it is a RECLAIM THRESHOLD, not a kill boundary, because the kernel compresses into zram rather than killing. Reproduced independently in substrate as a decisive pair (8MiB cap, 64MiB TOUCHED, one variable): swap=default gave rc=0 with memory.events max=261 and oom_kill=0, swap=0 gave rc=137 with oom_kill=1. THE LIMIT WAS HIT 261 TIMES AND NOTHING DIED. memory.max and memory.swap.max=0 are ONE control in two files and neither is meaningful alone, so a best-effort swap write returns a scope that looks capped and contains nothing.},
  check    = {selftest:scripts/cgroup-scope,selftest},
  note     = {kind=report by=substrate of=cgroup-scope ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the decisive pair (test 2b) requires BOTH arms: swap at default must SURVIVE the capped touched allocation and swap=0 must exit 137. A single-arm 137 cannot say which of the two writes did the work.},
  section  = {finding}
}

@misc{gate-F36-lens-refutes-owed-unstatable-baseline-still-not-mine,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the runtime tenant lens refutes the OWED-UNSTATABLE verdict on pycodemod, so gate-F30's first reason is stale — but the baseline still must not be minted because run_selftests --tenants has no lens arm and still prints that refuted verdict},
  check    = {standing},
