@misc{sql-F37-tui-dependency-repair-and-a-mangled-witness,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {reuse_tui's module-scope textual import made --build unexercisable without the TUI library, so the reroute could have been declared landed from a read of the source while remaining unrunnable — and registering the finding through bash command-substituted every backticked identifier out of the witness},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r6. Supersedes sql-F36, whose stored note the shell mangled. Witness — --build --live --cap 3 --min-fanin 900 now reports 15289 units, 75 shared subtrees, 1884 defCopy instances from the live store, so has_copy resolved through schema.columns_of. Two further faults were visible only by running — argparse refused the tenant flags because store.tenant reads sys.argv while argparse validates it (now declared, generated from store.TENANT_FLAGS so the roster cannot drift), and the cache's source provenance field was the constant catalog.db, now recording the tenant instead. Tenant refusal fires on --build with no flag, and --observe runs with no tenant because it opens nothing. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): SUPERSEDES sql-F36, WHOSE NOTE THE SHELL ATE. Registering sql-F36 through bash command-substituted every backticked term, so the stored witness has holes exactly where its identifiers belong — it reads as vague prose rather than a measurement. Backticks in a findings note are EXECUTABLE, and a corrupted witness is worse than an absent one because it still looks like a record. The ledger correctly REFUSED to let me re-register the same key (editing a witness silently would let a measurement be restated without a diff), so the repair is a successor, which is the discipline working. THE FINDING ITSELF, stated cleanly. reuse_tui.py imported the textual library at MODULE scope, so --build — which opens the store, derives orbit rows and writes a JSON cache, touching no widget — died with ModuleNotFoundError before argparse ran, on a machine without textual (this one). The batch half was hostage to the interactive half's dependency, so the mode I was sent to repair was UNEXERCISABLE: Ⓥ16 could have been declared landed from a read of the source while remaining unrunnable. This is round 5's lesson a third time — a fact about the artifact, reachable only by opening it, and here only by RUNNING it. REPAIR: the import is guarded, App falls back to the builtin object so the class body still evaluates, and run_tui (the one entry point needing widgets) exits naming the cause plus the batch alternatives — deferred, not swallowed. TWO MORE FAULTS FELL OUT OF RUNNING IT, neither readable. (1) argparse REFUSED --live and --sandbox, because store.tenant reads sys.argv directly while argparse validates it. The flags are now declared and GENERATED FROM store.TENANT_FLAGS, never spelled by hand, so the roster cannot drift from the resolver's (gate-F19). (2) The cache's source provenance field was the constant catalog.db, already wrong before the reroute and wrong in a NEW way after. It records the TENANT now, which is the only thing that distinguishes two real stores whose wrong-tenant read is silent (sql-F2). MEASURED: --build --live --cap 3 --min-fanin 900 gives 15289 units, 75 shared subtrees, 1884 defCopy instances from the live store — so has_copy resolved through schema.columns_of. Refusal fires on --build with no flag. --observe runs with NO tenant, correctly, opening nothing.}
}

@misc{meta-F5-the-kind-menu-omitted-the-falsifiable-rung,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The findings ledger's witness kinds were present in its docstring but not in the refusal a filer actually reads, and the kind that fits a REFUSAL-shaped repair did not exist at all, so 150 of 163 witnesses defaulted to the one that cannot fail},
  check    = {selftest:python3,.claude/agents/findings.py,--kinds},
  note     = {kind=finding by=witness-adequacy-write. Re-derived census 2026-08-24: 150 standing, 8 unwitnessed, 3 mode, 2 selftest over 163 witnesses. The commissioning brief said 161 standing; the tree says 150. Two decorrelated instruments agree (pycodemod --calls _standing gives 150 call sites; the new --kinds mode reads each closure's __code__.co_names). Cause located in register()'s unknown-kind refusal, which listed only standing, unwitnessed and mode -- two vacuous kinds and one documentation kind -- while the docstring and usage line both named selftest. A docstring is not what arrives at the moment of need. Second half: no kind could express holds-when-the-command-refuses, which is the shape most repairs in this repo take, so standing was the only registerable option for exactly the findings where a false green is most expensive. Added refuses: as selftest:'s dual, reordered the refusal, added --kinds. ⚑ The --kinds mode's own first cut printed five zeroes and 163 unknown (matched bare stems against co_names, which holds the underscore-prefixed names) -- a false zero in the instrument built to measure adequacy, caught only because a five-way zero is obviously wrong. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓐ3 ⟡witness-adequacy. RE-DERIVED CENSUS, and the commissioning figure was wrong: 150 of 163 witnesses are standing, not 161. The rest are 8 unwitnessed, 3 mode: and 2 selftest:. Two decorrelated instruments agree (pycodemod --calls _standing gives 150 call sites; the new --kinds mode reads each registered closure's __code__.co_names). THE CAUSE IS NOT A HABIT, IT IS A MENU. register's unknown-kind refusal is the only kind-list a filer reads at the moment of need, and it named only standing, unwitnessed and mode: -- two vacuous kinds and one documentation kind. The docstring and the --register usage line both named selftest:, but a docstring is not what arrives when a guess is refused. AND THE SECOND HALF WAS A GENUINELY MISSING KIND: a repair in this repo is usually a REFUSAL, which holds when its command FAILS, so selftest: (which demands exit 0) is unregisterable for exactly the findings where a false green is most expensive, and standing was the only option left. Added refuses: as selftest:'s dual, reordered the refusal to lead with the falsifiable kinds, and added --kinds so the partition is a mode rather than a paragraph. This witness runs --kinds, so it reports 2 if the census stops working.}
}

@misc{meta-F6-standing-is-three-populations-not-one,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The 150 standing witnesses are three populations -- measured facts, repair-landed claims, and judgements -- and only the first is legitimately standing; the second is where a false green is expensive and the third should be unwitnessed},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=witness-adequacy-write. Ⓐ3 partition, filed UNWITNESSED because assigning 150 findings to buckets is itself a judgement no command makes. (A) MEASURED FACTS: sql-F2, sql-F3, swarm-F12-memory-max, build-F1, gate-F1, tower-F1. The kind's designed case; leave standing. (B) REPAIR-LANDED: sql-F9, sql-F22, sql-F30, sql-F32, sql-F34, gate-F10, gate-F22, gate-F-fixture-category, agda-F13-reframe and others. A revert would leave the witness green; convert to refuses:/selftest: one at a time as discriminators are established. (C) JUDGEMENTS AND LESSONS: meta-F1..F5, gate-F14, gate-F24, gate-F27, sql-F18, sql-F25, tmi-F4. standing OVERSTATES these -- it claims a measurement was taken where the content is an adjudication -- and unwitnessed is the honest kind. ⚑ NOT ACTED ON WHOLESALE, and the ledger's own gate-F14 is why: a declaration that cannot fail is worse than none, and a partition asserted by an agent reading notes is that. It becomes witnessable when a mode reads each note for a repair-verb and reports population (B) mechanically; that mode does not exist and would require the notes to be structured, which they are not. ⚑ ALSO NOTE the boundary is not always clean: sql-F13-fixture-is-not-a-negative-control is BOTH a lesson and a repair, and swarm-F12 already exists as two separate keys splitting exactly along the (A)/(B) line, which is the natural resolution -- file the lesson and the repair as separate findings rather than forcing one key into one bucket. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓐ3 PARTITION. The 150 standing witnesses are THREE populations and only one of them is legitimately standing. (A) MEASURED FACTS about how something behaves -- sql-F2 wrong-tenant-read-is-silent, sql-F3 sampled-state-cannot-see-events, swarm-F12-memory-max-alone-does-not-contain, build-F1, gate-F1, tower-F1. These are what the kind was designed for and re-measuring them is possible but expensive and would mostly re-derive a fact nobody disputes. LEAVE STANDING. (B) REPAIR-LANDED claims -- sql-F9, sql-F22, sql-F30, sql-F32, sql-F34, gate-F10, gate-F22, gate-F-fixture-category, swarm-F12-repaired, agda-F13-reframe and others. These are where a false green is expensive because the repair can be reverted with nothing noticing. CONVERT to refuses: or selftest: as discriminators are established, one at a time. (C) JUDGEMENTS AND LESSONS -- meta-F1 through meta-F5, gate-F14, gate-F24, gate-F27, sql-F18, sql-F25, tmi-F4. No command distinguishes these true from false, which is what unwitnessed exists for, and standing overstates them: standing claims a MEASUREMENT was taken, whereas these are adjudications. ⚑ WHY THIS IS FILED UNWITNESSED RATHER THAN ACTED ON WHOLESALE: assigning all 150 to a bucket is itself a judgement no command makes, and the ledger's own gate-F14 says a declaration that cannot fail is worse than none. A partition asserted by an agent reading notes is exactly that. It becomes witnessable when a mode reads each note for a repair-verb and reports the (B) population mechanically; that mode does not exist and would need the notes to be structured, which they are not.}
}

@misc{meta-F7-mode-verdict-was-a-disjunction,
