  check    = {selftest:python3,.claude/agents/findings.py,--kinds},
  note     = {kind=finding by=witness-adequacy-write. Ⓐ7 mode-kind-disjunction, REPAIRED. All three mode: witnesses returned 'X is documented (or no longer dispatched)'. ROOT: toolmodes --divergence lists only tools that HAVE a divergence, so absence from its output means either DOCUMENTED or GONE and the witness reported both identically -- census-green-is-a-fact-about-the-census written into the verdict STRING, where a filer cannot reach it. SPLIT by adding a SECOND DECORRELATED LENS: --find <mode> reports [usage] / [optlist+usage] for a declared mode and 'declared by NO tool' otherwise. Absent-from-divergence plus present-in-find is DOCUMENTED (0, and the verdict now names the declaration source); absent from BOTH is GONE (1). ⚑ A DELETED MODE IS OPEN, NOT CLOSED -- refuses:-shaped reasoning applied to mode:. The finding recorded that a capability the tool HAS is undiscoverable and named a usage line as its repair; if the mode vanished the repair did not land, the SUBJECT did. Reading that as closed lets any finding be retired by deleting the thing it is about, the cheapest false green a ratchet can grant. Precedent: sql-F10-verify-is-a-census-artifact, where a non-dispatching mode must NOT be documented and that judgement was filed as its own finding rather than absorbed into a green. DISCRIMINATION ESTABLISHED BY EXECUTION on four probes: --cycles on agda_defs gives 0 DOCUMENTED [optlist+usage]; --builders on pipeflow gives 1 still-dispatches; a nonexistent mode on a real tool gives 1 GONE; any mode on a nonexistent tool gives 1 GONE. Under the old code probes 1, 3 and 4 all returned 0 with the same sentence. ⚑ THE SECOND LENS PARSES THE ROW, NEVER THE BLOCK: --find prints a 'NOT on:' line listing every other tool by path, so a substring test would report a mode as declared by the one tool explicitly said not to have it. ⚑ ALL THREE LIVE mode: ROWS ARE CURRENTLY TRUTHFUL, so this changed no verdict today; it removed a way for a future one to be wrong. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓐ7 mode-kind-disjunction REPAIRED. All three mode: witnesses returned the sentence 'X is documented (or no longer dispatched)' -- ONE green covering two OPPOSITE states, and no care at the filing site could fix it because the ambiguity was in the verdict STRING every registration inherits. ROOT: toolmodes --divergence lists only tools that HAVE a divergence, so absence from its output means either documented or gone, and the witness could not tell which. SPLIT by adding a SECOND DECORRELATED LENS: --find <mode> reports [usage] / [optlist+usage] for a declared mode and 'declared by NO tool' otherwise. Absent-from-divergence plus present-in-find is DOCUMENTED (0, and the verdict now names the declaration source); absent from BOTH is GONE (1). ⚑ A DELETED MODE IS OPEN, NOT CLOSED, and that is refuses:-shaped reasoning applied to mode:. The finding recorded that a capability the tool HAS is undiscoverable and named a usage line as the repair; if the mode vanished then the repair did not land, the SUBJECT did. Reading that as closed would let any finding be retired by deleting the thing it is about -- the cheapest possible false green. Precedent: sql-F10-verify-is-a-census-artifact, where a non-dispatching mode must NOT be documented and that judgement was filed as its own finding rather than absorbed into a green. DISCRIMINATION ESTABLISHED BY EXECUTION on four probes: --cycles on agda_defs gives 0 DOCUMENTED [optlist+usage]; --builders on pipeflow gives 1 still-dispatches; a nonexistent mode on a real tool gives 1 GONE; any mode on a nonexistent tool gives 1 GONE. Under the old code probes 1, 3 and 4 all returned 0 with the same sentence. ⚑ AND THE SECOND LENS PARSES THE ROW, NEVER THE BLOCK: --find prints a 'NOT on:' line listing every other tool by path, so a substring test would report a mode as declared by the one tool explicitly said not to have it. ⚑ ALL THREE LIVE mode: ROWS ARE CURRENTLY TRUTHFUL -- agda_defs --cycles, fix_notinscope --summary and --classify are all genuinely documented, so this repair changed no verdict today; it removed a way for a future one to be wrong.}
