  note     = {kind=finding by=main. Verdict right, citation wrong. sql-F9 exit 2 cited a dry-run hint. sql-F38 exit 1 cited a bare caret. Repair is _evidence, which picks a refusal-announcing line and flags the fallback as not-a-refusal-line. It chooses the QUOTE, never the verdict. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓐ11 message-presence-is-not-refusal, and the defect is in the CITATION rather than the verdict. _refuses reads an exit code and is correct; beside that verdict it printed tail[-1], the last non-blank output line. MEASURED 2026-08-24 on this ledger's own witnesses: sql-F9 exits 2 and cited 'pass --dry-run to see WHOSE backend that PID currently is' (a help hint), sql-F38 exits 1 and cited a bare caret from a SQL error. Two of four refuses witnesses displayed evidence saying nothing about the refusal, so an auditor is handed a plausible sentence that cannot distinguish a fired guard from a command that failed for an unrelated reason. ORIGIN: the negative control on sql-F22 showed refusal messages STILL PRINT under a reverted refusal, so message-presence never established refusal; the converse also holds, message-ABSENCE from the cited line never established its lack. REPAIR: _evidence scans for a line announcing a refusal and falls back to the last line EXPLICITLY FLAGGED as not-a-refusal-line. It chooses which line to QUOTE and never the verdict, because scoring an exit code by message text is the confusion the selftest/refuses split exists to end. sql-F9 now cites the guard, sql-F38 the GroupingError. NOT VERIFIED BY REVERT: the discrimination rests on the two measured before/after citations.}
