@misc{gate-F43-pycheck-verdict-is-closure-wide-not-file-scoped,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {hook_pycheck's verdict on one named .py is closure-wide, not file-scoped -- ruff sees only the file (--stdin-filename) while mypy is handed a bare operand and follows imports, so a clean drained module is refused for its dependencies debt.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. The witness. python3 scripts/hook_pycheck.py --check-file jea/metalanguage/schema_rows.py exits 1 FINDINGS. The venv ruff run with --config pyproject.toml --no-cache --statistics on the same path prints 'Found 154 errors'. The venv mypy run with --config-file pyproject.toml on the same path emits rows for jea/metalanguage/store.py and jea/metalanguage/schema_decl.py, files not named on the command line. python3 substrate/type_scope.py jea/metalanguage/schema_rows.py --own separates them and prints '228 message(s) with the closure resolved -- 228 OWN, 0 CLOSURE-INDUCED, 0 closure-SUPPRESSED'. type_scope is the only reader with the OWN/CLOSURE split and --check-file has no such mode. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): MEASURED 2026-09-02. hook_pycheck --check-file names ONE path and returns a verdict over that file's whole IMPORT CLOSURE, because pycheck_checkers passes the tempfile as a bare mypy OPERAND and mypy follows imports. On jea/metalanguage/schema_rows.py the ruff half is 154 findings ON THE FILE (ruff is fed via --stdin-filename and sees only that source), while the mypy half's report is dominated by store.py and schema_decl.py rows the named file does not own. substrate/type_scope.py --own is the ONLY reader that separates them and reports 228 OWN / 0 CLOSURE-INDUCED for that file. THE CONTRACT A GREEN ASSERTS: not 'this file is clean' but 'this file AND everything it imports from source are clean' -- which is why pyproject's files=[substrate] comment states a file is only checkable to this bar once its import closure is. THE BLIND SPOT: a drained module that is itself clean is REFUSED for a dependency's debt, and the refusal message names the dependency's path, so an author reads it as their own file being dirty.}
}

@misc{gate-F50-per-file-narrowing-blocked-by-baseline-not-predicate,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {For most Agda-side gates the PREDICATE is already per-file — what is inherently whole-tree is the ratchet BASELINE COMPARISON (is this key NEW), not the predicate, so a per-file narrowing yields a VIOLATION verdict and never a ratchet verdict.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. MEASURED 2026-09-02. Read the census() of check_ban_ratchet (line 85), check_public_ratchet (line 67) and check_def_proof_separation.sh — each is 'for path in agda_files() then a predicate over that file's own text', a per-file predicate implemented as a corpus sweep. Contrast check_carrier_locality.judge, whose OWN docstring states the obstruction exactly - 'its unique filter (a carrier declared in exactly one file) is a global property, so scanning a subset and judging it would call every carrier unique' - and whose scan() ALREADY takes a paths= argument, so the per-file question was anticipated and refused at judge() rather than at scan(). THE BLIND SPOT a per-file green would assert. For a set-ratchet one file cannot answer NEW-vs-PAID because the key set is compared to a baseline, and 11 of 20 baselines are EMPTY so every key reads NEW. A per-file 'clean' would assert only 'this file contributes no census keys', which is strictly weaker than the gate verdict and says nothing about relocation, since key_move RELOCATED is a whole-census 1-to-1 pairing. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Most Agda-side gate predicates are already per-file; what is whole-tree is the ratchet BASELINE COMPARISON, not the predicate.}
}

