@misc{suffixless-claim-now-expressible,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {claims-column-cannot-express-a-suffixless-artifact},
  claim    = {A claims cell may declare a bare filename as well as a suffix, so a suffixless artifact is routable and the two key spaces cannot collide.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,scripts/hook_structural_query.py,--selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude. CORRECTS claims-column-cannot-express-a-suffixless-artifact, kept verbatim as residue. Measured - grep against agda/Makefile now fires and names substrate/make_rules.py where it previously returned no violation, while grep against agda/Flat.mk still routes on the .mk key. claims() reads suffix and filename tokens into one map because a suffix key always begins with a dot and a filename key never does. Two properties are pinned by cases: a suffix claim beats a filename claim for the same file, so a specific name cannot shadow a general kind, and the case fold is over BOTH sides - a first cut folded only the argument, which compares two spellings of it against one spelling of the claim, and the lowercase file did not route until a FAILING case established it. The suite went 52 to 59, and the seven added cases exist because it passed identically at 52 before and after the capability landed. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): CORRECTS claims-column-cannot-express-a-suffixless-artifact, kept verbatim as residue. That finding said a suffix-keyed claim structurally cannot express a suffixless artifact and that writing Makefile into the claims column would be matched as an extension. Both were true of the mechanism as it stood and neither is now: claims() reads a second token form, a bare filename, into the SAME map - a suffix key always begins with a dot and a filename key never does, so the key spaces cannot collide and no second roster was needed. MEASURED via the hook's own verdict: grep against agda/Makefile now fires and names substrate/make_rules.py, where it previously returned no violation. Two design points a case each pins - the suffix claim wins when both could match, so a filename claim cannot shadow a kind claim; and the case fold is over BOTH sides, which a FAILING case established after a first cut folded only the argument and left the lowercase spelling unrouted.}
