@misc{syspath-drain-is-three-populations,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The sys.path drain is three populations with three repairs, and only the module-level third is mechanical.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/insert_scope_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude. Measured by substrate/insert_scope over the tracked python tree - 328 module-level, 122 function-scoped, 42 guarded, of 492 total. A module-level insert runs once at import and is deletable once its directory is importable another way. A function-scoped one runs per call, so removing it moves import timing rather than merely removing a mutation. A guarded one encodes a condition that deletion erases. The function-scoped sites cluster in _selftest, selftest and main, which is the code that verifies everything else. Scope is tracked by walking with parents rather than ast.walk, which is flat and loses exactly this - and FUNCTION beats GUARDED when both apply, because the timing question dominates the condition question. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The 492 sys.path mutation sites are not one queue. Measured by substrate/insert_scope over the tracked python tree: 328 module-level, 122 function-scoped, 42 guarded. Only the module-level ones are mechanically drainable - removing a function-scoped insert changes WHEN an import happens (it runs per call, not once at import), and removing a guarded one deletes the CONDITION along with the mutation. The function-scoped sites cluster in _selftest, selftest and main - entry points reaching for tooling they deliberately do not import at module level, which is the code that verifies everything else. A drain planned against the flat total would treat 164 semantic edits as hygiene.}
}

@misc{drainable-set-is-three-of-492,
