  claim    = {One half of the census cycle is a single selftest import, so the knot is cuttable without touching production code.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/baseline_health_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude. check_baseline_health imports gate_census at module scope and calls baseline_health() from its census - structural and load-bearing. The reverse edge is ONE site, gate_census.py:911, inside the gate's own selftest, importing check_baseline_health only to assert against its census. Measured by attr_reads with the dotted-receiver form, which finds exactly one reverse use. The site's comment gives sound reasoning - the gate's census is the authority so importing beats restating - and that reasoning is what ties the knot. substrate/baseline_health already computes the same reading, is clean and lives in the package, so repointing the selftest cuts the cycle while keeping the authority argument intact. Non-vacuity established before trusting the agreement - both readers name zero defects, but the extracted one produces 20 rows across states EMPTY and ok while the origin's strong() returns 11 and unread() returns 1, so this is agreement between live readers rather than two empty sets. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The census knot's gate_census <-> check_baseline_health cycle exists ONLY inside a selftest. check_baseline_health imports gate_census at module scope and calls gate_census.baseline_health() from its census - a genuine structural dependency. The reverse edge is a single site, gate_census.py:911, inside the gate's own selftest function: 'import check_baseline_health as _bh' purely to assert against its census rather than restate it. Production code carries no such edge. Measured by attr_reads check_baseline_health.* which finds exactly one reverse use. The comment at the site gives sound reasoning - the gate's census is the authority, so importing beats re-deriving - and that reasoning happens to tie a knot. substrate/baseline_health computes the same reading, is clean, and lives in the package, so pointing the selftest there cuts the cycle without losing the authority argument. The two readers agree: both name zero defects, and the agreement is NON-VACUOUS - the extracted reader produces 20 rows across states EMPTY and ok, and the origin's own strong() returns 11 and unread() 1.}
}

@misc{exact-and-still-the-wrong-question,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {census-knot-is-a-selftest-only-cycle},
  claim    = {A measurement can be provably exact and still answer the wrong question, when the cost model is uniform where the domain is not.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/feedback_arc_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude. substrate/feedback_arc returns a proven MINIMUM of two cuts for the census knot and names the structural edges. One direction of that cycle is a module-scope import whose caller consumes the callee's output. The other is one line inside a selftest, importing the peer only to assert against its census rather than restate it. Both are one edge, so a solver minimising edge COUNT cannot tell them apart, and its minimum is correct and unusable. This is neither approximation error nor a defect in the solver - it is a domain the cost model does not model. A peer reports the same shape from the other side, having measured that 15 of 17 boundary suites derive ROOT from __file__ and cited it for a packaging decision the number did not bear on, then defended it until an install proved the mechanism could not work at all. The missing axis is whether an edge is load-bearing AT RUNTIME or only in a test - a third category beside the import-versus-read distinction that closure readers already draw, and one neither repo has a name for. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A measurement can be provably exact and still answer the wrong question, when the cost model is uniform where the domain is not. Measured: substrate/feedback_arc returns a proven MINIMUM of 2 cuts for the census knot and names the structural edges. One direction of that cycle is a module-scope import whose caller genuinely consumes the callee's output; the other is a single line inside a selftest importing the peer only to assert against its census. Both are one edge, so a solver minimising edge COUNT cannot distinguish them - the minimum is correct and unusable. This is not approximation error and not a bug in the solver; it is a domain the cost model does not model. A peer reports the same shape from a different direction - they measured that 15 of 17 boundary suites derive ROOT from __file__ and used it to argue a packaging decision the number did not bear on, then defended it until an install proved the mechanism could not work at all. The missing axis here is whether an edge is load-bearing AT RUNTIME or only in a test, which is a third category beside the import-vs-read distinction closure readers already draw.}
}

@misc{prefer-the-probe-that-fails-loudly,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {exact-and-still-the-wrong-question},
  claim    = {An empty result and a blind probe are indistinguishable at the call site, so prefer the probe that would fail loudly over the one that returns an empty set.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/gate_population_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude. Four instances this session, three local. The literal-search mode showed only a docstring mention for a real import, because the import is FUNCTION-SCOPED and that mode reports the enclosing function, so prose and absence rendered identically. The attribute-read mode returned zero for a live capability and is now RETIRED for false zeros, refusing rather than answering and naming its successor. The summit capability lookup returned no-entry for cycle, scc and tarjan across 389 spellings while substrate demonstrably owns a feedback-arc-set solver, which is a fact about the index delivered in the grammar of a fact about the world. A peer contributes two more: a counted grep returned 0 on a log whose line was truncated mid-token, briefly clearing them of an interference they had caused, and a bazel file query listed a file their action never received because the queried provider yields a library's SOURCES while its data lives in RUNFILES. Every probe answered honestly about what it could see. The readers shipped this session apply the rule - attr_reads prints its ten unresolvable shapes beside a zero, census_call refuses rather than returning an empty census, editable reports UNKNOWN rather than clean, and agda_coverage asserts its found key by name so an always-empty answer cannot pass as coverage. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): An empty result and a blind probe are indistinguishable at the call site, so prefer the probe that would FAIL LOUDLY over the one that returns an empty set. Four instances this session, three mine: pycodemod --literal showed only a docstring mention for a real import, because the import is FUNCTION-SCOPED and that mode reports the enclosing function - prose and absence render identically. pycodemod --attr returned zero for a live capability and is now RETIRED for returning false zeros, refusing rather than answering, and naming its successor. summit capability returned no-entry for cycle, scc and tarjan across 389 spellings while substrate demonstrably owns a feedback-arc-set solver - a fact about the index reported in the grammar of a fact about the world. A peer reports two more: grep -c returned 0 on a log whose line was TRUNCATED mid-token, briefly clearing them of an interference they had caused, and cquery --output=files listed a file their action never received because ctx.files gives a py_library's SOURCES while its data lives in RUNFILES. Every one answered honestly about what it could see. The readers this session ships apply the rule - attr_reads names its ten unresolvable shapes beside a zero, census_call refuses rather than returning an empty census, and editable reports UNKNOWN rather than clean.}
}

@misc{file-cycles-are-co-location-artifacts,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {exact-and-still-the-wrong-question},
  claim    = {A file-level import cycle whose def graph is acyclic is a co-location artifact, and extraction resolves it where cutting only severs a real dependency.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/baseline_health_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude, from an operator observation. Measured on the census knot - gate_census.selftest reaches check_baseline_health.census, and five defs in check_baseline_health reach gate_census.baseline_health. That is a chain, selftest to census to baseline_health, with no def reaching itself. The mutual FILE dependency is an artifact of co-location, because baseline_health and selftest both live in gate_census.py, so two independent one-way def dependencies present as one cycle. The operator's argument is stronger than edge weighting - a def-level cycle cannot execute properly, so a file cycle with an acyclic def graph is always resolvable by lifting the shared def into a third module. Cutting removes a dependency someone has. Extraction relocates the shared def so no caller loses anything, and the cycle becomes a DAG. substrate/baseline_health.py already IS that extraction, built earlier in the session without recognising it as this cycle's solution. Weighting stays the right reader for a cycle that is genuinely def-level. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A file-level import cycle can be a DAG at def level, and when it is, extraction beats cutting. Measured on the census knot: gate_census.selftest reaches check_baseline_health.census, and five defs in check_baseline_health reach gate_census.baseline_health. That is a CHAIN - selftest to census to baseline_health - with no def reaching itself. The mutual file dependency is an artifact of co-location: baseline_health and selftest both live in gate_census.py, so two independent one-way def dependencies read as one cycle. Operator's point, and it is stronger than edge weighting: a def-level cycle cannot execute properly, so a file cycle whose def graph is acyclic is always resolvable by lifting the shared def into a third module. Cutting an edge REMOVES a dependency someone has; extraction RELOCATES the shared def so no caller loses anything. substrate/baseline_health.py already is that extraction - built earlier in this session without recognising it as the cycle's solution. Weighting remains the right reader for a cycle that is genuinely def-level, which this one is not.}
}

@misc{a-detected-def-cycle-is-a-reader-defect,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {file-cycles-are-co-location-artifacts},
  claim    = {A detected module-scope def cycle is a defect in the detector, because such a cycle cannot execute at all.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/def_cycle_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude, from an operator correction. Python evaluates a module top to bottom, so a genuine cycle among module-scope definitions would reference an unbound name and fail on import. A reader reporting one has therefore found something about itself. I was about to ship edge-weighting as the right tool for genuinely def-level cycles, which is unsound by construction - the operator's phrase. substrate/def_cycle now returns SUSPECT for that case rather than a legitimate verdict, and names call-time resolution as the usual shape to unroll. Measured over six knot pairs - 4 co-location artifacts each resolvable by lifting a single def (baseline_health, provides, blame), and 2 NOT MUTUAL. The not-mutual verdict is itself a correction: a first cut classified those as artifacts and rendered LIFT one of (none reached), advice naming nothing, which reads as a verdict while admitting the file edge came from somewhere unseen. pycodemod against _pycodemod_selftest, which an earlier run of mine called a clean two-cycle, carries no backward def edges at all. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A module-scope def cycle cannot execute - Python evaluates a module top to bottom, so the second definition would reference an unbound name - therefore a reader that DETECTS one has found a defect in itself, not a property of the code. Operator: 'If we detect a def-level cycle, that is a bug in our detector. Chances are there is some kind of lazy evaluation pattern happening we can unroll.' I had been about to ship edge-weighting as the right tool for genuinely def-level cycles, which is unsound by construction. substrate/def_cycle now returns SUSPECT for that case rather than treating it as a legitimate verdict, and names call-time resolution as the usual shape to unroll. Measured over six knot pairs: 4 co-location artifacts resolvable by lifting a single def (baseline_health, provides, blame), 2 NOT MUTUAL. The not-mutual verdict is itself a correction - a first cut classified those as artifacts and rendered 'LIFT one of: (none reached)', advice naming nothing, which reads as a verdict and is an admission that the file edge came from somewhere the reader cannot see. pycodemod against _pycodemod_selftest, which an earlier run of mine called a clean 2-cycle, has no backward def edges at all.}
}

@misc{not-mutual-pairs-resolved-differently,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {a-detected-def-cycle-is-a-reader-defect},
  claim    = {One not-mutual pair was never a cycle and the other is real, and the disagreement that looked like a reader defect was a function-scope blind spot.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/def_cycle_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude. agda_defs against split_census carries three def edges one way and ZERO def edges plus ZERO bare edges the other, so it is not a cycle at all and the knot reading claimed a dependency that does not exist. pycodemod against _pycodemod_selftest is genuinely mutual - bare-name imports both directions, def edges one way only, so the tool imports its selftest without calling any of its definitions. I first accused bare_edges of a false positive because the binding reader reported ZERO for _pycodemod_selftest, _pycodemod_census and dagcone in that file while finding sys at line 722. Both readers are correct about different questions. The binding reader reports MODULE-SCOPE bindings and those imports sit inside _dispatch, one function spanning lines 1878 to 4051. A 2173-line function is why a module-scope reader sees nothing, and it is the same function-scope blind spot that earlier made a literal search report a real import as a docstring mention - hit from the opposite direction. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The two NOT-MUTUAL knot pairs have different explanations, and chasing them corrected an accusation I made against my own reader. agda_defs against split_census is NOT A CYCLE - three def edges one way, zero def edges and zero bare edges the other. The knot reading claimed a mutual dependency that does not exist. pycodemod against _pycodemod_selftest IS mutual: bare-name imports both directions, def edges one way only, so pycodemod imports its selftest without calling any of its defs. I first read that as a false positive in bare_edges, because pycodemod --binding reported ZERO bindings for _pycodemod_selftest, _pycodemod_census and dagcone in that file while finding sys at line 722. Both readers are right about different questions: --binding reports MODULE-SCOPE bindings, and those imports sit inside _dispatch, a single function spanning lines 1878 to 4051. A 2173-line function is why a module-scope reader sees nothing - the same function-scope blind spot that made --literal report a real import as a docstring mention earlier in this session, now hit from the other direction.}
}

@misc{dispatch-drain-blocked-by-one-parameter,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {not-mutual-pairs-resolved-differently},
  claim    = {A 2173-line dispatch is blocked from decomposition by one shared local, and that local is a parameter rather than a wall.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/def_split_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude, answering the operator's question about what blocks draining _dispatch. Measured by substrate/def_split over 70 top-level statements - args is read by 45 arms, _flags by 3, _invoked and _need by one each. Four shared locals in total. Passing args as a parameter frees 45 arms at once, and 21 arms are liftable already, several of them 30 to 51 lines, standalone functions spelled as if-blocks that read only args and module-level names. The finding count is a consequence of the shape rather than the problem - reading roughly ten thousand findings as lint debt schedules ten thousand fixes, while reading the shape schedules one signature change and a handful of extractions. A first cut of the reader reported 78 shared locals gating 64 arms because it walked each prior statement entirely and counted every loop variable and comprehension binding as state a later arm depended on. Only a binding at a statement's own top level escapes into function scope in a way a later arm could deliberately read, plus nested defs, which stay callable however deeply they nest. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The 2173-line pycodemod._dispatch is blocked from decomposition by ONE local, and that local is a parameter rather than a wall. Measured by substrate/def_split over its 70 top-level statements: args is read by 45 arms, _flags by 3, _invoked and _need by 1 each - four shared locals total. Making it _dispatch(args) frees 45 arms at once, and 21 arms are liftable RIGHT NOW, several of them 30 to 51 lines, standalone functions already spelled as if-blocks reading only args and module-level names. The finding count is a consequence of the shape, not the problem: reading ~10k findings as lint debt schedules ten thousand fixes, while reading the shape schedules one signature change plus a handful of extractions. A first cut of the reader reported 78 shared locals gating 64 arms, because it walked each prior statement entirely and counted every loop variable and comprehension binding (p, i, a, k) as state a later arm depended on. Only a binding at a statement's OWN top level escapes into function scope in a way a later arm could deliberately read - plus nested defs, which remain callable however deeply they nest.}
}

@misc{extraction-lands-repoint-blocked,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {dispatch-drain-blocked-by-one-parameter},
  claim    = {An extraction can land its new home and remain a copy, because the repoint needs the origin editable and the origin is what was too dirty to edit.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/contract_report_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude. substrate/contract_report holds the mode-contract audit lifted from a 51-line arm of pycodemod._dispatch, the largest arm reading no locals. The new home is clean with 15 cases, and extraction.check nonetheless returns INCOMPLETE because the origin still defines scan_contract and still holds the rendering. Completing the repoint means editing a file carrying roughly ten thousand findings, which the per-file gate refuses. The drain's wall therefore applies to the drain's own method - migrating code out is half the act, and the half that removes debt from the origin needs the origin editable. What the extraction buys regardless is that the rendering has a name, a home, cases, and three properties the origin held only in comments: an empty roster exits UNMEASURED rather than clean, UNKNOWN is not scored as failure (ok is False rather than not ok), and the three debts stay partitioned rather than summed. A positional boolean in the row constructor was made keyword-only in passing, since a transposed verdict reads plausible with flag and reason intact. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The first dispatch-arm extraction landed as a COPY, and the extraction checker says so rather than reporting progress. substrate/contract_report holds the mode-contract audit lifted from a 51-line arm of pycodemod._dispatch - the largest arm reading no locals. The new home is clean and carries 15 cases. But extraction.check returns INCOMPLETE: the origin still defines scan_contract and still holds the rendering, because completing the repoint means editing a file carrying roughly ten thousand findings, which the per-file gate refuses. So the drain's own wall applies to the drain's own method - migrating code out is only half of it, and the half that removes debt from the origin needs the origin editable. What the extraction buys anyway: the rendering now has a name, a home, cases, and three properties the origin held only in comments - an empty roster exits UNMEASURED rather than clean, UNKNOWN is not scored as failure (ok is False, never not ok), and the three debts stay partitioned rather than summed. A positional boolean in the row constructor was also caught and made keyword-only, since a transposed verdict reads entirely plausible with flag and reason intact.}
}

@misc{block-escaping-bindings-were-missed,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {dispatch-drain-blocked-by-one-parameter},
  claim    = {Four block-escaping binding forms were read as liftable, an under-report that would have licensed a breaking extraction.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/def_split_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude, from a linux-sources inbox audit dated 2026-09-04. Python has no block scope, so a for-target, a with-as name, a walrus binding and an except-as name each survive their statement and a later arm can read them. Measured before the fix - substrate/def_split reported every such arm as LIFTABLE. The earlier over-report I had corrected merely wasted a look. This under-report would have produced broken code, which is the direction that matters. Re-measuring pycodemod._dispatch with the forms covered gives 47 blockers gating 56 arms and 14 liftable, against the 4 and 21 reported an hour earlier. The headline survives because args at 45 arms is still dominant and still a parameter, but the tail was wrong. One of my own cases also failed correctly and was rewritten - I had asserted a rebound loop variable is never shared state, and it is, because the name leaks. The true statement is narrower - an arm that binds and reads a name within itself depends on nothing earlier. The fix I reached for was broader than the defect I was fixing. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A peer audit's checklist of AST binding forms found four real blind spots in substrate/def_split, all in the dangerous direction. Python has no block scope, so a for-target, a with-as name, a walrus binding and an except-as name all survive their statement and a later arm can read them. Measured before the fix: def_split reported every such arm as LIFTABLE, which licenses an extraction that breaks. The earlier over-report I had corrected only wasted a look; this under-report would have produced broken code. Re-measuring pycodemod._dispatch with the forms covered: 47 blockers gating 56 arms and 14 liftable, not the 4 and 21 I reported an hour earlier. The headline survives - args at 45 arms is still the dominant blocker and still a parameter - but the tail was wrong. A case of mine also failed correctly and had to be rewritten: I had asserted a rebound loop variable is never shared state, and it is, because the name leaks. The true statement is narrower - an arm that binds and reads a name WITHIN ITSELF depends on nothing earlier. The fix I reached for was broader than the defect I was fixing. Source: linux-sources inbox audit, 2026-09-04.}
}

@misc{bounds-printed-in-band-not-in-prose,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {block-escaping-bindings-were-missed},
  claim    = {A checkable qualifier belongs in the output rather than in prose, because a hedge in prose does not survive a summary while a bound in the output does.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/def_split_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude, adopting a linux-sources discipline. substrate/def_split prints BLIND_TO and OVER_REPORTS beside every result, so a reader quoting its counts carries what those counts could be missing. Both lists were MEASURED against fixtures rather than reasoned about. Probing eight binding forms found seven the reader missed - two genuine and fixable (a function-scoped import and a match capture, both now caught, and the dispatch this was written for is full of function-scoped imports), four irreducible for an AST binder and therefore declared (globals or setattr, exec, star-import, nonlocal), and one a known OVER-report (a deleted name still reads as shared state, which blocks an extraction rather than licensing a broken one - the safe direction, disclosed instead of fixed). A caveat list that drifts from the reader is worse than none, so cases pin each claim against a fixture and assert that the fixed forms are no longer listed as blind. PLR0911 fired on the binder and was right for the usual reason - two families of thing in one function - split into statement-bound and pattern-bound. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Adopted a peer's discipline - if the qualifier is checkable, put it in the OUTPUT rather than a sentence, because a hedge in prose does not survive a summary while a bound in the output does. substrate/def_split now prints BLIND_TO and OVER_REPORTS beside every result, so a reader quoting its counts also carries what those counts could be missing. Both lists were MEASURED against fixtures, not reasoned about: probing eight binding forms found seven the reader missed, of which two were genuine and fixable (a function-scoped import and a match capture, both now caught, and the dispatch this was written for is full of function-scoped imports), four are irreducible for an AST binder and are declared (globals/setattr, exec, star-import, nonlocal), and one is a known OVER-report (a del-ed name still reads as shared state, which blocks an extraction rather than licensing a broken one - the safe direction, disclosed instead of fixed). A caveat list that drifts from the reader is worse than none, so cases pin each claim against a fixture and assert the fixed forms are NOT still listed as blind. PLR0911 also fired on the binder and was right for the usual reason - two families of thing in one function, split into statement-bound and pattern-bound.}
}

@misc{retirement-was-never-blocked-by-the-hook,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {extraction-lands-repoint-blocked},
  claim    = {The gate-retirement path was never blocked by the hook, because the hook is shell and the per-file python gate has no jurisdiction over it.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,scripts/hook_shellcheck.py,--selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude. For four turns I treated the hook as an equal wall to the origins. The reasoning was that gate_kinds derives its roster from the hook's own run call sites, each entry carrying a script path like scripts/check_private_ratchet.py, so retiring an origin means editing the hook. Measured - .githooks/pre-commit is a SHELL script, routed through hook_shellcheck rather than hook_pycheck, and shellcheck at style severity returns clean. Running ruff against it reports 409 errors of which 338 are invalid-syntax, which is ruff parsing shell as Python. Mistaking that output for a verdict is exactly how an imaginary wall gets built. The real blocker is narrower than I stated: what cannot be edited is each ORIGIN, to delete its now-duplicated definition, not the roster that names it. A retirement that removes the whole origin file plus its hook line touches only shell and a deletion, and the python gate refuses neither. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The retirement path for four extraction origins was never blocked by the hook, and I had assumed it was for four turns. The reasoning was: the hook invokes gates by script path (gate_kinds derives its roster from the hook's own run call sites, and each entry carries a script field like scripts/check_private_ratchet.py), so deleting an origin means editing the hook, and the hook must therefore be as uneditable as the origins. Measured: the hook is .githooks/pre-commit, a SHELL script. It goes through hook_shellcheck, not hook_pycheck, and shellcheck --severity=style returns clean. The per-file python gate has no jurisdiction over it. Running ruff against it reports 409 errors of which 338 are invalid-syntax - that is ruff parsing shell as Python, noise rather than a verdict, and mistaking it for one is how a wall gets invented. So the blocker on all four copies is narrower than stated: what cannot be edited is each ORIGIN (to delete its now-duplicated definition), not the roster that names it. A retirement that deletes the whole origin file and its hook line touches only shell plus a deletion, neither of which the python gate refuses.}
}

@misc{the-origins-are-untracked,
  section  = {finding},
  rests-on = {retirement-was-never-blocked-by-the-hook},
  claim    = {The gate origins are untracked, so retiring one by deletion destroys an uncommitted file git cannot restore.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/private_equivalence_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=claude. git status reports scripts/check_private_ratchet.py as untracked while its replacement substrate/private_gate.py is staged as an addition. The retirement was otherwise complete - the hook line now reads python3 -m substrate.private_gate --quiet, shellcheck passes at style severity, and the replacement exits 0 where the origin refuses. What stopped the deletion is that every safety argument I had assembled - paydown-only baseline preserved, censuses set-identical and non-vacuous, hook editable because it is shell - covers CORRECTNESS and says nothing about RECOVERABILITY. A tracked file deleted in error is one checkout away. An untracked one is simply gone. Two further consumers bind the origin and belong in the same act rather than after it: substrate/ratchet_family.py keys its R9-residue roster entry on the gate NAME, and substrate/private_equivalence_selftest.py exists to compare against the origin and fails loudly when it is absent - correct for a comparison whose subject vanished, wrong as a permanent state, so it retires with the origin rather than surviving it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The gate origins this session set out to retire are UNTRACKED, so deleting one destroys an uncommitted file that git cannot restore. git status reports scripts/check_private_ratchet.py as ?? - never committed - while the replacement substrate/private_gate.py is staged as A. The retirement was otherwise complete: the hook line now reads python3 -m substrate.private_gate --quiet, shellcheck passes at style severity, and the replacement exits 0 where the origin refuses. What stopped the deletion is that the safety argument I had been making (the baseline is paydown-only, the censuses are set-identical, the hook is shell and editable) covers CORRECTNESS and says nothing about RECOVERABILITY. A tracked file deleted in error is one git checkout away; an untracked one is gone. Two further consumers also bind the origin and need handling in the same act rather than after it - substrate/ratchet_family.py:56 keys its R9-residue roster entry on the gate NAME, and substrate/private_equivalence_selftest.py exists to compare against the origin and fails loudly when it is absent, which is correct behaviour for a comparison whose subject vanished and wrong as a permanent state.}
}

@misc{gate-F21-silent-exit-zero-is-not-a-cli,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {gate_population.py exited 0 printing nothing on a bare run AND on --help, so the population omission it exists to surface was unreachable from a command line},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.gate_population,--selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. A silent exit 0 is indistinguishable from a tool that ran and found nothing. Its findings (known_misses, outside) were reachable only by importing the module. Same class as the four false zeroes its own docstring carries, arriving through the CLI rather than the predicate. Repaired with a MODES dispatch + USAGE and 8 cases: a bare run and an unknown flag must return REFUSED, MODES and USAGE must name the same set, and each renderer must produce a counted non-empty reading. Now: --outside reports 2 gates (private_gate, type_gate) a scripts-scoped census cannot see. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate_population.py had no CLI at all — bare AND --help both exited 0 printing nothing, so its own findings (known_misses, outside) were reachable only by importing it. A silent exit 0 is indistinguishable from a tool that ran and found nothing, which meant the omission this reader exists to surface was itself unreachable from a command line. Same class as the four false zeroes its docstring already carries, arriving through the CLI rather than through the predicate. Fixed with a MODES dispatch table plus USAGE, and cases asserting a bare run REFUSES (exit REFUSED=2) rather than succeeding silently, and that MODES and USAGE name the same set.}
}

@misc{gate-F22-build-lib-doubles-every-path-census,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {build/lib is setuptools staging of the two declared packages and was never gitignored, so every path-enumerating census counted each module twice against a stale copy},
  check    = {selftest:python3,scripts/run_selftests.py,--declarations},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. MEASURED: selftest-contract refused with 531 NEW keys, all build/lib paths, on a turn whose only change was git add over existing files. The guard exists in exactly one census: gate_population.GATE_HOMES declares its homes rather than discovering them, and its docstring names this hazard by name, so the reader that anticipated the defect is the one immune to it. Fixed by ignoring the tree once rather than excluding it per-census N times. Second consequence: a duplicate makes a retirement half-land, since deleting an origin while its build copy remains leaves it visible to every path-enumerating reader. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): build lib is setuptools staging copy of the two DECLARED packages (pyproject.toml:735) and was never gitignored, so every module under scripts and substrate existed TWICE in the working tree and every path-enumerating census counted it twice — against a STALE copy, since a build stages whatever the sources said at build time. MEASURED: selftest-contract refused with 531 NEW keys, every one a build lib path, on a turn whose only change was git add over files that already existed. gate_population.GATE_HOMES declares its homes rather than discovering them and its docstring names this exact hazard, so the one reader that anticipated the defect is the one immune to it. A per-census exclusion would need writing N times and drifting N ways. Also a duplicate makes a RETIREMENT half-land: deleting the private-ratchet origin while the build copy remains leaves it visible to every path-enumerating reader.}
}

@misc{gate-F23-dotted-prefix-selects-a-different-resolution,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {a dotted scripts. prefix selects ordinary import resolution, which never arms sibling_finder, so four ratchet gates read UNMEASURABLE under one spelling and reported real censuses under the other},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.ratchet_family_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. MEASURED: ratchet_family went 3 of 7 measurable to 7 of 7 by dropping the prefix. scripts is a package, so the dotted name resolves via gate_module._by_name where a gate's bare sibling import has nothing to resolve against. The bare stem fails that and falls through to _by_location, which arms sibling_finder over the gate directory. An unmeasurable census reads as nothing-to-see rather than as a defect, so four items were silently unassessed for the life of the roster. Two spellings, one capability, different answers - the --calls emit shape. Wrong-diagnosis record: the prefix was first read as a dead path and would have been removed as a harmless no-op, and one ratchet_census_show call with the bare name showed it was active instead. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ratchet_family.unmeasurable asked the census for scripts.<gate>, and that dotted prefix SELECTS A DIFFERENT IMPORT RESOLUTION. scripts is a package, so the dotted name resolves ordinarily via gate_module._by_name, where a bare import ratchet inside the gate has nothing to resolve against. The BARE stem fails that path and falls through to _by_location, which arms sibling_finder over the gate directory. Same gate, two spellings: UNMEASURABLE under one, 75 added of 490 held under the other. Four of seven roster items read UNMEASURABLE for the life of the roster and were measurable all along — and an unmeasurable census reads as nothing-to-see rather than as a defect, so four ratchet items were silently unassessed. Now 7 of 7 measurable. Also a wrong-diagnosis record: the prefix was first read as a dead path building scripts/scripts.<gate>.py, which would have been harmless to remove. One call to ratchet_census_show with the bare name settled it. The prefix was ACTIVE, not redundant.}
}

@misc{gate-F24-the-dotted-prefix-was-one-site-not-a-class,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the dotted-prefix resolution defect was one site, not a class - every other gate_module caller passes a bare stem or filename},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.ratchet_family_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. Recorded so the sweep is not re-run. gate_module is the single resolver, so pycodemod --calls gate_module bounds the population at 8 real caller sites. Clean by construction: churn_report GATE_SCRIPT map, tool_population HOOK, argument_consumed GATE, record_migrate literal. Two take the name as a parameter (commit_refusal.authority and the equivalence selftest), so their spelling belongs to callers outside this population. Method note worth keeping: --calls census was the wrong first question, since census is a common name across 43 unrelated defs, and --literal reports the enclosing function so it returned 0 sites for calls that plainly exist. The discriminating query is the RESOLVER name, not the capability name. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): SWEEP RESULT, recorded so it is not re-run: the gate-F23 dotted-prefix defect was ONE site, not a class. gate_module is the single resolver, and pycodemod --calls gate_module bounds the population at 8 real caller sites outside the def and its own selftest. Every one passes a bare stem or bare filename - churn_report's GATE_SCRIPT map, tool_population's HOOK, argument_consumed's GATE, record_migrate's pycheck_show.py literal - and two (commit_refusal.authority, and the equivalence selftest) take the name as a PARAMETER, so their spelling belongs to callers outside this population. ratchet_family was the only prefixed call. Method note: --calls census was the wrong first question because census is a common name across 43 unrelated defs, and --literal reports the ENCLOSING FUNCTION so it returned 0 sites for calls that plainly exist. The discriminating query is the resolver name, not the capability name.}
}

@misc{gate-F25-bare-and-dotted-reach-different-homes,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {a bare stem reaches scripts/ and arms sibling_finder while a dotted name reaches an installed package, so neither spelling is universally right and a migrated census needs the second map},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.ratchet_family_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. The mirror image of gate-F23. MEASURED both ways on the migrated R9-residue roster entry: bare private_gate gave 6 of 7 measurable with ModuleNotFoundError for a module present on disk, dotted substrate.private_census gave 7 of 7. Self-correction worth keeping: I wrote a comment asserting gate_module resolves a bare stem against either home BEFORE measuring, and it was false - the same write-the-conclusion-first shape the working rules name. churn_report now carries PACKAGE_CENSUS beside GATE_SCRIPT because there are genuinely two homes, rather than teaching one loader both layouts. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The MIRROR IMAGE of F23, and neither spelling is universally right. gate_module resolves a BARE stem against GATE_DIR (scripts/) by construction - that is what lets it arm sibling_finder for a directory of scripts that is not a package. A DOTTED name resolves by ordinary import, reaching an installed package. So F23 was a dotted name applied to a scripts-resident gate (which never armed the finder), and this is the reverse: repointing the migrated R9-residue roster entry at a bare private_gate reported ModuleNotFoundError for a module that is present on disk. MEASURED both ways: bare private_gate gave 6 of 7 measurable, dotted substrate.private_census gave 7 of 7. I wrote a comment claiming gate_module resolves a bare stem against either home BEFORE measuring it, and that comment was false. The two homes need two spellings, which is why churn_report now carries PACKAGE_CENSUS beside GATE_SCRIPT rather than one map teaching one loader both layouts.}
}

@misc{gate-F26-mode-detector-blind-to-the-preferred-idiom,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the mode-coverage detector knew only the in-sys.argv idiom, so every dispatch-table tool read as having NO modes rather than uncovered ones},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.mode_coverage_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. MEASURED on make_rules.py: 0 modes read, then 5 after adding the table pattern, all 5 genuinely uncovered and previously invisible. An absence where the truth was a gap is the direction that hides work - the census reports nothing to do exactly where the modes are. The repo PREFERS the table idiom (PLR0911 pushes a branch chain into one, and the tree's comments call it the correct shape), so the blind spot tracked the shape the tree converges on. Second defect in the same file: _CASE_COUNT was a hand-typed sum of ones, so a new arm ran while the printed denominator stayed at the old total - a green that was a fact about the literal. Now derived with len(_ARMS). Framing correction: opened as a silent-CLI class, but the first two candidates were libraries with no CLI by design - the class was the detector's pattern, not the modules. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mode_coverage discovered modes ONLY through the pattern --x in sys.argv, so a dispatch TABLE (MODES = {--x: fn} routed by MODES.get) was invisible and every table-dispatched tool reported no advertised mode read - UNMEASURED, not clean. That is an ABSENCE where the truth was a GAP, the direction that hides work: the census says nothing to do exactly where the uncovered modes are. MEASURED on make_rules.py: 0 modes read, then 5 after adding the table pattern, and all 5 were genuinely uncovered and had been invisible. Worse, the repo PREFERS the table - PLR0911 pushes a branch chain into one, and the tree's own comments call it the correct shape - so the detector was blind to precisely the idiom the tree converges on. Second defect found in the same file: _CASE_COUNT was a hand-maintained 1 + 1 + ... sum, so adding an arm left the printed denominator behind and the suite reported 15/15 while running 16 cases. Now len(_ARMS). Correction worth keeping: I opened this as silent-CLI-class and the first two candidates (tool_cli, public_census) are LIBRARIES with no CLI by design - running them bare is meaningless, not a defect. The real class was the detector's pattern, not the modules.}
}

@misc{gate-F27-a-text-scanner-reads-its-own-documentation,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {a mode detector that scans source text matched its own documentation, and its default roster was the hardcoded-instance defect its docstring condemns in its origin},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.mode_coverage_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. The first sighted sweep reported 35 of 35 modes uncovered across 16 tools - a total that is itself the tell. Three defects. One: the CLI default was two hardcoded scratch tools, so a bare run measured neither the package nor anything relevant, though the repair had already moved the roster to an argument. Now derived from the directory. Two: TABLE_RE matched any flag-shaped dict key, catching all four entries of tool_cli MODIFIERS - a modifier-to-owner lookup in a file documenting that modifiers are not modes - while missing that file's real tuple-declared modes. Narrowed to require a callable value. Three: six flags occurred only in prose, including --x inside the reader's own comment explaining the table pattern. Docstrings and comments now blanked via ast and tokenize spans. Self-correction: I left comments unstripped by reasoning the tokenizer drops them before dispatch, which is true of the program and irrelevant to a text scanner. Result 35 to 15 across 6 tools, every row a real dispatch with no case. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The mode-coverage sweep ran for the first time with sight and reported 35 of 35 modes uncovered across 16 tools - a total that is itself the tell, since a census finding EVERYTHING broken usually has a false-positive problem. Three distinct defects, each caught by a witness rather than by eye. FIRST: the CLI default roster was two hardcoded scratch tools, which is the exact guard-from-the-instance defect this module's own docstring condemns in its origin. The repair had moved the roster to an argument and left a literal as the default, so a bare run measured two scratch tools and reported on the package not at all. Now derived from the directory. SECOND: my TABLE_RE matched any flag-shaped dict KEY, so it caught all four entries of tool_cli's MODIFIERS map - a modifier-to-owner lookup in a file whose docstring says A MODIFIER CARRIES NO VERDICT OF ITS OWN - while MISSING that file's real modes, which are declared in tuples. Narrowed to require a callable value. THIRD and sharpest: a reader that scans SOURCE TEXT matches its own documentation. Six flags occurred only in prose, including --x inside this reader's own comment explaining the table pattern. Docstrings and comments are now blanked via ast and tokenize spans. I first left comments unstripped by reasoning that the tokenizer drops them before dispatch - true of the PROGRAM, irrelevant to a text scanner. 35 to 15 across 6 tools, every remaining row a real dispatch with no case.}
}

@misc{gate-F28-the-suite-name-was-the-last-blind-spot,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the coverage reader knew one suite-function name, so it searched an empty body for every package module and reported every mode uncovered},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.mode_coverage_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. SUITE was the single name _selftest while the package spells its runner selftest and holds assertions in _cases. MEASURED: all 15 remaining rows were this blind spot, including gate_population's three modes that had cases written the previous turn. Fixed by making SUITE a tuple and unioning every match rather than returning the first, since a suite here splits into a runner and a cases builder. FOURTH instance of one shape in a single file - default roster, dispatch idiom, prose exclusion, suite name - each a hardcoded spelling standing in for a population. Cumulative 35 uncovered to 7 across 4 tools: 24 were reader blind spots, 4 closed by real cases for the make_rules renderers. Method note: four gate refusals were spent trying to land the rename as a scratch script fighting quote lints, when keeping the NAME and changing only its VALUE and membership test was two ordinary edits that never left the file inconsistent. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): SUITE was the single name _selftest, but this package spells its runner selftest and holds the assertions in _cases, so suite_body returned EMPTY for every module in the package and covers searched an empty string. Every mode therefore read as uncovered. MEASURED: all 15 remaining rows of the sighted sweep were this one blind spot, and gate_population's three modes - which I had written cases for in the previous turn - were among them. The census was reporting the READER'S vocabulary as the TREE'S debt. Fixed by making SUITE a tuple of the three spellings and unioning every match rather than returning the first, since this package splits a suite into a runner and a cases builder and the mode is usually exercised in the second. This is the FOURTH instance of one shape in a single file: the default roster, the dispatch idiom, the prose exclusion, and the suite name were each a hardcoded spelling standing in for a population. Cumulative result 35 uncovered to 7 across 4 tools, of which 24 were reader blind spots and 4 were closed by writing real cases for make_rules renderers. Method note: I burned four gate refusals trying to land the rename as a scratch script fighting quote lints, when keeping the NAME and changing only its VALUE and membership test was two ordinary single-region edits that never left the file inconsistent.}
}

@misc{gate-F29-the-origin-was-blind-to-the-tree-root,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the retiring public-ratchet origin could not see the tree's own top-level module, which carries a wholesale re-export - so its census reported a clean tree exactly where it could not reach},
  check    = {refuses:python3,-m,substrate.public_gate,--quiet},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. public_census holds 491 where the origin holds 490, the extra being agda/Substrate.agda::Substrate.Cocycle. Verified by reading the file: it is the top-level module, outside the enumeration root the origin scans, and line 15 is an open-import-public of exactly the kind the gate refuses. The divergence is a FIX and the baseline RISES by a key rather than matching - preserving the origin's number would have meant preserving its blind spot to keep a gate green. Replacement substrate/public_gate.py written and verified, reporting 36 NEW with 97 paydowns held back, the same verdict the origin gives. BLOCKED at the last step: gen_build_makefiles.py:793 bakes the origin path into the generated check-public recipe and carries 292 findings, so the per-file gate refuses every edit. Its refusal names the trap: extracting one concern strands, since wiring it in is another edit to the same file. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): public_census holds 491 keys where the retiring origin holds 490, and the extra is agda/Substrate.agda::Substrate.Cocycle. VERIFIED BY READING THE FILE: agda/Substrate.agda is the tree's top-level module, sitting OUTSIDE the enumeration root the origin scans, and its line 15 is 'open import Substrate.Cocycle public' - a wholesale re-export of exactly the kind the gate exists to refuse, in the one file the gate could never look at. So the divergence is a FIX, not a defect: a census that cannot reach a file reports a clean tree there, and the gap was invisible for as long as the origin was the only reader. The replacement gate substrate/public_gate.py is written and verified, reporting 36 NEW and 97 paydowns held back - the same verdict the origin gives, since the ratchet cannot record paydowns while any addition is present. BLOCKED at the last step: scripts/gen_build_makefiles.py:793 bakes the origin's path into the generated check-public recipe, and that file carries 292 findings so the per-file gate refuses every edit to it. Its own refusal names the trap - extracting one concern strands, because wiring the extraction in is another edit to the same file. Deleting the origin would therefore break make check-public.}
}

@misc{gate-F30-the-model-and-the-emitter-are-different-lifetimes,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the build generator's import-graph MODEL and its make-syntax EMITTERS have different lifetimes, and that seam is both the monolith's drain line and the bazel migration's},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.agda_dag_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. gen_build_makefiles.py carries 292 findings and refuses every edit, blocking the public-origin retirement. module_outline shows eleven definitions with the mass concentrated: flat_text at 392 lines and root_text at 128 are emitters, over half the file, while parse_tree plus the Kahn walk are a ~90-line backend-independent model. Five consumers already treat the model as a library, including substrate/agda_import_dag.py which is in the package and imports it by BARE NAME - why tests/test_agda_census_drain.py fails with ModuleNotFoundError in a plain run. Extracted as substrate/agda_dag.py, clean and typed, with two defects fixed: kahn_depth called sys.exit(2) on a cycle so no caller could ask whether a graph was acyclic without being killed by the answer, and it discarded the critical-path depth that is the honest lower bound on parallel wall time while buildtime.py divides by a hardcoded J. Agreement verified: every module the generated Flat.mk declares is in the extracted model. Only 4 mypy error codes in the origin, so the 292 is overwhelmingly style rather than deep type debt. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gen_build_makefiles.py carries 292 findings and refuses every edit, blocking the public-origin retirement. Its own refusal says the unit of repair is the whole file and that extracting one concern strands. But module_outline shows the mass is concentrated and the seam is clean: eleven definitions, of which flat_text at 392 lines and root_text at 128 are make-syntax EMITTERS - more than half the file - while parse_tree plus the Kahn walk are a ~90-line MODEL of the import graph that is backend-independent. The model survives a bazel migration, the emitters do not, so that seam is both the drain's first cut and the migration's. FIVE consumers already treat the model as a library, reaching into the refusing file: fix_notinscope, split_census, build_census, and substrate/agda_import_dag.py which is ALREADY IN THE PACKAGE and imports it by BARE NAME, which is why tests/test_agda_census_drain.py fails with ModuleNotFoundError in a plain run. Extracted as substrate/agda_dag.py, written clean, typed, with two defects fixed: the origin's kahn_depth called sys.exit(2) on a cycle so no caller could ASK whether a graph was acyclic without being killed by the answer (now returns a Cycle value), and it computed the critical-path depth - the honest lower bound on parallel build wall time - and discarded it, while buildtime.py --predict divides by a hardcoded J. Agreement verified against the live tree: every module the generated Flat.mk declares is present in the extracted model. Only 4 mypy error codes in the origin, so the 292 is overwhelmingly style, not deep type debt.}
}

@misc{gate-F31-one-repoint-fixed-a-live-breakage-four-are-walled,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {one of five consumers of the extracted DAG model was repointable, and that repoint fixed a live ModuleNotFoundError rather than merely moving an import},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,pytest,tests/test_agda_census_drain.py,-x,-q,--no-header},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. substrate/agda_import_dag.py is in the package and editable, so it repointed to substrate.agda_dag: tests/test_agda_census_drain.py failed in the roster run with ModuleNotFoundError for gen_build_makefiles and now 14 pass. Its old docstring DEFENDED the bare-name import because stubs/gen_build_makefiles.pyi is keyed on the module name and a qualified import would bypass the stub - right about the STUB, wrong about the REACH, since a bare name resolves only when something arranged the path and a package module cannot assume that. The repoint retires the stub rather than relocating it, agda_dag being natively typed under py.typed, and removed four type-ignore casts by replacing a dict-of-object memo with a typed carrier. The other four consumers (build_census, split_census, fix_notinscope) carry findings and refuse edits, so each needs its own extract-and-retire. Residue worth naming: build_census.py:261 already prints that DAG depth is the honest parallel floor and that buildtime.py divides by a hardcoded J=6 - the exact defect agda_dag.topo fixes, documented in a file that cannot be edited to consume the fix. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Of the five consumers reaching into the refusing generator for its import-graph model, exactly ONE was repointable: substrate/agda_import_dag.py, which is in the package and therefore editable. The repoint fixed a LIVE BREAKAGE rather than merely moving an import - tests/test_agda_census_drain.py failed in the roster run with ModuleNotFoundError: No module named gen_build_makefiles, and now 14 pass. The old shape carried a docstring DEFENDING the bare-name import: stubs/gen_build_makefiles.pyi is keyed on the module NAME, so a qualified import would bypass the stub and mypy would follow untyped source. That reasoning was right about the STUB and wrong about the REACH - a bare name resolves only when something arranged the path, which a package module cannot assume. The repoint RETIRES the stub rather than relocating it, since substrate.agda_dag is natively typed under py.typed, and it removed four type-ignore-assignment casts because the hand-rolled dict-of-object memo is replaced by a typed carrier. The other four consumers - build_census, split_census, fix_notinscope - all carry findings and refuse edits, so each needs its own extract-and-retire. Sharpest residue: build_census.py:261 already PRINTS that DAG depth is the honest parallel floor and that buildtime.py divides by a hardcoded J=6, which is exactly the defect agda_dag.topo now fixes, documented in a file that cannot be edited to consume the fix.}
}

@misc{gate-F32-the-gate-roster-was-stated-three-times,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the build's gate roster was spelled three times in one uneditable file with nothing reconciling them, and both omission modes are silent},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.build_targets_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. root_text lists the same eleven gates in the .PHONY line, in the check: aggregate's prerequisites, and again as recipe blocks. A target absent from .PHONY still runs and one absent from check: never fires, so neither omission announces itself. Adding or repointing a gate means editing three places in a file the per-file gate refuses, which is exactly what blocked check-public for the public-origin retirement. Extracted as substrate/build_targets.py, a declared tuple of Target rows (name, argv, rationale) from which all three lists derive. The rationale is data rather than decoration: the comment above a recipe is the only place the build says why a gate exists. AGREEMENT VERIFIED BOTH WAYS against the live agda/Makefile - no declared target missing from it, no check- target in it that the roster lacks - with check-public's intended recipe divergence asserted separately from its name agreement so a deliberate change cannot hide an accidental one. The emitters remain and are the next cut: flat_text at 392 and root_text at 128 are make syntax that dies at a backend change, while this roster is policy a bazel emitter reads unchanged. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): root_text spells the same eleven gates THREE TIMES with nothing reconciling them: once in the .PHONY line, again as the check: aggregate's prerequisites, and a third time as the recipe blocks. Three hand-maintained lists whose only relationship is that a human typed them consistently - and both omissions are SILENT, since a target absent from .PHONY still runs and a target absent from check: simply never fires. Adding a gate means editing three places in a file the per-file gate refuses to let anyone edit, which is why check-public could not be repointed for the public-origin retirement. Extracted as substrate/build_targets.py: a declared tuple of Target rows carrying name, argv and rationale, from which all three lists derive. The rationale is DATA, not decoration - the comment above a recipe is the only place the build says why a gate exists, and a roster dropping it would emit a makefile less informative than the code it replaced. AGREEMENT VERIFIED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS against the live agda/Makefile: no declared target is missing from it, and it declares no check- target the roster lacks. check-public is repointed in DATA to substrate.public_gate, with its intended recipe divergence asserted separately from its name agreement, so a deliberate change cannot conceal an accidental one. The emitters remain: flat_text at 392 lines and root_text at 128 are make SYNTAX and die at a backend change, while this roster is build POLICY that survives one - a bazel emitter reads it unchanged.}
}

@misc{gate-F33-the-emitter-makes-the-roster-load-bearing,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {emitting the gate section from the declared roster turns it from a second opinion into the authority, and make itself validates the result},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.make_emit,--selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. substrate/make_emit.py renders the root makefile's gate section from build_targets. Until something emitted from it, the generator's three hand-maintained lists stayed in charge and the roster was a built-then-orphaned rung. Validated by the actual consumer - make -f the emitted file --dry-run check resolves all eleven gates in roster order, with check-public running the package module. The selftest compares STRUCTURALLY, parsing emitted text with make_rules and comparing targets, because a string diff would fail on a reflowed comment and pass on a recipe running the wrong command. The drift the roster prevents was already real: the origin's aggregate starts with check-defproof while its recipe blocks start with check-makefiles. Two self-inflicted defects caught in the same turn. First, the module was written with no CLI, so writing it to a file produced an empty file and exit 0 - the gate-F21 shape reproduced by omission one turn after fixing it elsewhere. Second, its dispatch table used a bare lambda, which is untyped and degrades callers to Any. The coverage census then caught the new --phony mode uncovered immediately, which is the instrument repaired earlier this session working on my own work. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): substrate/make_emit.py renders the root makefile's gate section FROM the declared roster, which is what turns build_targets from a second opinion into the authority - until something emits from it, the generator's three hand-maintained lists remain in charge and the roster is a built-then-orphaned rung. VALIDATED BY THE ACTUAL CONSUMER, not just by my reader: make -f <emitted> --dry-run check resolves the full chain, all eleven gates in roster order, with check-public running python3 -m substrate.public_gate --quiet and the aggregate closing. The selftest compares STRUCTURALLY rather than textually - the emitted text is parsed by make_rules and compared as TARGETS - because a string diff against the live file would fail on a reflowed comment and pass on a recipe running the wrong command, exactly backwards. Confirmed the drift the roster prevents was already real: the origin's check: aggregate lists check-defproof first while its recipe blocks start with check-makefiles, two literals that had diverged. Two self-inflicted defects caught and fixed in the same turn: the module was first written with NO CLI, so writing it to a file produced an EMPTY file and exit 0 - the gate-F21 silent-success shape reproduced by omission one turn after fixing it elsewhere - and its dispatch table first used a bare lambda, which is untyped and degrades every caller's return to Any. The coverage census then caught the new --phony mode uncovered immediately, which is the instrument repaired earlier this session working on my own work.}
}

@misc{gate-F34-a-second-emitter-proves-the-seam,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {a second emitter reading the same model turns backend-independence from an assertion into a measurement, and it shares no code with the first},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.bazel_emit,--selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. substrate/bazel_emit.py emits bazel targets for the whole Agda tree from agda_dag, importing neither make_emit nor anything make-shaped. The two emitters share no code deliberately - make and bazel disagree at the grammar level about tabs versus parens, mtime versus content hashing, phony targets versus no such concept - while the one thing they share is the model, already shared. A common base class would manufacture the coupling the migration exists to remove. Load-bearing case: the target-name mapping is injective over the live tree, checked rather than assumed, since bazel labels cannot contain dots while underscore is already legal in an Agda module name and a collision would silently merge two compile targets. Three unresolved design questions are printed INTO the emitted file rather than omitted: whether membudget or bazel governs concurrency, the shared mutable .agdai interface directory a sandbox does not provide, and the stamp chain whose stamps are filesystem-held claims about postgres. Nothing is wired - no bazel and no workspace exist here - so whether the emitted text builds the tree is UNMEASURED and the module says so rather than implying a migration. summit capability bazel returns no entry for that spelling, a fact about the index and an intake ask to file. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): substrate/bazel_emit.py emits bazel BUILD targets for the whole Agda tree from the SAME model make_emit reads, and it imports neither make_emit nor anything make-shaped. That is what converts the backend-independence claim from an assertion into a measurement: agda_dag and build_targets were extracted on the claim that a different build system reads them unchanged, and a claim like that is cheap until a second emitter exists. The two emitters deliberately share NO code - make and bazel disagree at the grammar level about tabs versus parens, mtime versus content hashing, and phony targets versus no such concept, while the one thing they share is the model, which is already shared. A common base class would manufacture the coupling the migration exists to remove. LOAD-BEARING CASE: the target-name mapping is injective over the live tree, checked rather than assumed, because bazel labels cannot contain dots while underscore is ALREADY LEGAL in an Agda module name - a collision would silently merge two compile targets and drop one module's dependencies. THREE THINGS IT DOES NOT RESOLVE, printed into the emitted file rather than omitted: whether membudget's semaphore or bazel's resource model governs concurrency (together they would double-bound), the shared mutable .agdai interface directory a sandbox does not provide, and the ingest/promote/drain stamp chain whose stamps are claims about postgres held on the filesystem, which is what a hermetic model refuses. NOTHING IS WIRED: no bazel and no workspace exist in this checkout, so whether the emitted text builds the tree is UNMEASURED and the module says so rather than implying a migration. Also: summit capability bazel returns no entry for that spelling, which is a fact about the index and an intake ask to file.}
}

@misc{gate-F35-a-claim-is-not-merged-by-existing,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {a warrant added to a paperkit project is not merged until it is CITED in its rendered section, and an undeclared check verb reports UNRESOLVABLE rather than failing},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.concept_families_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. Merging the bazel corpus claims into catalog/worklist taught two things the plan had wrong. First: the gate reported a claim tagged for a section but not cited, so the projection is claim-driven while the prose is authored and coverage requires both - add-warrants-then-reproject is wrong, it is add-warrants, cite them, reproject. Second: the blocker filed was not the binding one. bibstruct --add's item: guard fires only for item: checks while the corpus claims carry traces:, and the real gap was paper.toml declaring only checks.item, so the gate said UNRESOLVABLE - not a refutation - which is the tri-state separating an unrunnable check from a failing one. Declaring the verb closed it, all five claims passed, and the gate's failure list stayed byte-identical to baseline. Third, smaller: the findings registrar re-executes a witness command string and my shell quoting did not survive that round trip, refusing two commands that pass when run directly. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Adding a warrant to a paperkit project does not merge it. The gate reported 'claim tagged section=measured but not cited' - a warrant must be CITED in its rendered section, so the projection is claim-driven while the prose is authored, and coverage requires BOTH. A bib entry nothing cites is an orphan the gate names rather than a claim that landed. I had recorded the merge as add-warrants-then-reproject; it is add-warrants, cite them, reproject. SECOND FINDING, same turn: the blocker I filed was not the binding one. I recorded step 2 as blocked on bibstruct --add's item: guard, true but firing only for item: checks while the corpus claims carry traces:. The real gap was catalog/worklist/paper.toml declaring only [checks.item], so the gate reported UNRESOLVABLE - 'this engine has no traces: verb, NOT a refutation' - the tri-state distinguishing an unrunnable check from a failing one. Declaring the verb closed it and all five merged claims passed, with the gate's failure list byte-identical to baseline. THIRD: the registrar refused two witness commands because it re-executes the string and my quoting did not survive the round trip, which is its own small instance of a claim about a command not surviving transport.}
}

@misc{gate-F36-a-premise-is-not-a-retraction,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {keying the retraction binding on the presence of rests-on bound three ordinary claims and reported each as failing to name a successor - a claim with a premise is not a claim that retracts something},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.concept_families_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. The retraction binding was two constants for one retraction, carrying a comment that already recorded the hazard of two hand-kept rosters already disagreeing. Generalising it to a derivation was right and the first predicate was not. Keying on rests-on bound A5, B3 and DEPS, ordinary claims that declare a premise, and the retraction witness reported each as a deletion wearing a correction's clothes. Three sound claims became three false OPEN items, which is the manufacturing direction. The membership test is the retract SECTION, already declared in the rubric. Both directions re-verified: A5 back to its ban-family verdict, R14-v2 still bound and reporting its genuine OPEN state, suite 17 of 17 throughout. The binding now derives from the bib the way _ratchet derives from ratchet_family ITEMS. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Generalising the retraction binding from a hardcoded pair to a derivation, the first cut keyed on the presence of rests-on and OVER-BOUND in the direction that manufactures findings. A5, B3 and DEPS are ordinary claims that declare a premise, and the retraction witness then reported each as 'does not name its successor - a deletion wearing a correction's clothes'. Three sound claims turned into three false OPEN items. A CLAIM WITH A PREMISE IS NOT A CLAIM THAT RETRACTS SOMETHING: the membership test is the retract SECTION, which the rubric already declares, not the justification edge which many non-retractions legitimately carry. Both directions re-verified after the fix - A5 back to its ban-family verdict, R14-v2 still bound and reporting its real OPEN state. The binding now derives its roster from the bib the way _ratchet() derives from ratchet_family.ITEMS, so the two-constant pair whose own comment recorded 'two hand-kept rosters, already disagreeing' is gone rather than extended sevenfold.}
}

@misc{gate-F37-two-owners-of-one-population,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the suite walker and git's ignore were two owners of one population, so gitignoring the build tree fixed git's view and left the roster counting every package module twice},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.suite_discovery_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. MEASURED: run_selftests --declarations went 674 to 393 once the walker asked git check-ignore. The repair landed in one reader and never reached the other, the same shape paperkit found between components.bzl and its bib. The obvious fix was wrong both ways - adding build to the name list leaves the two-owner condition for the next generated directory, and deriving everything from gitignore would admit agda and docs, which are tracked and excluded only because they hold no Python. The honest relation is an implication rather than an identity: everything git ignores is skipped, not everything skipped is ignored. The walker now unions a name list for tracked-but-not-a-suite-root with git check-ignore for generated trees, and a git that cannot answer ignores nothing so the tempdir fixture stays runnable. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Substrate had two enumerations of its own corpus with no common owner: git's ignore and the suite walker's _SKIP_TOP name list. Gitignoring build/ removed the duplicated package copy from GIT's view, and suite_discovery walks the FILESYSTEM without consulting gitignore, so the selftest roster kept counting every package module twice against a stale copy. MEASURED: run_selftests --declarations went from 674 discovered to 393 once the walker asked git. The repair landed in one reader and never reached the other, which is the same shape paperkit found between components.bzl and its bib. AND THE OBVIOUS FIX WAS WRONG BOTH WAYS: adding build to _SKIP_TOP leaves the two-owner condition for the next generated directory, while deriving everything from gitignore would admit agda and docs, which are TRACKED and excluded only because they hold no Python. The honest relation is an implication, not an identity - everything git ignores is skipped, not everything skipped is ignored - so the walker now unions a name list for tracked-but-not-a-suite-root with git check-ignore for generated trees. A git that cannot answer ignores nothing, which keeps the tempdir fixture runnable.}
}

@misc{gate-F55-divergence-reads-a-redirect-as-a-claim,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {toolmodes --divergence cannot tell a redirect notice from a mode claim, so it reports a correctly-documented pointer as an unbacked mode},
  check    = {mode:scratch/findings.py:--register},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED -- --divergence reports scratch/findings.py UNBACKED for --list and --register. That file documents both flags in order to REDIRECT readers to .claude/agents/findings.py, which owns and dispatches them. The census is right that they are not dispatched there and wrong about what that means. Sibling of gate-F27 with a different mechanism. F27 is a scanner matching its OWN prose, this is a scanner reading ANOTHER file's correct redirect. AND THE FILING DEMONSTRATED A SECOND GAP. The first witness used mode:scratch/findings.py:--register, which asserts the flag is UNDOCUMENTED and reported DOCUMENTED, the OPPOSITE polarity of the claim it holds. meta-F13 built a polarity probe for the refuses and selftest kinds, but a mode: witness is not probed, so this one was born inverted and only a manual run caught it. The honest kind is unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes documented-as-a-redirect from documented-as-a-mode. The other 9 UNBACKED rows are unverified and calling them a population would repeat the error.}
}

@misc{gate-F56-every-climode-consumer-is-behind-the-per-file-gate,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {every climode consumer carries per-file-gate findings, so promoting the module made a dotted import possible without making any existing site repointable},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED with pycodemod --types over the whole importer population -- no-untyped-def, misc and no-untyped-call appear in all seven scratch and jea consumers censused together, plus mdstruct and schema.py separately. The per-file gate is all-or-nothing, so changing ONE import line requires the WHOLE consumer to pass. THE PROMOTION IS THEREFORE NOT YET A REPLACEMENT. substrate/climode.py is reachable, differentially verified against the origin, and used by exactly one consumer written clean this session. The other thirteen keep their sys.path insert until their own files are drained, and the origin cannot retire until they do. UNWITNESSED IS THE HONEST KIND because no predicate distinguishes a consumer that CANNOT be repointed from one nobody HAS repointed, and the discriminating quantity is a per-consumer finding count no registered kind reads. Third instance of a correct one-line repair sitting behind a whole-file gate, after bibstruct --add and the findings.py polarity probe, which is evidence the class is structural rather than three coincidences. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓝ164 repoint-the-fourteen is BLOCKED, and uniformly. substrate/climode.py was promoted so the 14 bare sys.path-dependent importers could be repointed to a dotted import. MEASURED with pycodemod --types over all of them: every consumer carries findings -- no-untyped-def, misc and no-untyped-call appear in all seven scratch/jea files censused together (47 keys, 16 codes), mdstruct 9 codes, schema.py 7 codes. The per-file gate is all-or-nothing, so changing ONE import line requires the WHOLE consumer to pass. THE PROMOTION IS THEREFORE NOT YET A REPLACEMENT: substrate/climode.py is reachable and correct and exactly one consumer (substrate/bib_modes.py, written clean this session) uses the dotted path. The other 13 keep the insert until their own files are drained. UNWITNESSED IS THE HONEST KIND: no predicate distinguishes a consumer that cannot be repointed from one nobody has repointed yet, and the discriminating quantity is a per-consumer finding count that no registered kind reads. This is the same shape as the bibstruct and findings.py drains -- a correct one-line repair behind a whole-file gate -- arriving a third time, which is evidence the class is structural.}
}

@misc{gate-F57-run-selftests-accepts-paths-and-ignores-them,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {run_selftests accepts positional paths and ignores them, so a targeted-looking invocation returns a whole-tree verdict},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED with a positive control -- the path substrate/no_such_suite_at_all.py does not exist, is accepted silently, and the full roster runs. READ THE BINDING: main() calls discover() with NO arguments and never consults sys.argv for paths, while ratchet.check_argv validates FLAGS only, so a positional passes the guard and is discarded. THE DANGEROUS HALF IS THAT THE RUN LOOKS TARGETED and a reader taking its exit code as a fact about the named suites chases nothing. That happened to me -- I read a targeted-looking run's output, found only OTLP export errors, reported it as a pass for thirteen suites, and the notification then said it exited 1 on the tree's standing state. UNWITNESSED IS THE HONEST KIND AND THE POLARITY PROBE ENFORCED IT. Filing as refuses: was refused at registration because the command exits 0 today, which means the refusal is not installed and the finding is OPEN. It converts to refuses: the moment a positional arm lands in check_argv or discover() is filtered by the paths given. Sibling of gate-F56 in that the repair is known and small, and distinct in that nothing blocks it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): run_selftests accepts positional paths, ignores them, and runs the full roster. MEASURED with a positive control -- the path substrate/no_such_suite_at_all.py does not exist, is accepted silently, and 405 suites run. READ THE BINDING: main() calls discover() with NO arguments and never consults sys.argv for paths, while ratchet.check_argv validates FLAGS only, so a positional passes the guard and is discarded. THE DANGEROUS HALF IS THAT THE RUN LOOKS TARGETED. An invocation naming four suites returns a whole-tree verdict, and a reader taking exit 1 as a fact about those four chases nothing. That happened to me: I read a targeted-looking run's output file, found only OTLP export errors, and reported it as a pass for thirteen suites before the notification said it exited 1. UNWITNESSED IS THE HONEST KIND AND THE PROBE ENFORCED IT. Filing this as refuses: was refused at registration because the command exits 0 today -- meaning the refusal is not installed, so the finding is OPEN and refuses: is right but PREMATURE. It converts to refuses: the moment a positional arm lands in check_argv or the runner filters discover() by the paths given.}
}

@misc{gate-F58-the-drain-changed-the-write-spelling,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the drain changed the write SPELLING as well as the file, so repointing the census alone would report UNMEASURED against a module full of writes},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED -- substrate/write_path.py defaults to scratch/bibstruct.py, which the drain reduced to a shim, so the roster names a file with no writes. Its render correctly reports no-write-path-found UNMEASURED-not-clean rather than banking a zero. But repointing at substrate/bib_write.py ALSO reports nothing, because the detector keys on open with a mode argument and the drained module writes with Path.write_text. BOTH HALVES ARE LIVE AT ONCE -- a location that moved AND a call shape that changed -- so a roster fix alone leaves the census measuring nothing while looking repointed. Teach the opener roster the pathlib write methods FIRST, then repoint. UNWITNESSED is the honest kind because no predicate distinguishes a tool that genuinely has no writes from a detector that cannot see the writes it has, which is the same ambiguity render names in its own empty case. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): T27's write-path census went blind for TWO reasons at once and only one is a stale roster. MEASURED: substrate/write_path.py defaults to ('scratch/bibstruct.py',) which the drain reduced to a shim, so the roster names a file with no writes -- and render() correctly reports 'no write path found -- UNMEASURED, not clean' rather than banking a zero. But repointing it at substrate/bib_write.py ALSO reports nothing, because the detector keys on open(..., mode) and the drained module writes with Path.write_text. THE DRAIN CHANGED THE SPELLING THE DETECTOR RECOGNISES, not merely the file it lives in. A roster fix alone would leave the census reporting UNMEASURED against a module full of writes, which reads as a repoint that worked. This is census-keyed-on-one-spelling with both halves live at once: keyed on a LOCATION that moved and on a CALL SHAPE that changed. The repair is to teach OPENER about the pathlib write methods and then repoint, in that order -- roster first would look green-adjacent while measuring nothing. UNWITNESSED because no predicate distinguishes 'this tool genuinely has no writes' from 'this detector cannot see the writes it has'; that is the same ambiguity render() names in its own empty case.}
}

@misc{gate-F59-coherence-reads-one-ledger-of-six,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {mdstruct --coherence compares the plan against ONE worklist while the tree carries six ledgers, so its unwitnessed column reports keys that are fully witnessed elsewhere},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. THE CAPABILITY DART's UNREGISTERED GATE WOULD NEED ALREADY EXISTS -- coherence is bidirectional, reporting orphan items and unwitnessed plan labels in one pass. What it lacks is the population. MEASURED: it reports gate-F55 through gate-F58 as in-plan-with-no-item, and every one is registered and answering in the oracle ledger, gate-F55 returning DOCUMENTED on probe. It also lists bare symbol prefixes as items, so the label grammar admits a prefix as a key. THE SHAPE IS THE RECURRING ONE: a reader's population is narrower than the question asked of it, and the shortfall reads as a fact about the substrate rather than about the method. CASSIAN'S GATE IS INSTRUCTIVE BY CONTRAST -- it greps ALL docs against ALL rows and hard-fails, which is why a claim living only in prose is illegal there by construction rather than a modelling problem to route around. UNWITNESSED IS THE HONEST KIND because no predicate distinguishes a genuine orphan from one witnessed in a ledger this reader does not consult, and the discriminating quantity is the ledger roster it does not take. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mdstruct --coherence is the bidirectional prose-vs-warrant reader DART's UNREGISTERED gate would need, and it already exists -- but it compares against ONE worklist while the tree carries six ledgers, so its UNWITNESSED column is dominated by false positives. MEASURED: it reports gate-F55/F56/F57/F58 as in-plan-with-no-item, and every one is registered and answering in .claude/agents/findings.py -- gate-F55 returns DOCUMENTED on probe. It also reports bare symbol prefixes as items, so the label grammar admits a prefix as a key. THE SHAPE IS THE ONE THIS REPO KEEPS RECORDING: a reader's population is narrower than the question asked of it, and the gap is reported as a fact about the SUBSTRATE rather than about the METHOD. DART's answer is instructive by contrast -- cassian's gate greps ALL docs against ALL rows and hard-fails, which is why a claim living only in prose is illegal there by construction rather than a modelling problem to route around. UNWITNESSED IS THE HONEST KIND: no predicate distinguishes an orphan that is genuinely unwitnessed from one witnessed in a ledger this reader does not consult, and the discriminating quantity is the ledger roster the reader does not take.}
}

@misc{gate-F60-the-parity-reader-red-is-first-about-its-own-table,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the chirality reader reports INCONSISTENT on the live bib, and the first cause to rule out is its own kind-chirality table rather than the corpus},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED: 58 conflicts over 125 edges, 4 components over 67 nodes. The table assigns one chirality bit per field name -- rests-on entails reads consumes at 0, from and enables at 1 -- on the model that each is a dart of ONE entailment relation. But --field enables shows SUITE enabling ten targets and T11 and T17 each enabling eight including S1, which is a work-ORDERING fan-out rather than a retraction-style entailment. And --field join shows this bib storing PROSE PUNCTUATION in a field beside the edge fields, so its fields carry document-rendering roles as well as entailment ones. A table assigning one bit per field name cannot express a field used two ways. THE THREE-WAY VERDICT IS DOING ITS JOB AND THIS IS THE THIRD ARM. The operator ruled before the reader existed that a red must name genuinely-inconsistent, sigma-shift, and reader-defect without choosing between them. Reading the first arm off a red would be the census-green failure INVERTED -- a fact about the instrument reported as a fact about the subject. UNWITNESSED because the arithmetic alone cannot separate the three, which is why the verdict names all three. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The chirality reader is wired to the live bib and reports INCONSISTENT -- 58 conflicts over 125 edges, 4 components over 67 nodes -- and the FIRST cause to rule out is the reader's own kind-chirality table, not the corpus. MEASURED: the table assigns rests-on and entails and reads and consumes chirality 0, and from and enables chirality 1, on the model that each is a dart of ONE entailment relation. But bibstruct --field enables shows SUITE enables T11, T17, T24 and seven more, while T11 and T17 each enable eight targets including S1 -- a work-ORDERING fan-out, not a retraction-style entailment. And bibstruct --field join shows the projection stores PROSE PUNCTUATION in a field alongside the edge fields, so this bib's fields carry document-rendering roles as well as entailment ones. A kind table that assigns one chirality bit per field name cannot express a field used two ways. THE THREE-WAY VERDICT IS DOING ITS JOB AND THIS IS THE THIRD ARM: the operator ruled before the reader existed that a red must name genuinely-inconsistent, sigma-shift, and reader-defect without choosing, and reading the first arm off a red would be the census-green failure inverted -- a fact about the instrument reported as a fact about the subject. UNWITNESSED because no predicate distinguishes the three from the arithmetic alone, which is precisely why the verdict names all three.}
}

@misc{gate-F61-the-parity-conflict-count-was-a-traversal-artifact,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F60-the-parity-reader-red-is-first-about-its-own-table},
  claim    = {A conflict recorded on every re-encounter of an already-coloured node reports one violated constraint twice, once per endpoint, because the parity walk stores each edge in both directions},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.chirality_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. MEASURED, the live worklist read 58 conflicts over 125 edges. Keying on the unordered pair-and-kind gives 29, exactly halved, so every row had been a mirror. The three-way verdict was the casualty, because a red whose members are duplicated cannot be partitioned into genuinely-inconsistent, sigma-shifted, or reader-defect when each arm is inflated by an unknown factor. gate-F60 reasoned about the kind table from a number this reader manufactured. The suite read 27 of 27 throughout, because every case asserted THAT conflicts were found and never how many distinct ones. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The chirality reader recorded a conflict on every re-encounter of an already-coloured node, while _adjacency stores each edge in BOTH directions (correct: the parity constraint is symmetric). So one violated constraint reported twice, once per endpoint, and the live worklist read 58 conflicts over 125 edges. Keying on the unordered (pair, kind) drops it to 29 -- exactly halved, confirming every row had been a mirror. The cost was the three-way verdict itself: a red whose MEMBERS are duplicated cannot be partitioned into genuinely-inconsistent, sigma-shifted, or reader-defect, because every arm is inflated by an unknown factor. gate-F60 reasoned about the kind table from a number this reader had manufactured. A false RED banks as confidently as a false green, and the suite read 27/27 throughout because every case asserted THAT conflicts were found, never how many distinct ones.}
}

@misc{gate-F62-the-reader-ignored-its-own-sigma-declaration,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F61-the-parity-conflict-count-was-a-traversal-artifact},
  claim    = {A module can declare a sigma-mate distinction in one constant and not honour it in the walk that reads it, inverting the chirality bit for every such edge},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.chirality_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. TAU_MATE's own comment declared enables to be the SIGMA-mate of rests-on, other endpoints, while _adjacency added every kind at its declared endpoints. The witness is an AGREEMENT rather than a contradiction, because the worklist declares both A5-as rests-on A5-as-reach and A5-as-reach enables A5-as, which are one constraint stated from both ends and were reported as a conflict. AND THE COUNT ROSE FROM 29 TO 33 UNDER A CORRECT REPAIR, which is the sharper half. Conflict's docstring already warned the reported edge is whichever closed an odd cycle during the walk, so the count is traversal-dependent and cannot be read as progress or as regression. The discriminator for the three-way verdict has to be something other than the population size. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): TAU_MATE's comment in substrate/chirality.py already declared that from is the TAU-mate of rests-on (endpoints unchanged) while enables is its SIGMA-mate (other endpoints). _adjacency honoured neither, adding every kind at its declared endpoints, so enables carried its bit at the UNSWAPPED pair -- the bit inverted for every sigma-mate edge in the corpus. The witness is an AGREEMENT, not a contradiction: the worklist declares both A5-as rests-on A5-as-reach and A5-as-reach enables A5-as, one constraint stated from both ends, and the reader called it a conflict. AND THE CONFLICT COUNT WENT UP, 29 to 33, which is the second finding: the count is traversal-dependent, as Conflict's own docstring warns, so it is not a quality measure and cannot be read as progress OR regression. A file can declare a distinction and not honour it, in the module written to stop chirality being decided by ear.}
}

@misc{gate-F63-the-odd-cycle-is-the-discriminator-the-count-cannot-be,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F62-the-reader-ignored-its-own-sigma-declaration},
  claim    = {A parity conflict that reports only its closing edge discards the implicated set, and the implicated set is the invariant a traversal-dependent count cannot supply},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.chirality_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. Conflict's docstring already said the reported edge depends on traversal order and every edge on the cycle is equally implicated, so the surfaced member is not the fact. Keeping the BFS parent tree and recovering the cycle as tree-paths-to-common-ancestor plus the closing edge makes the three-way partition askable, where two prior repairs had moved the count in opposite directions and settled nothing. AND THE FIRST RUN FOUND FOUR CLAIMS NO COUNT COULD SHOW. A two-node cycle means one PAIR carrying two kinds, and A5 with A5-renaming, DEPS with T26, R12b with CENSUS, and OBS with OBS-DEADLINE each declare both from and rests-on, or its sigma-mate enables, between the same two claims. That is R14-v2's vacuous self-grounding, the retraction target and the justification named as one object, present four more times and invisible until the cycle was reported. The control is that a cycle is NOT a component, so a tail hanging off the refuting triangle must not appear. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The parity reader recorded only the edge that CLOSED each odd cycle and discarded the cycle itself, so the three-way verdict had nothing to partition on. Conflict's own docstring had said the reported edge depends on traversal order and every edge on the cycle is equally implicated -- the implicated SET is the invariant, the surfaced member is not. Keeping the BFS parent tree and recovering the cycle as tree-paths-to-common-ancestor plus the closing edge makes the partition askable. AND IT IMMEDIATELY FOUND FOUR CLAIMS NO COUNT COULD SHOW: two-node cycles mean one PAIR carrying two kinds, and A5/A5-renaming, DEPS/T26, R12b/CENSUS and OBS/OBS-DEADLINE each declare both from and rests-on (or its sigma-mate enables) between the same two claims. That is R14-v2's vacuous self-grounding -- the retraction target and the justification named as the same object -- present four more times and invisible until the cycle was reported. The control is that the cycle is NOT the component: a tail hanging off the triangle must not appear, or the partition is the component again.}
}

@misc{gate-F64-from-carries-two-relations-and-only-four-entries-retract,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F63-the-odd-cycle-is-the-discriminator-the-count-cannot-be},
  claim    = {The from field carries a document reading chain and a retraction dart under one name, so a chirality table keyed on field name cannot express either without misreading the other},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.chirality_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. MEASURED rather than inferred. bibstruct --field section reports only four entries in section=retract while --field from reports a from edge across most of the corpus, so the great majority of from uses are not retraction darts. They are a head-to-tail chain within a section, which the plan had already recorded for the intake bib and which holds inside the worklist too. The four two-node cycles are therefore where a chain edge and a genuine grounding edge land on the same pair, not four claims each needing an operator ruling. That makes from, and not enables, the overloaded field, which is the per-use chirality question arriving from the other side. AND THE TWO-NODE READING WAS UNVERIFIED WHEN IT WAS ACTED ON, since four instances agreeing with a helper written in the same turn is corroboration by artifact, so a case now pins that a length-2 cycle can only arise from a doubled pair, with a plain path as the negative control. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓝ173 was framed as a tau-mate double-declaration needing per-pair arbitration. MEASURED against the bib rather than inferred: --field section reports only four entries in section=retract, while --field from reports a from edge on most of the corpus. So the great majority of from uses are not retraction darts at all -- they are a DOCUMENT READING CHAIN, head-to-tail within a section, which the plan had already recorded for the intake bib and which is equally true inside the worklist. The four two-node cycles are where the chain edge and a genuine grounding edge land on the SAME pair, not four claims each needing a ruling. That makes from, not enables, the overloaded field, which is Ⓝ172's per-use chirality arriving from the other side: a table keyed on field NAME cannot express a field carrying two relations. AND THE TWO-NODE READING ITSELF WAS UNVERIFIED WHEN I ACTED ON IT -- four instances agreeing with a helper written the same turn is corroboration by artifact, so a case now pins that a length-2 cycle can only come from a doubled pair, with a plain path as the control.}
}

@misc{gate-F65-a-declared-tau-pair-is-a-completed-closure-not-a-clash,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F64-from-carries-two-relations-and-only-four-entries-retract},
  claim    = {A pair declaring both chiralities of one relation is a completed V4 closure and must contribute one constraint, not two contradictory ones},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.chirality_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. Operator ruling, that all chain edges are parity-bearing and the DAG is a lattice of darts, refuted the repair this reader was about to make. Filtering document-chain from edges out of the parity graph would have deleted real darts and called the result consistent, a false green bought by removing evidence. The actual defect was internal disagreement, since derive already treated a declared tau-mate as a completed closure and returned None because nothing remains to construct, while the walk read the same two edges as two independent constraints with opposite bits. Folding a declared tau pair to one dart cleared two of the four doubled pairs. AND THE FIRST FOLD COLLAPSED EVERYTHING, caught by the case's own control, because re-spelling every predictor edge left nothing carrying bit 1, so no graph could refute and the suite went from one failure to eight. A tau pair is one dart only when both spellings are declared on the SAME pair, which makes the fold a property of the edge set rather than of an edge. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Operator ruling: all chain edges are parity-bearing, the DAG is a lattice of darts. That refuted the repair this reader was about to make -- filtering document-chain from edges out of the parity graph, which would have deleted real darts and called the result consistent, a false green bought by removing evidence. The actual defect: derive already treated a declared tau-mate as a COMPLETED CLOSURE, returning None because there is nothing left to construct, while _adjacency read the same two edges as two independent constraints with opposite bits. The solver and the closure disagreed about one relation and the closure was right. Folding a declared tau pair to one dart cleared two of the four doubled pairs. AND THE FIRST FOLD COLLAPSED EVERYTHING, caught by the case's own control: re-spelling EVERY predictor edge left nothing carrying bit 1, so no graph could refute and the suite went from one failure to eight. A tau pair is one dart only when BOTH spellings are declared on the SAME pair, which makes the fold a property of the edge set rather than of an edge.}
}

@misc{gate-F66-from-is-projection-order-in-one-population-and-a-dart-in-another,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F65-a-declared-tau-pair-is-a-completed-closure-not-a-clash},
  claim    = {The from field spells projection ordering in one population and a reading chain in another and a retraction dart in a third, so a reader that treats it as one edge kind manufactures contradictions},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. Three surviving two-node cycles were reported as same-orientation double darts and a genuine V4 contradiction, with a proposal to identify and drop the wrong edge in each. The operator refused both halves, that it is a matter of perspective and that two bidi edges of different kinds between the same nodes needs explaining. MEASURED with bibstruct --entries and --field join. Most original worklist entries carry a join field holding prose punctuation, a period or a comma or comma-and, which is the projection's sentence connector, and it co-occurs with from. So from on those entries is projection ORDERING rather than entailment, meaning render this claim then this glue then the next. A5 from A5-renaming with join comma-and sits beside A5 rests-on A5-renaming, the real grounding dart, so they are one dart and one rendering instruction rather than two darts. AND THE BIB CARRIES TWO CONVENTIONS, since merged bazel-intake entries carry note instead of join and use from as a reading chain, while only the four retract-section entries use it as a dart. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I reported three surviving two-node cycles as same-orientation double darts, a genuine V4 contradiction, and proposed identifying and dropping the wrong edge in each. Operator: it is a matter of perspective, and why would there be two bidi edges of different kinds between the same nodes. Both corrections land. MEASURED with bibstruct --entries and --field join: the worklist carries a join field on most entries holding prose punctuation -- a period, a comma, comma-and -- which is the projection's sentence connector, and it co-occurs with from. So from on those entries is PROJECTION ORDERING, not entailment: render this claim's sentence, then this glue, then the next. A5 from A5-renaming with join=comma-and sits beside A5 rests-on A5-renaming, which is the real grounding dart. They are not two darts between the same nodes, they are one dart and one rendering instruction spelled in a field EDGE_FIELDS declares to be an edge. AND THE BIB HAS TWO CONVENTIONS: original worklist entries carry join, merged bazel-intake entries carry note and use from as a reading chain, and only the four section=retract entries use it as a dart. So from carries at least THREE relations and the reader collapses all of them.}
}

@misc{gate-F67-f66-misread-a-precedence-relation-as-an-identity,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F66-from-is-projection-order-in-one-population-and-a-dart-in-another},
  rests-on = {gate-F63-the-odd-cycle-is-the-discriminator-the-count-cannot-be},
  claim    = {A field checked earlier in a renderer overrides a later one rather than redefining it, so reading precedence as identity turns a correct co-occurrence into a false claim about what the later field means},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.chirality_selftest},
  note     = {kind=retraction by=main. RETRACTS gate-F66. MEASURED against paperkit project.py weave via pycodemod --source. The join field is tested first and continues, while from is consulted only in the else branch as the clause-adjacency test, so join OVERRIDES from for connector purposes and gate-F66 read that as an identity. AND F66's own sentence, that join is spelled in a field EDGE_FIELDS declares to be an edge, is false, since pycodemod --literal join over bib_corpus.py returns zero sites and paperkit files join under scalar. What survives is gate-F64's correct half, that from chains are document-order, but that is a USE of the edge inside projection rather than a redefinition of the edge. Found by an adversarial audit rather than by the author, which is the witness that self-assessment did not reach it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): RETRACTS the reading in gate-F66. F66 said the join field marks from as projection-ordering rather than entailment. MEASURED against paperkit/paperkit/project.py weave, via pycodemod --source: join is tested FIRST and continues, while from is consulted only in the else branch as the clause-adjacency test. That is a PRECEDENCE relation, join overriding from for connector purposes, and F66 read it as an IDENTITY relation. And F66's own sentence, that join is spelled in a field EDGE_FIELDS declares to be an edge, is false: pycodemod --literal join over substrate/bib_corpus.py returns zero sites, so join was never in the roster. Paperkit files it under _SCALAR. What survives from F64 is the correct half, that from chains are document-order, but that is a USE of the edge in projection rather than a redefinition of it. Found by an adversarial audit, not by the author.}
}

@misc{gate-F65-chirality-suite-is-gated-verdict-is-not,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {substrate/chirality_selftest.py and chirality_bib_selftest.py ARE run by the pre-commit 'tool selftests' gate via run_selftests.py dynamic discovery, but the chirality VERDICT is consumed by no gate, so a red INCONSISTENT blocks nothing},
  check    = {refuses:python3,-m,substrate.chirality_bib},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. Witnesses. run_selftests.py --list shows substrate/chirality_bib_selftest.py and substrate/chirality_selftest.py among 409 suites, and --dry-run --last marks both 'new suite, never run'. Both suites PASS standalone at 42/42 and 11/11. module_importers.py substrate/chirality_bib.py reports 1 import site, its own selftest. substrate/chirality.py reports 3 sites, all chirality_bib plus the two suites. No .githooks file mentions chirality (pre-commit, pre-push, post-commit, prepare-commit-msg read whole). worklist_gate.py invokes paperkit entry points gate.py/project.py/discriminate.py and never chirality. bibstruct --field check over warrants.bib plus 6 agent bibs shows every check is item:KEY or traces:PATH with no shell command, so no bib check runs chirality. Meanwhile python3 -m substrate.chirality_bib exits 1 with INCONSISTENT, 31 conflicts over 125 edges, 4 components over 67 nodes. A GREEN 'tool selftests' therefore asserts only that the chirality READER's own cases pass. It asserts nothing about the corpus the reader measures. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the chirality SUITE runs under the tool-selftests gate while the chirality VERDICT gates nothing; the refusal holds while python3 -m substrate.chirality_bib exits nonzero and no gate consumes it}
}

@misc{gate-F68-a-refuses-witness-was-bound-to-an-unrelated-nonzero-exit,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F65-chirality-suite-is-gated-verdict-is-not},
  claim    = {A refuses witness bound to a command whose nonzero exit has an unrelated cause is invisible to an exit-code polarity probe, because the code is right and the reason is wrong},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. A dispatched agent filed the chirality suite-versus-verdict finding with kind refuses over python3 -m substrate.chirality_bib. The finding is that nothing consumes the verdict, while the witness asserts a refusal is still installed, and the probe prints no refusal line found beside its exit-1 row. The command exits nonzero because the CORPUS is inconsistent, so repairing the corpus flips the witness to closed while the finding stays true. This is the gate-F55 inverted-witness class arriving from a peer agent rather than the author, the third instance this session. AND _probe_polarity CANNOT SEE IT, since that guard checks the exit code and the code here is genuinely nonzero, so a witness bound to the right code for the wrong reason passes registration cleanly. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A dispatched agent filed gate-F65-chirality-suite-is-gated-verdict-is-not with kind refuses:python3 -m substrate.chirality_bib. The finding is that NOTHING CONSUMES the chirality verdict. The witness asserts that a refusal is still installed. Those are different claims and the probe says so in its own output, printing (no refusal line found) beside the exit-1 line. The command exits nonzero because the CORPUS is inconsistent, not because any refusal fired, so repairing the corpus would flip the witness to closed while the finding, that no gate reads the verdict, stays exactly as true. This is the gate-F55 inverted-witness shape arriving from a peer agent rather than from the author, and it is the third instance this session. AND THE POLARITY PROBE DID NOT CATCH IT because _probe_polarity covers refuses and selftest by checking the exit code, which here is genuinely nonzero -- the probe cannot see that the nonzero has an unrelated cause. A witness bound to the right exit code for the wrong reason is invisible to an exit-code probe.}
}

@misc{gate-F69-the-staleness-warning-went-stale,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F68-a-refuses-witness-was-bound-to-an-unrelated-nonzero-exit},
  claim    = {A paragraph recording that a reassurance went stale can itself go stale in the opposite direction, warning about a defect since fixed and citing a line that moved},
  check    = {selftest:python3,scratch/pycodemod.py,--forwards,may_write,write},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. may_write's docstring records run_selftests.py:724 as calling it positionally and raising TypeError, crashing a Phase A gate. MEASURED, pycodemod --literal may_write over that file returns 0 sites in 0 of 1 file, so the cited call site does not exist. The docstring also says 4 of 9 call sites forward the keyword while --forwards may_write write reports 4 of 8. The paragraph's own closing sentence states the governing rule, that a per-call-site status belongs in the census that derives it rather than in prose which must be re-edited to stay true, and the paragraph then states a per-call-site status in prose. Found while answering a peer's question about the commit-path guard, not by an audit of the file. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): may_write's docstring records a live crash: run_selftests.py:724 calls it POSITIONALLY and raises TypeError, crashing a Phase A gate, while the docstring claimed the callers were fine. MEASURED NOW: pycodemod --literal may_write over scripts/run_selftests.py returns 0 sites in 0 of 1 file. The call site does not exist. The docstring also says 4 of 9 sites forward the keyword and --forwards reports 4 of 8. So the paragraph written to record A REASSURANCE THAT WENT STALE has itself gone stale, in the opposite direction: it now warns about a defect that was fixed and cites a line number that moved. Its own text names the rule it broke -- a per-call-site status belongs in the census that derives it, not in prose that must be re-edited to stay true -- and then states a per-call-site status in prose. The census mode it names is the correct reader and answers in one call.}
}

@misc{gate-F70-the-standing-example-was-not-a-standing-witness,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F69-the-staleness-warning-went-stale},
  claim    = {An example offered to show that a population may already be repaired can be drawn from outside that population, leaving a true conclusion resting on evidence that does not bear on it},
  check    = {selftest:python3,.claude/agents/findings.py,--kinds},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. The plan argues the standing witnesses are a population nobody examined member by member and cites gate-F22, opened at random and found to be already selftest. MEASURED, gate-F22 is an ambiguous prefix over two keys and the ledger refuses to resolve it rather than picking one. Disambiguated, gate-F22-build-lib-doubles-every-path-census probes as selftest and passes, so it is not in the standing population and never was. The conclusion holds and its stated evidence does not. AND THE COUNT HAS NOT MOVED ALL SESSION, with standing held while the behavioural kinds grew, so every finding filed this session went to a falsifiable kind and none paid down the vacuous population. Under the mtools frame this stops being housekeeping and becomes a migration blocker, because mtools witnesses every warrant one-to-one with a real pytest case and carries no vacuous witnesses, so a substrate witness that asserts nothing cannot survive interning. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The plan argues the 223 standing witnesses are a population nobody examined member by member, and offers gate-F22 as evidence: I opened a standing witness at random and it is already selftest, so the convertible ones may convert to kinds that already exist. MEASURED: gate-F22 is an AMBIGUOUS prefix over two keys and the ledger refuses to resolve it rather than picking. Disambiguated, gate-F22-build-lib-doubles-every-path-census probes as selftest and PASSES -- so it is not in the standing population at all and never was. The conclusion stands and its stated evidence does not: an example drawn to show that standing witnesses may already be behavioural was drawn from a witness that is not standing. AND THE COUNT HAS NOT MOVED ALL SESSION -- standing held at 223 while behavioural grew from 74 to 82, so every finding filed this session went to a falsifiable kind and none paid down the vacuous population. This now blocks the mtools migration rather than being substrate housekeeping, because mtools witnesses every warrant one-to-one with a real pytest case and carries no vacuous witnesses.}
}

@misc{gate-F71-list-modes-on-this-surface-execute,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F70-the-standing-example-was-not-a-standing-witness},
  claim    = {A mode named as a listing can execute its whole population, and nothing in the flag distinguishes the cheap reader from the expensive one},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. Three instances this session. run_selftests.py invoked bare IS the run rather than a mode list, and an agent dispatch guessed it and ran the full roster for roughly twenty minutes. run_selftests.py --times re-runs rather than reading recorded durations, and the same dispatch hit it, overwriting the recorded run a second time. findings.py --list runs every registered witness as a subprocess rather than listing keys, and reached the 120 second timeout before being stopped. The cheap readers exist but are spelled differently in each case, --last for the runner and --kinds for the ledger, so the tell is not in the name and the expensive direction is the default reading. Cost so far is two full roster runs overwriting substrate's recorded selftest state, with the prior record gone. This is the guess-a-mode rule failing in a shape the rule does not name, because the mode was not guessed but read and misjudged for cost. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Three times this session a mode whose NAME reads as a cheap listing turned out to execute the population. run_selftests.py bare IS the run, not a mode list -- an agent dispatch guessed it and ran the full roster for about 20 minutes. run_selftests.py --times likewise re-runs rather than reading recorded durations, and the same dispatch hit it, overwriting the recorded run a SECOND time. findings.py --list runs every registered witness as a subprocess rather than listing keys, and hit the 120s timeout before being stopped. The cheap readers on this surface are spelled differently in each case: --last reads run_selftests' record, --kinds is findings' cheap census. THE TELL IS NOT IN THE NAME. A reader cannot distinguish list-the-keys from run-them-and-report by the flag, and the expensive direction is the default reading. Cost so far: two full roster runs overwriting substrate's recorded selftest state, whose prior record 405 suites and 7589 cases is gone. This is the guess-a-mode rule failing in a shape the rule does not name -- the mode was not guessed, it was READ and misjudged for cost.}
}

@misc{gate-F72-the-swap-architecture-is-a-precondition-no-authority-states,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F71-list-modes-on-this-surface-execute},
  claim    = {A refusal whose correctness depends on a host design property is unreadable as a refusal when no authority states that property, so a vendor softening it to best-effort looks like portability rather than defeat},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. cgroup-scope refuses when memory.swap.max cannot be written and carries the measurement in its header, swap.max=max giving rc 0 and oom_kill 0 against swap.max=0 giving rc 137 and oom_kill 1 on the same bytes. That refusal is correct only because this host is designed to swap. Operator, verbatim, this host is designed to swap, it uses zram and zswap. Cassian confirmed the property from their own charter, measured swapon as a 32G swapfile plus 9.2G zram at 9.0G used, and reported reaching the identical class independently with a k8s pod limit being a hard ceiling only under swap.max=0. Two repos, no shared derivation, so the class is structural. THE GAP is that no substrate authority states the swap architecture as the precondition the refusal rests on, so a reader gets the measurement without the design property. mat230's vendored copy softened the refusal to best-effort and documents the false premise in a comment reading swap off equals cap is RSS, on a machine where swap is architectural. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): cgroup-scope REFUSES when memory.swap.max cannot be written, and its own header carries the measurement: swap.max=max gives rc=0 oom_kill=0 while swap.max=0 gives rc=137 oom_kill=1 on the same bytes. That refusal is correct ONLY BECAUSE this host is designed to swap. Operator: this host is DESIGNED to swap, it uses zram and zswap. Cassian confirmed the same property from their own charter and measured swapon --show as 32G swapfile plus 9.2G zram at 9.0G used, and reported hitting the identical class independently, a k8s pod memory limit is a hard ceiling only with swap.max=0, cgroup-scope FAILS GREEN. So two repos reached the finding without shared derivation, which makes it structural. THE GAP: no substrate authority states the host swap architecture as the PRECONDITION the refusal rests on. A reader who sees the _die and asks why gets the measurement but not the design property, and a vendored copy that softens it to best-effort looks like a reasonable portability concession rather than a guaranteed defeat. mat230's vendored copy did exactly that and additionally documents the false premise in a comment reading swap off equals cap is RSS.}
}

@misc{gate-F73-consumer-liveness-is-the-fourth-link,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F72-the-swap-architecture-is-a-precondition-no-authority-states},
  claim    = {A defect chain can hold at the mechanism, the code and the environment and still be wrong, because none of those three establishes that anything still runs the code},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. I escalated mat230's vendored cgroup-scope as the highest-priority item in the membudget audit, a live silently-defeated memory cap. The operator has since stated mat230 is retired, so it is a stale artifact and the escalation was overstated. The chain had three sound links. Substrate measured the mechanism, I verified the code divergence, cassian verified the host precondition on a machine designed to swap. Every link held and nobody asked whether anything still runs it. I had written three reads none sufficient alone, and the honest completion is four, with consumer liveness as the fourth. A vendored copy in a repo nobody runs is hygiene and the same copy under load is an incident, while the diff looks identical, so liveness cannot be read off the artifact. This is a measurement extended past the domain it covers, occurring at the scale of an audit chain rather than a single claim. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I escalated mat230's vendored cgroup-scope as the audit's highest-priority item, a live silently-defeated memory cap. The operator has since said mat230 is retired, so it is a stale artifact and the escalation was overstated. THE CHAIN HAD THREE SOUND LINKS AND STILL REACHED A WRONG CONCLUSION. Substrate MEASURED the mechanism, swap.max=max giving rc 0 and oom_kill 0 against swap.max=0 giving rc 137 and oom_kill 1. I VERIFIED the code divergence, mat230 using best-effort where substrate refuses. Cassian VERIFIED the host precondition, 32G swapfile plus 9.2G zram at 9.0G used on a host designed to swap. Every link held and nobody asked whether anything still RUNS the code. I wrote three reads none sufficient alone; the honest completion is FOUR and the fourth is CONSUMER LIVENESS. The code is wrong plus the host qualifies does not establish a live failure. A vendored copy in a repo nobody runs is a hygiene item and the same copy in a repo under load is an incident, and the diff looks identical, so liveness cannot be inferred from the artifact. This is a measurement extended past the domain it covers, at the scale of an audit chain rather than a single claim.}
}

@misc{gate-F74-nine-refuse-and-twentyeight-have-no-verdict,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F73-consumer-liveness-is-the-fourth-link},
  claim    = {A commit-refusal survey reporting nine refusals and twenty-eight unmeasurables is nine known-red and twenty-eight unknown, never twenty-eight broken and never any green},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. Paperkit observed that substrate's membudget files are uncommitted, so three peers audited a snapshot nobody can fetch. Measured wider, git status short with untracked suppressed produces 141KB of paths, and substrate/commit_refusal reports 295 staged paths, nine gates that WOULD REFUSE, and twenty-eight UNMEASURABLE. The nine are real accumulated debt across ban, claims, rederive, discharge, sumtype, public, mode contracts, divergence and the tool mode census. The twenty-eight are not a claim those gates are broken, because verdict's docstring says it asks each authority rather than executing, so unmeasurable means the reader declined a gate that writes on a bare run, or exposes no census, or will not import under a bare name. The tool renders it as no verdict not clean. Kind is unwitnessed because the command exits 1 while any gate would refuse, so a selftest witness would be born open and a refuses witness would assert something else entirely, which the polarity probe caught at registration. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Paperkit observed substrate's membudget files are uncommitted and three peers therefore audited an unfetchable snapshot. Measured wider: git status --short --untracked-files=no produces 141KB of paths, and substrate/commit_refusal reports 295 staged paths with NINE gates that WOULD REFUSE and TWENTY-EIGHT reported UNMEASURABLE. The refusing nine are real accumulated debt across the ban ratchet, claims, rederive, discharge, sumtype, public, mode contracts, divergence and the tool mode census. THE TWENTY-EIGHT ARE NOT A CLAIM THAT THOSE GATES ARE BROKEN. verdict's own docstring says it asks each authority rather than executing, so UNMEASURABLE means this reader declined to run a gate that writes on a bare invocation, or one exposing no census, or one whose module will not import under a bare name. That is a deliberate read-only bound and the tool renders it as NO VERDICT, NOT CLEAN. The honest summary is nine known-red, twenty-eight unknown, zero measured-green. Kind is unwitnessed because commit_refusal exits 1 while any gate would refuse, so a selftest witness would be born open and a refuses witness would assert a refusal is installed rather than that this census holds -- the polarity probe caught exactly that at registration.}
}

@misc{gate-F75-headers-and-budget-disagree-on-one-file,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F74-nine-refuse-and-twentyeight-have-no-verdict},
  claim    = {A straight ASCII apostrophe in an ATX heading makes mdstruct --headers drop that heading silently, because pandoc smart-quotes the AST text and the span matcher compares it against raw source lines},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. linux-sources reported --headers omitting ordinary headings and isolated the cause to one character after falsifying six hypotheses. REPRODUCED MINIMALLY here: five headings differing in one character, and the one carrying a straight apostrophe is absent while the same sentence with U+2019 parses. The preceding section's span extends over the dropped heading, so a subsection is reported under the wrong parent. CROSS-READER DISAGREEMENT CONFIRMS IT, since --budget reports eighteen headings on the live file where --headers reports twelve. MECHANISM READ FROM THE SOURCE, as headers returns AST text with no line numbers, so spans come from a second pass matching that text against raw lines, and pandoc normalises the apostrophe between the two. THIS IS A SECOND INSTANCE OF A CLASS THE MODULE'S OWN DOCSTRING RECORDS AS FIXED, where the same five modes were blind to frontmatter'd markdown while budget read it fine, which is evidence the earlier fix was scoped to its trigger rather than to the class. The failure is silent and the output is well-formed, and mdstruct is retiring into mikemol.mdstruct. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): linux-sources reported mdstruct --headers silently omitting two ordinary ATX headings from a file in mtools, and reproduced here. CONFIRMED BY CROSS-READER DISAGREEMENT: --budget reports 18 headings on that file including the two, while --headers reports 12 and omits them, placing their content inside the preceding section's span so a subsection is reported as a child of the wrong parent. --spans by heading text also returns zero sections for one of them, so the loss is in the AST parse rather than the rendering. THE FAILURE IS SILENT AND THE OUTPUT IS PLAUSIBLE: a well-formed section list with correct-looking spans whose only tell is that the numbering jumps. THIS IS A SECOND INSTANCE OF A CLASS mdstruct's OWN DOCSTRING RECORDS AS FIXED, where --headers, --tables, --rows, --items and --coherence were blind to frontmatter'd markdown while --budget read it fine -- same modes, same asymmetry, different trigger, which is evidence the earlier fix was scoped to its trigger rather than to the class. linux-sources falsified four hypotheses before handing it over: not the flag glyph, not the em-dash, not a letter-suffixed number, not following a horizontal rule. Cause not yet isolated. mdstruct is one of the tools retiring into mikemol.mdstruct, so a census reader that under-reports without saying so is a consolidation hazard.}
}

@misc{gate-F76-a-console-script-outlives-its-package,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F75-headers-and-budget-disagree-on-one-file},
  claim    = {An installed console script whose package is absent is worse than no entry point, because it is on PATH and it answers},
  check    = {refuses:/home/mikemol/github/substrate/.venv/bin/mdstruct,--headers,/dev/null},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. Paperkit reported and substrate verified that substrate/.venv/bin/mdstruct raises ModuleNotFoundError for mikemol.mdstruct on any invocation, so inside substrate's own venv the name resolves two ways and one is a corpse. THIS IS THE PACKAGING ROOT CAUSE FROM THE OTHER SIDE. The corpus holds that membudget could not be shared because its bash core is undistributable, so consumers vendored or re-ported. Here a distribution's entry point shipped while its implementation did not, which is the same seam failing in the opposite direction. AND THE CENSUS ALREADY PAID FOR THE NAMING HAZARD, since paperkit told linux-sources their mdstruct invocation could not exist and linux-sources told paperkit the same, each correct about their own binary and wrong about the other's, because neither named a path. Five implementations exist under the github tree with incompatible CLIs. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Paperkit reported and substrate verified: substrate/.venv/bin/mdstruct is an installed console script for the mtools distribution whose package is absent, raising ModuleNotFoundError No module named mikemol.mdstruct on any invocation. So inside substrate's own venv the name mdstruct resolves two ways -- scratch/mdstruct.py, which works, and an entry point that is a corpse. THIS IS THE PACKAGING ROOT CAUSE ARRIVING FROM THE OTHER SIDE. The corpus's central claim is that membudget could not be shared because its bash core is undistributable, so consumers vendored or re-ported. Here a distribution's ENTRY POINT was installed while its package was not, which is the same seam failing in the opposite direction: the call surface shipped and the implementation did not. AND IT IS A NAMING HAZARD THE CENSUS ALREADY PAID FOR, since paperkit told linux-sources their mdstruct invocation could not exist and linux-sources told paperkit the same, both correct about their own binary and wrong about the other's, because neither named a path. Five mdstruct implementations exist under ~/github with incompatible CLIs. A dead entry point on PATH is worse than an absent one because it answers.}
}

@misc{gate-F77-the-lease-generalizes-to-agent-lockout-tagout,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F76-a-console-script-outlives-its-package},
  claim    = {The lease generalizes to granular lockout/tagout for concurrent agents editing one tree, which is a different axis from the build-versus-non-build split the whole census assumed},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. Operator supplied the reason the census was commissioned, that the lease-shape phenomenon generalizes to multiple agents modifying the same tree and communicating to avoid stepping on each other. Four filings and two second-order passes missed it, framing the residual question as scheduler-coordinated build actions versus lease-coordinated everything else, which is a claim about BUILD work where the generalization is about CONCURRENT WRITERS. Substrate's own suite_claims docstring already states the mechanism in lockout-tagout terms, that the label is the mechanism, at most one live lease may carry it, no scheduler and no registry and no lockfile, and the claim's lifetime is the holder process's lifetime so the kernel releases it on any exit including SIGKILL. That last property is why it beats a hand-rolled agent lock, since the failure that kills a hand-rolled lock is a crashed holder leaving a stale lock nobody can clear, and pid-plus-starttime liveness with kernel fd-release makes that unrepresentable. AND THIS SESSION IS THE UNSERVED CASE, since four agent sessions wrote into one untracked tree, negotiated a path convention by hand, split it two ways, and found the split by running find. The census recorded that as a coordination lapse when it was an unserved requirement for the machinery under audit. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Operator, supplying the reason the membudget census was commissioned at all: the lease-shape phenomenon generalizes to multiple agents modifying the same tree, communicating to prevent each other from stepping on each other -- granular lockout/tagout. FOUR FILINGS AND TWO SECOND-ORDER PASSES MISSED THIS ENTIRELY. The corpus framed the residual question as scheduler-coordinated build actions versus lease-coordinated everything-else, and treated the lease layer as what a build graph cannot see. That framing is about BUILD work. The generalization is about CONCURRENT WRITERS. SUBSTRATE'S OWN suite_claims DOCSTRING ALREADY DESCRIBES THE MECHANISM IN LOCKOUT-TAGOUT TERMS: the label is the mechanism, at most one live lease may carry it, no scheduler no registry no lockfile, and the claim's lifetime IS the holder process's lifetime so the kernel releases it on any exit including SIGKILL. THAT LAST PROPERTY IS WHY IT BEATS A HAND-ROLLED AGENT LOCK. The failure that kills a hand-rolled lock is a crashed holder leaving a stale lock nobody can clear, and pid-plus-starttime liveness with kernel fd-release makes it unrepresentable. AND THIS SESSION IS THE UNSERVED CASE: four agent sessions wrote into one untracked mtools tree, negotiated a path convention by hand, split it two ways, and discovered the split by running find. The census recorded that as a coordination lapse when it was an unserved requirement for the very machinery under audit.}
}

@misc{gate-F78-hkey-strips-markup-but-not-apostrophes,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F75-headers-and-budget-disagree-on-one-file},
  rests-on = {gate-F76-a-console-script-outlives-its-package},
  claim    = {A normaliser written to reconcile two spellings reconciles only the spellings its author had seen, and the heading it cannot match is silently absorbed by its predecessor},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=retraction by=main. CORRECTS gate-F75's stated mechanism. linux-sources localised the defect and refuted smart-quoting as the cause, since the character is 0x27 and --budget reads the same file correctly. Read from the binding, spans anchors each pandoc heading by matching rendered text against raw source through _hkey while --budget does not use that path, and _hkey strips backticks asterisks and underscores but not apostrophes, so pandoc rendering 0x27 as U+2019 makes the keys differ, the forward scan fails, and the uncrossed cursor lets the previous span swallow the heading. THE DOCSTRING OF _hkey RECORDS THE SAME CLASS ONE ITERATION EARLIER, since it exists because rendered text never compared equal for headings with inline markup, and it fixed the markup case only. The consequence is a silently mis-targeted write, because append-section and replace-section target by span, so a bounded write against a parent lands inside a child with well-formed output and no error, in a tool that refuses a heading-carrying body as re-parenting. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): linux-sources localised the mdstruct heading-loss defect and REFUTED the mechanism substrate filed in gate-F75. The character is 0x27 ASCII, not U+2019, and --budget reads the same file with correct spans while --headers and --spans lose the heading and extend its predecessor over it. So it is not pandoc mangling the AST. READ FROM THE BINDING: spans() anchors each pandoc heading by matching rendered text against raw source lines through _hkey, and --budget does not use that path. _hkey normalises by stripping backticks, asterisks and underscores, folding whitespace and casefolding. It does not touch apostrophes, and pandoc renders 0x27 as U+2019, so the keys differ, the forward scan never matches, the heading is dropped, and because the cursor does not advance the previous section's span swallows it. THE DOCSTRING OF _hkey RECORDS THE IDENTICAL CLASS ONE ITERATION EARLIER: it was written because rendered text never compared equal to raw source for headings with inline markup, and it fixed the markup case only. A normaliser written to reconcile two spellings reconciled the spellings its author had seen. AND THE CONSEQUENCE IS A SILENT MIS-TARGETED WRITE, since --append-section and --replace-section target by span, so a bounded write against a parent lands inside a child with well-formed output and no error. The tool refuses a body carrying a heading as re-parenting while silently mis-locating a write against a heading it cannot see.}
}

@misc{gate-F79-a-measurement-contaminated-by-its-own-reporting,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F78-hkey-strips-markup-but-not-apostrophes},
  claim    = {A count taken over a corpus that discusses the count is contaminated by its own reporting, and cross-vantage review does not check a number nobody re-runs},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. The edit-during-execution figure was reported as 91 occurrences over 11 days, split 41 before a BES cutover and 50 after, and all four parties carried it. Re-derived for a consolidation the raw count came back 89 then 88, and an append-only transcript cannot shrink, which forced a split by record type. Fifty of the ninety-one were the reporting party's own messages discussing the error, since only tool-output records carry occurrences, so the number grew as the census discussed it and shrank when compaction rewrote earlier messages. Filtered to tool results the count is 32 over 8 days, 21 pre-cutover and 11 post, AND THE CONCLUSION REVERSES, because four filings state that a shared executor does not close the class while it roughly halves it, with eleven remaining occurrences still justifying a local-tree lease. What survives is that every occurrence is one repo editing itself during its own build, so an agent with a background build is already two writers. The durable result is the method one, that three orders of review across four parties never re-derived the most-cited figure in the corpus. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Paperkit retracted the most-cited number in the membudget corpus. The edit-during-execution count was reported as 91 occurrences over 11 days, split 41 before a BES cutover and 50 after, and all four parties carried it into their filings. Re-derived for a consolidation, the raw count came back 89 then 88 -- and an append-only transcript cannot shrink, which is what forced a split by record type. FIFTY OF THE NINETY-ONE WERE THE REPORTING PARTY'S OWN MESSAGES DISCUSSING THE ERROR, since only tool-output records carry occurrences. The number GREW AS THE CENSUS DISCUSSED IT and shrank when compaction rewrote earlier messages. Filtered to tool results: 32 occurrences over 8 days, 21 pre-cutover and 11 post. THE CONCLUSION REVERSES. Four filings state that a shared remote executor does not close the class; measured correctly it roughly HALVES it, and eleven remaining occurrences still justify a local-tree lease. What survives is the lead finding, that every occurrence is one repo editing itself during its own build, so an agent with a background build is already two writers. AND THE METHOD RESULT IS THE DURABLE PART: three orders of cross-vantage review across four parties, and the most-cited figure in the corpus was never re-derived by anyone. CROSS-VANTAGE READING DOES NOT CHECK A NUMBER NOBODY RE-RUNS, and a measurement taken over a corpus that discusses the measurement is contaminated by its own reporting.}
}

@misc{gate-F80-the-gc-arm-is-exercised-and-the-obvious-test-cannot-reach-it,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F79-a-measurement-contaminated-by-its-own-reporting},
  rests-on = {gate-F73-consumer-liveness-is-the-fourth-link},
  claim    = {A branch can be unreachable by the test every party writes rather than merely unexercised, and the interface a reader would check the result with can destroy the evidence},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. The gc-before-believing row was the one member of the shared core no party had made fire, with four parties each running a SIGKILL arm and all four exercising release. Two source facts explain it, verified independently by substrate and linux-sources. cmd_status runs ensure and gc before reading, so the observer mutates the state it reports. And the claim-path gc sits inside the contention polling loop behind two guards, firing only when a contender finds a lease whose owner is dead, so a holder-plus-killer shape never reaches the branch because nothing arrives to contend. CLOSED BY A THREE-ROLE PROBE WITH THREE CONTROLS, written as a program after an earlier improvised attempt was declared invalid for an unattributable kill. The controls are that the lease appeared, the holder is dead, and the ledger still carries the lease after the death, and the third is the whole probe because a kernel that already removed it would let a contender pass without reaching the gc branch at all. All three held and the contender acquired with rc 0 by reaping a dead holder. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gc-before-believing-a-holder was the one member of the shared core that no party had made fire. Four parties each ran a SIGKILL arm and all four exercised RELEASE, not gc. TWO SOURCE FACTS EXPLAIN WHY, both verified independently by substrate and linux-sources. First, cmd_status runs ensure and gc before it reads, so the observer mutates the state it reports and a clean status after a kill cannot distinguish gc from kernel release. Second, the claim-path gc sits INSIDE the contention polling loop behind two guards, firing only when a contender finds a lease AND its owner is dead. SO THE ROW WAS NOT UNEXERCISED, IT WAS UNREACHABLE BY THE TEST EVERY PARTY WROTE: a holder-plus-killer shape never brings a contender, so nothing arrives to reach the branch. CLOSED BY A THREE-ROLE PROBE WITH THREE CONTROLS, written as a program rather than improvised after an earlier improvised attempt was declared INVALID for an unattributable kill. Controls: the lease appeared, the holder pid is dead, AND THE LEDGER STILL CARRIES THE LEASE AFTER THE DEATH -- the third is the whole probe, because if the kernel had already removed it the contender would never reach the gc branch and a pass would be a false positive for the wrong mechanism. Result: all three controls held and the contender acquired with rc 0, reaping a dead holder. The claim-path gc is exercised.}
}

@misc{gate-F81-no-header-population-at-all,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Substrate has neither an SPDX nor a copyright line in any of its 556 promoted files, so its reported header convention was generalised from one example and never censused.},
