  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. MEASURED: install_freshness reports STALE, 1 file differs, DELETED __editable___substrate_tooling_0_1_0_finder.py. substrate is an editable distribution, which is how .claude/agents/findings.py reaches substrate.* without the sys.path surgery the operator refused. Ⓝ137-prime's dispatch swap depends on that path. ⚑ Also an intake hazard: a peer evaluating substrate modules cannot see this from any repo-level measurement — install_freshness is the only reader, and nothing runs it on a schedule. ⚑⚑ CAUSE NOT DIAGNOSED: uv sync, a deleted indexed module, or something else — three causes, distinguishable only by a probe nobody has run. Reporting the reading, not a cause. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): substrate/install_freshness.py reports STALE: one file differs from the checkout, DELETED __editable___substrate_tooling_0_1_0_finder.py. ⚑ WHY THIS IS NOT COSMETIC RIGHT NOW: substrate is installed as an EDITABLE distribution, and that is the mechanism by which a script outside the package reaches substrate.* without sys.path surgery — the surgery the operator refused outright. Ⓝ137-prime's drain depends on exactly that path: .claude/agents/findings.py lives outside the package and must import substrate.dict_extent / witness_polarity / witness_roster when the dispatch lands. A broken editable finder makes that import fail at the moment of the swap, and the failure would present as the new modules being wrong rather than the install being stale. ⚑⚑ IT IS ALSO A HAZARD FOR INTAKE BY PEERS: mtools is evaluating whether to adopt or receive substrate modules, and a stale editable install is a fact about THIS machine's environment that no repo-level measurement reports. install_freshness is the only reader that sees it, and nothing runs it on a schedule. ⚑ NOT YET DIAGNOSED: whether the finder file was removed by a uv sync, by the deletion of a module it indexed, or by something else. Reporting the