  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Both findings name the same mechanism and both supersede gate-F75. gate-F78 was first and credits linux-sources. ⚑ The duplicate came from a METHOD defect: bibstruct refused a from-edge because the key was not in the ONE bib it read, and I generalised that to unpaired-across-six. findings.py gate-F78 answers fully. ⚑⚑ Committed while reading --pairing, whose whole purpose is stopping one finding being filed under two spellings and whose note says RE-KEY do not file twice. gate-F78 is absent from the drift BECAUSE it is paired. ⚑ Content is not redundant: F78 carries the mis-targeted-write consequence and the attribution, F82 the case-and-control pair. The KEYS are what want re-keying. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-F82 DUPLICATES gate-F78. Both name the same mechanism (mdstruct._hkey strips markup but not the apostrophe pandoc renders as U+2019, spans forward-cursor drops the heading and every section after), both supersede gate-F75, and gate-F78 was filed FIRST and credits linux-sources for localising it. ⚑ HOW THE DUPLICATE HAPPENED, AND IT IS A METHOD DEFECT NOT A MEMORY ONE: I tried to attach from=gate-F78 to a bib entry, bibstruct refused because the key was not in the file it read, and I concluded the finding was unpaired across all six ledgers. That conclusion came from ONE reader answering about ONE bib. The resolver — findings.py gate-F78 — answers it fully and always could. I then re-derived the mechanism from scratch and filed it as new. ⚑⚑ THIS IS THE EXACT CLASS THE PAIRING CENSUS EXISTS TO CATCH, COMMITTED WHILE READING THAT CENSUS. --pairing reports SPELLING-PAIRs precisely so one finding under two spellings is not filed twice, and its own note says RE-KEY, do not file twice. gate-F78 does not appear in the drift because it IS paired. I read a report whose subject is duplicate filings and filed a duplicate. ⚑ WHAT EACH CARRIES THAT THE OTHER DOES NOT: F78 has the mis-targeted-write consequence (append-section and replace-section target by span, so a bounded write lands inside a child silently) and the linux-sources attribution. F82 has the case-and-control pair. Neither is redundant in content and the KEYS are redundant, which is what wants re-keying.}
