@misc{gate-F94-two-exe-bit-readers-disagree-on-one-population,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F92-a-flag-lost-in-transit-left-a-green-check-on-the-wrong-proposition},
  claim    = {The exe-bit census and the exe-bit repair tool disagree on one population, and the repair tool is the one that is wrong.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. MEASURED: file_mode reports 21 shebang-no-exec disagreements including every module created this session, fix_exe_bit run bare reports none, and the per-file gate sides with file_mode by refusing an edit on EXE001. ⚑ The sweep also misreported its denominator — given two paths it printed 1 repaired of 1 reported while both were in the census, silently narrowing operands it does not take. ⚑⚑ The class this creates: the gate passes a NEW file because the bit cannot be wrong before the file exists, and it becomes wrong at creation — so witness_polarity, dict_extent, witness_roster, imported_constant and their suites all staged clean and now carry a live EXE001 surfaced only by a later edit. ⚑ file_mode names its own blind spot in band: the git INDEX mode is unread, so a staged 100644 still ships. Three readers, no single answer. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): MEASURED, two readers one question opposite answers: substrate/file_mode.py reports 21 shebang-no-exec disagreements including every module created this session, and scripts/fix_exe_bit.py run bare reports NO shebang/mode mismatches. The per-file pycheck gate agrees with file_mode — it refuses an edit to substrate/bib_check_selftest.py on EXE001 — so the sweep is the reader that is wrong, and it is the one whose whole job is repairing this. ⚑ THE SWEEP ALSO MISREPORTED ITS OWN DENOMINATOR. Given two paths it printed 1 file repaired of 1 reported while both were in file_mode's list, so it silently narrowed the population it was handed rather than refusing an argument it does not take. A tool that accepts operands and acts on a subset is worse than one that refuses them. ⚑⚑ THE CONSEQUENCE IS A CLASS OF STAGED-BUT-DEFECTIVE FILES. The gate passes a NEW file at write time because the file does not exist yet and the bit cannot be wrong. It becomes wrong at creation. So every module written this session staged clean and now carries a live EXE001 that only a LATER edit to the same file surfaces — witness_polarity, dict_extent, witness_roster, imported_constant and their suites, all already staged. ⚑ AND file_mode NAMES ITS OWN BLIND SPOT IN BAND: the git INDEX mode is not read here, so a staged 100644 on a file this reader calls executable still ships. Three readers then, not two, and no single one answers the question a commit needs.}
}

@misc{gate-F95-the-sweep-scanned-three-trees-and-reported-on-the-repo,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F94-two-exe-bit-readers-disagree-on-one-population},
  claim    = {The exe-bit sweep reported clean over a corpus it never scanned, because its tree roster was frozen before the promoted package existed.},
  check    = {refuses:python3,scripts/fix_exe_bit.py,some/path.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. READ THE BINDING: _TREES was the literal tuple scripts, scratch, jea, and substrate was not in it — the package postdated the roster. The tool correctly swept three clean trees and printed no mismatches, which reads as a verdict about the repo. ⚑ Nothing in its output separated SCANNED-AND-CLEAN from NEVER-SCANNED, so it stayed authoritative while file_mode reported 21 and the gate refused edits on the same rule. ⚑⚑ The fix is not a fourth entry: _trees() now calls corpus.roots(), which discovers roots and applies the skip vocabulary at both levels. The module header already stated this rule for the FILE population — derived from ruff, never from a find — and the TREE population had escaped it. ⚑ MEASURED after: --check reports 21 matching file_mode, and post-repair file_mode reports 0. The blocked edit landed and bib_check went 21/22 to 22/22. ⚑⚑ Second defect fixed in transit: main read no positionals, so two paths printed 1 repaired of 1 reported — a full sweep reading as a narrowing. It now refuses a path operand, cased. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ⟡fix-the-exe-sweep LANDED, and the cause was a frozen tree roster rather than a broken repair. READ THE BINDING: fix_exe_bit._TREES was the literal tuple scripts, scratch, jea — and substrate was not in it. The promoted package POSTDATED the roster, so every module written there was outside the sweep's population. The tool correctly repaired three trees that happened to be clean and printed no shebang mode mismatches, which reads as a verdict about the repo. ⚑ NOTHING IN ITS OUTPUT DISTINGUISHED SCANNED-AND-CLEAN FROM NEVER-SCANNED, which is why it stayed authoritative while file_mode reported twenty-one and the per-file gate refused edits on the same rule. ⚑⚑ THE FIX IS NOT A FOURTH ENTRY. _trees() now calls corpus.roots(), which DISCOVERS top-level directories and applies the skip vocabulary at both levels, so a tree added tomorrow is swept with no edit. The module's own header already stated this rule for the FILE population — derived from ruff, never from a find — and the TREE population had escaped it. A roster beside an authority that already answers the question is the frozen-list defect this repo keeps paying for. ⚑ MEASURED AFTER: --check reports 21, matching file_mode exactly, and after the repair file_mode reports 0 disagreements. The blocked edit then landed and bib_check went 21/22 to 22/22. ⚑⚑ A SECOND DEFECT FIXED IN TRANSIT: main read no positional operands, so passing two paths printed 1 file repaired of 1 reported — a full sweep that read as a narrowing. It now REFUSES a path operand with the reason, and a case pins it.}
}

@misc{gate-F96-the-header-census-used-a-reader-blind-to-comments,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F95-the-sweep-scanned-three-trees-and-reported-on-the-repo},
  claim    = {Both the header census and its correction rest on a reader that cannot see comments, so substrate's licence-header population is UNMEASURED rather than zero.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. MEASURED: pycodemod --literal SPDX-License-Identifier scripts/ returns 0 sites in 0 of 131 files while scripts/fix_exe_bit.py line 2 IS that string. --literal searches STRING CONSTANTS and a licence header is a COMMENT, so the zero was a fact about the reader in both directions — gate-F81's claim to mtools and my correction of it. ⚑ I misread this same reader three times earlier in the session, recorded the lesson each time, then used it for a fourth textual question. ⚑⚑ No reader owns header presence: tool_find header and tool_find copyright both return nothing, and file_mode reads shebangs rather than licence lines. The number mtools needs to size migration cost does not exist yet, and building it is WORK rather than grounds for a grep. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): RETRACTION OF A RETRACTION, and the instrument was wrong both times. gate-F81 told mtools substrate has NO header population, citing pycodemod --literal SPDX-License-Identifier substrate/ returning 0 sites in 0 of 556 files. Then I read scripts/fix_exe_bit.py and found a real SPDX line plus a copyright line, and told the operator gate-F81 was imprecise — zero under substrate, not zero repo-wide. ⚑⚑ BOTH READINGS REST ON A READER THAT CANNOT ANSWER THE QUESTION. MEASURED: --literal SPDX-License-Identifier scripts/ returns 0 sites in 0 of 131 files, while scripts/fix_exe_bit.py line 2 IS that exact string. pycodemod --literal searches STRING CONSTANTS, not source text, and a licence header is a COMMENT. So the census never saw a header anywhere and could not have — the zero was a fact about the reader in both directions. ⚑ I MISREAD THIS READER THREE TIMES EARLIER IN THE SAME SESSION (searching for a source substring, for an attribute read, for a call spelling) and each time recorded it as a lesson, then used it for a fourth textual question and banked the zero as a population. A tool whose limit I had just written down. ⚑⚑ AND NO READER OWNS HEADER PRESENCE: tool_find header and tool_find copyright both return nothing, and file_mode reads shebangs rather than licence lines. So the honest state is UNMEASURED — not zero under substrate, not zero repo-wide, and not the correction I was about to send. The number mtools needs to size migration cost does not exist yet.}
