  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.file_header_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. substrate/file_header.py + suite 26/26, the missing mode gate-F96 named. Reads the PROLOGUE — comment lines before the first statement — and carries spdx and copyright separately with complete and partial derived. ⚑ The two-fact split is the design: a peer measured two repos as mirror images, one gating SPDX only and one Copyright only, each having run the arm its own config would pass. A single boolean reproduces that blind spot. ABSENT is distinguished from PARTIAL because a gate reporting them together cannot be paid down. ⚑⚑ The live case is the assertion the old reader failed: entry on scripts/fix_exe_bit.py finds both lines where --literal reported 0 of 131 in the same tree. ⚑ The false-positive arm is equally load-bearing — this module's own docstring quotes the SPDX token, so a whole-file scan would count every module documenting the rule as conforming to it. ⚑⚑ Both new files immediately reproduced gate-F94 by being written without the executable bit, which argues for a post-write hook over a periodic sweep. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): substrate/file_header.py + suite 26/26 — the missing mode gate-F96 named, built rather than routed around. It reads the PROLOGUE (comment lines before the first statement) and reports SPDX and copyright as TWO INDEPENDENT facts, with complete and partial as derived properties. ⚑ THE TWO-FACT SPLIT IS THE DESIGN, NOT ERGONOMICS. A peer measured two repos as MIRROR IMAGES — one gating SPDX only, one Copyright only, each having run the arm its own config would pass. A single has_header boolean reproduces that blind spot exactly: green on either line, unable to say which is missing. Nine cases assert the two separately, and ABSENT is distinguished from PARTIAL because a gate reporting them together cannot be paid down. ⚑⚑ THE LIVE CASE IS THE ASSERTION THE OLD READER FAILED: file_header.entry on scripts/fix_exe_bit.py finds both lines, where pycodemod --literal reported 0 of 131 for that token in the same tree. ⚑ AND THE FALSE-POSITIVE ARM MATTERS AS MUCH: file_header's own docstring QUOTES the SPDX token while explaining the convention, so a whole-file scan would count every module documenting the rule as conforming to it. A case pins that a docstring mention does not register, and another that a comment after the first statement is not a prologue. ⚑⚑ AND THE NEW FILES IMMEDIATELY REPRODUCED gate-F94: both were written without the executable bit and file_mode caught them, repaired by the now-working sweep. The class fires on every file created, which is an argument for a post-write hook rather than a periodic sweep.}
}

@misc{gate-F98-the-census-inverts-three-claims-i-made,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F97-header-presence-now-has-a-reader},
  claim    = {Substrate's promoted package is the MOST header-conforming tree in the repo, inverting three successive claims that it had no header population.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.file_header_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. The census ran: substrate 431 of 568 with BOTH lines, scripts 49 of 131, tests 13 of 14, and scratch jea el-atlas at zero. I told mtools three times that substrate had no header population — a measured zero, then zero-under-substrate, then UNMEASURED. The first was maximally wrong: the tree named as empty is nearly complete. ⚑ A reader that cannot see the subject returns the same answer as an absent subject — --literal searches string constants and a header is a comment, so it read zero for the tree at 431 too. ⚑⚑ The migration cost reported to a peer was therefore backwards: I said substrate-derived code arrives needing headers, when most of the promoted package already conforms and the bare trees are not the migration candidates. ⚑ ZERO PARTIALS across the whole corpus — no file carries one line without the other, so the mirror-image defect two peer repos measured does not occur here, visible only because the reader reports the two facts separately. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE CENSUS RAN AND IT INVERTS EVERY CLAIM I MADE ABOUT THIS. file_header.py now has a CLI and reports per-tree conformance over the corpus. substrate carries 431 of 568 files with BOTH lines — the MOST conforming tree in the repo. I told mtools three times that substrate had no header population: first as a measured zero (gate-F81), then as zero-under-substrate-not-repo-wide (retracted in gate-F96), then as UNMEASURED. The first was not merely wrong, it was maximally wrong — the tree I named as empty is the one that is nearly complete. ⚑ THE INSTRUMENT WAS BLIND AND THE CONCLUSION WAS CONFIDENT, WHICH IS THE WHOLE SHAPE. pycodemod --literal searches string constants and a licence header is a comment, so it returned zero for every tree including the ones at 431 and 49. A reader that cannot see the subject returns the same answer as a subject that is absent. ⚑⚑ AND THE MIGRATION COST I REPORTED TO A PEER WAS THEREFORE BACKWARDS. I told mtools substrate-derived code arrives needing headers, and used that to explain real intake cost. For the promoted package the opposite holds — most of it already conforms, and the trees that do not (scratch 509 of 509 bare, jea 140 of 140, el-atlas 64 of 64) are not the migration candidates. ⚑ ZERO PARTIALS ACROSS 1447 FILES: no file carries one line without the other, so the mirror-image defect two peer repos measured does not occur here. That is a real finding about substrate and it is only visible because the reader reports the two facts separately.}
}

@misc{gate-F99-the-bib-write-path-now-probes-command-checks,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F98-the-census-inverts-three-claims-i-made},
  claim    = {The bib write path now probes a command check's polarity, closing the asymmetry where the witness path guarded a hazard the durable record did not.},
  check    = {refuses:python3,scratch/bibstruct.py,--add,wire-probe-never-written,section=finding,claim=x,check=refuses:true,--bib,.claude/agents/gate-oracle.bib},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. MEASURED end to end both directions: --add with check=refuses:true is REFUSED at exit 2 naming both causes, and check=refuses:false reaches the DRY RUN diff writing nothing. bib_write_handlers 29/29 unchanged. ⚑ findings.py --register probed polarity at filing time and bibstruct --add probed nothing — validate reads duplicate key, required fields, brace balance and edge targets and never inspected what a check SAYS. One hazard, two paths, one guard, and the unguarded path writes the durable record. ⚑⚑ Two arms over two questions, not one widened test: an item check names a witness that must RESOLVE, a command check names a command whose exit code must match its kind's polarity. Each falls through for a check it does not own, so item, unwitnessed, standing and traces stay writable. ⚑ The module header was updated with the code — it explained the traces fall-through by a mechanism that has now changed. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ⟡wire-bib-check LANDED — the second arm of the check guard fires at the write path. MEASURED END TO END, both directions against the live tool: --add with check=refuses:true is REFUSED at exit 2 naming both causes (premature refusal versus census-not-predicate), and --add with check=refuses:false reaches the DRY RUN diff and writes nothing. bib_write_handlers 29/29 unchanged, so the item arm's contract survived. ⚑ THE ASYMMETRY IT CLOSES: findings.py --register probed a witness's polarity at filing time and bibstruct --add probed nothing — bib_entry.validate reads the duplicate key, required fields, brace balance and edge targets, and never inspected what a check SAYS. One hazard, two paths, one guard, and the unguarded path writes the DURABLE record. ⚑⚑ THE GUARD IS TWO ARMS OVER TWO QUESTIONS, NOT ONE WIDENED TEST. An item check names a witness that must RESOLVE, asked of the resolver. A command check names a COMMAND whose exit code must already match its kind's polarity, which no static reading of the text can establish. Each falls through for a check it does not own, so item, unwitnessed, standing and traces are all still writable. ⚑ AND THE MODULE HEADER WAS UPDATED WITH THE CODE. It said a traces check is not probed BECAUSE witness_key returns None — true, and now the reason is different: both arms fall through rather than one. A comment explaining a behaviour by a mechanism that has changed is the stale-half this repo keeps paying for.}
}

@misc{gate-G1-the-unsafe-default-is-moot-because-nothing-runs-it,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-F99-the-bib-write-path-now-probes-command-checks},
  claim    = {Substrate's fan-out defence defaults off exactly as a peer traced, and no gate invokes the reporter at all, so the defence has never run in either mode outside its suite.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. RE-RAN rather than accepted: classify takes strict=False and the suspect branch is gated behind strict, so the default returns churn for a 1-old-to-N-new fan-out — the shape the module docstring names as false absolution. Their binding read was exact. ⚑⚑ The answer to their question is neither yes nor no: module_importers substrate.churn_report finds ONE import site, its own selftest, and a literal sweep over scripts, substrate and .githooks finds only its usage string and two docstring mentions. No gate runs it. ⚑ That is worse than what they reported rather than a dismissal — a defence defaulting off is dangerous when something calls it, and a reporter nothing calls is the unwired-capability class. The strict path is unexercised outside its suite and the default path's exposure is zero for the same reason. ⚑⚑ --calls classify reports 11 defs across unrelated modules, so those 52 call sites are mostly other functions — reading it as traffic to the ratchet classifier would be census-keyed-on-one-spelling. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mtools-ce traced substrate's fan-out defence to churn_report.one(gate, strict=False) threading to ratchet_churn.classify, and asked whether any gate runs churn_report WITHOUT --strict, because that gate would be absolving fan-outs. RE-RAN IT RATHER THAN ACCEPTING THE VERDICT, and their reading of the binding is EXACT: classify takes strict: bool = False and the suspect branch is gated behind strict and rivals >= len(olds), so the default path returns churn for a 1-old-to-N-new fan-out — the shape the module's own docstring names as the false-absolution bug. ⚑⚑ BUT THE ANSWER TO THEIR QUESTION IS NEITHER YES NOR NO. module_importers substrate.churn_report reports ONE import site: its own selftest. A literal sweep over scripts, substrate and .githooks finds only its usage string and two docstring mentions. NO GATE RUNS IT AT ALL. So the unsafe default absolves nothing today, and the module is a built-then-orphaned rung rather than a live hazard. ⚑ THAT IS A WORSE FINDING THAN THE ONE THEY REPORTED, NOT A DISMISSAL OF IT. A defence defaulting off is dangerous when something calls it. A whole reporter nothing calls is the unwired-capability class, and the fan-out defence has therefore never run in production in either mode — so the strict path is unexercised outside its suite and the default path's exposure is zero for the same reason. ⚑⚑ AND --calls classify ANSWERED ABOUT A NAME, NOT A CAPABILITY: it reports 11 defs across unrelated modules (def_cycle, walk_scope, dep_addressing, reach_verdict and more), so the 52 call sites are mostly other functions entirely. Reading that census as traffic to the ratchet classifier would have been the census-keyed-on-one-spelling shape.}
}

@misc{gate-G2-the-intake-manifest-is-four-axes-and-three-are-measured,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G1-the-unsafe-default-is-moot-because-nothing-runs-it},
  claim    = {The intake manifest joins three existing readers to name 478 ship-ready modules, and a fourth axis the operator requires is measured by nothing.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/intake_manifest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Ruling: substrate's clean code moves to mtools because mtools publishes it AS PACKAGES, retiring symlink and copy vendoring across four repos, with github exposure not gated on pypi. The unit is therefore a valid DISTRIBUTION rather than whatever is clean. ⚑ Built as a JOIN of promote_frontier, file_header and adopter_import rather than a new census. MEASURED 478 of 1448: 429 substrate plus 49 scripts. ⚑⚑ Paydown order inverts the guess — no-header 952, not-promotable 820, reaches-unpackaged 69. ⚑ The conjunction is not the cheapest of the three: a promotable module can still reach into scratch at module scope, invisible to promotability because reaching a CLEAN unpackaged module violates no cleanliness property. ⚑⚑⚑ FOURTH AXIS UNMEASURED: bazel-action-per-test with a claim/test bijection is a property at CASE grain. case_count reports 191 suites with an arm roster and 54 whose total is not a literal addition — a blocker rather than a reporting gap, since N actions cannot be emitted from an unenumerable roster. ⚑ THIS ENTRY'S OWN CHECK WAS REFUSED ONCE by the guard wired this session: the comma form that protects findings.py --register from flag-dropping is read as literal argv here, so the defence created a different mangling and the probe caught it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): OPERATOR RULING: substrate's clean code moves to mtools, because mtools publishes it AS PACKAGES, which retires symlink and copy vendoring across all four repos, and github exposure does not wait on pypi. So the migration unit is whatever forms a valid DISTRIBUTION rather than whatever is clean. ⚑ BUILT THE MANIFEST AS A JOIN OF THREE EXISTING READERS RATHER THAN A NEW CENSUS: promote_frontier owns clean-with-every-transitive-callee-clean, file_header owns both-licence-lines, adopter_import owns imports-nothing-unpackaged. MEASURED: 478 of 1448 could ship today, being 429 substrate plus 49 scripts. ⚑⚑ THE PAYDOWN ORDER INVERTS THE GUESS: no-header blocks 952, not-promotable 820, reaches-unpackaged only 69. The reach-back problem I expected to dominate is the smallest of the three. ⚑ THE CONJUNCTION NEEDED ITS OWN NAME BECAUSE IT IS NOT THE CHEAPEST OF THE THREE: a module can be promotable and still reach into scratch at module scope, and that reach-back is invisible to promotability because reaching a CLEAN unpackaged module violates no cleanliness property. Publishing on the promotable count alone ships a distribution that cannot be imported. ⚑⚑⚑ AND THERE IS A FOURTH AXIS NOTHING MEASURES. The operator requires ported tests to be BAZEL-ACTION-PER-TEST with the bib a bijection (claim-per-test, test-per-claim), which is a property of a suite at CASE grain. case_count reports 191 suites declaring an arm roster and 54 whose total is not a literal addition — under per-test actions that is not a reporting gap but a blocker, since you cannot emit N actions from a roster you cannot enumerate. The render says three-of-four in band so the ready count is not over-read.}
}

@misc{gate-G3-i-proposed-repairing-the-fix,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G2-the-intake-manifest-is-four-axes-and-three-are-measured},
  claim    = {The 54 unreadable-total suites are not a repair queue — Ⓝ37j ruled the computed form better than the literal, and three suites were converted INTO it as the remedy.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. I called them port-blocking and asked whether they were one shape or 54 repairs. A transcript mine recovered today's research and it inverts that. ⚑ Witness turn 55255: cases=len(_KEEP)+len(_DROP) TRACKS ITS DATA, a literal under-reports the moment the fixture moves, and refusing the tracking form would make the census reward the shape that drifts. ⚑⚑ Three suites were deliberately converted to _CASE_COUNT = len(_ARMS) after measured incidents where added arms ran, passed and still reported the old total. ⚑ case_count is a DAMAGE MEASUREMENT not an enumerator and not a gate — its docstring says it reports and does not refuse, and below-floor is its only hard verdict because an arm returns a failure count. I read an instrument's honest refusal as a defect census. ⚑⚑ The one open question is narrower: family_roster was widened to an enumerated len() grammar and case_count._int_of was not. Whether case_count adopts it is bounded and unsettled. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I told the operator the 54 unreadable-total suites were PORT-BLOCKING and asked whether they were one shape or 54 repairs. The operator pointed out we researched this today. A transcript mine recovered it and THE CONCLUSION INVERTS MY PROPOSAL. ⚑⚑⚑ Ⓝ37j RULED THE COMPUTED FORM BETTER THAN THE LITERAL IT WOULD BE REPAIRED TO. Witness turn 55255: option 2 is backwards, cases=len(_KEEP)+len(_DROP) TRACKS ITS DATA — add a path to _DROP and the count grows with the assertions the arm actually makes, while a literal 9 under-reports from the moment the fixture moves. Refusing the tracking form would make the census reward the shape that drifts. ⚑⚑ AND THREE SUITES WERE DELIBERATELY CONVERTED INTO THE UNREADABLE FORM AS THE REMEDY: _CASE_COUNT = len(_ARMS) landed in md_spans_selftest, md_write_selftest and mode_coverage_selftest after measured incidents where two added arms ran, passed, and reported 6/6. So the 54-row list is not a work queue and treating it as one runs the arc backwards. ⚑ WHAT case_count ACTUALLY IS: a DAMAGE MEASUREMENT, not an arm-roster enumerator and not a gate — its own docstring says it reports and does not refuse, and its only hard verdict is below-floor because an arm returns a failure count so its assertion count is not statically decidable. I read an instrument's honest refusal as a defect census. ⚑⚑ THE ONE GENUINELY OPEN QUESTION IS NARROWER THAN MY PROPOSAL: family_roster was WIDENED to admit an enumerated len() grammar and case_count._int_of was NOT, deliberately or otherwise. Whether case_count should adopt that grammar is one bounded question, unsettled, and is not a re-survey.}
}

@misc{gate-G4-action-per-test-and-portable-are-different-questions,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G3-i-proposed-repairing-the-fix},
  claim    = {arm_shape measures pytest-portability, not action-addressability, so reading it as the action-per-test blocker would over-block by 703 arms.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/arm_shape.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. arm_shape reports 1909 arms over 189 suites: mechanical 1206, multi-assert 615, raises-check 88. ⚑ READ THE BINDING: Arm.mechanical is appends <= 1 and not raises_check, and its docstring says it reports whether a SINGLE-ASSERT REWRITE preserves every assertion — portability to one pytest assert. Action-per-test asks whether an arm is addressable as its own action, and a 2-append arm is one addressable arm carrying two facts. ⚑⚑ So the 615 are blocked from the BIJECTION half, not the action half: claim-per-test needs one claim per arm, and a two-fact arm needs two claims or a split. The 88 raises-check arms are harder because a try/except arm has no single failure branch to name. ⚑ Nothing joins the sides — arm_shape owns the arm roster, the bibs own the claims, and no reader computes the bijection. One reader, not a survey. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE FOURTH INTAKE AXIS IS PARTLY MEASURED ALREADY, by a reader the transcript mine surfaced. arm_shape reports 1909 arms across 189 suites, split mechanical 1206, multi-assert 615, raises-check 88. ⚑⚑ BUT IT ANSWERS A NARROWER QUESTION THAN BAZEL-ACTION-PER-TEST NEEDS, and reading it as the answer would have over-blocked by 703 arms. READ THE BINDING: Arm.mechanical returns appends <= 1 and not raises_check, and its docstring says it reports whether a SINGLE-ASSERT REWRITE preserves every assertion. That is portability to one pytest assert. Action-per-test asks whether an arm is ADDRESSABLE AS ITS OWN ACTION, and a 2-append arm is one addressable arm carrying two facts — not blocked from being an action at all. ⚑ SO THE 615 MULTI-ASSERT ARMS ARE BLOCKED FROM THE BIJECTION HALF, NOT THE ACTION HALF. claim-per-test and test-per-claim require one claim per arm, and an arm asserting two facts either needs two claims or needs splitting. The 88 raises-check arms are the harder population because a try/except arm has no single failure branch to name. ⚑⚑ AND NOTHING JOINS THE TWO SIDES. arm_shape owns the arm roster, the bibs own the claims, and no reader computes the bijection — so the fourth axis has a measured LEFT side and an unmeasured relation. That is the gap, and it is one reader rather than a survey.}
}

@misc{gate-G5-the-arms-are-already-the-claims,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G4-action-per-test-and-portable-are-different-questions},
  claim    = {The bijection is not a join over the two current populations, because the arm names are already the per-test claims and the bib holds tree-level properties instead.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. MEASURED both sides. bibstruct --field claim over the worklist returns 67 entries and every one is a TREE-LEVEL property witnessed by a whole suite or census — T21 on n-of-m reporting, A5-using on import filters, bz-warrants on audit shape. ⚑⚑ The arm names are already claim-shaped prose: a_step_that_refuses_propagates_rather_than_being_swallowed, an_unknown_target_is_refused_by_name. Each names one falsifiable fact in a claim's voice. 1909 arms against 67 entries — the populations differ by a factor a join would have to invent, and it would report 1842 unmatched arms and 67 unmatched claims as artifacts of the framing. ⚑ Under bazel-action-per-test the arm name becomes the action label AND the claim, and the bib's role shifts to the tree-level properties those actions roll up to. ⚑⚑ Nearly built against the wrong reader: suite_claims is MEMBUDGET claim tags, not bib claims — two arcs one word, caught by reading the interface rather than the name. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ⟡the-bijection-reader IS MISFRAMED, and reading both sides settles it in one turn. I planned a reader joining arm_shape's 1909 arms against bib claims to compute claim-per-test and test-per-claim. THE TWO SIDES ARE NOT THE SAME KIND OF THING. bibstruct --field claim over the worklist returns 67 entries and every one is a TREE-LEVEL property — T21 every population-visiting tool reports n of m, A5-using no Substrate import carries a using filter, bz-warrants audit-as-warrants beats audit-as-prose. Those are witnessed by a whole suite or a census, not one per arm. ⚑⚑ MEANWHILE THE ARM NAMES ARE ALREADY CLAIM-SHAPED PROSE: a_step_that_refuses_propagates_rather_than_being_swallowed, an_unknown_target_is_refused_by_name, the_render_states_its_blind_spots. Each names one falsifiable fact in the voice a claim uses. THE ARMS ARE THE CLAIMS ALREADY — at 1909 of them against 67 bib entries, the two populations differ by a factor the join would have had to invent. ⚑ SO claim-per-test AND test-per-claim ARE NOT A JOIN OVER EXISTING DATA. Under bazel-action-per-test the arm name becomes the action label and the claim it asserts, and the bib's role changes from holding per-test claims to holding the tree-level properties those actions roll up to. A reader computing a bijection between the current two populations would report 1842 unmatched arms and 67 unmatched claims and both numbers would be artifacts of the framing. ⚑⚑ AND I NEARLY BUILT IT AGAINST THE WRONG READER TWICE: suite_claims is about MEMBUDGET claim tags, not bib claims — two arcs one word, caught by reading its interface rather than its name.}
}

@misc{gate-G6-an-append-is-a-failure-branch-not-a-claim,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G5-the-arms-are-already-the-claims},
  claim    = {The 615 multi-append arms are not bijection violations, because an append is a failure branch and exclusive branches report one claim several ways.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/arm_shape_selftest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. READ THE BINDING: _appends counts out.append calls, and an append is a FAILURE REPORT not an assertion. ⚑⚑ The canonical case is an if-elif — arm_shape_selftest.a_multi_append_arm_is_not_mechanical appends once if the arm was called mechanical and once ELIF the count read wrong, so only one branch can fire. One claim, two messages, and its docstring calls it the POSITIVE CONTROL for a single property. ⚑ So N appends in exclusive branches is one claim with N messages, and N appends in sequence is N claims. arm_shape cannot separate them because it counts calls rather than reading control flow — and its mechanical property does not try to, being scoped to whether a single-assert rewrite preserves every assertion, a different question a conservative over-count answers correctly. ⚑⚑ The reader the fourth axis needs is EXCLUSIVE-VERSUS-SEQUENTIAL appends, one predicate over the arm body. Third reframing of this axis in two ticks, each found by reading a binding rather than reasoning about a count. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ⟡split-the-615 IS ALSO MISFRAMED, and one arm settles it. I carried 615 multi-assert arms as blocked from the claim-per-test bijection on the reasoning that a two-append arm asserts two facts and so needs two claims. READ THE BINDING: _appends counts out.append calls, and an append is a FAILURE REPORT rather than an assertion. ⚑⚑ THE CANONICAL CASE IS AN IF-ELIF. arm_shape_selftest.a_multi_append_arm_is_not_mechanical appends once if the arm was called mechanical and once ELIF the append count read wrong — only one branch can ever fire. That is ONE claim reported two ways, not two claims. Its own docstring calls it the POSITIVE CONTROL for a single property. ⚑ SO THE 615 ARE NOT 615 BIJECTION VIOLATIONS. An arm with N appends in exclusive branches is one claim with N failure messages, and an arm with N appends in sequence is N claims. arm_shape cannot tell those apart because it counts calls rather than reading control flow, and its mechanical property does not try to — its docstring scopes it to whether a SINGLE-ASSERT REWRITE preserves every assertion, which is a different question that a conservative over-count answers correctly. ⚑⚑ THE READER THE FOURTH AXIS ACTUALLY NEEDS IS EXCLUSIVE-VERSUS-SEQUENTIAL APPENDS, not a join and not a split. That is one predicate over the arm body, and it is the third reframing of this axis in two ticks — each one found by reading a binding rather than by reasoning about a count.}
}

@misc{gate-G7-the-mechanism-was-right-and-the-magnitude-was-wrong,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G6-an-append-is-a-failure-branch-not-a-claim},
  claim    = {Reading control flow confirms an append is a failure branch and refutes the implication that the multi-claim population would shrink.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.arm_claims_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. substrate/arm_claims.py plus suite 22/22 counts CLAIMS: exclusive if-elif branches contribute one between them, sequential appends one each, a loop body one over a population. ⚑⚑ The live case holds exactly as gate-G6 argued — the motivating arm reads 2 appends and 1 claim. But the corpus census reports 616 several-claim arms against arm_shape's 615 multi-append, so the exclusive-branch shape is REAL AND RARE and the population did not shrink. ⚑ It rose by one, because a raises-check arm outside arm_shape's multi bucket carries two sequential claims — the readers disagree per-arm in both directions while agreeing in aggregate, which is why the differential case asserts the per-arm relation rather than comparing totals. ⚑⚑ The value is not the 616 but that 1277 arms are KNOWN-single rather than assumed-single, with 5 UNDECIDED reported as a fact about the reader rather than counted as satisfied. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): BUILT THE READER gate-G6 CALLED FOR, AND IT REFUTES gate-G6'S IMPLICATION. substrate/arm_claims.py plus suite 22/22 counts CLAIMS rather than appends: exclusive if-elif branches contribute one claim between them, sequential appends contribute one each, a loop body is one claim over a population. ⚑⚑ THE MECHANISM IS CONFIRMED AND THE MAGNITUDE IS NOT. The live case holds — arm_shape_selftest.a_multi_append_arm_is_not_mechanical reads 2 appends and 1 claim, exactly as gate-G6 argued. But over the corpus the claim census reports 616 several-claim arms against arm_shape's 615 multi-append, so the exclusive-branch shape is REAL AND RARE. I implied the 615 would shrink materially once control flow was read. It did not. ⚑ THE COUNT EVEN WENT UP BY ONE, because a raises-check arm that arm_shape classified out of its multi bucket carries two sequential claims. The two readers disagree per-arm in both directions while agreeing almost exactly in aggregate — which is why a differential case asserts the per-arm relation (a claim count never EXCEEDS an append count) rather than comparing totals. ⚑⚑ SO THE BIJECTION WORK IS REAL AND ROUGHLY THE SIZE FIRST ESTIMATED, arrived at by a reader that can defend the number rather than by a proxy. THE VALUE IS NOT THE 616, IT IS THAT 1277 ARMS ARE NOW KNOWN-SINGLE rather than assumed-single, plus 5 UNDECIDED reported as a fact about the reader instead of silently counted as satisfied.}
