  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. MEASURED: --headers returned 15 headings with SB-08 absent and SB-07 spanning 158-194, swallowing it whole, while --budget on the same file reported 16 headings including SB-08 at 969 bytes. Renaming the heading to drop the apostrophe restored it and corrected SB-07 to 158-180. ⚑⚑ Cross-repo and silent: the brief names §Q-8 what-you-re-derived THE HIGHEST-VALUE ITEM IN THE WHOLE CENSUS, and an apex reading the leg with --headers would have seen a predecessor that simply reads longer. A missing section does not announce itself. ⚑ Caught only because the momentum prompt carries the gate-F82 warning and I cross-checked with --budget, which does not use the anchoring path. A party without that warning files a leg with a hole in it. ⚑⚑ The repair is a workaround on the ARTIFACT not the tool — mdstruct still carries the defect and still cannot be edited behind the all-or-nothing gate. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): FILED THE deps-build LEG AND THE APOSTROPHE DEFECT FIRED ON IT IMMEDIATELY. gate-F82 records that mdstruct --headers loses every section from the first apostrophe-bearing heading onward because _hkey does not strip the apostrophe pandoc rewrites to U+2019. My SB-08 heading read What I re-derived — the census's own highest-value item. MEASURED: --headers returned 15 headings with SB-08 absent and SB-07's span reading 158-194, swallowing SB-08 whole, while --budget on the same file reported 16 headings including SB-08 at 969 bytes. Renaming to remove the apostrophe restored it and corrected SB-07's span to 158-180. ⚑⚑ THE CONSEQUENCE IS CROSS-REPO AND WOULD HAVE BEEN SILENT. The census brief names §Q-8 what-you-re-derived as THE HIGHEST-VALUE ITEM IN THE WHOLE CENSUS, and any peer or apex reading my leg with --headers would have seen SB-07 run to line 194 with no gap and no error. A missing section does not announce itself — the predecessor simply reads longer. ⚑ I CAUGHT IT ONLY BECAUSE THE MOMENTUM PROMPT CARRIES THE WARNING and I cross-checked with --budget, which does not use the anchoring path. A party without that warning files a leg with a hole in it. ⚑⚑ THE REPAIR I APPLIED IS A WORKAROUND ON THE ARTIFACT, NOT THE TOOL: I renamed my heading. mdstruct still carries the defect, still cannot be edited (9 mypy codes behind an all-or-nothing gate), and every other markdown artifact in this repo and in mtools remains exposed.}
}

@misc{gate-G9-the-replacement-normaliser-is-built,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G8-the-heading-defect-fired-on-my-own-census-leg},
  claim    = {The heading normaliser is rebuilt clean by transformation kind rather than by character, so every pandoc rewrite folds and not just the apostrophe.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.md_hkey_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. substrate/md_hkey.py plus suite 18/18. The origin folds three markup characters and no punctuation, so an ASCII apostrophe never equals pandoc's U+2019, the forward cursor never advances, and the previous span swallows the heading. ⚑ Folding only the apostrophe would repeat the origin's mistake one character over — it fixed the markup case it had seen. SMART carries all seven smart-typography substitutions. ⚑⚑ Order is cased: smart folds precede markup strips because an em dash folds to two hyphens, and whitespace collapses last. ⚑ The negative arm is what makes the suite worth anything — a constant-returning normaliser satisfies every equality case, so four cases assert discrimination including that em and en dashes do NOT fold together. ⚑⚑⚑ The gate refused the literal characters as ambiguous with ASCII, which is this file's own subject — now named unicode escapes, identical bytes and explicit codepoints. NOT WIRED: mdstruct's nine mypy codes block the two call sites. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓑ0 ⟡fix-mdstruct-blindness — THE REPLACEMENT IS BUILT. substrate/md_hkey.py plus suite 18/18. The origin folds three markup characters and no punctuation, so an ASCII apostrophe never compares equal to pandoc's U+2019 rendering, the forward cursor never advances, and the previous section's span silently swallows the heading. ⚑⚑ THE FIX IS BY TRANSFORMATION KIND, NOT BY CHARACTER. Folding only the apostrophe would be the origin's own mistake one character over — it fixed the markup case it had seen and left every other rewrite. So SMART carries all seven of pandoc's smart-typography substitutions: both single quotes, both double quotes, em dash, en dash, ellipsis, each folded to the ASCII an author types. ⚑ THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING AND CASED: smart folds run BEFORE markup strips, because an em dash folds to two hyphens and a later strip must not treat them as markup. Whitespace collapses last so a fold introducing a space cannot leave two. ⚑⚑ THE NEGATIVE ARM IS WHAT MAKES THE SUITE WORTH ANYTHING — a normaliser returning a constant satisfies every equality case, so four cases assert it still DISCRIMINATES, including that an em dash and an en dash do NOT fold together. ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE GATE REFUSED THE LITERALS AS AMBIGUOUS WITH ASCII, WHICH IS THE DEFECT'S OWN SUBJECT. RUF001 is right in general and pointed at the one file where that ambiguity is the topic. The table now uses named unicode escapes — identical bytes, explicit codepoints, and better for an auditing reader anyway. NOT YET WIRED: mdstruct carries nine mypy codes behind the all-or-nothing gate, so its two call sites cannot import this until that file can be written.}
}

@misc{gate-G10-a-code-count-is-not-an-error-count,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G9-the-replacement-normaliser-is-built},
  claim    = {A pycodemod --types code count is not an error count and is measured over the import closure, so ranking blocked files by it compares units that share no scale.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. I called mdstruct the smallest blocked monolith on nine keys across nine error codes. ⚑⚑ Running mypy directly returns errors on essentially every function — hundreds under those nine codes, because disallow_any_expr makes one untyped def poison every downstream expression. --types answers WHICH KINDS, not HOW MANY. ⚑ And the first run compounded it: without --follow-imports=skip mypy reported errors in scratch/vfs.py rather than mdstruct at all, because mypy FOLLOWS IMPORTS — so --types on a file is a fact about its CLOSURE. Same shape pyproject already records, where admitting one hook pulled in three unannotated modules and produced hundreds of Any rows belonging to unpromoted files. ⚑⚑⚑ Consequence for the queue: findings.py at 8169 RUFF findings and mdstruct at nine MYPY codes are one ruff count and one code count from different instruments, not two ends of a spectrum. Any leverage ordering over those numbers sorted on units with no shared scale. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ⟡swap-the-normaliser IS BLOCKED, and getting there corrected how I have been reading the blocker all session. I called mdstruct THE SMALLEST BLOCKED MONOLITH on the strength of pycodemod --types reporting NINE KEYS ACROSS NINE ERROR CODES, and read nine as small. ⚑⚑ A CODE COUNT IS NOT AN ERROR COUNT. Running mypy directly returns errors on essentially every function in the file — hundreds of them under nine distinct codes, since disallow_any_expr makes one untyped def poison every expression downstream of it. Nine codes over hundreds of errors is a monolith, not a near-clean file, and --types answers WHICH KINDS rather than HOW MANY. ⚑ AND THE FIRST RUN COMPOUNDED IT: mypy without --follow-imports=skip reported errors in scratch/vfs.py rather than mdstruct at all, because mypy FOLLOWS IMPORTS and the closure is part of the check. So --types on a file is a fact about its CLOSURE, which is the same shape as pyproject's own note that admitting one hook pulled in three unannotated modules and produced hundreds of Any rows belonging to files nobody had promoted. ⚑⚑⚑ THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE QUEUE IS THAT I HAVE BEEN RANKING BY THE WRONG FIGURE. findings.py at 8169 ruff findings and mdstruct at nine mypy codes are not two ends of a spectrum — they are one ruff count and one code count, measured by different instruments, and neither is comparable to the other. Any leverage ordering that sorted blocked files by these numbers was sorting on units that do not share a scale.}
}

@misc{gate-G11-the-five-undecided-were-thirty-seven,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G10-a-code-count-is-not-an-error-count},
  claim    = {The five UNDECIDED arms were the visible tip of a thirty-seven-arm miscount, because the claim counter walked a try statement's body and none of its other branches.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.arm_claims_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. READ THE BINDING: _claims_of's Try arm walked only stmt.body — never handlers, orelse or finalbody — so a raises-check arm appending in its handler found no appends and reported UNDECIDED. ⚑⚑ Widening it (body plus handlers plus orelse as an EXCLUSIVE chain taking the maximum, finalbody ADDITIVE because it always runs) moved UNDECIDED 5 to 0, several-claim 616 to 653, one-claim 1277 to 1245. The five were arms whose appends were ENTIRELY inside a try. The other thirty-two had appends inside and outside, so they got a plausible undercount rather than an honest refusal. ⚑⚑⚑ The undecided rows were the visible tip of a silent miscount, which is the argument for having a third outcome at all — an arm reporting 1 when the answer is 2 is the same gap wearing a number, and only the loud five made the quiet thirty-two findable. ⚑ My prior report of 1277 known-single was wrong by thirty-two, overstating readiness. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ⟡the-5-undecided IS CLOSED, and the population was never five. READ THE BINDING on the first two: both append inside a try/except, and _claims_of's Try arm walked ONLY stmt.body — never handlers, never orelse, never finalbody. So for a raises-check arm it found no appends at all and reported UNDECIDED. ⚑⚑ THE REPAIR MOVED THIRTY-SEVEN ARMS, NOT FIVE. After widening the arm to treat body plus each handler plus orelse as an EXCLUSIVE CHAIN (exactly one path runs, so take the maximum) and finalbody as ADDITIVE (it always runs, so it sums), the census reads UNDECIDED 5 to 0, several-claim 616 to 653, and one-claim 1277 to 1245. The five were the arms whose appends were ENTIRELY inside a try — the other thirty-two had appends both inside and outside, so they got a plausible-looking undercount instead of an honest refusal. ⚑⚑⚑ THE UNDECIDED ROWS WERE THE VISIBLE TIP OF A SILENT MISCOUNT, WHICH IS THE ARGUMENT FOR HAVING THEM. An arm reporting UNDECIDED is a reader admitting a gap. An arm reporting 1 when the answer is 2 is the same gap wearing a number, and only the five loud ones made the thirty-two findable. A reader without a third outcome would have shipped 1277 known-single with 32 of them wrong and nothing to look at. ⚑ AND MY PRIOR REPORT OF 1277 KNOWN-SINGLE WAS THEREFORE WRONG BY THIRTY-TWO, in the direction that overstates readiness.}
}

@misc{gate-G12-the-fourth-axis-does-not-join,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G11-the-five-undecided-were-thirty-seven},
  claim    = {The fourth intake axis is measured but cannot become a fourth column, because arm_claims reports over suites and the manifest reports over modules.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/intake_manifest.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. The manifest printed THREE AXES OF FOUR and NOTHING MEASURES THEM. arm_claims was built two ticks later, so the caveat was false and I went to reword it. ⚑⚑ Reading the binding showed the JOIN is the wrong shape rather than merely unbuilt: manifest() emits one row per MODULE over 1452, arm_claims one row per ARM over 189 suites, so most modules have no suite and a fourth column would invent a verdict for each. That is the over-glue census-kit names — an identification with no witness, against rows that cannot object. ⚑ The banner now says the axis IS measured, by a named reader, over a DIFFERENT population, and that a ready module is SILENT on it rather than passing — two facts a merged column collapses. ⚑⚑ A second undecided decision surfaced: whether one multi-claim arm blocks its whole module. A roll-up presumes that answer, so two censuses side by side is the honest report. ⚑ Docstring and banner carried the same stale claim and were fixed together. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ⟡manifest-carries-a-stale-caveat — CORRECTED, AND THE CORRECTION IS NOT THE ONE I SET OUT TO MAKE. The intake manifest printed THREE AXES OF FOUR — bazel-action-per-test and the claim-test bijection are a property of a module's SUITE at CASE grain, and NOTHING MEASURES THEM. I built arm_claims two ticks later, so the caveat was false and I went to reword it. ⚑⚑ READING THE BINDING SHOWED THE JOIN IS THE WRONG SHAPE, NOT MERELY UNBUILT. manifest() emits one Module row per MODULE over the corpus. arm_claims reports one row per ARM over 189 suites, against 1452 modules — so most modules have NO SUITE AT ALL and a fourth column would have to invent a verdict for every one of them. That invention is the over-glue the census-kit skill names: an identification with no witness, performed against rows that cannot object. ⚑ SO THE HONEST CORRECTION IS THE OPPOSITE OF A FOURTH COLUMN. The banner now says the fourth axis IS measured, by a named reader, over a DIFFERENT POPULATION — and that a module counted ready is SILENT on it rather than passing it. Silent and passing are the two facts a merged column would have collapsed. ⚑⚑ AND A SECOND DECISION SURFACED THAT NOBODY HAS MADE: whether one multi-claim arm blocks its whole module. A roll-up presumes that answer. Until it is ruled on, two censuses side by side is the honest report. ⚑ THE DOCSTRING CARRIED THE SAME STALE CLAIM AS THE BANNER and both were fixed together — a corrected banner over a stale docstring is the half-repair this repo keeps recording.}
}

@misc{gate-G13-the-drift-floor-was-a-different-ledger,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G12-the-fourth-axis-does-not-join},
  claim    = {A steady drift figure over a multi-ledger census cannot distinguish new work pairing cleanly from old work in another ledger being paid nothing down.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Drift moved 32 to 30 for the first time this session, and the reason it had not moved is the finding. Every filing this session went to gate-oracle.bib and every one paired, so --pairing read a steady 32 tick after tick and I read that as MY work being clean. It was — and the residual is almost entirely agda-oracle and sql-oracle rows nothing I was doing touched. ⚑⚑ The census reports ONE number over SIX ledgers, so a constant reading collapses holding-the-line and paying-nothing-down, and I took the former reading for a dozen ticks. ⚑ The rows are substantive, confirmed by reading two rather than assuming: agda-O4 records that a Bash-only subagent cannot create a file, a constraint that bound two dispatches this session on the parent side and is therefore not agda-specific. agda-Q2 records DivStr's law-free z reading as the wedge terminal at one layer and the multiplicative zero at the next, so a zero-free carrier typechecks while its nilpotency notions are nonsense — and it WATCHES GradedDivStr, going red if a z appears there. ⚑⚑ Neither was junk and both had sat unpaired. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ⟡pair-the-agda-witnesses — DRIFT MOVED FOR THE FIRST TIME THIS SESSION, 32 to 30, and the reason it had not moved is the finding. Every pairing I filed all session went into gate-oracle.bib, and every filing paired cleanly, so --pairing read a steady 32 tick after tick. I took that steadiness as MY work being clean. It was: the residual 32 is almost entirely agda-oracle and sql-oracle rows that NOTHING I WAS DOING TOUCHED. ⚑⚑ A CONSTANT DRIFT FIGURE OVER A MULTI-LEDGER CENSUS IS TWO FACTS COLLAPSED — new work pairing, and old work in another ledger not being paid down. The census reports one number over six ledgers, so a steady reading cannot distinguish holding-the-line from paying-nothing-down, and I read it as the former for a dozen ticks. ⚑ AND THE ROWS ARE SUBSTANTIVE, WHICH I CONFIRMED BY READING TWO RATHER THAN ASSUMING. agda-O4 records that a Bash-only subagent cannot create a file — the no-chaining hook refuses heredoc, -c and printf redirection while --body-file needs a file it cannot make. That constraint bound TWO dispatches this session on the parent side, so it is not agda-specific at all. agda-Q2 records DivStr's law-free z reading as the wedge terminal at one layer and the multiplicative zero one layer up, so a zero-free carrier typechecks while its nilpotency notions are nonsense — and it WATCHES GradedDivStr as the escape, going red if a z ever appears there. ⚑⚑ NEITHER WAS JUNK AND BOTH HAD SAT UNPAIRED, which is the argument for paying a drift floor down rather than treating it as background.}
}

@misc{gate-G14-the-run-file-now-carries-leg-content,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G13-the-drift-floor-was-a-different-ledger},
  claim    = {Making the run file the freeze instrument made it mandatory to read, and it now carries other legs' findings, so a leg cannot poll for the freeze without breaching independence.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. VERIFIED from the artifact rather than the peer's summary: the run file grew 141 to 546 lines and --budget confirms §G, §S marked NOT YET CALLED, §M and §N. §G adopts the artifact form over a locally-evaluable condition for the stated reason — a six-files-present test produces no accounting artifact while §6 demands every party marked filed/declined/no-response before the apex. ⚑⚑ But reading §G required opening the run file, and §T now quotes other legs at length: linux-sources' 407-second verdict action against cassian's 0.36-second critical path, cassian's formulation verbatim, cassian's declined capability and its reasoning. The section is titled by its conclusion, not its provenance, so it is read before it can be identified. ⚑ The embargo forbids reading another LEG and says nothing about the BRIEF, so folding leg content into the brief turns the one artifact every leg MUST poll into the channel the embargo exists to close. Independence becomes unenforceable by the leg. ⚑⚑⚑ Same shape as the defect §G fixed — a control-flow artifact acquiring content the control flow did not anticipate. Raised rather than resolved: whether that content is testimony or contamination is the dispatcher's ruling. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE FREEZE MECHANISM IS REPAIRED AND VERIFIED FROM THE ARTIFACT, AND THE REPAIR OPENED A SECOND HOLE. VERIFIED rather than taken on report: the run file grew from 141 lines to 546, and mdstruct --budget confirms §G (the freeze is a row in §V, poll the file), §S (marked NOT YET CALLED), §M and §N all present. §G credits the raise and adopts the artifact form over a locally-evaluable condition for the reason substrate gave — a six-files-present test produces no accounting artifact while §6 requires every party marked filed slash declined slash no-response before the apex begins. Embargo holds, confirmed from §S rather than from a peer's summary of it. ⚑⚑ BUT READING §G REQUIRED OPENING THE RUN FILE, AND THE RUN FILE NOW QUOTES OTHER LEGS' FINDINGS AT LENGTH. §T carries linux-sources' 407-second verdict action against cassian's 0.36-second critical path, cassian's formulation quoted verbatim, and cassian's declined capability with its reasoning. I read it before I could tell what it was — the section is titled by its conclusion, not by its provenance. ⚑ THE EMBARGO SAYS DO NOT READ ANOTHER LEG AND SAYS NOTHING ABOUT THE BRIEF, so a dispatcher who folds leg content into the brief makes the one artifact every leg MUST poll into a channel the embargo was written to close. Independence is now unenforceable by the leg: I cannot poll for the freeze without reading whatever else has been added. ⚑⚑⚑ AND THIS IS THE SAME SHAPE AS THE DEFECT §G FIXED — a control-flow artifact acquiring content the control flow did not anticipate. The fix made the run file mandatory to read, which is exactly what makes putting findings in it costly. Raised rather than resolved: whether that content is testimony a leg may hold, or contamination, is the dispatcher's ruling and not mine.}
}

@misc{gate-G15-an-unpaired-witness-is-two-different-artefacts,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G14-the-run-file-now-carries-leg-content},
  claim    = {An unpaired witness is two different artefacts — an instrument gap waiting on a fixture, or a completed measurement whose conclusion is that the assigned work was misframed.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Second consecutive paydown, drift 30 to 28, and the two rows differ in KIND. ⚑ agda-F3 is an INSTRUMENT GAP honestly kinded unwitnessed: the diagnose path cannot separate exited-0-because-clean from exited-0-on-a-stale-log, because nothing constructs a tree with known failures AND a stale log at once. Two worlds, one byte. Its kind IS the finding — a falsifiable kind would need the fixture built first. ⚑⚑ sql-F27 is the opposite: a MEASURED inversion of a prior verdict. Handed the-repair-is-declaring-the-tier, reading schema_decl inverted it — the four relations are convenience VIEWS the declaration refuses by construction, since view-purge deleted the emit and kept the reason unflagged so a future reader could not turn it back on. And the owed work is not what was handed: nine base tables are admissible but two already exist in another tier, so the tier as written collides and nobody has adjudicated it. ⚑⚑⚑ Paying these down in bulk would flatten both into rows-cleared, and the second is a live blocker carrying a prerequisite decision for an operator. ⚑ The ledger is chosen by SUBJECT not by filer: agda-oracle.bib and sql-oracle.bib, not the gate ledger every filing of mine this session has used. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): SECOND CONSECUTIVE DRIFT PAYDOWN, 30 to 28, and the two rows are different KINDS of thing — which is the finding rather than the count. ⚑ agda-F3 is an INSTRUMENT GAP, honestly kinded unwitnessed: the diagnose path cannot separate exited-0-because-clean from exited-0-on-a-stale-log, because no command constructs a tree with known failures AND a stale log at once. The passing exit code is produced by two different worlds and the byte is identical, so a reader banking that zero banks whichever world it is in. Its kind IS the finding — converting it to a falsifiable kind would require building the fixture first. ⚑⚑ sql-F27 is the OPPOSITE: a fully MEASURED inversion of a prior round's verdict. It was handed the-repair-is-declaring-the-tier and reading schema_decl inverted it — the four relations are convenience VIEWS that the declaration refuses BY CONSTRUCTION, since view-purge deleted the emit and kept the reason unflagged precisely so a future reader could not turn it back on. And the owed work is not what was handed: nine base tables are admissible but two of them ALREADY EXIST in another tier, so the tier as written collides and nobody has adjudicated it. ⚑⚑⚑ SO AN UNPAIRED WITNESS IS NOT ONE BACKLOG. One is a gap waiting on a fixture nobody can build today. The other is a completed measurement whose CONCLUSION is that the assigned work was misframed — carrying a prerequisite decision for an operator. Paying them down in bulk would have flattened both into rows-cleared, and the second one is a live blocker somebody needs to see. ⚑ AND THE LEDGER IS CHOSEN BY SUBJECT, NOT BY WHO FILES: agda-F3 went to agda-oracle.bib and sql-F27 to sql-oracle.bib, not to gate-oracle.bib where every filing of mine this session has landed.}
}

@misc{gate-G16-a-truncation-that-cuts-the-wrong-two-thirds-looks-complete,
