E the cut. The truncation removed two of six and READ AS COMPLETE. Caught only by re-running --budget afterward. ⚑ A TRUNCATION THAT CUTS THE WRONG TWO-THIRDS IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM ONE THAT WORKS, because both produce a shorter file that parses. That is the swallowed-heading defect one level up: the artifact does not announce what is missing, and the operation looks successful from its own output. ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE REPAIR WAS TO SELECT BY NAME WITH AN ASSERTION THAT EVERY CONTROL SECTION SURVIVES — a positive control over the population rather than a boundary guess. Same move as a census stating its denominator: the cut is verified by what REMAINS being enumerated, not by the cut looking clean. ⚑ NOTED WITHOUT CLAIMING IT: they report the --budget cross-check is now load-bearing in their own workflow and is what caught the bad cut. That is their measurement, not mine.}
}

@misc{gate-G17-a-known-good-member-is-what-makes-an-abort-visible,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G16-a-truncation-that-cuts-the-wrong-two-thirds-looks-complete},
  claim    = {Without a known-good member in the set, an early abort and a total failure produce the same output, so the passing case is what makes the measurement readable.},
