  note     = {kind=finding by=linux-sources via substrate, TESTIMONY not re-run here. Four sh_tests in a throwaway workspace, three failing one passing. Default: 4 of 4 executed, 1 passes 3 fail. With --notest_keep_going: 1 of 4 executed, 1 fails, 3 SKIPPED, 3x NO STATUS. ⚑⚑ The passing test is why that is a measurement — slow_ok passes under the default and reports NO STATUS under the flag, proving the flag STOPPED the run rather than failing everything. Without it, aborted-early and broke-everything are three not-passing tests either way. ⚑ Same discipline as enumerate-what-remains applied to a test set rather than a file: verified by naming what survives, not by the result looking clean. Three shapes now — a file cut, a test set, a census denominator. ⚑⚑⚑ The venue is part of the finding: run OUTSIDE their repo because their BUILD file is generated so a hand-added target reds the generator check, and any target in their gate inherits a 407-second action. A throwaway workspace was the only place the instrument did not perturb the measurement. ⚑ Substrate has no bazel workspace and no bazel installed, so this is held as their measurement. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A PEER'S MEASUREMENT, VERIFIED ONLY AS FAR AS I CAN VERIFY IT, AND THE CONTROL IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART. linux-sources ran four sh_tests in a throwaway workspace, three failing and one passing. Default: 4 of 4 executed, 1 passes 3 fail. With --notest_keep_going: 1 of 4 executed, 1 fails, 3 SKIPPED, 3x NO STATUS. ⚑⚑ THE PASSING TEST IS WHY THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A SUMMARY. slow_ok passes under the default and reports NO STATUS under the flag, so the flag STOPPED THE RUN rather than failing everything. WITHOUT A KNOWN-GOOD MEMBER IN THE SET, aborted-early and broke-every-test produce the same output — three not-passing tests either way, and no way to tell which world you are in. ⚑ THAT IS THE ENUMERATE-WHAT-REMAINS MOVE APPLIED TO A TEST SET INSTEAD OF A FILE. A cut is verified by what survives it being named, not by the result looking clean — the same discipline as a census printing its denominator, and the same one that caught a truncation removing the wrong two-thirds. Three shapes now: a file cut, a test set, a census. ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE VENUE CHOICE IS PART OF THE FINDING. They ran it OUTSIDE their own repo deliberately, because their BUILD file is generated so a hand-added target reds the generator check, and any target in their gate inherits a 407-second action that defeated the first attempt. A throwaway workspace was not convenience — it was the only place the measurement could be taken without the instrument perturbing the thing measured. ⚑ NOT RE-RUN BY ME: substrate has no bazel workspace and no bazel installed, so I hold this as their measurement rather than as a fact I have checked. Reported as testimony.}
}

@misc{gate-G18-the-unpaired-backlog-is-two-populations-not-one,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G17-a-known-good-member-is-what-makes-an-abort-visible},
  claim    = {The unpaired backlog is two populations with opposite costs of delay — instrument gaps that wait harmlessly and measured inversions carrying decisions nobody has seen.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Third consecutive paydown, 28 to 26, six rows read across three ticks. ⚑ Every agda row read has been an INSTRUMENT GAP kinded unwitnessed: F3 cannot separate exited-0-because-clean from exited-0-on-a-stale-log, F9 cannot show whether a clash guard reports or falls silent, both because the distinguishing input is a fixture nobody builds. O4 is the same family one level out. ⚑⚑ Every sql row read has been a COMPLETED MEASUREMENT INVERTING A HANDED VERDICT, kinded standing. F27 was handed declare-the-tier and reading the binding showed the relations are views refused by construction with two tables colliding against an existing tier. F28 shows the sqlite census used an instrument blind to the spelling both real opens use, and its one number covers three verdicts by owner. ⚑ The costs of delay are opposite: an instrument gap waits harmlessly and converts when a fixture exists, while a measured inversion means the operator does not know the assigned work was misframed. ⚑⚑⚑ The partition predicts which rows to read first, which a flat backlog cannot — read sql and gate rows first, leave fixture-blocked ones. ⚑ A pattern over six rows, not a law: eleven agda rows are unread and the first one carrying a decision falsifies it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THIRD CONSECUTIVE PAYDOWN, 28 to 26, and six rows read across three ticks now support a partition rather than an impression. EVERY agda ROW READ HAS BEEN AN INSTRUMENT GAP kinded unwitnessed — agda-F3 cannot separate exited-0-because-clean from exited-0-on-a-stale-log, agda-F9 cannot show whether a clash guard reports or falls silent, and both for the same reason: the distinguishing input is a fixture nobody builds. agda-O4 is the same family one level out, a constraint on what a Bash-only agent can construct at all. ⚑⚑ EVERY sql ROW READ HAS BEEN A COMPLETED MEASUREMENT THAT INVERTS A HANDED VERDICT, kinded standing. sql-F27 was handed declare-the-tier and reading schema_decl showed the relations are views the declaration refuses by construction, with two base tables colliding against an existing tier — a live unadjudicated blocker. sql-F28 shows the sqlite census was taken with an instrument blind to the spelling both real opens use, and that its one number covers three unrelated verdicts by owner. ⚑ SO THE BACKLOG IS TWO POPULATIONS WITH OPPOSITE COSTS OF DELAY. An instrument gap waits harmlessly — nothing rots, and it converts the day a fixture exists. A measured inversion carries a decision someone needs, and every tick it sits unpaired is a tick the operator does not know the assigned work was misframed. ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE PARTITION PREDICTS WHICH ROWS TO READ FIRST, which a flat backlog cannot. Reading two per tick found one of each both times by accident of ordering. Reading the sql and gate rows first would surface the blockers and leave the fixture-blocked ones where they harmlessly are. ⚑ STATED AS A PATTERN OVER SIX ROWS, NOT A LAW: the remaining sixteen include eleven agda rows I have not read, and the prediction is falsifiable by the first agda row that turns out to carry a decision.}
}

@misc{gate-F20-sql-lift-corpus-excludes-scratch,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A green sql-census gate asserts freshness over a corpus that excludes the largest tool directory, because its file walk globs two non-recursive directories.},
  check    = {excludes:jea/metalanguage/sql_lift.py:_py_files:scratch},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle via substrate. Witness registered in findings.py. The hook's own Phase-0 rationale says the census interns EVERY .py in the repo so its figures MEASURE THE CORPUS THEY LIVE IN — and _py_files globs jea/metalanguage and scripts non-recursively. scratch, the largest tool directory, is not among them. ⚑ So the comment states the property the walk does not have, and a new scratch tool cannot move the count the comment says it moves. The gate is green over a population it never scanned. ⚑⚑ SAME CLASS AS THE FROZEN TREE ROSTER IN THE EXE-BIT SWEEP, which swept three trees that happened to be clean and printed no-mismatches as a verdict about the repo — and the same repair applies: DISCOVER the roots rather than list them, so a tree added tomorrow is covered without an edit. Nothing in either tool's output distinguished SCANNED-AND-CLEAN from NEVER-SCANNED. ⚑ AND THE RATIONALE IS THE TELL. A comment asserting total coverage above a walk that globs two directories is the frozen-roster shape wearing documentation: the claim reads as verification and is the thing most likely to stop anyone checking. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the hook's own Phase-0 rationale (.githooks/pre-commit:72) says `sql_lift --check-census` 'interns EVERY .py in the repo, so its figures MEASURE THE CORPUS THEY LIVE IN' — but _py_files globs TWO non-recursive dirs, jea/metalanguage/*.py and scripts/*.py. scratch/ (the LARGEST tool directory) is not among them, so a green sql-census gate asserts freshness over a corpus excluding most of the tools, and a new scratch/*.py cannot move the count the comment says it moves}
}

@misc{gate-G19-the-partition-held-and-the-non-agda-rows-are-gone,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G18-the-unpaired-backlog-is-two-populations-not-one},
  claim    = {Every non-agda unpaired row is now paired and each carried a live repair, so the remaining backlog is bookkeeping rather than discovery unless the partition breaks.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,.claude/agents/findings.py,--pairing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Fourth consecutive paydown, 26 to 24, and the partition is confirmed by EXHAUSTION rather than sampling — both remaining non-agda rows were read and both were measured inversions carrying repairs. The witness-only column is now entirely agda. ⚑ sql-F26 inverts its own framing: catalog.db holds exactly the ten EVENT-tier relations, all already in postgres, a STRICT SUPERSET (terms 12252 against 48214). Nothing unmigrated. And both tools reading it are ALREADY BROKEN — postgres placeholders a sqlite3 connection cannot execute, plus a table the file never contained. A repair of dead tools, not a migration, and an import census would have missed that neither runs. ⚑⚑ gate-F20 is the frozen-roster shape wearing documentation: the rationale claims the census interns EVERY .py while the walk globs two non-recursive directories, omitting the largest tool directory. Same class as the exe-bit three-tree roster, same repair — discover the roots. ⚑⚑⚑ The exhaustion buys a prediction: fourteen rows remain, all agda-prefixed, so if the partition holds none carries a decision and the paydown from here is bookkeeping. Falsifiable by the first agda row carrying a live blocker. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): FOURTH CONSECUTIVE PAYDOWN, 26 to 24, AND THE PARTITION IS NOW CONFIRMED BY EXHAUSTION RATHER THAN BY SAMPLING. gate-G18 predicted that the sql and gate rows carry decisions while the agda rows are fixture-blocked instrument gaps, and said to read the former first. Both remaining non-agda rows were read this tick and both were measured inversions carrying live repairs. The witness-only column is now ENTIRELY agda. ⚑ sql-F26 inverts its own framing: catalog.db holds exactly the ten relations of the EVENT tier, all already in postgres, which is a STRICT SUPERSET rather than a copy — terms 12252 against 48214. Nothing in it is unmigrated. AND BOTH TOOLS READING IT ARE ALREADY BROKEN — sppf_query raises on postgres placeholders a sqlite3 connection cannot execute, and reuse_tui additionally names a table the file has never contained. So it is a REPAIR of dead tools, not a migration of live data, and an import census would have reported two sites to migrate while missing that neither runs. ⚑⚑ gate-F20 is the frozen-roster shape wearing documentation. The hook's own rationale says the census interns EVERY .py so its figures measure the corpus they live in — and the walk globs two non-recursive directories, omitting scratch, the largest tool directory. A green gate asserting freshness over a population it never scanned, with the comment being the thing most likely to stop anyone checking. Same class as the exe-bit sweep's three-tree roster, same repair: discover the roots. ⚑⚑⚑ WHAT THE EXHAUSTION BUYS IS A PREDICTION ABOUT THE REMAINDER. Fourteen witness-only rows remain and every one is agda-prefixed, so if the partition holds they are all fixture-blocked and none carries a decision — which means the paydown from here is bookkeeping rather than discovery. That is falsifiable by the first agda row that turns out to carry a live blocker, and it changes what the next ticks are worth.}
}

@misc{gate-G20-a-snapshot-stash-reads-as-history,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G19-the-partition-held-and-the-non-agda-rows-are-gone},
  claim    = {A staged file reads as tracked and its snapshot stashes read as history, so three git instruments disagree about whether a peer can fetch it.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. A peer reported file_header.py tracked and asked whether the operator had ruled. RE-MEASURED: git status --short reports A for module and suite — staged — and HEAD is unchanged from session start. ⚑⚑ Their reading was defensible: ls-files reports a STAGED file as tracked because the index IS the tracking record. Tracked-in-the-index and fetchable-by-a-peer are different properties and only the second matters for intake — the word collapses them. ⚑⚑⚑ git log --all is worse: it shows the file in thirty-two commits, every one a STASH (index on main, WIP on main) created by the snapshot guard arming each bibstruct --add. A peer sees dense history and concludes the file is fetchable. None is on a branch and no clone carries them. ⚑ Three instruments, one file: ls-files says tracked, log --all says thirty-two commits, log on the branch says nothing exists. Only the third answers the question. The guard and its commits are correct — the defect is that a stash is indistinguishable from history to the reader most likely to check. ⚑⚑ And the kind is a second finding: filed first as refuses with git log as the command, and the polarity probe REFUSED it naming cause (b) — a census exits 0 whether it finds rows or none. The guard caught its author reaching for a falsifiable kind a census cannot support. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A PEER REPORTED file_header.py TRACKED IN SUBSTRATE AND ASKED WHETHER THE OPERATOR HAD RULED. RE-MEASURED, AND IT IS NOT COMMITTED. git status --short reports A for both the module and its suite — STAGED — and git log --oneline shows HEAD unchanged from session start. ⚑⚑ THEIR READING WAS DEFENSIBLE AND THAT IS THE FINDING. git ls-files reports a STAGED file as tracked, because the index IS the tracking record. Tracked-in-the-index and fetchable-by-a-peer are different properties and only the second is what an intake decision needs — the word tracked collapses them. ⚑⚑⚑ AND git log --all IS WORSE, BECAUSE IT SHOWS THE FILE IN THIRTY-TWO COMMITS. Every one is a STASH — index on main or WIP on main — created by the snapshot guard that arms each bibstruct --add. A peer running git log --all sees dense history and concludes the file is fetchable. Not one of those commits is on any branch, and a clone or fetch carries none of them. ⚑ THREE INSTRUMENTS DISAGREE ABOUT ONE FILE: ls-files says tracked, log --all says thirty-two commits, log on the branch says nothing exists. Only the third answers what a peer is asking. The snapshot guard is correct and its commits are correct — the defect is that a stash is indistinguishable from history to the reader most likely to check. ⚑⚑ AND THE KIND IS THE SECOND FINDING: I filed this as refuses: with git log as the command and the polarity probe REFUSED IT, correctly, naming cause (b) — a census exits 0 whether it finds rows or none, so no exit code can carry the claim. The guard wired this session caught its author reaching for a falsifiable kind a census cannot support.}
}

@misc{gate-G21-a-control-validates-one-predicate-and-a-changed-predicate-orphans-it,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G20-a-snapshot-stash-reads-as-history},
  claim    = {A positive control validates one predicate, so changing the predicate orphans it in place while it keeps printing and keeps looking like a control.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=mtools-ce via substrate, adopted. They reproduced gate-G20 and found worse their side: a blocker census used git ls-files for ten ticks so every TRACKED was staged-only, and the claim that a move subgraph was landable was never true. Their comparison work stands — it came from reading files, not availability. Now three-valued: FETCHABLE, staged only, untracked. ⚑⚑⚑ The keeper is not the ls-files bug. Their CONTROL had silently stopped controlling: a file chosen to prove ls-files could see something read staged-only under the new fetchability predicate — the SAME OUTPUT as every subject it was meant to discriminate against. Nothing announced it. It kept printing and would have passed review. ⚑⚑ This session has built positive controls all night — a known-good member so an abort is visible, an apostrophe-free heading so a resolution means something, a passing case so a refusal is not vacuous — and NONE records which predicate it validates. Any survives a predicate change while ceasing to discriminate. ⚑ Their repair is enumerate-before-filter arriving at control SELECTION: re-chosen by measurement, four candidates failing before one passed, the control taken FROM the tree rather than proposed to it. ⚑⚑ Substrate has not audited its own controls for this — owed work, named not done. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A PEER REPRODUCED gate-G20 AND FOUND A SHARPER DEFECT THEIR SIDE, WHICH I AM ADOPTING RATHER THAN ADMIRING. Their blocker census used git ls-files for ten ticks, so every TRACKED it printed was staged-only — and the claim ten-of-thirty-two-tracked-the-move-subgraph-is-landable was never true. Their comparison work stands because it came from reading files directly rather than from availability, but the availability claim did not. Now three-valued: FETCHABLE, staged only, untracked. ⚑⚑⚑ THE PART WORTH KEEPING IS NOT THE ls-files BUG. Their control had silently stopped controlling. A file was chosen to prove ls-files could see SOMETHING — a positive control for that predicate. When the predicate changed to fetchability, the control read staged-only: THE SAME OUTPUT AS EVERY SUBJECT IT WAS MEANT TO DISCRIMINATE AGAINST. Nothing announced it. It kept printing, kept looking like a control, and would have passed review. ⚑⚑ A CONTROL VALIDATES ONE PROPERTY, AND CHANGING THE PREDICATE ORPHANS IT IN PLACE. That is a class this session has not named: I have been building positive controls all night — a known-good member so an abort is visible, a heading with no apostrophe so a resolution means something, a passing case so a refusal is not vacuous — and NONE of them carries a record of which predicate it validates. Any of them survives a predicate change while silently ceasing to discriminate. ⚑ AND THEIR REPAIR IS THE SAME DISCIPLINE AS ENUMERATE-BEFORE-FILTER, ARRIVING AT CONTROL SELECTION: they re-chose by MEASUREMENT rather than belief, testing four candidates that failed before one passed. The control had to be taken FROM the tree rather than proposed to it. ⚑⚑ SUBSTRATE HAS NOT AUDITED ITS OWN CONTROLS FOR THIS AND THE OWED WORK IS NAMED HERE RATHER THAN DONE: every control in every suite written this session validates a predicate nobody recorded.}
}

@misc{gate-G22-i-audited-my-own-controls-and-found-one,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G21-a-control-validates-one-predicate-and-a-changed-predicate-orphans-it},
  claim    = {Substrate had the orphaned-control defect too — a case asserting a file exists on disk, sitting green among seventeen that tested the module.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.md_hkey_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Acted on gate-G21 rather than recording it. md_hkey_selftest's live arm asserted the filed census leg still exists — chosen when that leg was the live artifact, and under this suite's actual predicate (does the normaliser fold pandoc's rewrites) it discriminated NOTHING. It would have passed with md_hkey deleted. ⚑⚑ One of eighteen green cases with nothing distinguishing it: the suite read 18 of 18 with a member that could not fail for any reason connected to the module. The peer's finding reproduced independently. ⚑ The replacement is a differential against the origin's rule, TRANSCRIBED not imported — mdstruct carries mypy findings behind an all-or-nothing gate, so importing it would make this suite's admission depend on an unrepairable file. A second case asserts the origin still folds markup, so a transcription error in the direction that matters cannot fake both. ⚑⚑⚑ The differential carries a claim the old case could not: not the-normaliser-folds but the-normaliser-folds-WHERE-THE-ORIGIN-DID-NOT. If the origin case ever passes, the origin folds punctuation and this module is redundant — a finding, not a green. 19 of 19. ⚑ The rest of the audit is owed: every other control this session records no predicate, and this one was found by reading rather than by a mechanism. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ACTED ON gate-G21 RATHER THAN RECORDING IT, AND SUBSTRATE HAD THE DEFECT TOO. Audited md_hkey_selftest's live arm and found an orphaned control: a case asserting the filed census leg still exists on disk. It was chosen when that leg was the live artifact, and under this suite's ACTUAL predicate — does the normaliser fold pandoc's rewrites — it discriminated NOTHING. It would have passed with md_hkey deleted. ⚑⚑ IT WAS ONE OF EIGHTEEN GREEN CASES AND NOTHING DISTINGUISHED IT. The suite read 18 of 18 with a member that could not fail for any reason connected to the module. That is the peer's finding reproduced independently: a control keeps printing, keeps looking like a control, and passes review. ⚑ THE REPLACEMENT IS A DIFFERENTIAL AGAINST THE ORIGIN'S OWN RULE, transcribed rather than imported — mdstruct carries mypy findings behind an all-or-nothing gate, so importing it would make this suite's admission depend on a file nobody can repair. The transcription is kept honest by a second case asserting the origin still folds markup: a transcription error in the direction that matters cannot fake both. ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE DIFFERENTIAL CARRIES A CLAIM THE OLD CASE COULD NOT. The property is not the-normaliser-folds but the-normaliser-folds-WHERE-THE-ORIGIN-DID-NOT, and only a comparison holds that. If the origin case ever passes, the origin folds punctuation too and this module is redundant — which is a finding rather than a green. 19 of 19. ⚑ THE REST OF THE AUDIT IS OWED: every other control written this session still records no predicate, and this one was found by reading rather than by any mechanism.}
}

@misc{gate-G23-the-dead-case-was-a-template-not-three-accidents,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G22-i-audited-my-own-controls-and-found-one},
  claim    = {Three of three audited live arms opened with the same unfailable case, so the dead control is a template this session reproduced rather than three accidents.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.arm_claims_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Each _live_cases opens with an early return for an absent fixture, then opens its success list with the same row spelled the same way — a literal True paired with is-present prose. After the early return that row cannot fail. ⚑ md_hkey carried it as a file-existence check, repaired last tick. imported_constant and arm_claims carried it as a bare True, and arm_claims carried a SECOND dead row, the-live-roster-is-non-empty, implied by target-is-not-None one line above. ⚑⚑ The generative cause is that presence is a PRECONDITION of the arm, not one of its findings — the early return handles absence, so restating presence in the success path asserts the branch you are in. The template made it feel like symmetry: the failure list names the fixture so the success list names it too. ⚑⚑⚑ The repair is substitution where a claim exists, not deletion. arm_claims now asserts arm_shape counts the SAME arm's appends as two while arm_claims counts one claim — the differential gate-G6 rests on, going red if the readers ever agree. A dead row removed is a smaller suite. A dead row replaced by a differential is a suite holding the claim its module was built for. ⚑ Three arms of a larger population audited. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): AUDITED THREE LIVE ARMS AND ALL THREE CARRIED A DEAD CASE, WHICH MAKES IT A TEMPLATE RATHER THAN THREE ACCIDENTS. Each _live_cases opens with an early return reporting an absent fixture, then opens its success list with the SAME ROW SPELLED THE SAME WAY: a literal True paired with is-present prose. After the early return that row cannot fail. It asserts nothing and occupies a case slot while reading as a control. ⚑ md_hkey carried it as a file-existence check and was repaired last tick. imported_constant and arm_claims carried it as a bare True. arm_claims carried a SECOND dead row — the-live-roster-is-non-empty — implied by target-is-not-None on the line directly above it. ⚑⚑ THE GENERATIVE CAUSE IS THAT PRESENCE IS A PRECONDITION OF THE ARM, NOT ONE OF ITS FINDINGS. The early return already handles absence, so restating presence in the success path is asserting the branch you are in. I wrote the same shape three times because the template made it feel like symmetry — the failure list names the fixture, so the success list names it too. ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE REPAIR IS NOT DELETION BUT SUBSTITUTION WHERE A CLAIM EXISTS. arm_claims now asserts that arm_shape counts the SAME arm's appends as two while arm_claims counts one claim — the differential gate-G6 rests on, which goes red if the two readers ever agree. A dead row removed is a smaller suite. A dead row replaced by a differential is a suite that holds the claim its module was built for. 18 of 18 and 26 of 26. ⚑ THREE ARMS AUDITED OF A LARGER POPULATION: every suite written this session has a _live_cases and only three have been read.}
}

