@misc{gate-G25-a-roster-rendered-usage-reads-as-no-self-description,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A tool whose usage is rendered from its mode roster reads as having NO self-description, because toolmodes.bare parses declaration text statically from the tool's own source and a computed usage string exists in no string constant there.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,scratch/toolmodes.py,--selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. The drained bibstruct shim prints eleven well-formed modes on a bare run and the census calls it undescribed, where it read 12 documented before the drain. bib_cli.usage renders from bib_modes.MODES precisely so an undocumented mode is unrepresentable - the declaration is what makes a mode dispatch - so the unreadable form is the STRONGER one. Sixth instance of the class bare's own docstring names for sppf_db, a fact about the METHOD rendered as a fact about the SUBSTRATE, and the first where improving a declaration made it invisible. The paired witness gate-G25a was filed on an INVENTED mode name and refuses on argument validation rather than on the defect, recorded here rather than silently replaced. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): toolmodes.bare reads self-description statically from five declaration shapes in a tool's own source. The drained bibstruct shim publishes a sixth: bib_cli.usage renders the mode list from the bib_modes roster, so the usage text exists in no string constant of scratch/bibstruct.py and the census reports NO SELF-DESCRIPTION for a tool that prints eleven well-formed modes on a bare run. The shim had 12 documented modes before the drain and reads as undescribed after. The unreadable form is the STRONGER one: usage() was written so that an undocumented mode is unrepresentable, since the roster that declares a mode is what makes it dispatch. Sixth instance of the class bare's own docstring names for sppf_db - a fact about the METHOD rendered as a fact about the SUBSTRATE - and the first where improving a declaration made it invisible.}
}

@misc{gate-G25a-entries-is-declared-by-no-tool,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {toolmodes --find entries reports --entries as declared by NO tool while scratch/bibstruct.py dispatches it and prints it in a bare-run usage line, both clauses true and jointly absurd.},
  check    = {refuses:python3,scratch/toolmodes.py,--check-declared,--entries},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. The discriminating instance of gate-G25, and the same pair bare's docstring records for sppf_db --promote-core. ⚑ THIS WITNESS IS MISFRAMED AND THE MISFRAMING IS THE SECOND FINDING. It refuses on an INVENTED mode name that toolmodes rejects at argument validation, so it reports the defect's presence for a reason unrelated to the defect and would go green the day a mode of that name lands for any purpose. A witness keyed on a spelling its author made up is the census-keyed-on-one-spelling shape committed inside a filing about a census misreading a spelling. Kept rather than deleted because --register correctly refuses silent edits, and the honest kind here is unwitnessed until a mode that asserts a dispatching flag is findable exists. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The discriminating command for gate-G25. toolmodes --find entries reports --entries as NAMED but not declared - declared by NO tool - while scratch/bibstruct.py dispatches it and prints it in a well-formed usage line on a bare run. Both clauses true and jointly absurd, the same pair bare's docstring records for sppf_db --promote-core. This witness is filed PREMATURE and says so: no --check-declared mode exists yet, so the command fails for the wrong reason. It converts to a real refusal when a mode that asserts a dispatching flag is findable lands.}
}

@misc{gate-G27-the-drained-guard-cannot-resolve-a-refuses-witness,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The drained bibstruct guard refuses a bib entry for a live refuses-witness, because witness_resolves keys on the runner's exit code alone and three parties share exit 2.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. ⚑ THIS FINDING'S OWN DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG AND THE CORRECTION IS THE CONTENT. I filed it claiming witness_resolves keys on the exit code alone. Reading the binding refutes that: resolves returns True immediately when returncode != UNKNOWN_KEY, so exit 1 resolves, and it reads exit 2 WITH its message plus an explicit empty-output refusal - the Ⓝ168′ repair, already landed and correct. The guard is sound. What actually happened is that findings.py <key> exits 1 when run directly and 2 under the guard's subprocess for the same key, so the discriminator is the ENVIRONMENT, not the code and not the message. See gate-G26, which carries the measurement. Kept rather than deleted because a retracted diagnosis with its refutation is worth more than a silent replacement, and because I predicted this entry would be REFUSED by the guard and it was written without complaint - the prediction failing is what exposed the real cause. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): bibstruct --add refused check=item:gate-G26 saying findings.py does not answer for it because the runner exits 2, while running findings.py gate-G26 prints the claim and the correct REFUSES verdict at exit 1. This is the Ⓝ168 collision arriving in the DRAINED guard: Ⓝ168′ closed it in the origin by reading the message beside the exit code, and witness_resolves.resolves keys on returncode != UNKNOWN_KEY alone. Three parties share exit 2 here - the resolver not knowing a key, the resolver answering with an honest gap, and the runner failing before it reaches the resolver - and only the middle one resolves. Filed unwitnessed because no predicate available today distinguishes a guard that CANNOT resolve a live witness from one that correctly refuses an absent one, which is the finding restated at the witness layer.}
}

@misc{gate-G26-mode-census-row-refuses-without-a-baseline,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The mode-census row of commit_refusal reports a count where it owes ABSENT, because it diffs toolmodes.census per-TOOL tuples against a mode-keyed baseline that gate_census --baselines does not list.},
  check    = {refuses:python3,-m,substrate.commit_refusal},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. ⚑⚑ THE ORIGINAL DIAGNOSIS IS REFUTED BY READING THE BINDING, AND THE CORRECTED ONE IS SHARPER. I filed this claiming the row diffs per-TOOL tuples against a mode-keyed baseline that does not exist. commit_refusal.verdict shows otherwise: the census is real and correct, and the defect is that baselines.get(gate.script) returns None because toolmodes is absent from gate_census's baseline_health roster of nineteen scripts. None is neither ABSENT nor EMPTY, so the row falls past the vacuous guard AND past the zero-tolerance level check, and a LEVEL renders as a DELTA. verdict's own comment says a four-figure added under an EMPTY baseline is the SIZE OF THE POPULATION and not evidence anything regressed, and that rendering the two identically is what sends an operator to fix a number that was never a defect count. That is exactly what happened here, to me, for two ticks. ⚑ CORROBORATING WITNESS: verdict's sample-truncation comment names 'one census keys on a whole parsed mode-table' - this gate - so the shape was known and the missing piece is only the baseline STATE reaching the renderer. The repair is roster membership, not a new reader. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): commit_refusal reports 'tool mode census in sync' as REFUSES OVER 101 added of 101 held while toolmodes --check reports the block FRESH. Two instruments, one subject, opposite verdicts. Read the binding: toolmodes.census returns (artifact, relpath, record) tuples - the per-TOOL derived picture - and the gate diffs those against a mode-keyed baseline, so the example row it prints is a tool tuple rather than a mode. gate_census --baselines lists nineteen ratchets and NONE is toolmodes, so the baseline does not exist. A row with no baseline is reporting a COUNT where it owes ABSENT or UNMEASURABLE, and it refuses a commit on a measurement it cannot make. The third-outcome collapse in its most expensive direction: the same gap wearing a number is invisible, and here the number also blocks.}
}

@misc{gate-G28-a-hand-rolled-gate-is-censused-as-a-ratchet,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {commit_refusal keys a gate's verdict on its SCRIPT while toolmodes carries two gate mechanisms in one file, so the hook-wired census-sync --check gate is rendered with the ratchet arithmetic of the unwired --nearmiss ratchet.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,scripts/gate_census.py,--selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. ⚑⚑⚑ THIS FINDING WAS FILED TWICE WRONG AND THE THIRD READING IS THE ONE THAT HOLDS. First I claimed the row diffs per-tool tuples against a nonexistent baseline. Then I claimed gate_census --successors proves toolmodes is hand-rolled rather than a ratchet. Both refuted by reading the binding. pycodemod --calls set_ratchet scratch/toolmodes.py finds a real call at line 2433, so it IS a ratchet consumer. The kind column said otherwise because successor_hints computes rats from ratchet_consumers, which walks scripts/ ONLY - so any ratchet outside that directory is classified hand-rolled BY FALLTHROUGH, and the label is an artifact of the search directory rather than a reading of the script. ⚑⚑ THE ACTUAL CAUSE IS TWO GATE MECHANISMS IN ONE FILE. cmd_nearmiss mints .nearmiss-baseline through set_ratchet and its own docstring says it is DELIBERATELY NOT WIRED into pre-commit. The hook-wired gate is the census-sync --check, which is green. commit_refusal.verdict keys on gate.script, so it finds the unwired ratchet's shape and applies its arithmetic to the wired census-sync gate. A script is not a gate, and the identity commit_refusal assumes between them is what produces the bogus row. ⚑ THE REGISTERED WITNESS REMAINS VACUOUS and says so - gate_census --selftest is green regardless. Kept with all three readings because the two refutations are the evidence for the third. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): commit_refusal.verdict calls ratchet_census.census on EVERY hook-wired gate and renders the result as ratchet arithmetic, but gate_census --successors classifies scratch/toolmodes.py as hand-rolled rather than ratchet - twelve of thirty-five gate scripts are ratchets and toolmodes is not one. A hand-rolled census-sync gate has no baseline by design, so baselines.get returns None, which is neither ABSENT nor EMPTY, so the row falls past the vacuous guard AND past the zero-tolerance level branch and prints 101 added of 101 held. The gate's actual refusal path is --check, which reports FRESH. Two instruments on one subject with opposite verdicts, and the hand-rolled one is correct. gate_census's banner already declares the three kinds - ratchet, census-sync, pure-violation - so the classification exists and the consumer does not consult it.}
}

@misc{gate-G29-ratchet-ness-is-derived-within-one-directory,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {ratchet_consumers derives ratchet-ness by walking scripts/ only, so a ratchet in any other directory is invisible and successor_hints then reports it as hand-rolled by fallthrough rather than as UNREAD.},
  check    = {refuses:python3,scripts/gate_census.py,--check},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. The docstring records FOUR prior false zeros from progressively wider predicates - mention-vs-call, a one-line os.path.join reporting 3 of 15, an inline call site, and a scalar-over-denominator ratchet omitted entirely. This is the FIFTH, on the axis none of those four touched. pycodemod --calls set_ratchet over scratch/ substrate/ jea/ finds scratch/toolmodes.py:2433, a live consumer minting .nearmiss-baseline. ⚑ THE OMISSION IS RENDERED AS A POSITIVE KIND CLAIM, which is the sharp half - the module provides UNREAD for exactly this case and the fallthrough does not use it, so a gate the census cannot see is reported as a gate of a known kind. That is the sentence the docstring itself writes, one axis over. ⚑⚑ FILED unwitnessed AFTER AN EXPERIMENT REFUTED THE OBVIOUS WITNESS. refuses:gate_census --check was correct polarity for the wrong reason - it refused because the block was STALE. Running --sync --apply turned it green with the directory bound untouched, and the ledger's own polarity check reported the refusal as GONE. A witness that measures block freshness cannot hold a claim about roster scope. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ratchet_consumers documents itself as ratchet-ness derived not listed, and its docstring records FOUR prior false zeros from progressively wider predicates - mention-vs-call, a one-line os.path.join that reported 3 of 15, an inline call site, and a scalar-over-denominator ratchet omitted entirely. This is the FIFTH, on the axis none of those four touched: the loop walks os.listdir(REPO/scripts) only, so a ratchet anywhere else cannot appear however it is written. pycodemod --calls set_ratchet over scratch/ substrate/ jea/ finds scratch/toolmodes.py:2433, a live consumer minting .nearmiss-baseline. The consequence is not just an omitted row - successor_hints classifies anything absent from that roster as hand-rolled BY FALLTHROUGH, so the omission is rendered as a positive kind claim rather than as UNREAD, which is the state the module provides for exactly this case. A census that cannot see a gate cannot report that it cannot see it, in the function whose own docstring says that sentence.}
}

@misc{gate-G30-a-suite-line-scan-disagreed-with-the-hook-parser,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A suite arm that scanned the hook's raw lines by substring reported a toolmodes invocation that is not a sync gate, disagreeing with gate_status's shell-aware parser which shows exactly one, wired --check.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.wired_mode_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. gate_status.gates parses only run lines and refuses outright on a hook that does not parse as shell, while a raw line scan also sees comments and non-run lines - so the disagreement was between MY reader and the AUTHORITY, not within the hook. Driving the arm through commit_refusal.call_rows(authority(gate_status), gates) takes the suite from 16/17 to 18/18. ⚑ THE FAILING CASE WAS THE MEASUREMENT and not noise: it is what showed the suite had reintroduced a textual query over a structured artifact, inside the module built to stop one. ⚑ AND pycodemod --literal toolmodes.py over the hook RETURNS ZERO because it reads Python string constants and the hook is shell, so that zero is a fact about the reader rather than about the hook. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The first live arm of wired_mode_selftest filtered the hook's raw lines by substring for toolmodes.py and reported an invocation that is not a sync gate, failing 16/17. gate_status --list shows exactly one toolmodes row, wired --check. The disagreement was between MY reader and the AUTHORITY, not within the hook: gate_status.gates parses only run lines and refuses outright on a hook that does not parse as shell, while a raw line scan also sees comments and non-run lines. Driving the case through commit_refusal.call_rows(authority(gate_status), gates) gives 18/18. ⚑ THE FAILING CASE WAS THE MEASUREMENT and not noise - it is what showed the suite had reintroduced a textual query over a structured artifact inside the module built to replace one. ⚑ AND pycodemod --literal toolmodes.py .githooks/pre-commit RETURNS ZERO, because it reads Python string constants and the hook is shell. That zero is a fact about the reader.}
}

@misc{gate-G31-a-fixture-path-decided-the-guard-under-test,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A suite arm built its fixture with a nonexistent script path, so an earlier guard short-circuited and the arm asserted one guard's message while exercising another's.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.commit_refusal_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. verdict asks writes_on_import BEFORE the census-sync guard, and import_writes.why_not_import answers 'does not exist - not imported' for a missing path. The arm used _gate's default scripts/fixture.py, so it received the missing-file message. ⚑ TWO OF ITS THREE CLAIMS PASSED ANYWAY because both guards return UNMEASURABLE - only the claim that read the DETAIL string could tell them apart. A fixture whose incidental-looking field decides the outcome is a case that never reaches the code it names, and an arm asserting only the state would have gone green against an untested guard forever. Naming a real script takes the suite from 25/25 to 29/29. ⚑ THE PARTIAL FAILURE WAS THE MEASUREMENT, and it is an argument for asserting the REASON rather than only the verdict wherever two branches share a state. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The first cut of the census-sync arm built its gate with _gate's default script scripts/fixture.py, which does not exist. verdict asks writes_on_import BEFORE the sync guard, and import_writes.why_not_import answers 'does not exist - not imported' for a missing path, so the arm received the missing-file message while asserting the sync one. It failed on the detail check and passed on state and refusal, because BOTH guards return UNMEASURABLE - so two of its three claims were satisfied by the wrong branch entirely. ⚑ A FIXTURE WHOSE IRRELEVANT-LOOKING FIELD DECIDES THE OUTCOME IS A CASE THAT NEVER REACHES THE CODE IT NAMES, and the state assertion alone would have passed forever. Naming a real script takes the suite from 25/25 to 29/29. ⚑ THE PARTIAL FAILURE WAS THE MEASUREMENT: had all three claims been state-shaped the arm would have gone green against an untested guard.}
}

@misc{gate-G32-the-sync-guard-corrected-six-rows-not-one,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Wiring the wired-mode guard into commit_refusal corrected six gate rows rather than the one it was built for, and five of those had been UNMEASURABLE for unrelated reasons so only their stated reason changed.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. The census-kit mode B remainder for a change to a reader other sessions consult. ⚑ I PREDICTED ONE ROW WOULD MOVE AND SIX DID - selftest roster current, gate census in sync, tool mode census in sync, sql census in sync, apex gate, and jea regression - which is the population wired_mode's docstring derived from gate_status --list, confirmed by behaviour rather than by argument. Five already read UNMEASURABLE from an unrelated cause (no census, module not found), so their verdict is unchanged and their reason is now correct rather than accidentally right. A reader taking those old rows as evidence about those gates was reading a coincidence. ⚑ DECLINED, NOT OVERLOOKED: gate_census.ratchet_consumers still walks scripts/ only, filed as gate-G29 - a different owner, and widening it would change what other readers see. ⚑ THE RE-DERIVATION WORTH RECORDING: nothing in commit_refusal could tell a gate from its script, though gate_status.gates has always yielded the argv. The field was parsed and discarded, which is why this was a narrow repair and not a new reader. Other parties on this object: gate_status runs the same roster, gate_census classifies the same scripts. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): census-kit mode B remainder for wiring wired_mode into commit_refusal. WHAT CHANGED: gates wired as --check or --check-census now report UNMEASURABLE naming their wired mode, instead of being censused as ratchets. The refusing count went 9 to 8. WHAT I EXPECTED AND WHAT HAPPENED: I predicted one row would move, toolmodes. Six moved - selftest roster current, gate census in sync, tool mode census in sync, sql census in sync, apex gate, and jea regression. Five of those already read UNMEASURABLE for UNRELATED reasons (no census, module not found), so their verdict is unchanged and only their REASON is now correct rather than accidentally right. A reader treating the old rows as evidence about those gates was reading a coincidence. WHAT I DECLINED: I did not change gate_census.ratchet_consumers, whose scripts-only walk is filed separately as gate-G29, because that is a different owner and widening it would change what other readers see. WHAT I RE-DERIVED THAT SHARED MACHINERY SHOULD HAVE OFFERED: nothing in commit_refusal could distinguish a gate from its script, though gate_status.gates has always yielded the argv - the field was parsed and discarded, which is why this was a narrow repair rather than a new reader. WHO ELSE TOUCHES THIS: gate_status runs the same roster, gate_census classifies the same scripts, and any session reading commit_refusal's output sees these rows.}
}

@misc{gate-G33-the-rederive-additions-are-not-this-sessions,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The rederive ratchet's 42 additions are Agda codemod tools untouched this session, not this session's own work, so the symbol ranked head for three ticks on a false premise inferred from a sample key.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. Running check_rederive_ratchet bare enumerates the additions: scope_source, fix_notinscope, retire_mod_tip, telescope_migrate_gen, rewire_to_leaves, split_census. The premise came from reading commit_refusal's sample key declscan::scratch/split_lemmas.py#3 and inferring authorship because I had been writing python that tick. ⚑ A SAMPLE KEY IS AN ILLUSTRATION, NOT A PROVENANCE CLAIM - commit_refusal's own comment says the sample illustrates a verdict rather than characterising the population, and I read it as the latter three times. ⚑ THE GATE NAMES A CONSEQUENCE I HAD NOT SEEN: 24 genuine paydowns cannot record while any new key is present, so the additions block progress already made. The repair is re-pointing 42 derivation sites at the agda_lex, agda_defs and agda_imports authorities across roughly thirty files, which the gate states in its own refusal. ⚑ THE RANKING LESSON IS THE TRANSFERABLE HALF: a leverage claim resting on WHO AUTHORED a population needs the population enumerated, and one bare run of the owning gate settles it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I ranked pay-the-rederive-42 head for three consecutive ticks on the premise that its additions were this session's own and therefore needed no adjudication. Running check_rederive_ratchet bare refutes that: all 42 new keys name Agda codemod tools - scope_source, fix_notinscope, retire_mod_tip, telescope_migrate_gen, rewire_to_leaves, split_census - none touched this session. The premise came from reading the commit_refusal sample key declscan::scratch/split_lemmas.py#3 and inferring authorship from the fact that I had been writing python that tick. ⚑ A SAMPLE KEY IS AN ILLUSTRATION, NOT A PROVENANCE CLAIM, and commit_refusal's own comment says the sample exists to illustrate a verdict rather than to characterise the population. ⚑ THE GATE ALSO NAMES A CONSEQUENCE I HAD NOT SEEN: 24 genuine paydowns cannot record while any new key is present, so the additions are blocking real progress that is already done. The repair is re-pointing 42 derivation sites at the agda_lex, agda_defs and agda_imports authorities across roughly thirty files - Agda codemod surgery, correctly named by the gate, and outside a bounded tick. Filed unwitnessed because no command distinguishes a misframed ranking premise from a correct one.}
}

@misc{gate-G34-widening-the-roster-alone-would-bank-a-false-absent,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Widening the ratchet roster past scripts/ without also widening the baseline path resolution would report an out-of-tree baseline as ABSENT, which commit_refusal short-circuits to a vacuous pass.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. Scoping gate-G29's repair before making it. ratchet_consumers walks scripts/ only, so toolmodes' --nearmiss ratchet is unrostered - but _health_row resolves baselines as os.path.join(HERE, b) with HERE = scripts/, while cmd_nearmiss joins .nearmiss-baseline to ROOT. A roster widened alone finds no file, reports ABSENT, and verdict turns ABSENT into VACUOUS before any census runs. Converting a missing row into a free pass is worse than the omission it repairs, so the change is two changes: the walk AND the path resolution. ⚑ THE CONSEQUENCE TODAY IS NIL AND THAT IS WORTH STATING: toolmodes --nearmiss --list reports zero near-miss flags, so the unrostered ratchet is clean and no verdict is currently wrong. Latent, not live - a reader should not infer urgency from the finding's existence. ⚑ THE GENERAL SHAPE: a repair that moves a population past a boundary must widen every reader scoped to that boundary, or the first reader reports absence where the second would have reported state. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Scoping gate-G29's repair before making it, and the obvious fix is wrong on its own. ratchet_consumers walks scripts/ only, so scratch/toolmodes.py's --nearmiss ratchet is absent from the baseline roster. But _health_row resolves every baseline as os.path.join(HERE, b) with HERE = scripts/, while cmd_nearmiss joins .nearmiss-baseline to ROOT. So a roster widened alone would locate no file and report ABSENT - and commit_refusal.verdict short-circuits ABSENT to VACUOUS before any census, which is a free pass. Converting a missing row into a false green is worse than the omission. TWO CHANGES ARE NEEDED, NOT ONE: the walk and the path resolution. ⚑ AND THE CONSEQUENCE TODAY IS NIL, MEASURED: toolmodes --nearmiss --list reports zero near-miss mutation flags, so the unrostered ratchet is genuinely clean and no verdict is currently wrong because of it. That makes this latent rather than live, which is a different priority and worth stating rather than leaving the reader to assume urgency from the finding's existence. Filed unwitnessed because no command distinguishes a roster scoped too narrowly from one that is complete.}
}

@misc{gate-G35-a-regrouping-reader-must-match-its-authoritys-partition,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A reader that regroups another reader's census must reproduce its partition exactly, or an else-branch silently absorbs a bucket and inflates the population it reports.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.claim_concentration_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. claim_concentration groups arm_claims.census by suite. My first cut partitioned three ways where arm_claims partitions four - single, several, silent at count zero, undecided - so every arm asserting nothing fell into the else branch and was counted as multi-claim. The blocked population read 665 against the authority's 654, inflated by exactly the silent count. ⚑ THE DISCREPANCY WAS THE MEASUREMENT and I nearly banked it as rounding. Two instruments on one subject disagreeing means one is not measuring its label, and here the newer one was wrong. ⚑ A SILENT ARM IS A DEFECT OF A DIFFERENT KIND, owned by dead_case, and counting it as blocking overstates the price in the exact direction this reader was built to prevent. ⚑ THE SUITE'S LIVE ARM IS A DIFFERENTIAL AGAINST THE AUTHORITY, not a fixture reading, because a fixture would only have proved the grouping agrees with my transcription of the partition - which was the thing that was wrong. It goes red the moment the two partitions diverge again. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): claim_concentration groups arm_claims.census by suite to answer whether a rollup rule's cost concentrates or spreads. My first cut partitioned three ways - undecided, single, else-several - where arm_claims partitions FOUR: single, several, silent (count 0), undecided. Every arm asserting nothing fell into the else branch and was counted as multi-claim, so the blocked population read 665 where the authority says 654, inflated by exactly the silent count. ⚑ THE DISCREPANCY WAS THE MEASUREMENT and I nearly banked it as rounding - two instruments on one subject disagreeing means one is not measuring its label, and here the newer one was wrong. ⚑ A SILENT ARM IS A DEFECT OF A DIFFERENT KIND, owned by dead_case, and counting it as blocking overstates the price in the exact direction this reader exists to prevent. The live arm of the suite is a DIFFERENTIAL against the authority rather than a fixture reading, because a fixture would only have proved the grouping agrees with my transcription of the partition - which was the thing that was wrong.}
}

@misc{gate-G36-a-multi-claim-arm-may-be-a-relation-not-a-collapse,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A multi-claim arm whose claim is a RELATION between outcomes cannot be split without destroying it, so the claim-per-arm bijection does not hold uniformly and a mechanical split of the 654 would lose content.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. Read three arms of module_trim_selftest.py, the heaviest suite at 25, before splitting any. All three are positive-plus-control. the_three_refusals_are_never_collapsed asserts UNSAFE and UNKNOWN and CLEAN carry DIFFERENT messages - split into three, each message is checked and the distinctness is asserted by nobody. only_a_real_main_guard_is_recognised plants four negatives beside one positive, and its own docstring says a predicate that always said yes would pass every other dispatch arm in the suite. a_stranded_import_may_vanish_but_a_body_line_may_not asserts an ASYMMETRY, and each half passes trivially alone. ⚑ ONE CLAIM PER ARM IS RIGHT FOR INDEPENDENT FACTS AND WRONG FOR A RELATION, where the several-claim count IS the relation under test. arm_claims counts out.append sites and structurally cannot separate the two - that is not a defect in it, it measures what it says. ⚑ THE FACT MAY BE AN EDGE, arriving in the bijection target itself. What is owed before splitting is a reader that distinguishes an independent-claim arm from a relation-arm, and three of three sampled from the worst suite is enough to stop rather than proceed. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Popping split-the-heaviest against module_trim_selftest.py, the tree's heaviest suite at 25 multi-claim arms, and reading three of them before splitting. All three are positive-plus-control structures whose claim IS the relation between the outcomes. the_three_refusals_are_never_collapsed asserts UNSAFE, UNKNOWN and CLEAN carry DIFFERENT messages - split into three, each message is checked and the distinctness assertion is made by nobody. only_a_real_main_guard_is_recognised plants four negatives beside one positive and its docstring says a predicate that always said yes would pass every other dispatch arm. a_stranded_import_may_vanish_but_a_body_line_may_not asserts an ASYMMETRY - split into two halves, each passes trivially and the asymmetry is asserted by nobody. ⚑ SO THE BIJECTION'S PREMISE DOES NOT HOLD UNIFORMLY. One claim per arm is right for arms asserting independent facts, and wrong for an arm whose claim is a relation between outcomes, where the several-claim count IS the relation. arm_claims counts out.append sites and structurally cannot tell the two apart, which is not a defect in it - it measures what it says. ⚑ A MECHANICAL SPLIT OF THE 654 WOULD DESTROY CONTENT, and three of three sampled from the heaviest suite is enough to stop and say so rather than proceed. What is needed before splitting is a reader that separates independent-claim arms from relation-arms. Filed unwitnessed because no command distinguishes the two today.}
}

@misc{gate-G37-the-relation-classifier-reads-a-minority-and-says-so,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {relation_arm separates a multi-claim arm whose claims probe one predicate against several inputs from one bundling independent facts, and it classifies a minority of the population while reporting the rest UNDECIDED.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.relation_arm_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. The reader gate-G36 named as owed. The discriminator is the predicate name in each claim-bearing guard, with the attribute leaf taken so a qualified call and a bare one read as one spelling rather than two. A guard that appends nothing is plumbing and does not vote. ⚑ THE REACH IS A FLOOR AND NOT A CENSUS, and quoting the relation count as the population would be the false-green shape one level up from the census this corrects. The suite ASSERTS the minority reach rather than footnoting it, because a widening that silently classified unread arms as INDEPENDENT would license exactly the destructive split this prevents - independent licenses a split and undecided does not, so an unreadable guard must land on the safe side. ⚑ TWO OF THE THREE ARMS gate-G36 RESTS ON ARE READ FROM THE LIVE SUITE rather than transcribed, because my reading of them is the claim under test, and both classify as relations - including the asymmetry arm whose two halves each pass trivially alone. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): relation_arm is the reader gate-G36 named as owed: it separates a multi-claim arm whose claims probe ONE predicate against several inputs - a positive and its controls, where the several-claim count IS the relation - from an arm bundling independent facts. The discriminator is the predicate name in each claim-bearing guard, with the attribute leaf taken so a qualified call and a bare one read as one spelling. ⚑ IT CLASSIFIES A SMALL MINORITY OF THE POPULATION AND REPORTS THE REST UNDECIDED. That is a FLOOR and not a census, and quoting the relation count as the population would be the false-green shape one level up from the census this corrects. The suite ASSERTS the minority reach rather than footnoting it, because a later widening that silently classified unread arms as INDEPENDENT would license exactly the destructive split this prevents - independent licenses a split, undecided does not, and unreadable guards must land on the safe side. ⚑ TWO OF THE THREE ARMS gate-G36 WAS FILED ON ARE READ FROM THE LIVE SUITE, not transcribed, because my reading of them is the claim under test. Both classify as relations, including one whose two halves each pass trivially alone.}
}

@misc{gate-G38-the-unread-arms-named-their-own-discriminator,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The dominant unread shape in relation_arm was assign-then-compare, and the two arms sampled state in their own docstrings that the discriminator IS the pair, so the reader was calling self-described relations unclassifiable.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.relation_arm_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. Reading two undecided arms rather than guessing the shape: got = _kinds(fixture) then if got != expected, twice over different fixtures. The guard's test is a bare Name, so a reader stopping at the comparison saw no predicate one line up. a_narrowing_assert_is_load says the first check alone could not tell. identity_narrows_only_against_none says without the is-False half a rule saying LOAD to every identity comparison passes. The arms had already stated the fact the classifier needed. Resolving a local bound from a call moved relations 54 to 72 and independent 24 to 51, undecided 576 to 531. ⚑ LAST ASSIGNMENT WINS IS STATED RATHER THAN INHERITED FROM DICT ORDER, because these arms rebind one name across successive probes and a first-wins rule would read the second guard against the first fixture's call. ⚑ THE CONTROL IS THE SAME SHAPE OVER DIFFERENT PREDICATES, without which binding resolution would report every assign-then-compare arm as a relation and freeze that family as unsplittable. ⚑ IT REMAINS A FLOOR - the majority are still undecided and the suite asserts that, so the improvement cannot read as coverage. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): relation_arm classified a small minority and reported the rest UNDECIDED. Reading two of the unread arms rather than guessing the shape showed the dominant form is assign-then-compare: got = _kinds(fixture) then if got != expected, twice over different fixtures. The guard's test is a bare Name, so a reader stopping at the comparison saw no predicate one line up. ⚑ BOTH ARMS SAY IN THEIR OWN DOCSTRINGS THAT THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE PAIR - a_narrowing_assert_is_load says the first check alone could not tell, and identity_narrows_only_against_none says without the is-False half a rule saying LOAD to every identity comparison passes. So the reader was calling two SELF-DESCRIBED relations unclassifiable, and the arms had already stated the fact the classifier needed. Resolving a local bound from a call moved relations 54 to 72 and independent 24 to 51, with undecided 576 to 531. ⚑ LAST ASSIGNMENT WINS IS STATED RATHER THAN INHERITED FROM DICT ORDER, because these arms rebind one name across successive probes and a first-wins rule would read the second guard against the first fixture's call. ⚑ IT IS STILL A FLOOR: the majority remain undecided, and the suite asserts that rather than letting the improvement read as coverage.}
}

@misc{gate-G39-an-aggregate-reader-could-not-be-audited-against-a-case,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A reader that prints only a population and a truncated sample cannot be audited against a single case, so verifying one arm's verdict after a widening reached for a shell pipe the no-chaining hook correctly refused.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.relation_arm_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. Widening relation_arm to read an attribute of a bound local moved undecided 531 to 456, relations 72 to 80, independent 51 to 118. ⚑ INDEPENDENT MORE THAN DOUBLED WHILE RELATIONS BARELY MOVED, the dangerous asymmetry because independent LICENSES a split while undecided withholds one. Checking a specific arm needed a per-arm lookup the tool did not have, so --named was added: aggregate-only is a defect in the reader, not in the query. ⚑ THE PREDICTION FAILED AND THAT WAS THE MEASUREMENT. I expected a_bucket_reports_where_its_decisions_concentrate to be a relation and the reader said UNDECIDED, because one guard is a BoolOp of two membership tests that _called does not walk. That showed the independent jump came from elsewhere rather than from misclassified relations, and confirmed the unreadable case lands on the withholding side. ⚑ THE FIELD IS PART OF THE PROBE: ban_witness_selftest reads Bucket.n in one arm and Bucket.shape in another, one constructor and two genuinely different questions, so keying on the constructor alone would fuse arms that do not relate. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Widening relation_arm to read an attribute of a bound local moved undecided 531 to 456, relations 72 to 80, and independent 51 to 118. ⚑ INDEPENDENT MORE THAN DOUBLED WHILE RELATIONS BARELY MOVED, and that asymmetry is the dangerous direction because independent LICENSES a split. Verifying one arm required asking what verdict it received - and the reader could not answer. It prints a population and a truncated sample, so the reflex was a shell pipe over its output, which the no-chaining hook correctly refused as judgement in the turn. ⚑ AN AGGREGATE-ONLY READER CANNOT BE AUDITED AGAINST A CASE, which is a defect in the reader and not in the query. Added --named, and the arm I checked came back UNDECIDED where I had PREDICTED relation: its guard is a BoolOp of two membership tests that _called does not walk. The prediction failing is what showed the independent jump came from elsewhere rather than from misclassified relations, and it confirmed the unreadable case lands on the withholding side. ⚑ THE FIELD IS PART OF THE PROBE: ban_witness_selftest reads Bucket.n in one arm and Bucket.shape in another, same constructor and genuinely different questions, so keying on the constructor alone would fuse two arms that do not relate.}
}

@misc{gate-G40-a-membership-test-puts-the-probe-on-the-right,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Resolving a comparison by its left operand missed every membership test, where the probe is on the right and the left is a literal, and two widenings built on that wrong model failed before the function was read.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.relation_arm_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. ⚑ THE PREDICTION FAILED TWICE. I filed a_bucket_reports_where_its_decisions_concentrate as an unreadable BoolOp boundary, then widened BoolOp handling on that model, re-ran, and the arm was STILL undecided - the widening could not fire because each operand is a Compare whose left is a string literal. Only opening _called showed the Compare arm was the defect and BoolOp was a symptom. Inferring the shape was wrong on both attempts and reading the binding settled it. Undecided moved 456 to 308, relations 80 to 155, independent 118 to 191. ⚑ TWO PINNED CASES WENT RED WHEN THE WORK LANDED - the BoolOp boundary was pinned as a limitation one tick and paid down the next, which is a tracker case working rather than a regression. ⚑⚑ THE THIRD RED IS THE TRANSFERABLE ONE: the reach case asserted UNDECIDED was the MAJORITY, and three widenings falsified it. A reach floor stated as a majority claim expires the moment the reach improves, so it tracked a NUMBER rather than the property it was written to protect. Restated as that property - the reader still withholds - because a classifier reading everything would have silently promoted its unreadable cases to INDEPENDENT, the direction that licenses a destructive split. ⚑ THE PER-FILE GATE NAMED THE SEAM at eight returns, and extracting the comparison arm to its own function was the right grain rather than a limit to satisfy. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): relation_arm resolved a Compare by taking .left unconditionally. For a membership test the probe is on the RIGHT and the left is a string literal, so the guard a-not-in-got.shape-or-b-not-in-got.shape resolved to nothing. ⚑ THE PREDICTION FAILED TWICE BEFORE I READ THE FUNCTION. First I filed the arm as an unreadable BoolOp boundary. Then I widened BoolOp handling on that model, re-ran, and the arm was STILL undecided - the widening could not fire because each operand was a Compare whose left is a literal. Only opening _called showed the Compare arm was the defect and BoolOp was a symptom. That is read-the-binding, and inferring the shape was wrong on both attempts. Undecided moved 456 to 308, relations 80 to 155, independent 118 to 191. ⚑ TWO PINNED CASES WENT RED WHEN THE WORK LANDED, which is a tracker case working: I had pinned the BoolOp boundary as a limitation last tick and paid it down this tick. ⚑ AND A THIRD RED WAS MORE INTERESTING - the reach case asserted undecided was the MAJORITY, which three widenings falsified. A reach floor stated as a majority claim expires the moment the reach improves, tracking a NUMBER rather than the property it protects. Restated as the property: the reader still withholds, because a classifier reading everything would have silently promoted its unreadable cases to INDEPENDENT, the direction that licenses a destructive split.}
}

@misc{gate-G41-a-right-verdict-on-a-wrong-probe-is-a-coincidence,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Two arms read as relations on the builtins str and len, which are argument-agnostic, so the verdicts were right and the evidence would have fused any two guards sharing those wrappers.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.relation_arm_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. Auditing the relation list rather than banking it. len(a) and len(b) over different objects record one wrapper name, a false positive in the FREEZING direction that marks a splittable arm destructive-to-split. ⚑ A RIGHT VERDICT ON A NON-LOAD-BEARING PROBE IS A COINCIDENCE and survives only until an arm where the coincidence does not hold. Resolving through transparent builtins to the argument cleared both, and a_conflict_is_reported_once correctly left the relation list. ⚑⚑ THE FIX THEN REGRESSED A VERIFIED RELATION AND THE PREDICTION CAUGHT IT. I expected an_unknown_target_is_refused_by_name to resolve and it went UNDECIDED, because it binds text from str of exc and except-as is not an Assign, so resolving through the builtin found no binding. Binding a handler name to the call its try body invokes closed it, and the arm now probes plan - the call under test - rather than the incidental str. ⚑ THE REGRESSION WAS ENTIRELY IN THE WITHHOLDING DIRECTION while it stood, which is the safe side, but a regression I introduced is recorded rather than absorbed. ⚑ THE PER-FILE GATE NAMED THE SEAM AGAIN at seven returns and extracting the call arm to its own function was the right grain, the second time this reader's dispatch has been split by a refusal rather than by taste. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Auditing the relation list rather than banking it: two arms read as relations on the probes str and len - argument-agnostic builtins, not domain predicates. Reading both showed the VERDICTS were right and the EVIDENCE was not: len(a) and len(b) over different objects record one wrapper name, so any two guards sharing a builtin fuse into a false relation. That is a false positive in the FREEZING direction, marking a splittable arm destructive-to-split. ⚑ A RIGHT VERDICT ON A NON-LOAD-BEARING PROBE IS A COINCIDENCE, and it survives only until an arm where the coincidence does not hold. Resolving through transparent builtins to the argument cleared both. ⚑⚑ AND THE FIX REGRESSED A VERIFIED RELATION, WHICH THE PREDICTION CAUGHT. I expected an_unknown_target_is_refused_by_name to resolve and it went UNDECIDED: it binds text = str(exc), and resolving str through to exc found nothing because except-as is not an Assign. Binding a handler name to the call its try body invokes closed it, and the arm now probes plan - the call under test - rather than the incidental str. ⚑ THE COST WAS ALL IN THE WITHHOLDING DIRECTION while it stood, which is the safe side, but a regression I introduced is recorded rather than absorbed.}
}

@misc{gate-G42-the-splittable-population-is-dominated-by-field-pairs,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The INDEPENDENT population is dominated by guards reading different fields of ONE result, which are relations asserted jointly by design, so splitting them would destroy content the classifier was built to protect.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. Enumerating the splittable arms before splitting one. The listing shows solve.verdict with solve.conflicts, consider.verdict with consider.writable, coverage.covered with coverage.uncovered, _verdict.refuses with _verdict.state. Reading two confirms them: a_vacuous_gate_does_not_refuse_and_is_not_at_baseline says the gate cannot refuse AND it is equally wrong to call it clean, and an_unmeasurable_gate_is_not_clean_and_does_not_refuse says BOTH HALVES ARE ASSERTED because each alone is satisfiable by the wrong fix. ⚑ THE PREVIOUS TICK CAUSED THIS. Making the field part of the probe stopped Bucket.n and Bucket.shape fusing - correct there, where two fields are two questions - and wrong here, where two fields of one result are one law. A widening that fixed a false positive in the freezing direction manufactured a false NEGATIVE in the licensing direction, which is the more expensive one. ⚑⚑ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS NOT THE FIELD BUT WHETHER THE FIELDS COME FROM ONE CALL. Bucket is constructed twice with different arguments, so its reads are two experiments. A single verdict read twice is one experiment examined from two angles. Same syntax, opposite meaning, and only the binding separates them. ⚑ SPLIT-THE-INDEPENDENT IS REFUTED AS A SAFE PAYDOWN until that is read, and no arm was split. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Enumerating the splittable arms before splitting one, rather than trusting the INDEPENDENT count. The listing is dominated by guards probing DIFFERENT FIELDS OF ONE RESULT - solve.verdict with solve.conflicts, consider.verdict with consider.writable, coverage.covered with coverage.uncovered, _verdict.refuses with _verdict.state. Reading two confirms they are relations: a_vacuous_gate_does_not_refuse_and_is_not_at_baseline says the gate cannot refuse AND it is equally wrong to call it clean, one law over two fields, and an_unmeasurable_gate_is_not_clean_and_does_not_refuse says BOTH HALVES ARE ASSERTED because each alone is satisfiable by the wrong fix. ⚑ THE PREVIOUS TICK CAUSED THIS. I made the field part of the probe so Bucket.n and Bucket.shape would not fuse - correct for that pair, where two fields are two questions, and wrong here where two fields of one result are asserted jointly by design. A widening that fixed one false positive manufactured a false NEGATIVE in the licensing direction. ⚑⚑ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS NOT THE FIELD, IT IS WHETHER THE FIELDS COME FROM ONE CALL. Bucket is constructed twice with different arguments, so its two reads are two experiments. A single verdict read twice is one experiment examined from two angles. Same syntax, opposite meaning, and only the binding tells them apart. ⚑ SPLIT-THE-INDEPENDENT IS REFUTED AS A SAFE PAYDOWN until that distinction is read, and no arm was split this tick.}
}

@misc{gate-G43-the-listing-mode-refuted-the-symbol-that-motivated-it,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A listing mode built to start a paydown refuted that paydown on its first run, because a count said the population was safe to split and the enumeration showed it was not.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.relation_arm_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. The relation_arm census spells out only RELATIONS, so the population the reader exists to make actionable was the one it would not name - gate-G39's aggregate-vs-case gap in the other column. A count says how much work there is and never which work to start. --splittable lists each arm with its probes, because the probes ARE the seam a split would cut on. ⚑ IT REFUTED ITS OWN MOTIVATING SYMBOL IMMEDIATELY: the listing is dominated by guards over different fields of ONE result, which are relations asserted jointly by design. See gate-G42. No arm was split. ⚑⚑ HAD I TRUSTED THE COUNT I WOULD HAVE DESTROYED CONTENT ACROSS THE WHOLE INDEPENDENT SET, which is the strongest argument in this arc for making a population enumerable rather than reporting its size. The suite asserts that neither a relation nor an undecided arm may appear in that listing, because either one there is a licensed destruction. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The relation_arm census spells out only RELATIONS, so the population the reader exists to make actionable - the splittable arms - was the one it would not name. That is gate-G39's aggregate-vs-case gap in the other column: a count says how much work there is and never which work to start. Added --splittable, which lists each arm with its probes because the probes ARE the seam a split would cut on. ⚑ THE MODE REFUTED ITS OWN MOTIVATING SYMBOL ON FIRST RUN. I built it to start split-the-independent and the listing showed that population is dominated by guards over different fields of ONE result - relations asserted jointly by design. No arm was split. ⚑ A COUNT SAID THE PAYDOWN WAS SAFE AND THE ENUMERATION SAID IT WAS NOT, which is the strongest argument in this arc for making a population enumerable rather than reporting its size. Had I trusted the INDEPENDENT count I would have destroyed content in 169 arms. The suite asserts no relation and no undecided arm can appear in that listing, because either one there is a licensed destruction.}
}

@misc{gate-G44-call-identity-is-not-the-relation-discriminator,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Keying the relation verdict on which CALL produced the probed value fixes the field-pair arms and breaks the two-construction arms, because both syntactic shapes occur in both meanings.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. gate-G42 proposed call identity as the discriminator. Implemented and refuted by measurement. It fixed a_vacuous_gate_does_not_refuse_and_is_not_at_baseline, which went independent to relation on two fields of one _verdict call - correct, since its arm says the gate cannot refuse AND it is equally wrong to call it clean. ⚑ AND IT BROKE each_bucket_reports_its_own_added_keys IN THE SAME MOVE, which became INDEPENDENT on Bucket built at two lines - while that arm's docstring says the populated and empty buckets are ONE LAW AT TWO POINTS, DELIBERATELY LEFT FUSED. ⚑⚑ TWO FIELDS OF ONE CALL CAN BE ONE LAW OR TWO QUESTIONS, AND TWO CONSTRUCTIONS CAN BE ONE LAW OR TWO EXPERIMENTS. The syntax does not carry the distinction. What does carry it is written in the docstrings - both arms state their own status in prose - and no AST reading reaches that. ⚑ REVERTED RATHER THAN SHIPPED, because trading a false negative in the licensing direction for a false positive in the freezing direction is not progress, and a reader wrong in both directions on different populations is worse than one that withholds. The suite returned to green unchanged and the population is byte-identical to before the experiment. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G42 proposed that the discriminator between a relation and a bundle is whether the probed fields come from ONE call. Implemented it - tag each binding with its call site, key the verdict on the call rather than the field - and it is REFUTED by measurement. It fixed the field-pair arms: a_vacuous_gate_does_not_refuse_and_is_not_at_baseline went from independent to relation on _verdict@106.refuses beside _verdict@106.state, which is correct because its arm says the gate cannot refuse AND it is equally wrong to call it clean. ⚑ AND IT BROKE THE TWO-CONSTRUCTION ARMS IN THE SAME MOVE. each_bucket_reports_its_own_added_keys became INDEPENDENT on Bucket@209.n beside Bucket@213.n - and that arm's own docstring says the populated and empty buckets are ONE LAW AT TWO POINTS, DELIBERATELY LEFT FUSED. ⚑⚑ SO BOTH SYNTACTIC SHAPES OCCUR IN BOTH MEANINGS. Two fields of one call can be one law or two questions. Two constructions can be one law or two experiments. The syntax does not decide it, and what does decide it is written in the docstrings, which no AST reading reaches. ⚑ REVERTED RATHER THAN SHIPPED. Trading a false negative in the licensing direction for a false positive in the freezing direction is not progress, and a reader that is wrong in both directions on different populations is worse than one that withholds. The INDEPENDENT column stays untrusted.}
}

@misc{gate-G45-the-arms-declare-their-own-jointness-in-prose,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {More than half the structurally-INDEPENDENT arms declare their claims joint in their own docstrings, in three measured prose forms, which is the distinction no syntactic reading could carry.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.joint_prose_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. gate-G44 established that both syntactic shapes occur in both meanings and that the distinction is written in the prose. joint_prose reads it: CONJUNCTION as in names what is covered AND what is not, CONTRAST as in returns the seed it found not merely True, and DECLARED FUSION as in BOTH HALVES ARE ASSERTED. ⚑ IT REPORTS A FLOOR AND THE SUITE ASSERTS THE ASYMMETRY IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. A hit is evidence FOR jointness and a miss is SILENCE, never evidence against, because an arm may be joint and say nothing. A prose reader returning independent from an absent word would be the third refuted discriminator and would fail in the licensing direction, which is the expensive one. ⚑⚑ MY DOUBT ABOUT THE HITS WAS THE ERROR AND THAT IS THE THIRD FAILED PREDICTION IN TWO TICKS. I judged two contrast matches incidental and opened both. a_missing_census_is_unread asserts known is false AND why names UNREAD - the rather-than in its docstring is exactly what its guards implement. omitting_the_builder_skips_the_build asserts three properties of one skipped path, and its docstring says an absent CALL not an absent MESSAGE, which is why more than one guard is needed. The live cases therefore read those arms rather than my summary of them. ⚑ THE READER IS A SECOND OPINION, not a replacement: relation_arm reads structure and this reads intent, and the useful reading is where they DISAGREE. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G44 established that no syntactic reading separates a relation from a bundle, because both shapes occur in both meanings, and that what carries the distinction is written in the docstrings. joint_prose reads that: three measured prose forms - CONJUNCTION (names what is covered AND what is not), CONTRAST (returns the seed it found, not merely True), and DECLARED FUSION (BOTH HALVES ARE ASSERTED). Run over the structurally-INDEPENDENT population, MORE THAN HALF declare themselves joint. ⚑ IT IS A FLOOR AND THE SUITE ASSERTS THAT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. A hit is evidence FOR jointness and a miss is SILENCE, never evidence against - an arm may be joint and say nothing. A prose reader returning independent from an absent word would be the third refuted discriminator and would fail in the licensing direction. ⚑⚑ AND MY DOUBT ABOUT THE HITS WAS THE ERROR, WHICH IS THE THIRD FAILED PREDICTION IN TWO TICKS. I judged two contrast matches incidental and opened both: a_missing_census_is_unread asserts known is false AND why names UNREAD, and omitting_the_builder_skips_the_build asserts three properties of one skipped path. Both genuinely joint. The prose was stronger evidence than I credited, so the live cases read those arms rather than my summary of them.}
}

@misc{gate-G46-the-conjunction-signal-fires-on-the-design-paragraph,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The conjunction prose signal fires on the design paragraph rather than the claim structure, so both audited conjunction hits were false while all three contrast and declared hits were real.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.joint_prose_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. Auditing the prose reader's hits rather than banking them. a_binding_runs is a sequential pipeline - unbound then raises then empty - three claims about successive stages where each is a precondition for the next. a_stamp_path_cannot_collide_across_directories asserts two independent properties over two stamp calls. Both matched an and that sits in prose explaining why the arm exists, not in prose about what its guards assert. ⚑ THAT IS A CLASS-LEVEL DISTINCTION AND NOT NOISE: the weak signal reads prose ABOUT the arm and the strong signals read prose about the CLAIMS. ⚑⚑ THE CLASS IS REPORTED AND NOT COUNTED. Dropping it would lose the evidence that it fires, and counting it would freeze splittable arms - the over-reach that already cost two structural discriminators. The joint population narrowed by the whole conjunction class. ⚑ AND MY SAMPLING HAD BEEN CONFIRMING RATHER THAN TESTING. Three earlier audits all validated the reader, which is what prompted opening the class I had predicted was weakest instead of another likely hit. A reader audited only where it succeeds has not been audited, and that is the transferable half. ⚑ ONE SUITE CASE WAS REPAIRED IN PASSING: it asserted a count by slicing the rendered text around a column label, which passes for the wrong reason the moment a row moves. It now asserts the property on the record. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Auditing the prose reader's hits rather than banking them. Five opened: all three contrast and declared matches are REAL, and BOTH conjunction matches are FALSE. a_binding_runs is a sequential pipeline - unbound, then raises, then empty - three claims about successive stages where each is a precondition for the next. a_stamp_path_cannot_collide_across_directories asserts two independent properties over two stamp calls. ⚑ BOTH MATCHED AN and IN THE DESIGN PARAGRAPH, which explains why the arm exists rather than describing what its guards assert. That is a CLASS-LEVEL distinction and not noise: the weak signal reads prose ABOUT the arm, the strong signals read prose about the CLAIMS. ⚑⚑ THE CLASS IS NOW REPORTED AND NOT COUNTED. Dropping it would lose the evidence that it fires and counting it would freeze splittable arms - the over-reach that already cost two structural discriminators. The joint population narrowed by the whole conjunction class. ⚑ AND MY SAMPLING HAD BEEN CONFIRMING RATHER THAN TESTING. Three earlier audits all validated the reader, which is what prompted opening the class I had predicted was weakest instead of another likely hit. A reader audited only where it succeeds has not been audited. ⚑ ONE SUITE CASE WAS ALSO REPAIRED: it asserted a count by slicing the rendered text around a column label, which passes for the wrong reason if a row moves. It now asserts the property on the record.}
}

@misc{gate-G47-the-contrast-class-fails-on-a-fixture-precondition,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The contrast prose class also produces false positives when one guard is a fixture precondition, and on that disagreement the structural reader is right while the prose reader is wrong.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. Auditing where the reader would FAIL rather than where it confirms, per gate-G46. the_generator_is_not_debt has two guards: was the parse seen at all, which guards the fixture, and is it reported as debt, which is the claim. The first is plumbing, not a second half of a joint law. ⚑ TWO HYPOTHESES FAILED IN SEQUENCE. I proposed match LOCATION as the discriminator - summary line versus design paragraph - since gate-G46's conjunction failures and this one all matched deep in a paragraph. Refuted at once: omitting_the_builder_skips_the_build also matches in a design paragraph and IS joint, asserting three properties of one skipped path. ⚑⚑ THE STRUCTURAL READER ALREADY SEPARATES THEM AND MY TRUST WAS BACKWARDS. relation_arm reports the false positive as INDEPENDENT on probes _derivations and any - two different predicates, one being the fixture check - while every verified joint arm probes one predicate or one result. On this disagreement STRUCTURE IS RIGHT AND PROSE IS WRONG, the opposite of what gate-G45's second-opinion framing implied. ⚑ SO THE PAIR IS NOT PROSE-OVERRIDES-STRUCTURE. A disagreement flags an arm for a human read rather than settling it, and the fixture-precondition guard is the shape that generates the disagreement. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Auditing the contrast class where it would FAIL rather than where it confirms, per gate-G46. the_generator_is_not_debt is a FALSE positive: its two guards are a fixture precondition (was the parse seen at all) and the actual claim (is it reported as debt). The first guards against the fixture breaking, not a second half of a joint law. So the contrast class is not uniformly reliable either. ⚑ AND TWO HYPOTHESES ABOUT WHY BOTH FAILED. I proposed that match LOCATION discriminates - summary line versus design paragraph - because gate-G46's conjunction failures and this one all matched deep in a paragraph. Refuted immediately: omitting_the_builder_skips_the_build also matches in a design paragraph and IS joint, three guards over one skipped path. ⚑⚑ THE STRUCTURAL READER ALREADY SEPARATES THEM AND I HAD THE TRUST BACKWARDS. relation_arm reports the_generator_is_not_debt as INDEPENDENT on probes _derivations and any - two different predicates, one of them the fixture check - while every verified joint arm probes one predicate or one result. On this disagreement STRUCTURE IS RIGHT AND PROSE IS WRONG, which is the opposite of the reading gate-G45 suggested. ⚑ SO THE PAIR IS NOT PROSE-OVERRIDES-STRUCTURE. A disagreement is a flag for a HUMAN read, not a verdict from either side, and the fixture-precondition guard is the shape that produces it.}
