  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. mtools left this open as article-shaped, on the ground that a package coordinate for a SKILL does not exist. Measured against substrate's own refusal and both parties had the halves swapped. hook_no_chaining._refusal builds four content lines carrying the whole rule - what was refused, why the judgement belongs in a program, run ONE tool call, and if no mode answers that is WORK - then appends the skill path as a FIFTH line after the advice is already actionable. mdstruct --budget over the target reports over half its bytes in a GENERATED mode census plus a tools roster and an artifact-to-tool table: a ROSTER, not advice, and a consumer holding two hook files has neither the tools nor the census. ⚑ DROPPING THE PATH THEREFORE COSTS A CONSUMER NOTHING THEY COULD HAVE USED. ⚑⚑ BUT ONE SECTION IS FULLY PORTABLE AND THE REFUSAL OMITS IT - a chained command cannot be approved in a way that sticks, so every chain is a permission prompt to be re-granted while a named mode is approved once, and composing converts durable tooling into disposable keystrokes. Nothing there is substrate-specific. ⚑⚑⚑ THE REFUSAL IS BACKWARDS ON BOTH HALVES: it drops the portable rule and points at the unreachable roster. Inlining the portable part and dropping the pointer makes the refusal strictly MORE useful to a consumer than it is today, which is why this is a repair rather than a tradeoff. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mtools left an open article-shaped question: the fix for a dangling skill-path successor is unobvious because a package coordinate for a SKILL does not exist, so the honest successor may be to name none. Measured against substrate's own refusal, the answer is a third option and both parties had the halves swapped. READING THE BINDING: hook_no_chaining._refusal builds four content lines carrying the whole rule - what was refused, why the judgement belongs in a program, and run ONE tool call, if no mode answers that is WORK - and then appends the skill path as a FIFTH line after the actionable advice is already complete. READING THE TARGET: mdstruct --budget over the skill reports over half its bytes in a GENERATED mode census, plus a tools-that-exist roster and an artifact-to-tool table. That is a ROSTER, not advice, and a consumer holding two hook files has neither the tools nor the census. ⚑ SO DROPPING THE PATH LOSES A CONSUMER NOTHING THEY COULD HAVE USED. ⚑⚑ BUT THE SKILL ALSO HOLDS ONE FULLY PORTABLE SECTION THE REFUSAL OMITS, and it is the load-bearing argument: a chained command cannot be approved in a way that sticks, so every chain is a permission prompt that must be re-granted while a named mode is approved once, and composing converts durable tooling into disposable keystrokes. Nothing in that is substrate-specific. ⚑⚑⚑ THE REFUSAL IS THEREFORE BACKWARDS ON BOTH HALVES: it drops the portable rule and points at the unreachable roster. The repair is not a package coordinate and not silence - it is INLINE THE PORTABLE PART AND DROP THE POINTER, which makes the refusal strictly more useful to a consumer than it is today.}
