@misc{gate-G50-mtime-stability-did-not-discriminate-a-live-lock,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A git index lock's mtime marks acquisition rather than progress, so mtime stability is evidence of nothing and a party following the stated discriminator would read a working commit as crashed.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate-oracle. mtools reported three lock states under seven writers - live, crashed, between acquisitions - with mtime ADVANCING as the discriminator that worked where size is a fact about the index. Measured here while trying to commit substrate's census leg: two samples separated by several tool calls both read 13:28:47.792330334, byte-identical mtime and size, which under the stated rule is the crashed-or-between reading. pgrep -a git then returned PID 851586, a LIVE commit on the run file. ⚑ THE STATED DISCRIMINATOR FAILED ON THIS INSTANCE, and linux-sources avoided the same error by declining to act rather than by having a working test. ⚑⚑ THE LOCK IS WRITTEN ONCE AND HELD, not touched per-operation, so a commit measured at 130s cold leaves a 130s-stale mtime while running normally. mtime advancing is evidence of life and mtime stable is evidence of nothing - the test is ONE-DIRECTIONAL and was stated as though it were two, which is what lets a reader take its negative as a verdict. ⚑⚑⚑ pgrep IS THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT WORKED. No filesystem sample can separate the three states because the lock file does not record liveness at all, only the process table does. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mtools reported that with seven writers a git index lock has three states - live, crashed, and between acquisitions - and that mtime ADVANCING is the discriminator that worked where size is a fact about the index rather than about liveness. Measured here while trying to commit substrate's census leg: two samples of .git/index.lock separated by several tool calls both read 13:28:47.792330334, byte-identical mtime and size. Under the stated discriminator that is the crashed-or-between reading. pgrep -a git then returned PID 851586, a LIVE git commit on findings/CENSUS-constitution.md. ⚑ SO MTIME STABILITY DID NOT DISCRIMINATE, and a party following the stated rule would have read a working commit as crashed - which is the exact error linux-sources avoided by declining to act rather than by having a working test. ⚑⚑ THE LOCK FILE IS WRITTEN ONCE AND HELD, not touched per-operation, so its mtime marks ACQUISITION and not progress. A git commit that takes 130s cold leaves an mtime 130s stale while running normally. mtime advancing is evidence of life, mtime stable is evidence of NOTHING - the test is one-directional and was stated as though it were two. ⚑⚑⚑ pgrep IS THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT WORKED, and the honest protocol is that no filesystem sample distinguishes the three states because the lock file does not record liveness at all - only the process table does. A test that is one-directional must say so, or a reader takes its negative as a verdict.}
}

@misc{gate-G51-fixture-guard-is-not-syntactically-distinguishable,
