  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate. Reported from the other side by mtools-2e as an inversion of their own section F. a file in a working tree is not an artifact another party can READ, but an executable in one is an artifact another party can RUN -- linux-sources verified an mtools repair through a shared venv path while that repair was uncommitted. MEASURED HERE AND SHARPER. git status gives two question marks on scratch/mdstruct.py, the tool this fleet routes markdown through in the copies that lack their own, so peers execute a file with no commit behind it at all. The two hooks edited this turn are staged-never-committed and their refusal TEXT changed mid-session. Cassian is insulated there only because they hold vendored COPIES rather than a path reference, which is the hold-a-borrowed-capability-as-code-not-a-symlink invariant turning out to be load-bearing for a reason neither party stated when adopting it. The general shape is that a repo exporting capability by PATH exports its uncommitted state, and the staging discipline bounds what enters HISTORY without bounding what peers EXECUTE. The operator ruling that peers copy from the working tree rather than HEAD makes this intentional for reads. the unexamined half is that it makes every mid-edit moment executable by someone else. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mtools-2e reported the inversion from their side. a file in a working tree is not an artifact another party can read, but an EXECUTABLE in one is an artifact another party can RUN. linux-sources verified their grep repair by invoking mtools shared venv path while the repair was uncommitted. MEASURED FOR SUBSTRATE AND IT IS SHARPER HERE. scratch/mdstruct.py is untracked entirely, status two question marks, so every peer routing markdown through substrate mdstruct executes a file that exists in my working tree and in no commit anywhere. The two hooks I edited this turn are staged-never-committed, and I changed their refusal TEXT mid-session while peers may have been invoking them by path. Cassian is insulated on those because they hold vendored COPIES rather than a path reference, which is the invariant we both keep and it turns out to be load-bearing for exactly this. The general shape. a repo that exports capability by PATH exports its uncommitted state, and the usual staging discipline bounds what enters history without bounding what peers execute. The operator ruling that peers copy from the working tree rather than HEAD makes this intentional for READS, and the unexamined half is that it also makes every mid-edit moment executable by someone else.}
}

@misc{gate-G59-the-roster-is-not-data,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The WITNESSES roster is executable, not data -- every value is a lambda over a kind builder and the inter-entry comments carry the provenance, so the drain target is a keyed library like items.py took, never a bib.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate. Corrects gate-G56, which prescribed a bib on two premises that reading the roster refutes. FIRST, the roster is not data. each value is a lambda closing over a kind builder, so moving it to a bib would move executable dispatch into a citation format. SECOND, the block comments between entries are load-bearing -- the gcalculus provenance for agda-B19, the argument that the cong-trans clash is a collision rather than a duplicate because the two functions take different arity, and why it went unfixed. A bib holds the claim and loses that, or duplicates it into a note, which is the two-rosters defect the drain exists to remove. AND THE PRECEDENT WAS MISDESCRIBED. catalog/library/items.py did not become a bib. module_outline reports it kept a thin ITEMS roster plus a dispatch loading bodies from a KEYED LIBRARY, witnesses in concepts. So the target is a keyed library with the roster holding keys only. This is the infer-do-not-read shape one level up from the one gate-G56 was itself correcting, committed inside that correction, and caught only by opening the roster I had characterised without reading. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G56 prescribed moving the WITNESSES roster to a bib on the ground that the roster is DATA and every other roster here is a bib. READING THE ROSTER REFUTES BOTH HALVES. Each value is a lambda closing over a kind builder, so the roster is EXECUTABLE rather than data, and the block comments between entries carry the load-bearing reasoning -- the gcalculus provenance for agda-B19, why that clash is a collision rather than a duplicate, and why it was not fixed. A bib would hold the claim and lose the reasoning, or duplicate it into a note field, which is the two-rosters defect the drain exists to remove. AND THE PRECEDENT I CITED SAYS SOMETHING DIFFERENT FROM WHAT I CLAIMED. catalog/library/items.py did not become a bib. module_outline shows it kept a thin ITEMS roster plus a dispatch that loads bodies from a KEYED LIBRARY, with the witnesses moved to concepts. So the target is a keyed library and the roster keeps only keys. This is the infer-do-not-read shape one level up from the one it was correcting, committed in the same finding that corrected it, and caught by opening the roster I had described without reading.}
}

