@misc{gate-G62-a-defect-in-the-origin-is-not-a-defect-in-the-adopter,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A defect measured in the origin repo is not automatically a defect in an adopter that also took the REFERENT -- gate-G55's reachability premise was refuted by measurement in cassian's tree, while its portability premise held there independently.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate, correcting my own gate-G55 on cassian-observability-da's measurement. I claimed the skill path resolves in substrate and nowhere else and that their readers were therefore getting a dead reference. FALSE THERE. the file is in their tree at 10779 bytes and is the routing table hook_structural_query parses at runtime, with their own check --only routes already asserting it exists. I measured my own EXPORT and generalised to a consumer state I never read. THE CONSEQUENCE IS THAT MY FIX IS RIGHT FOR ONE POPULATION AND LOSSY FOR ANOTHER -- correct for a consumer who took only the code, and for one who took the referent too it DELETES a live pointer, which is why they have not taken the re-vendor and are sequencing it instead. THE PORTABILITY HALF SURVIVES AND WAS ALWAYS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE. their pointer is reachable and still lands a reader on a tools-that-exist roster rather than the reason to obey, and the budget figure of 21618 of 40681 bytes generated census holds against their copy. Reachability and portability are separate questions and only the second carried the argument, so a correct fix nearly looked wrong because one of two justifications did not apply. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G55 claimed the skill path resolves in substrate AND NOWHERE ELSE, so every refusal a vendoring peer emitted ended by pointing their readers at a file they cannot open. cassian-observability-da REFUTED THE REACHABILITY HALF BY MEASUREMENT. the file is in their tree at 10779 bytes and it is not incidental -- it is the routing table hook_structural_query parses at runtime to decide what it claims, and their check --only routes already asserts it is a real local file with both arms. I measured my own EXPORT and generalised to a consumer state I never read, which is the infer-do-not-read shape aimed outward at another repo. THE CORRECTION THEY HANDED BACK IS THE GENERAL FORM. a defect measured in the origin repo is not automatically a defect in an adopter that also took the REFERENT, and the fix is correct for consumers who took only the code while being LOSSY for those who took both -- it deletes a pointer that is live in their tree. THE PORTABILITY HALF SURVIVES INTACT AND IS THE INDEPENDENT ONE. Their pointer is reachable and still lands a reader on a tools-that-exist roster rather than on the reason to obey, which the budget measurement of 21618 of 40681 bytes as generated mode census establishes against their copy too. So reachability and portability are different questions and only the second one carried the argument.}
}

@misc{gate-G63-the-mode-polarity-arm-is-built,
