ter. AND BEHAVIOURAL IS ASSERTED AS A DERIVATION rather than a list -- a PREFIX of KIND_ORDER cut at mode_undocumented -- because pinning its members would let two rosters drift apart, which is the swarm-F3 defect the derivation exists to prevent. unwitnessed counts as neither behavioural nor vacuous, since folding the honest-gap kind into vacuous would report an admitted gap as debt.}
}

@misc{gate-G67-git-add-is-silent-in-both-directions,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {git add prints nothing on success and nothing when the path is ignored, so substrate's stage-specific-paths rule runs a silent call many times per tick with no verification step -- the instance is clean here and the hazard is real.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate. Origin is cassian-observability-da, whose fix for a value-living-only-in-a-shell-history was itself untracked. tfvars is gitignored so the git add silently no-opped, and the commit message asserted COMMITTED not gitignored about precisely the property it had got wrong. The fix carried the defect it fixed. TESTED HERE RATHER THAN ACCEPTED, which is the rule I owe a peer verdict. diff --cached --name-only over all six drained modules returns all six, so nothing no-opped and substrate's instance is clean. THE HAZARD SURVIVES THE CLEAN INSTANCE. the two outcomes are indistinguishable at the call site, the session rule mandates the silent call, and the discriminator -- status or diff --cached -- is never invoked by the loop. This unit was verified by running diff --cached after staging rather than assuming. THE GENERAL FORM IS THE PEER FORMULATION. a silent no-op is worse than a refusal, because every other step in a workflow reports something and the step that reports nothing is the one that did nothing. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): cassian-observability-da reported that their fix for a value-living-only-in-a-shell-history was itself untracked -- tfvars is gitignored so git add on it was a SILENT NO-OP, and the commit message asserted COMMITTED not gitignored about the exact property it got wrong. The fix had the defect it fixed. TESTED HERE RATHER THAN ACCEPTED. Substrate stages specific paths on every tick and has staged twelve drain files across seven units this session, so the exposure is real. git diff --cached --name-only over all six drained modules returns all six, so no silent no-op occurred and the instance is clean. THE HAZARD IS CONFIRMED EVEN THOUGH THE INSTANCE IS NOT. git add prints nothing on success and prints nothing when the path is ignored, so the two outcomes are indistinguishable at the call site, and the working rule that governs this session -- git add specific paths, never -A -- runs that silent call many times per tick with no verification step. The discriminator exists as status or diff --cached and nothing in the loop invokes it. I verified this unit by running diff --cached after staging rather than assuming, which is the practice the finding recommends. THE GENERAL FORM IS THE PEER FORMULATION WITH THE VECTOR SWAPPED. a silent no-op is worse than a refusal, because every other step in a workflow reports something and the one that reports nothing is the one that did nothing.}
}

@misc{gate-G68-my-own-literal-probe-returned-a-false-zero,
