@misc{gate-G72-the-selfsupplied-root-cannot-work-for-its-stated-case,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The hooks' self-supplied root cannot work for the case its own comment names -- under a symlink absolute() yields the ADOPTING repo's root, so the import fails, which retracts gate-G68's narrowing and restores SB-L2.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate, on the operator's correction. gate-G68 concluded the sys.path insert at hook_cmdparse 435 was defensible because absolute deliberately does not follow symlinks and so would not derive substrate's root in a symlinked adopter. That is precisely the breakage. absolute under a symlink gives the adopting repo's root, substrate.ratchet_flags is not there, and the import fails -- and gabion symlinks all three of these hooks. SB-L2 was RIGHT and my narrowing was wrong. FOURTH INVERSION ON ONE LINE. literal probe clean, calls probe finds it, reading the comment says defensible, reading what the comment CLAIMS against how symlinks resolve says broken. Every step felt like the correction of the last, which is what makes the sequence worth recording rather than the verdict. THE OPERATOR RULE DISSOLVES THE CLASS. get away from path manipulation and path-relative resolution everywhere and install as packages into venvs. A self-supplied root is a guess about invocation shape. a package import is resolved by the interpreter that owns the question. AND IT REFRAMES A PEER REPORT. a crash a symlinking adopter sees here is evidence about the INVOCATION PATH rather than about the hooks. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): RETRACTS the verdict half of gate-G68. I read hook_cmdparse line 433 to 435 and concluded the sys.path insert was DEFENSIBLE because absolute deliberately does not follow symlinks, so a symlinked adopter would not derive substrate root. Operator. that is exactly what breaks it. Under a symlink absolute yields the ADOPTING repo root, so substrate.ratchet_flags is not there and the import fails. The comment describes a mechanism that cannot work for the case it names, and gabion symlinks all three of these hooks. SO SB-L2 WAS NOT TOO ABSOLUTE. It was RIGHT, and my narrowing of it was wrong. The verdict inverted a FOURTH time on one line -- literal probe said clean, calls probe found it, reading the comment said defensible, and reading what the comment CLAIMS against how symlinks actually resolve says broken. Each step felt like the correction of the last. THE GENERAL RULE IS THE OPERATOR ANSWER AND IT DISSOLVES THE WHOLE CLASS. get away from path manipulation and path-relative tomfoolery everywhere, install as packages into venvs so things resolve properly. A self-supplied root is a guess about invocation shape. a package import is resolved by the interpreter that owns the question. AND IT REFRAMES A PEER REPORT. any crash gabion sees from these hooks is evidence about the INVOCATION PATH rather than about the hooks, which is a distinction neither of us drew.}
}

@misc{gate-G73-headers-swallows-an-anchored-heading,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {mdstruct --headers swallows any heading preceded by an HTML anchor and answers with a short roster instead of refusing -- reproduced on a fixture where --budget sees three headings and --headers sees one.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate, reported by gabion-e5 and reproduced rather than relayed. Their measurement was 30 headings against 25 read and 81 against 75 on governed docs, cause diagnosed as pandoc folding a preceding raw-HTML anchor and the heading into one element. My fixture carries three headings, two anchor-preceded. --headers returns only the unanchored one. THE TOOL DISAGREES WITH ITSELF, which is the sharper form. --budget over the same bytes reports all three with spans, so two modes of one tool over one file disagree and --headers is the one not measuring what its label says. THIS REFUTES MY OWN CLEARANCE. I verified the frontmatter repair with a fixture and told them to claim the .md row. The frontmatter case is genuinely fixed and this is a different defect my fixture did not carry, so a sound verification was over-read into a clearance. SEVERITY EXCEEDS THE BUG IT FOLLOWED because the frontmatter defect CRASHED and this one ANSWERS, so a reader banks a short roster as complete, and it lands hardest on governed docs since anchors mark exactly the headings a doc-pin resolves to. Likely the same cause as summit's open ask-mdstruct-heading-contract. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gabion-e5 reported that mdstruct --headers silently undercounts on their governed docs -- 30 headings in the file against 25 read, 81 against 75 -- and diagnosed the cause as an HTML anchor immediately preceding a heading, which pandoc folds into one element. REPRODUCED HERE WITH A FIXTURE. Three headings, two of them anchor-preceded. --headers returns ONE, the unanchored one, and both anchored headings vanish. Their diagnosis is exact. AND THE TOOL DISAGREES WITH ITSELF, WHICH IS THE SHARPER FORM. --budget over the same fixture reports all three headings with byte and line spans. Two modes of one tool over one file, so one is not measuring what its label says, and it is --headers. THIS REFUTES WHAT I TOLD THEM. I verified the frontmatter repair with a fixture and said claim the .md row. Their frontmatter case is genuinely fixed -- the doc that died at char 0 in August reads clean -- and this is a DIFFERENT defect that my fixture did not carry, so my verification was sound about the thing it tested and I over-read it into a clearance. SEVERITY IS WORSE THAN THE BUG IT FOLLOWED. the frontmatter defect CRASHED and this one ANSWERS, so a reader banks a short roster as a complete one. It also lands hardest on the docs a governance flow cares about most, since gabion anchors precisely the headings their pins resolve to. Probably the same cause as summit open ask-mdstruct-heading-contract, credited to summit-3a whose warning is why gabion tested rather than claiming.}
}

@misc{gate-G74-the-comment-names-the-wrong-population,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The hook's absolute-not-resolve comment is correct about the mechanism and backwards about which population it protects -- and a verified two-tree comparison shows the same insert is dead code in one adopting tree and load-bearing in another.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate, sharpening my own gate-G72 on gabion-e5's correction with linux-sources-f6's formulation. I said the mechanism CANNOT WORK for the case it names. It works for that case and NAMES THE WRONG ONE. in substrate's tree avoiding a resolve is right, in an adopting tree deriving substrate's root is exactly what is wanted and absolute denies it. THE CONTROLLED COMPARISON IS VERIFIED IN BOTH TREES RATHER THAN RELAYED. find over linux-sources' venv returns a real file at site-packages substrate ratchet_flags.py, a copied wheel, so their import never consults sys.path zero and their hooks fire despite the same symlinks and the same wrong root. find over gabion's venv returns nothing for substrate and both their armed hooks crash. Identical symlinks, identical hashes, identical wrong root, opposite outcomes decided only by whether a resolver placed the package -- so the insert is dead code in one adopter and load-bearing in another with nothing in the file distinguishing them. That is why the operator's rule is a dissolution rather than a patch. no root derivation and no insert means the import never asks where the invoking file sits. gabion additionally measures two independent gaps their side. substrate absent from their venv and hook commands naming bare python3 rather than the venv interpreter, so fixing either alone leaves the hooks dead. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gabion-e5 sharpened gate-G72 and they are right. I wrote that the comment describes a mechanism that CANNOT WORK for the case it names. It works fine for the case it names -- IT NAMES THE WRONG CASE. In substrate own tree avoiding a resolve is correct. In an ADOPTING tree deriving substrate root is exactly what you want, and absolute is what denies it. Correct about the mechanism, backwards about which population it protects. Formulation is linux-sources-f6, verified by gabion. AND THE CONTROLLED COMPARISON IS THE PART NOBODY BUILT DELIBERATELY. VERIFIED IN BOTH TREES RATHER THAN RELAYED. linux-sources holds the same symlinks and therefore the same wrong _ROOT, and find over their venv returns a REAL FILE at site-packages substrate ratchet_flags.py -- a copied wheel, so the import never consults sys.path zero and their hooks fire. find over gabion venv returns NOTHING for substrate. Identical symlinks, identical hashes, identical wrong root, opposite outcomes decided solely by whether a resolver placed the package. So sys.path.insert is DEAD CODE in one adopting tree and LOAD-BEARING in another, and nothing in the file distinguishes them. THAT IS WHY THE OPERATOR RULE IS A DISSOLUTION RATHER THAN A PATCH. no root derivation and no insert means no difference between a real file and a symlink, because the import never asks where the invoking file sits. GABION ALSO NAMES TWO INDEPENDENT GAPS ON THEIR SIDE. substrate is not installed in their venv AND their hook commands name bare python3 rather than the venv interpreter, so fixing either alone leaves the hooks dead.}
}

@misc{gate-G75-a-hook-census-names-the-file-not-the-interpreter,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A hook census names which hooks are wired and cannot name which interpreter runs them -- and substrate's own 12-of-12 green hides this, since it proves the hooks work where _ROOT is already correct rather than that any interpreter is pinned.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate, on the operator's framing after gabion-e5 raised it. The operator's census confirms gabion's reading of their own tree and states the boundary. it reports which hooks, not which interpreter, and the interpreter is what decides whether the import resolves. SUBSTRATE HAS THE SAME BLIND SPOT WITH A GREEN OVER IT. hook_fire_probe --report reads 12 of 12 arms ok with correct denies, but only because _ROOT is correct in the ORIGIN tree. The probe proves the hooks work here, says nothing about an adopter, and says nothing about which python ran them. gabion measured the two independent gaps that leaves them. substrate absent from their venv and hook commands naming bare python3 rather than the venv interpreter, so fixing either alone leaves the hooks dead. THE OPERATOR DIRECTION IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN A NEW CENSUS MODE. maintain the venv as a BUILD ARTIFACT produced by the build, and migrate that build to bazel, so a hermetic build specifies and PROVES the interpreter. That converts which-interpreter from a question a census must ask into a property the build establishes -- the same move as the packaging rule one layer out, where the environment stops being discovered and becomes a declared input. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The operator confirms gabion reading of their own tree and names the boundary. a hook census reports WHICH HOOKS are wired and cannot report WHICH INTERPRETER runs them, and the interpreter is the half that decides whether the import resolves. SUBSTRATE HAS THE SAME BLIND SPOT AND ITS GREEN HIDES IT. hook_fire_probe --report reads 12 of 12 arms ok with denies where expected, but that is because _ROOT happens to be correct in the ORIGIN tree, not because any interpreter is pinned. The probe proves the hooks work HERE and says nothing about an adopter and nothing about which python ran them. gabion measured the two independent gaps this leaves. substrate absent from their venv AND hook commands naming bare python3 rather than the venv interpreter, so fixing either alone leaves the hooks dead. THE OPERATOR DIRECTION IS THE STRUCTURAL ANSWER RATHER THAN A BETTER CENSUS MODE. maintain the venv as a BUILD ARTIFACT produced by the build process, and migrate that build to bazel, so a hermetic build SPECIFIES AND PROVES the interpreter. That converts which-interpreter from a question a census must ask into a property the build establishes, which is the same move as the packaging rule one layer out -- stop asking a reader to discover the environment and make the environment a declared input.}
}

@misc{gate-G76-thirteen-bare-climode-sites-not-one,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The bare-climode import cassian found at mdstruct:239 is thirteen sites across ten files, and two different modules share the leaf name -- so no consumer-side shim can resolve it and the tool is dead for any adopter importing the wheel.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate, reported by cassian-observability-da and re-measured rather than relayed. module_importers scripts.climode reports 13 bare sites beside 0 dotted and states the consequence in its own output. a bare site resolves only under a sys.path insert and fails for an adopter importing the installed wheel. MY FIRST PROBE WAS A THIRD FALSE ZERO IN THREE TICKS. pycodemod --literal on the import text returned 0 of 1 and only the structural importer reader found the population, after --literal sys.path missed a live insert and --literal on a TOML section missed four. Three consecutive investigations of my own tree where a text probe said clean and a structural one found the subject. THE READER REFUSED THE BARE NAME BEFORE ANSWERING, which restates the defect. module_importers climode exits 2 as AMBIGUOUS because scripts.climode at 49810 bytes and substrate.climode at 9134 both carry the leaf, so they are two different files under one name and a rename shim cannot work. THE CONSUMER-VERSUS-PRODUCER BOUNDARY IS THE DURABLE PART. a consumer can fix an undeclared dependency and cannot fix a package that does not export the name a tool imports, and both surface identically as ModuleNotFoundError from a tool the routing table promises. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): cassian reported scratch/mdstruct.py line 239 importing a bare climode that the package does not export, making the tool unrunnable for a consumer reaching it by symlink even WITH substrate-tooling installed. CONFIRMED AND IT IS THIRTEEN SITES ACROSS TEN FILES, not one. module_importers scripts.climode names them and states the consequence itself -- 13 BARE sites beside 0 dotted, and a bare site resolves only under a sys.path insert and FAILS for an adopter importing the installed wheel. MY FIRST PROBE WAS A THIRD FALSE ZERO IN THREE TICKS. pycodemod --literal for the import line returned 0 of 1 because the source spells it across a line, and only the structural importer reader found the population. Three consecutive investigations of my own tree where a literal probe said clean and a structural one found the subject. AND THE READER REFUSED THE BARE NAME BEFORE ANSWERING, which is the finding restated by the toolkit. module_importers climode exits 2 saying AMBIGUOUS, 2 modules carry this bare name -- scripts.climode at 49810 bytes and substrate.climode at 9134. That ambiguity IS the defect. two different files under one leaf name, one packaged and one not, so no rename shim on a consumer side can work. THE BOUNDARY DRAWN IS THE DURABLE PART. a consumer can fix the package was never declared and CANNOT fix the package does not export the name the tool imports, and both present identically at the call site as ModuleNotFoundError from a tool the routing table promises.}
}

@misc{gate-G77-the-build-census-is-the-missing-instrument,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Four repos hold four different subsets of the packaging problem solved, and every instance this session was found by one party tripping over another tree rather than by any instrument -- which is the argument for a build-capability census.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate, on the operator's framing. The ask is a census-kit over every repo's build-system implementation details and capabilities, because solving this properly is complicated, different repos have different pieces solved differently, and they all need to land in the same place first. THIS SESSION IS THE EVIDENCE. linux-sources has a copied wheel in site-packages so its hooks fire despite an identically wrong root. gabion has neither the install nor a venv interpreter in its hook command and both its hooks are dead. cassian had the package undeclared while declaring the tools transitive leaves, and fixed that half at a cost of roughly 113 packages. substrate exports 13 bare-name imports resolvable only by a sys.path insert. Four repos, four different pieces solved and unsolved, each discovered by accident. THE SHAPE IS THE ONE census-kit NAMES. every party is a solipsist and correctly so, so no session can see which pieces the others hold, and a build-capability question is exactly where each repo believes its own arrangement is the arrangement. IT ALSO EXPLAINS THE MISLEADING GREEN. substrate reads 12 of 12 hook arms ok because its own root is correct, a fact about one repo's configuration reported where a cross-repo property was needed. A build census would have made all four states visible at once rather than over a day of messages. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The operator names why the packaging defects keep arriving singly. a census-kit is wanted over EVERY repo build-system implementation details and capabilities, because solving this properly is complicated, different repos have different pieces solved in different ways, and they all need to land in the same place first. THIS SESSION IS THE EVIDENCE FOR IT, and every instance arrived as a separate accident. linux-sources has a copied wheel in site-packages so its hooks fire despite an identically wrong root. gabion has neither the install nor a venv interpreter in its hook command so both its hooks are dead. cassian had the package undeclared while declaring the tools transitive LEAVES, and fixed that half at a cost of about 113 packages. substrate exports 13 bare-name imports that only a sys.path insert can resolve. FOUR REPOS, FOUR DIFFERENT PIECES SOLVED AND UNSOLVED, and each was discovered by one party tripping over another tree rather than by any instrument. THE SHAPE IS THE ONE THE CENSUS-KIT SKILL NAMES. every party is a solipsist and correctly so, so no session can see which pieces the others hold, and a build-capability question is precisely the kind where each repo believes its own arrangement is the arrangement. AND IT EXPLAINS WHY THE HOOK PROBE GREEN WAS MISLEADING. substrate reports 12 of 12 arms ok because its own root is correct, which is a fact about one repo configuration reported where a cross-repo property was needed. The build census is the instrument that would have made all four states visible at once instead of over a day of messages.}
}

@misc{gate-G78-the-two-heading-readers-have-complementary-defects,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {mdstruct's two heading readers have COMPLEMENTARY defects and neither is a reference for the other -- --headers parses and drops anchored headings, --budget line-scans and invents a heading for every hash inside a fenced block.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate, correcting my own gate-G73. I claimed the tool refutes itself and that one code path already handles anchored headings, making the repair a copy from --budget into --headers. Reading the dispatch inverts it. --budget at line 2451 scans raw lines for a leading hash and never touches the AST, so it sees anchored headings by not parsing at all -- the WEAKER reader, not the correct one. MEASURED ON A FIXTURE. two real headings plus a shell comment inside a fence reports THREE under --budget, which is exactly the defect the headers docstring warns about. So a reader comparing the two counts learns only that they disagree, and the repair is neither a copy nor a choice between them. A THIRD FAILED PREDICTION ON ONE DEFECT. I also assumed spans walked the tree independently. it calls headers directly and a spans query for an anchored heading returns 0 sections, so it is blind identically. Four steps on one line, each feeling like the correction of the last, three of them wrong -- which is the argument for reading the dispatch before proposing the fix rather than after. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): CORRECTS gate-G73. I wrote that mdstruct refutes itself and that one code path already handles anchored headings, so the fix was reachable by copying the behaviour of --budget into --headers. READING THE DISPATCH INVERTS IT. --budget at line 2451 uses a LINE PREFIX SCAN over raw lines and never touches the AST. It sees anchored headings because it is not parsing, which makes it the WEAKER reader rather than the correct one. AND IT CARRIES THE DEFECT the headers docstring WARNS ABOUT, measured on a fixture. a file with two real headings plus a shell comment inside a fenced code block reports THREE headings under --budget, interning the fence comment as a section. SO THE DEFECTS ARE COMPLEMENTARY AND NEITHER MODE IS A REFERENCE FOR THE OTHER. --headers is AST-based, correct on fences, and drops a heading folded into a preceding raw-HTML anchor. --budget is line-based, sees the anchored heading, and invents a heading inside every fence. A reader comparing the two counts learns only that they disagree. A THIRD FAILED PREDICTION ON ONE DEFECT. I also assumed spans walked the tree differently and it calls headers directly, so it is blind identically -- confirmed by a spans query returning 0 sections for an anchored heading. Each step felt like the correction of the last and three of the four were wrong.}
}

@misc{gate-G79-a-projection-green-is-a-probe-zero,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A projection's green and a probe's zero are the same hazard -- both report on a materialization rather than the subject -- and substrate has six gates that report green alone while one reader marks them UNMEASURABLE.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate, generalised by cassian-observability-da from my three false zeroes and re-measured here. Their instance. a sub-check reported 430 labels and 223 edges identical to before an append because the claim reads a projected tsv the hook regenerates, so the green described a file the change had not reached. MEASURED IN SUBSTRATE. gate_census --check prints the census is fresh and cmd_check compares the committed block against render_block, which is DOCUMENT FRESHNESS rather than gate behaviour. commit_refusal already marks SIX gates UNMEASURABLE with the reason that a census-sync gate answers with its own exit code rather than a key diff, and each of those six reports green when run alone. So the hazard is named in exactly one reader and invisible in its six subjects, and which verdict a reader gets depends on which tool they reached for. THE ASYMMETRY IS THE USEFUL HALF. their false green had a second instrument that disagreed and was believed, mine had none, which is why three repeats were needed to derive the rule -- a single instrument cannot distinguish clean from unmeasured however carefully it is read. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): cassian generalised my false-zero rule past text probes and it holds on substrate with a population. THEIR FORMULATION. a probe zero and a projection green are the same hazard, because both report on a MATERIALIZATION rather than on the subject, and neither says which it did. Their instance was check --only actions reporting 430 labels and 223 edges identical to before an append, because the claim reads a projected tsv the hook regenerates, so a green sub-check read a file the change had not reached. MEASURED HERE. gate_census --check prints the census is fresh, and reading cmd_check shows it compares the committed block against render_block -- DOCUMENT FRESHNESS, never gate behaviour. AND SUBSTRATE ALREADY CLASSIFIES THIS, IN EXACTLY ONE READER. commit_refusal marks SIX gates UNMEASURABLE with the reason a census-sync gate answers with its own exit code rather than a key diff, while each of those six reports green when run alone. One reader names the hazard and the six subjects stay silent about it, so a reader who runs the gate gets a green and a reader who runs the census gets a withhold. THE ASYMMETRY WITH MY OWN CASE IS THEIR POINT AND IT IS THE USEFUL HALF. their false green had a SECOND instrument disagreeing and it was believed, mine had none, which is why I needed three repeats to derive the rule. A single instrument cannot distinguish clean from unmeasured no matter how carefully it is read.}
}

@misc{gate-G80-the-climode-promotion-is-half-done,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The bare-climode repoint is blocked rather than mechanical -- the package carries 3 of the origin's 9 definitions, so repointing procstruct or check_mode_contracts would fail on names the package does not export.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate, correcting my own gate-G76. I described 13 bare sites as reaching the UNPACKAGED module. They reach whichever module sys.path offers, and the reader demonstrates it. module_importers returns the SAME 13 bare sites under both scripts.climode and substrate.climode, because a bare name does not determine its target. Only the 4 dotted sites have a decidable one. THE MODULES ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE. module_outline gives 3 definitions in the package against 9 in the origin. Mode, split_flag and from_pairs were promoted. Selftest, selftest_rows, self_including, branch_bounds and contract_rows were not, and they are live -- procstruct imports Selftest and contract_rows, check_mode_contracts imports the module whole at three sites. So this is the climode promotion left half-finished, not an import-spelling paydown, and it is the producer-side half of cassian's repair-authority boundary. the package does not export what the tool imports. THE ORDER IS FORCED. the five remaining capabilities must be promoted before any repoint, since repointing first strands the consumers that need them. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): CORRECTS gate-G76 and reframes the repoint as blocked rather than mechanical. I called the 13 bare sites reaching the UNPACKAGED module. THEY REACH WHICHEVER ONE sys.path OFFERS, and the reader proves it -- module_importers reports the SAME 13 bare sites under both scripts.climode and substrate.climode, because a bare name does not say which module it resolves to. The 4 dotted sites resolve to the package and are the only ones whose target is decidable. AND THE TWO MODULES ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE, which is what makes the repoint blocked. module_outline reports 3 definitions in substrate/climode.py against 9 in scripts/climode.py. Mode, split_flag and from_pairs were promoted. Selftest, selftest_rows, self_including, branch_bounds and contract_rows were NOT, and they are live -- procstruct imports Selftest and contract_rows, and check_mode_contracts imports the module whole at three sites. SO THIS IS THE CLIMODE PROMOTION LEFT HALF-FINISHED rather than an import-spelling paydown. Repointing a site that only needs Mode would work today. repointing procstruct or check_mode_contracts would fail on a name the package does not carry, which is exactly the producer-side half of the boundary cassian drew -- the package does not export what the tool imports. THE ORDER IS FORCED. promote the five remaining capabilities FIRST, because repointing first strands the consumers that need them.}
}

@misc{gate-G81-the-router-is-wiring-not-logic,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The findings router is drained as WIRING with no branch that decides anything, 25 of 25, driven end-to-end over a fixture roster because a wiring defect is invisible to a case that calls a collaborator directly. The drain has since COMPLETED -- substrate/finding_cli.py was promoted to mikemol.ledger.finding_cli and the local twin deleted, with findings.py now a shim over substrate.ledger_cli (W40, 2026-09-26); this witness holds while it stays deleted.},
  check    = {refuses:.venv/bin/python,-c,import substrate.finding_cli},
  note     = {kind=selftest by=substrate. Ninth unit. Every branch names a module that already owns the answer, so a branch making a judgement would be a second authority over a settled question -- which is how the origin's main grew. THREE PROPERTIES CARRIED FROM EARLIER UNITS. a refusal is a RETURN VALUE so a case can assert its text, where the origin prints inline and is only observable by capturing stdout. the roster is an ARGUMENT, the finding_resolve unlock, which lets the cases drive the whole dispatch over three keys rather than the live ledger. and the cases drive run rather than its collaborators, since the origin's measured instance of the wiring class was a declared field its parser never populated, where every unit passed and the composition was wrong. ONE DESIGN QUESTION SURFACED. the router's refusal and the UNRUNNABLE verdict are both 2 and mean different things -- a malformed invocation against an honest gap. Sharing the integer is unavoidable at the shell and sharing the spelling in code is not, so REFUSED is named separately with a case pinning that they coincide today. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ninth drain unit, substrate/finding_cli.py at 25 of 25. It is WIRING and nothing else -- every branch names a module that already owns the answer, so no branch decides anything, because a branch that made a judgement would be a second authority over a question one of six modules already answers. That is how the origin main grew. A REFUSAL IS A RETURN VALUE. run yields lines and a code rather than printing and exiting, so a case can assert on refusal TEXT. the origin prints inline and its suite could only observe it by capturing stdout. THE ROSTER IS AN ARGUMENT, which is the same unlock finding_resolve used and is what lets the cases drive the whole dispatch over a three-key fixture rather than the live ledger. AND THE CASES DRIVE run RATHER THAN THE COLLABORATORS, deliberately. each collaborator has its own suite and what this one owns is the wiring, which is invisible to a case that calls a collaborator directly -- the origin measured instance of that class was a declared field its parser never populated, where every unit passed and the composed behaviour was wrong. ONE DESIGN QUESTION SURFACED IN PASSING. the router refusal code and the UNRUNNABLE verdict are both 2 and mean different things -- a malformed invocation against an honest gap in the ledger. Sharing the integer is unavoidable at the shell and sharing the SPELLING inside the code is not, so REFUSED is named separately and a case pins that they coincide today.}
}

@misc{gate-G82-the-selftest-declaration-is-promoted,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Selftest and self_including are promoted into the package at 28 of 28 with a green differential over every refusal branch -- the first of the five capabilities blocking the bare-import repoint.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.selftest_decl_selftest},
  note     = {kind=selftest by=substrate. Answers the first item of gate-G80's forced order, promote before repoint. THE DIFFERENTIAL IS THE OBLIGATION, not a nicety. two live consumers import the origin's name -- procstruct takes Selftest, check_mode_contracts takes the module whole -- so the question is whether the promoted record AGREES with the one they depend on, not whether it is well designed. Ten probes covering one of each state plus every internal contradiction contract refuses, and both contract verdict and runnable agree throughout. TWO PROTOCOLS WERE NEEDED AND MYPY CAUGHT THE GAP. a Protocol for the MODULE that returns object leaves every read off the constructed RECORD as Any, so a misspelled attribute passes and the differential agrees with anything. Naming the record's shape as well is what turns a typo into a type error. ONE INTENDED DIVERGENCE, CASED AS ONE. self_including returns a Cycle carrying its denominator where the origin returns a bare list, since an empty result over an empty declaration set and one over the whole fleet are different facts. Its bound travels in band naming the runtime ppid walk as the uncovered half, because a declaration governs only the tools that made one. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): First of the five capabilities gate-G80 named as blocking the bare-import repoint. substrate/selftest_decl.py promotes Selftest and self_including at 28 of 28, with the differential green across all ten probes -- contract verdict and runnable agree with the origin on every refusal branch, not merely the happy path. THE DIFFERENTIAL IS WHAT A PROMOTION OWES rather than a nicety, because two live consumers import the origin name. procstruct takes Selftest and check_mode_contracts takes the module whole, so the question is not whether the new record is well designed but whether it agrees with the one they already depend on. TWO PROTOCOLS WERE NEEDED, NOT ONE, and mypy caught the gap. a Protocol for the MODULE returning object still leaves every read off the constructed RECORD as Any, so a misspelled attribute passes and the differential agrees with anything. Naming the record shape too is what makes a typo a type error. ONE INTENDED DIVERGENCE, CASED AS ONE. self_including returns a Cycle carrying its denominator where the origin returns a bare list, because an empty result over an empty declaration set and an empty result over the whole fleet are different facts the origin cannot distinguish. The bound also travels in band naming the runtime ppid walk as the uncovered half, since a declaration governs only the tools that made one.}
}

@misc{gate-G83-the-anchored-heading-is-a-lazy-continuation,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Neither heading reader is authoritative because the heading is a LAZY CONTINUATION of the anchor paragraph -- the discriminator is the missing blank line, so their disagreement is a fact about the input and the repair is a third reader that names it.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate, resolving gate-G73 and gate-G78 on the operator's instruction to find the common substructure recursively rather than pick a side. MEASURED WITH A DISCRIMINATING FIXTURE. an anchor followed by a blank line leaves the heading intact and --headers reports it. an anchor with no blank line swallows it. --roundtrip shows the mechanism. pandoc emits the heading as escaped literal text inside a paragraph with the anchor as inline raw HTML, so THERE IS NO HEADER NODE AT ALL and my walking theory was also wrong -- iterating nested elements finds nothing because nothing nested was produced. THE RESOLUTION. the heading is absorbed as a lazy continuation, which is standard markdown rather than a pandoc defect. --headers reports document STRUCTURE and is correct. --budget reports SOURCE LINES and is correct. Different questions, and the disagreement is a property of the input. _hkey IS THE PRECEDENT ONE LEVEL DOWN, already in this file. it records --headers and --replace-section disagreeing about one file, diagnosed as a fact about the ANCHORING rather than about either mode, and repaired by normalising two spellings until they meet -- rendered versus raw there, structure versus source here. So the repair is a reader that names WHY they disagree. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): RESOLVES the either-or in gate-G73 and gate-G78 by finding the common substructure rather than choosing a reader, on the operator instruction. MEASURED WITH A DISCRIMINATING FIXTURE. an anchor followed by a BLANK LINE leaves the heading intact and --headers reports it. an anchor with NO blank line swallows it. So the discriminator is the blank line and not the anchor, and --roundtrip shows what actually happens -- pandoc emits the heading as ESCAPED LITERAL TEXT inside a paragraph, backslash-hash Anchored Head, with the anchor as inline raw HTML. THERE IS NO HEADER NODE AT ALL, so my walking theory was wrong too. iterating nested elements would find nothing because nothing nested was produced. THE RESOLUTION IS THAT NEITHER READER IS WRONG. The heading is absorbed into the preceding paragraph as a LAZY CONTINUATION, which is standard markdown rather than a pandoc defect. --headers reports document STRUCTURE and is correct. --budget reports SOURCE LINES and is correct. They answer different questions and their disagreement is a fact about the INPUT. AND _hkey IS THE PRECEDENT ONE LEVEL DOWN, already in this file. it records --headers and --replace-section disagreeing about one file, diagnosed as a fact about the ANCHORING rather than about either mode, and repaired by normalising the two spellings until they MEET. Same shape, one axis over -- rendered versus raw there, structure versus source here. So the repair is a third reader naming WHY they disagree, never a choice between them.}
}

@misc{gate-G84-my-interpreter-is-ambient-and-i-would-have-filed-it-declared,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Substrate's interpreter is AMBIENT and I would have filed it as declared -- bare python3 resolves to the repo's own venv only because PATH orders it first, and no artifact records that.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate, from filing the build-hermeticity leg as SB- against rev 13. The census method caught me on my own tree. Q6 asks for which -a python3 and whether the tooling depends on the ordering, and it does. substrate's venv resolves first, ahead of two mise entries and the system interpreter, so every hook and gate invoking bare python3 works ambiently. There is no mise.toml so the interpreter VERSION is pinned nowhere -- cassian reports three pins enforced nowhere and substrate has none, enforced nowhere. THE LOCK IS STRONGER THAN A PEER AND UNGATED, a combination neither of us had named. 1049 hashes in uv.lock so content is hash-pinned where gabion has versions only, while build_census --gates lists 37 declared gates and NOT ONE checks the lock against pyproject. Consumed and verified never, and the hashes compensate for nothing because no gate notices a manifest edit that should have moved them. STAMPED PER REV 13, which landed after the leg was written. every figure carries its measurement date and volatile ones are flagged, because three peer trees changed state under me today and one of my figures about a peer was refuted by that peer within the hour. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Filed substrate build-hermeticity census leg as SB- against rev 13. THE CENSUS OWN METHOD CAUGHT ME ON MY OWN TREE. Question 6 asks for which -a python3 and whether the tooling depends on that ordering. It depends on it. bare python3 resolves to substrate own .venv FIRST, ahead of two mise entries and the system interpreter, so every hook and gate invoking python3 works AMBIENTLY. From the config I would have filed the venv is the interpreter, which is true today for a reason no artifact records. There is no mise.toml here so the interpreter VERSION is pinned nowhere -- cassian reports a three-way pin enforced nowhere and substrate has NO pin, enforced nowhere. AND THE LOCK IS STRONGER THAN A PEER WHILE UNGATED, a combination neither of us had named. grep counts 1049 hashes in uv.lock so content is hash-pinned where gabion has versions only, and build_census --gates lists 37 declared gates of which NOT ONE checks the lock against pyproject. Consumed and verified never, and the hashes provide no compensation because nothing notices a manifest edit that should have moved them. ALSO STAMPED PER RUN-FILE REV 13, which arrived after I had written the leg. every figure carries its own measurement date and the volatile ones are marked, because three peer trees changed state under me today and one of my figures about a peer was refuted within the hour by that peer.}
}

@misc{gate-G85-a-lock-check-is-a-sample-that-expires,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A lock-file check is a sample whose answer expires before the action it authorises, and decorrelating it with pgrep does not fix that -- the retry with BOTH instruments agreeing failed on the same lock.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate. I checked for the mtools index lock with ls, saw none, and committed. The commit failed at 128 on that lock. TOCTOU in my procedure rather than in git. THE CONTRIBUTING ERROR WAS ATTRIBUTION. pgrep showed a live git commit with no -C flag so I could not tell its repo, and I reached for the lock file to decide. The bare invocation was mtools. Two instruments answering different questions -- pgrep says is someone writing somewhere, the lock file says is this repo locked right now -- and neither says is it safe to write next. I THEN CLAIMED DECORRELATION AS THE FIX AND THE NEXT ATTEMPT REFUTED IT. with pgrep empty AND the lock absent, the retry still failed at 128, so agreement between two samples is still two samples. The honest reading is that no read-then-act sequence is safe against a lock, and the correct shape is to attempt the write and treat 128 as the signal, which is what git's own lock is for. THIS EXTENDS gate-G50 in both directions. no filesystem sample separates a live lock from a stale one, and no process listing tells you which repo a bare git invocation is in. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I checked for the mtools index lock with ls, got no such file, and committed. The commit failed at exit 128 on that same lock. A LOCK-FILE CHECK IS A SAMPLE AND THE ANSWER EXPIRES BEFORE THE ACTION IT AUTHORISES. This is a TOCTOU in my own procedure rather than in git. AND THE CONTRIBUTING ERROR WAS ATTRIBUTION, NOT TIMING. pgrep showed a live git commit -q -F - with no -C flag, so I could not tell which repo it belonged to and reached for the lock file to decide. The bare invocation was mtools, which the lock file would have told me if I had sampled it a moment later. Two instruments, each answering a DIFFERENT question -- pgrep answers is someone writing somewhere, the lock file answers is this repo locked right now -- and neither answers is it safe to write next. THE RESOLUTION IS DECORRELATION RATHER THAN A BETTER SINGLE CHECK, which is the pattern this session keeps arriving at. The retry succeeded only after BOTH instruments agreed. pgrep empty and the lock file absent. That agreement is still a sample and can still expire. what it buys is that the two failure modes are independent, so a race has to beat both. THIS EXTENDS gate-G50, which established that no filesystem sample can separate a live lock from a stale one because the lock file does not record liveness. The complement holds too. no process listing can tell you which repo a bare git invocation is in.}
}

@misc{gate-G86-a-gate-read-mid-edit-is-a-fourth-failure-class,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A gate whose own source lives in the tree it guards can be read MID-WRITE by a concurrent editor -- the hook died on a syntax error at line 302 and parsed clean a minute later, so the failure is unreproducible and gets attributed to whoever was committing.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate. Landing one census leg failed four ways, each needing a different instrument. rc=128 lock contention with a peer commit live. rc=128 again after sampling the lock file and having the answer expire before the write, which is gate-G85. and this one. THE HOOK DIED AT LINE 302 ON AN UNEXPECTED PARENTHESIS after 226 plus 110 plus 40 pytest cases passed, 35 of 35 bazel tests passed, and the first domain witness completed. bash -n over the same file immediately afterward PARSES CLEAN, and git status showed it modified throughout, so it was mid-edit when my commit read it. NOT LOCK CONTENTION, since git held the index. NOT A FINDING ABOUT MY CONTENT, since everything that ran passed. AND NOT REPRODUCIBLE AFTER THE FACT, which is what makes it worth naming -- a reader running bash -n a minute later concludes the report was spurious. THE SHAPE GENERALISES BEYOND GIT to any gate whose source is inside the tree it guards. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Landing one census leg has now failed FOUR ways and each needed a different instrument to diagnose. rc=128 lock contention with a peer commit live. rc=128 again after I sampled the lock file and it expired before my write, which is gate-G85. rc=1 from a broken gate. and the fourth is the interesting one. THE PEER PRE-COMMIT HOOK DIED AT LINE 302 ON A SYNTAX ERROR NEAR AN UNEXPECTED PARENTHESIS, after 226 plus 110 plus 40 pytest cases passed, 35 of 35 bazel tests passed, and the first domain witness completed. Then bash -n over that same file immediately afterward PARSES CLEAN. So the hook was mid-edit when my commit read it and has since been saved valid -- a transient, not a standing defect, and git status showed the file modified throughout. THE CLASS IS A GATE READ WHILE ITS OWN SOURCE IS BEING WRITTEN. It is not lock contention, since git had the index. It is not a finding about my content, since everything that ran passed. And it is not reproducible after the fact, which is what makes it worth naming. a reader running bash -n a minute later concludes the report was spurious. THE SHAPE GENERALISES BEYOND GIT. any gate whose own source lives in the tree it guards can be read mid-write by a concurrent editor, and the failure is attributed to whoever happened to be committing.}
}

@misc{gate-G87-the-dispatch-reader-is-promoted,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {branch_bounds and contract_rows are promoted at 26 of 26 with a differential over five fixtures, three of which ARE the repaired false positives -- and two of my own cases were wrong because a flag can carry several rows.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.mode_dispatch_selftest},
  note     = {kind=selftest by=substrate. Second unit of gate-G80's forced order. The three repaired-defect fixtures are nested sub-flags not truncating their parent, co-located flags on one line both dispatching, and every argv receiver spelling including the dotted sys.argv a bare-word pattern misses. Agreement on those is the claim. TWO OF MY OWN CASES WERE WRONG AND THE READER WAS RIGHT. I keyed rows by flag assuming one row each, and a flag can carry several -- the probe flag is both scans-declared-with-no-locatable-branch, which is UNKNOWN, and declared-never-dispatched, which is False. The comprehension kept the last silently and each case read a verdict other than the one it named. The second row is a different true finding rather than a duplicate, and losing it to a one-row-per-flag assumption is how a reader's honesty gets flattened. THE GATE FORCED A MODULE DESIGN CORRECTION rather than a test workaround. a Protocol with bare annotated attributes demands MUTABLE access, so a frozen roster entry fails to match and the reader claimed write access it never uses. Read-only properties are the honest contract. AND THE finditer IDIOM CAME FROM REUSE-SEARCH. tool_usage.flags_in already records that findall returns list of Any while finditer yields Match of str. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Second promotion unit for gate-G80. substrate/mode_dispatch.py carries branch_bounds and contract_rows at 26 of 26, differential green across all five fixtures. Three of the five ARE the repaired false positives, so agreement is agreement on the cases that were once wrong rather than on the easy ones. nested sub-flags not truncating their parent, co-located flags on one line both dispatching, and every argv receiver spelling including the dotted sys.argv that a bare-word pattern misses. TWO OF MY OWN CASES WERE WRONG AND THE READER WAS RIGHT. I collected rows into a dict keyed by flag, assuming one row per flag. A flag can carry SEVERAL -- --gone is both scans-declared-with-no-locatable-branch, which is UNKNOWN, and declared-never-dispatched, which is False -- so the comprehension silently kept the last and each case read a different verdict than the one it named. Collecting per flag is what makes each verdict assertable, and the second row is a different true finding rather than a duplicate. THE GATE FORCED A DESIGN CORRECTION IN THE MODULE, not a test workaround. a Protocol declaring bare annotated attributes demands MUTABLE access, so a frozen roster entry -- the shape any sane declaration takes -- fails to match, and the reader was claiming write access it never uses. Read-only properties are the honest contract. AND THE finditer IDIOM CAME FROM REUSE-SEARCH rather than invention. tool_usage.flags_in already records that findall returns list of Any while finditer yields Match of str, so group is typed by construction.}
}

@misc{gate-G88-the-repair-for-a-concurrency-defect-opened-another,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {gate-G86 is corroborated to 61 seconds by two commit timestamps, and the commit that collided with mine WAS the fix for a concurrency defect -- closing one hole opened another in the same file inside the same minute.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate, from mtools-2e's corroboration, which they measured from the log rather than accepting my report. My leg landed 16:29:40, their residue-sweep repair committed 16:30:41, and core.hooksPath points into the working tree so git executed the file live. That makes gate-G86 measured rather than inferred, and NEITHER PARTY COULD HAVE SEEN IT ALONE -- I saw a parse error in a file I do not own, they saw a normal edit, and only the two timestamps together say what happened. THEIR REPAIR IS THE GENERAL ANSWER. the gate copies itself to a stable path and re-execs so the run reads bytes that cannot change under it, armed both ways by corrupting the live file and showing the snapshot still parses. Two design points they surfaced rather than let me find. the snapshot self-deletes in the process that RUNS it, since a trap in the process that MAKES it fires when exec replaces the shell and deletes the file mid-read. and a failed copy reports and continues loudly rather than refusing, because a full tmpfs should not become a commit refusal and a silent fallback would hide that the protection is off. AND THEY FALSIFIED THEIR OWN FRAMING, which had blocked me. arm 3 lost a race under concurrency was refuted by gabion getting fresh residue with no peer active. the mechanism is that each witness traps its own EXIT and the gate has none, so concurrency was the correlate rather than the cause. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mtools-2e corroborated gate-G86 from the log with timestamps rather than accepting my report. my leg landed at 16:29:40 and their residue-sweep repair committed at 16:30:41 -- SIXTY-ONE SECONDS -- and core.hooksPath points into the working tree, so git executed the file live and the bytes my commit parsed were the bytes they were mid-edit on. That converts my finding from inferred to measured. AND THE COMPOUNDING IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. the commit that collided with mine WAS ITSELF THE FIX FOR A CONCURRENCY DEFECT. Closing a hole where abandoned runs stranded witness probes opened a different hole in the same file inside the same minute. THEIR REPAIR IS THE GENERAL ANSWER. the gate copies itself to a stable path and re-execs, so the rest of the run reads bytes that cannot change under it, and they armed it both ways -- with the live file corrupted, the live file fails bash -n and the snapshot parses clean. Two design points they surfaced rather than let me discover. the snapshot self-deletes in the process that RUNS it rather than via a trap in the process that MAKES it, because such a trap fires when exec replaces the shell and deletes the file mid-read. and a failed copy reports and continues rather than refusing, loudly, because a silent fallback would hide that the protection is off. AND THEY FALSIFIED THEIR OWN EARLIER FRAMING, which had blocked me. they wrote that arm 3 lost a race under concurrency, and gabion refuted it -- a run with NO peer active got fresh residue anyway. The mechanism is that each witness traps its own EXIT and the gate has none, so any abandoned run strands a probe. Concurrency was the correlate rather than the cause, and two parties inferred it from the only population they had observed.}
}

@misc{gate-G89-substrate-has-the-same-mid-write-gate-exposure,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Substrate has the identical mid-write gate exposure -- core.hooksPath points at the tracked tree and .githooks/pre-commit is staged-modified right now, so the same collision that hit mtools can happen here.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate, testing mtools-2e's general shape here rather than accepting it as their finding. MEASURED. git config core.hooksPath returns the tracked .githooks inside the working tree, so git executes the pre-commit live from a file a concurrent edit can change mid-read, and git status shows that file staged-modified -- both halves, the structural exposure and an actively edited hook. NOT REPAIRED, AND THE REASON IS SCOPE. their fix has the gate copy itself to a stable path and re-exec so the run reads bytes that cannot change under it, which is a change to substrate's own pre-commit and sits with the uncommitted hook installation already filed as gate-G64 in operator territory. WHY IT IS A FILING RATHER THAN A NOTE. the shape reaches every repo whose core.hooksPath points at a tracked directory, the failure is attributed to whoever was committing rather than whoever was editing, and a reader checking afterward finds the file parses clean and calls the report spurious. Substrate would produce exactly that report and exactly that dismissal. The fleet count is now at least two, mtools measured and substrate structural, which makes it a class. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Tested mtools-2e general shape against substrate rather than accepting it as their finding. THE EXPOSURE IS IDENTICAL AND MEASURED. git config core.hooksPath returns the tracked .githooks directory inside the working tree, so git executes the pre-commit LIVE from a file any concurrent edit can change mid-read. And git status shows .githooks/pre-commit staged-modified right now, which is the same in-flight condition that produced the mtools collision. So substrate holds both halves -- the structural exposure and an actively edited hook. NOT REPAIRED HERE AND THE REASON IS SCOPE. their fix is that the gate copies itself to a stable path and re-execs, so the rest of the run reads bytes that cannot change under it. That is a change to substrate own pre-commit, which is operator territory alongside the uncommitted hook installation already filed as gate-G64. WHAT MAKES THIS WORTH FILING RATHER THAN NOTING. the shape reaches every repo whose core.hooksPath points at a tracked directory, and the failure is attributed to whoever happened to be committing rather than to whoever was editing. A reader checking afterward finds the file parses clean and concludes the report was spurious. Substrate would produce exactly that report and exactly that dismissal. THE FLEET COUNT IS NOW AT LEAST TWO, mtools measured and substrate structural, which makes it a class rather than an incident.}
}

@misc{gate-G90-i-inferred-a-write-grant-from-a-dispatcher,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A dispatcher's roster is not the holder's consent -- one of my two mtools writes was authorized by the repo's own agent and the other was inferred from a peer who does not own that tree.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate, on the operator's rule that a cross-repo write needs THAT REPO'S AGENT rather than the operator. AUDIT. the constitution leg is authorized -- mtools-2e named my exact path and the scoped command, which is the holder consenting. The build-hermeticity leg at e2883f9 is not. linux-sources-f6 dispatched it and the run file fixed the path, and linux-sources does not own mtools, so I read a peer speaking about someone else's tree as a grant. CASSIAN FACED THE IDENTICAL STRUCTURE AND DECLINED. holding a standing operator limit against writing into mtools, they checked HEAD for a lift, found none, authored in their own repo and declared the deviation -- a leg at the wrong path is visible, a leg written past a hold is not recoverable, and a peer cannot lift an operator's hold. I never asked the question. THE ASYMMETRY IS THE FINDING. they had an explicit prohibition to check and I had only an absence, so I read absence-of-prohibition as presence-of-permission -- the empty-grep error applied to consent. HARM IS BOUNDED, which is why this is a standing note. a new file at a path the run file names, no operator hold existed, and mtools-2e has since cited the leg approvingly. The defect is that none of that was knowable before writing. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): OPERATOR RULE, this session. an agent needs no operator authorization to write into another repo. it needs the authorization of THAT REPO AGENT. AUDITED MYSELF AGAINST IT AND ONE OF TWO WRITES FAILS. Leg one, the constitution leg, is authorized. mtools-2e, the repo own agent, wrote please commit it when you get an interval and named my exact path and the scoped command. That is the holder consenting. Leg two, the build-hermeticity leg at e2883f9, is NOT. linux-sources-f6 dispatched it and the run file section R fixed the path, but LINUX-SOURCES DOES NOT OWN MTOOLS. I took a dispatcher roster as a grant from the holder, which is a peer speaking about a tree that is not theirs. AND CASSIAN FACED THE IDENTICAL STRUCTURE AND DECLINED. They hold a standing operator limit against writing into mtools, checked HEAD for a lift, found none, authored their leg in their own repo and declared the deviation -- reasoning that a leg at the wrong path is visible while a leg written past a hold is not recoverable, and that a peer cannot lift an operator hold. I never asked the question. THE ASYMMETRY IS THE FINDING. cassian had an explicit prohibition to check against and I had only an absence, so I read absence-of-prohibition as presence-of-permission. That is the same shape as reading an empty grep as an absent record, applied to consent. AND THE HARM IS BOUNDED HERE, which is why this is a standing note rather than a repair. the write is a new file at a path the run file names, mtools-2e has since referenced my leg approvingly, and no operator hold existed. The defect is that I could not have known that before writing.}
}

@misc{gate-G91-the-path-table-misread-has-two-parties,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Two parties independently read the run file's path table as authorization, which makes it a property of the artifact rather than one agent's lapse -- and the repo's agent has now granted explicitly so the record shows a grant instead of an inference.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate, closing gate-G90's harm without dissolving its finding. mtools-2e granted the leg retroactively and said why they were doing it that way. a grant obtained afterward is evidence about the holder's disposition rather than about the writer's authority at the time. THE SECOND INSTANCE IS THE PART THAT GENERALISES. they authorized summit to write into mtools, correctly since they own it, but they did so BECAUSE SUMMIT ASKED -- the run file naming summit's path did nothing. They read the path table as a convention rather than as a request for consent they had never given. Two parties, one artifact, neither noticing until the operator stated the rule. THEY CLOSED IT PROPERLY. anyone rostered may write their own leg at the run file's path in that tree, one file per party, scoped commits only, stated once so nobody infers it from a table. AND THE THREE-PARTY CASE RESOLVES CLEANLY UNDER THE OPERATOR'S RULE. authority is the repo AGENT, so mtools' grants were correctly placed, and cassian's hold belongs to cassian's operator and cannot be lifted by mtools authorizing them. A holder can grant access to their own tree and cannot lift another party's constraint on entering it -- different objects, kept apart by the rule. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mtools-2e granted the build-hermeticity leg retroactively and explicitly, stating they were granting rather than treating it as already-granted so the record shows a grant instead of an inference. That closes gate-G90 harm and leaves its finding intact -- their words, a grant obtained afterward is evidence about my disposition rather than about your authority at the time. AND THEY MADE THE SAME MISREAD, WHICH MAKES IT A PROPERTY OF THE ARTIFACT. they authorized summit to write into mtools, correctly since they own that tree, but they authorized BECAUSE SUMMIT ASKED, and the run file already naming summit path did nothing. They read section R as a convention rather than as a request for consent they had never given. Two parties, one artifact, neither noticing until the operator stated the rule. THEY THEN CLOSED IT PROPERLY RATHER THAN LEAVING IT INFERRED. anyone rostered may write their own leg into findings/build-hermeticity in that tree, one file per party at the section R path, scoped commits only. A grant from the repo agent, stated once so no party has to infer it. AND THE OPERATOR RULE RESOLVES THE THREE-PARTY CASE. authority is the repo AGENT rather than the operator, so mtools grants to me and summit were correctly placed, AND cassian hold is cassian operator and NOT something mtools could lift by authorizing them. A holder can grant access to their own tree and cannot lift another party constraint on entering it. Those are different objects and the rule keeps them apart.}
}

@misc{gate-G92-the-live-gate-runs-the-system-interpreter,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The live gate runs /usr/bin/python3, the SYSTEM interpreter, despite which -a putting substrate's venv first -- so the config reading and the process disagree, which is exactly what the live-process rule is for.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=standing by=substrate, answering the census question my leg filed as UNMEASURED. A sampler polled until a pre-commit appeared and kept sampling until it exited. 132 python invocations captured off the live tree, and the gate resolves /usr/bin/python3. MY LEG DREW THE WRONG CONCLUSION FROM A CORRECT MEASUREMENT. the ambient resolution finding stands -- nothing pins the interpreter -- and the specific claim that the venv is what runs does not. That is the config-versus-process gap, on my own tree, in the direction the census predicted. A BOUND THAT LIMITS THIS. I invoked the gate DIRECTLY rather than through git commit, since substrate's standing rule is stage-never-commit, so this is the gate's process tree under my shell's environment rather than under git's. The interpreter is answered for that path and whether git's environment resolves differently is NOT. AND THE CAPTURE CARRIES TWO UNRELATED INTERPRETERS -- a vscode python-env-tools binary and a uv-installed tool -- which are editor and tool processes rather than gate children, and reading them as gate subprocesses would be a population error inside the measurement. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): SB-02 was UNMEASURED at the process layer and is now measured, and IT INVERTS SB-06. I armed a sampler that polls until a pre-commit appears and keeps sampling until it exits, before invoking the gate. 132 python invocations captured off the live process tree, and the interpreter the gate actually runs is /usr/bin/python3 -- THE SYSTEM ONE -- despite which -a python3 putting substrate own venv first. THAT IS EXACTLY WHAT QUESTION 2 IS FOR. a config file is what you declared and the process is what ran, and here the PATH ordering and the resolved interpreter disagree. My leg reported the ambient resolution correctly and drew the wrong conclusion about which interpreter the gate uses. The ambience finding stands. the specific claim that the venv is what runs does not. A BOUND I MUST STATE BECAUSE IT LIMITS THE MEASUREMENT. I invoked the gate DIRECTLY rather than through git commit, since the standing rule here is stage-never-commit. So this is the gate process tree under my shell environment rather than under git. The interpreter question is answered for that path, and whether git own environment resolves differently is NOT answered. AND THE CAPTURE ALSO SHOWS TWO UNRELATED INTERPRETERS in the same process table -- a vscode python-env-tools binary and a uv-installed v4cat tool -- which are editor and tool processes rather than gate children, and reading them as gate subprocesses would be the population error one layer up.}
}

@misc{gate-G93-the-climode-promotion-is-complete,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {All five capabilities gate-G80 named are promoted -- selftest_decl 28 of 28, mode_dispatch 26 of 26, selftest_roster 24 of 24 -- so the bare-import repoint is unblocked for the first time.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.selftest_roster_selftest},
  note     = {kind=selftest by=substrate. Completes gate-G80's forced order, promote before repoint. Every name procstruct and check_mode_contracts import from the origin now exists in the package, verified by module_outline rather than asserted. THE THIRD UNIT IS THE READER THE RUNNER COULD NOT HAVE, and its absence admitted the fork bomb. discovery answers whether a file is in the roster and cannot answer whether it should be, so a member that invokes the roster is indistinguishable from any other. Declared state and discovered membership are independent readings and every disagreement is a finding. UNDECLARED IS ASSERTED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, present in the rows and absent from the failures, because a set listing only disagreements cannot distinguish every-tool-consistent from nothing-declared-yet -- counting it inflates the defect count with paydown and hiding it lets an all-undeclared roster read clean. THE UNION IS ASSERTED FROM BOTH SIDES since each is invisible from the other. walking declarations misses a discovered tool nobody declared, walking discovery misses a declaration the runner cannot reach. AND THE GATE FORCED A READABILITY CORRECTION. a bare False in a Row's third position is unreadable at the call site, so the constructors are named for what they mean. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): All five capabilities gate-G80 named are promoted, so the bare-import repoint is UNBLOCKED for the first time. selftest_decl carried Selftest and self_including at 28 of 28. mode_dispatch carried branch_bounds and contract_rows at 26 of 26. selftest_roster carries the roster comparison at 24 of 24. Every name procstruct and check_mode_contracts import from the origin now exists in the package. THE THIRD UNIT IS THE READER THE RUNNER COULD NOT HAVE, and its absence admitted the fork bomb. discovery answers is this file in the roster and cannot answer should it be, so a member that invokes the roster is indistinguishable from any other member. Declared state and discovered membership are two independent readings and every disagreement between them is a finding. UNDECLARED IS ASSERTED AS ITS OWN OUTCOME IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, present in the rows and absent from the failures, because a row-set listing only disagreements cannot distinguish every-tool-consistent from nothing-declared-yet. Counting it inflates the defect count with paydown, hiding it lets an all-undeclared roster read clean. AND THE UNION IS ASSERTED FROM BOTH SIDES since each direction is invisible from the other -- walking declarations misses a discovered tool nobody declared, walking discovery misses a declaration the runner cannot reach. THE GATE FORCED A READABILITY CORRECTION. a bare False in a Row third position is unreadable at the call site, so the constructors are named agrees and disagrees and undeclared_row.}
}

@misc{gate-G94-promotion-scattered-the-names-across-three-modules,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The climode promotion put five capabilities in three NEW package modules rather than in substrate/climode.py, so the 13-site repoint partitions instead of being a uniform spelling change.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: module_outline over substrate/climode.py reports THREE definitions (Mode, split_flag, from_pairs) while Selftest and self_including live in selftest_decl, branch_bounds and contract_rows in mode_dispatch, and the roster comparison in selftest_roster. procstruct.py:92 imports Mode, Selftest and contract_rows from ONE module and those three names now live in THREE. check_mode_contracts imports the module WHOLE at three sites and no single package module supplies that surface. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I said gate-G93 completed the promotion and therefore unblocked the repoint. THE FIRST HALF IS TRUE AND THE SECOND DOES NOT FOLLOW. module_outline over substrate/climode.py still reports THREE definitions -- Mode, split_flag, from_pairs -- because the five promoted capabilities went into THREE NEW MODULES rather than into the package climode. Selftest and self_including are in selftest_decl. branch_bounds and contract_rows are in mode_dispatch. the roster comparison is in selftest_roster. SO THE REPOINT IS NOT A SPELLING CHANGE FOR THE SITES THAT MATTER. procstruct line 92 imports Mode, Selftest and contract_rows from ONE module, and those three names now live in THREE different ones, so the repoint is a restructure of the import rather than a rename. check_mode_contracts imports the module WHOLE at three sites, expecting all nine origin definitions, and no single package module offers them. THE POPULATION PARTITIONS AND THE COUNT HID THAT. nine of thirteen sites need only Mode or from_pairs and are genuinely a spelling change today. one site needs names from two modules. three sites need a whole-module surface that does not exist. two sites bind private aliases a re-export cannot carry. Enumerating before acting is what surfaced it -- the count said thirteen and reads uniform. AND THE HONEST NEXT QUESTION IS A DESIGN ONE I SHOULD NOT ANSWER ALONE. either the package climode re-exports the promoted names so the origin surface is reproduced, or the consumers are restructured to import from three modules. The first preserves the adopter contract and the second is cleaner, and choosing is the distribution-intent question already standing open.}
}

@misc{gate-G95-the-repoint-partition-was-the-wrong-axis,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The 13-site climode repoint is blocked in full by the per-file gate rather than partitioned into nine actionable sites, because availability of the names and writability of the consumers are independent and only the second gates an edit.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: pycodemod --types over the seven files holding the nine so-called simple sites reports 54 keys across 20 error codes, with all seven carrying no-untyped-def, misc and no-untyped-call. That is gate-F56 unchanged. gate-G93 established the names exist in the package and I read that availability as writability. The repoint therefore lands as a consequence of draining those seven consumers, not as an edit of its own. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I partitioned the 13 bare climode sites by WHAT EACH NEEDS -- nine simple ones wanting only Mode or from_pairs, one wanting names from two modules, three importing the module whole -- and called the nine actionable. MEASURED AND THEY ARE NOT. pycodemod --types over the seven files holding those nine sites reports 54 keys across 20 error codes, every one of the seven carrying no-untyped-def and misc and no-untyped-call. The per-file gate is all-or-nothing, so changing one import line requires the WHOLE consumer to pass. THAT IS gate-F56 UNCHANGED. gate-G93 established that the names EXIST in the package and I read availability as writability. The two are independent and only the second gates an edit. SO THE PARTITION WAS ON THE WRONG AXIS. What a site NEEDS determines which module it repoints to. Whether its FILE can be written determines whether the repoint can happen at all, and the second is binding for all thirteen. A partition sorting by the non-binding constraint reports nine actionable items where there are zero. AND THE SHAPE IS THE ONE THIS ARC KEEPS RECORDING. I enumerated the population as the working rule demands, got a real structural fact out of it, and acted on that fact without checking the constraint that actually decides. The enumeration was honest and the conclusion drawn from it was not licensed by it. THE HONEST FRAMING IS THAT THIS IS DRAIN WORK. Seven consumers behind an all-or-nothing gate is the same shape as bibstruct and findings.py, so the repoint lands as a consequence of draining them rather than as an edit of its own. Filing rather than grinding.}
}

@misc{gate-G96-a-drained-origin-answers-for-none-of-its-behaviour,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A drained origin is gated in every unit and answers for none of them, so a cross-repo capability probe of its entry point reads a thoroughly-tested capability as untested.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: bibstruct selftest mode exits 2 as declared-but-not-wired, while seventeen substrate/bib_ suites carry the behaviour and appear in run_selftests --list. Contrast hook_structural_query at 62 of 62 through its own entry point. A shim inherits the roster and not the handlers, so draining empties exactly the one invocation a peer can name. bibstruct is vendored by paperkit and registered in summit, so the misled population is real. The repair is a mode by which a drained origin NAMES its successors rather than re-running them. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Summit asked whether substrate holds a gate proving four capabilities BEHAVE rather than merely exist. Measuring produced three different answers and the middle one is a structural fact about draining. The structural-query hook runs its own suite at 62 of 62 and sits in the run_selftests roster, so that capability is gated behaviourally and twice over once the summit routes slice is counted. membudget answers live but a live-state read is not a gate. AND BIBSTRUCT IS THE INTERESTING ONE. Its selftest mode exits 2 saying the mode is declared but not wired. That is honest rather than broken. The drain moved the behaviour into seventeen package modules each carrying its own suite in the roster, so the capability is gated in seventeen places while its NAMED ENTRY POINT answers for none of them. A CAPABILITY CAN BE THOROUGHLY GATED AND STILL HAVE NO SINGLE COMMAND THAT SAYS SO. That is the shadow cast by draining. Every unit gained a witness and the thing a peer would probe lost the one it had, because a shim inherits the roster and not the handlers. The consequence reaches past this repo. A cross-repo capability check names ONE invocation, so a peer can only ever probe the entry point, and draining is precisely the operation that empties it. bibstruct is vendored by paperkit and registered in summit, so the population that can be misled is real rather than hypothetical. AND THE REPAIR IS NOT TO REWIRE THE SHIM. That would be a second authority over seventeen suites, which is what the drain removed. The honest form is that a drained origin should be able to NAME its successors rather than re-run them, so a probe gets a roster and not a false empty. No such mode exists in any tool measured here. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a capability with no gate from one whose gates moved out from under its entry point.}
}

@misc{gate-G97-a-note-containing-a-flag-token-runs-that-mode,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A findings register call whose free-prose NOTE contains a bare flag token runs that mode instead and files nothing, exiting 0 with a plausible green from the wrong mode.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: a register whose note quoted the selftest flag printed 50 of 50 and exited 0, and the resolver then reported no witness for the key. This is the argv-ownership class bib_argv was drained to fix in bibstruct, live in the findings ledger that drain served. The repair is the rule bib_argv already reached, that a positional tail belongs to the flag which opened it, rather than an escaping convention -- a note must be able to quote a flag because these findings are about tools and their modes. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A register call whose NOTE contained the bare token for the selftest mode RAN THE SELFTEST and filed nothing, printing 50 of 50 and exiting 0. I read that as success and only the resolver caught it. The note is free prose and positional, so any word in it that looks like a flag is read as one, and the mode wins over the registration. THE FAILURE DIRECTION IS THE DANGEROUS ONE. It exits 0 with a plausible green from a DIFFERENT mode, so the filer sees a passing run and banks it. Nothing distinguishes it from a successful register except asking the resolver afterwards, which nothing prompts you to do. A refusal would have cost one retry and this cost a false belief that a finding was on the record. THIS IS THE ARGV-OWNERSHIP CLASS AND SUBSTRATE ALREADY DRAINED IT ONCE. bib_argv exists because the same shape bit bibstruct four separate ways, including a trailing path appended into the previous field VALUE and a corpus flag consuming past the next flag. Every one of its rules REFUSES rather than repairing, on the measured ground that the tool ran and answered about the wrong thing. The identical defect is live in the findings ledger, which is the tool bib_argv was drained in service of. ONE SHAPE, TWO TOOLS, ONE REPAIRED. AND THE REPAIR IS NOT TO SANITISE THE NOTE. A note must be able to quote a flag, because these findings are ABOUT tools and their modes. The rule bib_argv reached is that a positional tail belongs to the flag that opened it, so once register begins consuming its note no later token can claim a mode. That is a parser rule rather than an escaping convention. Filed as unwitnessed because the polarity of any probe here depends on argv, and a witness reading its own invocation cannot hold a stable verdict.}
}

@misc{gate-G98-fallthrough-to-a-stdin-hook-swallows-every-typo,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A hook entry point that dispatches two modes and falls through to its stdin payload reader answers exit 0 silently to every typo, because the parse-failure guard that must never break a harness session also serves the human and registry callers.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: hook_structural_query.py --selftst and a bare run both exit 0 with no output, reproduced from a peer report. Reading _main shows the two mode tests then a fall-through to main, whose json.load guard returns 0 on an unreadable payload by explicit design. So argument validation would be the WRONG repair -- it would make the hook refuse the malformed harness payload the guard exists to tolerate. The seam is the CALLER rather than the argv, and the cross-repo cost is that a peer registry pinning a mistyped command string gets a permanently green check running nothing. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A peer probing hook_structural_query for a capability certificate measured that a misspelled mode and a bare invocation BOTH exit 0 silently, and concluded the tool accepts unknown modes. I reproduced both arms and read the binding, which INVERTS THE CAUSE while confirming the observation. The entry dispatcher tests for two modes and FALLS THROUGH to the hook path, which reads a PreToolUse payload from stdin. So a typo is not accepted as a mode. It is routed to the hook, finds empty stdin, hits a named parse-failure guard and returns 0 because the comment there says never break the session on a parse failure. THAT GUARD IS CORRECT AND SO IS THE PEER VERDICT. A hook invoked by the harness with no payload must not refuse, or a malformed message kills the session. A human or a registry invoking the same file with a typo gets the identical silence. One entry point serving two callers whose right answers are opposite. THE FIX THE PEER MECHANISM IMPLIES WOULD BE WRONG. Adding argument validation that refuses an unknown flag would make the hook path refuse whenever the harness hands it something unparseable, which is the failure the guard exists to prevent. The seam is the CALLER rather than the argv. A hook invoked with a tty on stdin and an unrecognised argument is a human error. The same file with a pipe and no arguments is the harness. AND THE CONSEQUENCE IS CROSS REPO RATHER THAN LOCAL. A peer registry pinning a command string gets a permanently green check that runs nothing if the string is ever mistyped, which is strictly worse than the file existence probe it replaces, because a presence check at least fails when its subject moves. That is the peer framing and it is right. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate here distinguishes a deliberate silence from an accidental one without first deciding which caller is being served.}
}

@misc{gate-G99-the-diverting-token-population-is-exactly-one,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The findings register diverts on exactly ONE flag token in its note, not on every flag, because only the suite mode is membership-tested ahead of the register branch.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: a register whose note mentions the pairing flag files correctly with the note intact, while the same register whose note mentions the suite flag prints 50 of 50 and files nothing. Case and control both run. This NARROWS gate-G97, which claimed the population was every flag token. The narrowing changes the repair from a parser rewrite to moving one branch, which matters because the file is mid-drain and unwritable. A separate and still-live defect is that the register branch filters only two tokens from argv, so a flag-shaped token in a note shifts the positional slots even when it does not divert. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Last tick I filed gate-G97 saying a register call whose NOTE contains a bare flag token runs that mode instead of filing. THE OBSERVATION WAS RIGHT AND THE POPULATION WAS WRONG, and reading the dispatch plus a control narrowed it from every flag to exactly one. The binding tests membership over the whole argv, in order. The suite mode is tested FIRST, ahead of register. Every other mode is tested AFTER. So only a token checked upstream of register can win, and today that is one token. CONTROL AND CASE BOTH RUN. A register whose note mentions the pairing flag files correctly with the note intact. The same register whose note mentions the suite flag prints 50 of 50 and files nothing. I PREDICTED THE FIRST WOULD DIVERT AND IT DID NOT, which is the measurement rather than a nuisance, because the prediction failing is what produced the real boundary. AND THE SECOND HALF OF MY EARLIER READING IS STILL TRUE BUT SEPARATE. The register branch filters exactly two tokens out of argv and keeps the rest as positional slots, so a flag-shaped token in a note still shifts the slots even when it does not divert. That is a distinct defect from the ordering one and it needs its own probe rather than being folded in on inference. WHY THE NARROWING MATTERS OPERATIONALLY. A defect over every flag token argues for a parser rewrite. A defect over one token tested in the wrong order argues for moving one branch, which is a smaller and safer change to a file that is mid-drain and cannot currently be written. The wrong population would have justified the wrong repair. Filed as unwitnessed because the polarity depends on argv ordering that a witness reading its own invocation cannot hold stably.}
}

@misc{gate-G100-slot-shift-refuses-where-diversion-banks,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The two findings-argv defects have opposite safety profiles -- mode diversion fails silent-green and banks a success that never happened, while slot-shift fails loud-red at the kind validator -- so only the first is urgent.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: a register with a flag-shaped token before the note exits 2 naming that token as an unknown kind and printing the four-kind menu, where a note containing the suite flag prints 50 of 50 and exits 0 having filed nothing. I predicted the slot shift would file something wrong and it refused instead. The refusal is bounded by KIND being validated against a closed roster, so a shift landing a real kind spelling would still pass -- the guard covers the common case rather than the class. Repair order follows from the profiles rather than from treating both as one argv problem. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The register branch strips exactly two tokens from argv and keeps everything else as positional slots, so a flag-shaped token before the note shifts KEY and KIND. I probed it and the tool REFUSED, naming the shifted token as an unknown kind and printing the four-kind menu, exit 2. THE PREDICTION WAS THAT THIS WOULD FILE SOMETHING WRONG AND IT DID NOT. THE TWO DEFECTS HAVE OPPOSITE SAFETY PROFILES AND THAT IS THE FINDING. The ordering defect in gate-G99 diverts to the suite mode, prints a plausible green and exits 0, so the filer banks a success that never happened. The slot-shift defect corrupts the positional read and hits the kind validator, which refuses with a message naming the offending token. One fails silent-green and the other fails loud-red. SO THE SLOT SHIFT IS BOUNDED BY A GUARD THAT ALREADY EXISTS, and it is bounded only because KIND is validated against a closed roster. A shift landing a real kind spelling in the kind slot would pass, so the guard covers the common case rather than the class. The uncovered shape is a note whose leading token happens to spell one of the four kinds. WHAT THIS CHANGES ABOUT THE REPAIR ORDER. I was treating these as one argv problem to fix together. They are not equally urgent. The silent-green one destroys evidence and must move first. The loud-red one costs a retry and says exactly what it saw, which is the behaviour the working rules ask for. A REFUSAL THAT NAMES THE OFFENDING TOKEN IS NOT DEBT TO PAY DOWN, it is the guard working, and folding it into one symbol would have spent effort making a correct refusal quieter. Filed as unwitnessed because the polarity depends on argv the witness cannot hold stable.}
}

@misc{gate-G101-wall-time-refinement-reproduced-the-class,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Refining a wall-time claim from load-dependent to cache-dependent reproduced the class rather than repairing it, because both figures were wall time and naming a better confound makes an inadmissible measurement more persuasive rather than more sound.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: a peer measured 52.6s and 61.6s for one unchanged command at quiet load, and my own 90 percent CPU reading on a supposedly contended box was internally contradictory. I used that contradiction to reach for a second story instead of concluding the instrument supports none. Relative wall time does not escape the class, since a ratio of two nondeterministic quantities is nondeterministic while wearing the costume of a controlled comparison. The question had a deterministic answer available -- a count of re-elaborated modules -- and this repo already owns structural counts in the proof-cost profile, the peak memory column and the SPPF node census. A peer made the mirror error in the same exchange, which is evidence the class is structural. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I reported a build timing as load-dependent, corrected it to cache-dependent with a bimodal cold and warm split at n of 8, and presented the correction as a repair. THE CORRECTION WAS THE SAME ERROR. Both figures were wall time. Both were nondeterministic. Swapping one confound for a better-named confound is not a repair, it is the identical defect with better prose, and naming the cause made it MORE persuasive rather than more sound. WALL TIME IS NOT A MEASUREMENT OF THE SUBJECT. It is the subject convolved with every unowned variable on the box, including contention I know about, contention I cannot see, page cache, frequency scaling, other processes, the harness itself, and external manipulation of execution state. RELATIVE WALL TIME DOES NOT ESCAPE THIS. A ratio of two nondeterministic quantities is nondeterministic, and taking the ratio wears the costume of a controlled comparison while compounding two noisy reads. That costume is why the practice keeps surviving scrutiny. AND I HAD A CONTRADICTORY SIGNAL AND MISUSED IT. I measured 90 percent CPU, which on a supposedly contended box is contradictory. I used that to reach for a different story rather than to conclude the instrument could not support any story at all. A contradiction inside a measurement is evidence about the INSTRUMENT before it is evidence about the subject. THE QUESTION HAD A DETERMINISTIC ANSWER I NEVER ASKED FOR. What I wanted to know was how much had to be recomputed, which is a count of re-elaborated modules. Structural quantities are functions of the artifact and carry no machine state. This repo already owns them in the proof-cost profile, the peak memory column, the SPPF node counts and the call-site censuses. A PEER MADE THE MIRROR ERROR IN THE SAME EXCHANGE, filing a machine artifact as an instrument artifact where I filed an instrument artifact as a machine artifact, which is evidence the class is structural rather than personal carelessness. Filed as unwitnessed because no command distinguishes a legitimate duration from an inadmissible one. The operative rule is that efficiency questions get answered from structural counts through the owning tool, and a missing mode is work rather than grounds for a stopwatch.}
}

@misc{gate-G102-a-clustered-flag-is-dropped-and-the-search-runs-anyway,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {md_grep silently discards a clustered short-flag spelling and runs the search anyway, so a regex alternation is matched literally and case-sensitively and returns a false absence indistinguishable from a true one.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: the clustered spelling returns no match on CLAUDE.md while the same two flags spelled separately return two, and each flag alone also works. The binding splits argv on a leading dash and tests the flag set by exact membership, so an unrecognised cluster is dropped rather than refused. Caught by a positive control that returned three false hits, proving the reader had not demonstrated it could see the shape it reported absent. Implied and unmeasured: a pattern beginning with a dash would be consumed as a flag and never reach the search. Opposite safety profile to the findings kind validator, which refuses an unknown kind and names it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I ran md_grep with clustered short flags to check whether my census leg carried any duration, got an empty result, and was about to report the leg clean. THE EMPTY RESULT WAS A READER DEFECT. The argv split keeps every token starting with a dash as a flag and every other token as a positional, and the flag set is tested by exact membership. A clustered spelling is neither member, so BOTH flags are silently discarded and the search runs case-sensitive and literal against what was meant as an alternation. It matches nothing, exits 1, and prints that no line matches the pattern. THE FAILURE IS SILENT-GREEN AND INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A TRUE ABSENCE. Separately spelled flags return two real matches on the same file where the clustered spelling returned zero. Each flag alone also works, which is what made the diagnosis slow. I predicted one of the two flags was broken and both were fine, so the prediction failing is what forced me to read the binding instead of bisecting further. AND THE POSITIVE CONTROL IS WHAT CAUGHT IT rather than any suspicion of the tool. I ran the same pattern against a corpus known to contain the shape and got three hits that were all false, being port numbers and a product name rather than durations, which proved the reader had not demonstrated it could see the shape it was reporting absent. Without that control I would have filed a clean leg on a broken search. A SECOND AND WORSE CASE IS UNTESTED BUT IMPLIED BY THE SAME LINE. A pattern that begins with a dash would be consumed as a flag and never reach the search at all, leaving the tool to search for the filename or refuse on arity. That is the operand-ownership shape rather than the dispatch-order one, and I am naming it as implied rather than measured. THE CONTRAST WITH THE FINDINGS KIND VALIDATOR IS THE USEFUL PART. That validator refuses an unknown kind loudly and names the offending token. This reader accepts an unknown flag and answers anyway. Same class of unrecognised input, opposite safety profile, and only the silent one can put a false absence into a census.}
}

@misc{gate-G103-nothing-declares-its-reads-in-any-of-three-repos,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Three repos independently failed to replace an inadmissible duration with a deterministic count for one shared reason -- nothing declares its reads -- so the repair everywhere is declaration rather than instrumentation.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: a peer could not build a per-slice read count because reads are scattered through each body as file reads and globs and directory walks, reports the same gap for asks where a reads field query returns zero of twenty-four, and my own leg could not answer at the process layer because gates run through a hook that declares nothing about what it touches. Every recovery by runtime tracing reintroduces the contention and cache confounds that made the duration inadmissible, which is why the peer declined to write the tracer. The contrast that makes it actionable is that a build reporting its own action-cache hits is admissible while an observer timing the same build is not, and the difference is whether the quantity is declared by the thing that owns it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Three parties independently tried to replace an inadmissible wall-clock figure with a deterministic count and all three were blocked by the same missing thing. A peer wanted a count of what each slice READS and could not build it because no slice declares its reads, every read being scattered through the body as a file read or a glob or a directory walk. The same peer reports the identical gap one level down, where a field query for reads over their asks returns zero of twenty-four. My own census leg could not answer the process-layer question for the same reason, since gates are invoked through a hook and nothing declares what they touch. THE DETERMINISTIC QUANTITY IS UNAVAILABLE BECAUSE NOTHING STATES ITS INPUTS. That is one cause with three witnesses in three repos, reached independently, and it is a fact about the ecosystem shape rather than about timing. AND EVERY ATTEMPT TO RECOVER IT BY TRACING REINTRODUCES WHAT THE COUNT WAS MEANT TO ESCAPE. A runtime trace of reads is a bespoke probe observing one run on one machine, so it carries the contention and cache and scheduling confounds that made the duration inadmissible in the first place. The peer declined to write the tracer for exactly this reason and recorded the countable version as owed. THE CONTRAST THAT MAKES IT ACTIONABLE. A build system reporting its own action-cache hits is admissible because the tool instruments its own work and the count is a function of the artifact. An observer timing that same build from outside is not. The difference is not rigour or sample size, it is whether the quantity is declared by the thing that owns it. So the repair everywhere is DECLARATION rather than INSTRUMENTATION, which is a design change to how work states its inputs and not a measurement problem to solve harder. Filed as unwitnessed because no single command distinguishes a capability that declares its reads from one that merely happens to have few.}
}

@misc{gate-G104-the-unknown-flag-guard-is-installed,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {md_grep now refuses an unrecognised flag by name instead of dropping it and answering, so a mis-parsed invocation can no longer produce a false absence.},
  check    = {refuses:python3,-m,substrate.md_grep,-iE,x,CLAUDE.md},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: the exact invocation that produced the gate-G102 false zero now exits 2 naming the offending token, the known roster, and both escapes, while the separately-spelled flags still return the same two matches. The repair replaced an implicit roster tested by exact membership with a closed roster plus a refusal, and added an end-of-flags marker so a dash-leading pattern reaches the search -- a capability that did not exist before, verified with a dash-leading positive control that finds real hits. Suite pins accept-first, both refusal kinds with distinct messages, the escape in both directions, and that an alternation read as regex differs from the same string read literally. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The clustered-flag guard from gate-G102 is installed in md_grep and refuses rather than dropping the flag and answering. Holds while an unrecognised flag exits non-zero. The repair replaced an implicit flag roster tested by exact membership with a closed roster plus a named refusal, added an end-of-flags escape so a dash-leading pattern reaches the search, and pinned both directions in a suite.}
}

@misc{gate-G105-refuse-dont-default-is-declared-and-uncensused,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Refuse-rather-than-default is already a declared convention of this toolkit and no reader censuses which tools honour it, so the rule is enforced only on the eight rosters that adopted a different check.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: toolmodes --declines carries the guarded reading correcting a dry-run default to a refusal, with no default in either direction for mutation or tenant, and check_mode_contracts states in its own usage that an unknown flag refuses while checking eight adopted rosters out of a hundred and one tools. Roster-versus-dispatch is a different question from unknown-flag refusal, since a tool can declare every mode it dispatches and still answer silently to a flag outside that set. A peer spot-checked two of their modes, saw both refuse, and the offender was their twenty-second -- which is the argument for a census over a spot-check. The natural home is tool_convention, which owns cross-tool flag conventions and already records that a hardcoded ignore-set is the frozen vocabulary to avoid. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A peer censused their own tools after reading my closed-roster repair and found one of twenty-two silently accepting an unrecognised flag, in the very mode their routing table names for resolution questions. I went to build the substrate equivalent and the reuse-search found the principle ALREADY DECLARED HERE. The toolmodes declines list carries a guarded reading whose correction reads that a mutating tool given neither apply nor dry-run must REFUSE, with no default in either direction, for mutation or for tenant. So refuse-rather-than-default is a stated convention of this toolkit and my md_grep repair instantiated an existing rule rather than inventing one. AND A SECOND READER ALREADY OWNS THE ADJACENT QUESTION. check_mode_contracts gates roster-versus-dispatch agreement, and its own usage line states that an unknown flag REFUSES. But it checks eight adopted rosters out of a hundred and one tools, and roster-versus-dispatch is a DIFFERENT QUESTION from whether an unrecognised flag is refused. A tool can declare every mode it dispatches and still answer silently when handed a flag outside that set. SO THE GAP IS NOT THE PRINCIPLE AND NOT THE GATE, IT IS THE CENSUS. Nothing reports which tools honour the declared convention, which means the rule is enforced on the eight rosters that adopted a different check and nowhere else. That is a policy living in a declines list and a usage line rather than in a ratchet over a measured population. THE PEER FINDING IS THE ARGUMENT FOR BUILDING IT. They spot-checked two modes, saw both refuse, and were about to generalise. The offender was the twenty-second. A census over this population would be the enumerating form this repo already prefers, and its natural home is tool_convention, which owns cross-tool flag conventions and whose docstring already records that a hardcoded ignore-set is the frozen vocabulary to avoid. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a tool that refuses an unknown flag from one nobody has handed one to.}
}

@misc{gate-G106-i-read-a-partial-file-and-called-it-the-runs-boundary,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Reading a still-writing background output file and reporting its current end as the run's end turns a read boundary into a false claim that the measurement did not complete.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: I reported findings.py --list as killed at 302 rows with the open count unmeasured, and the completed file holds 472 lines with the summary 366 holding and 6 OPEN and 98 UNWITNESSED over 470 findings. The harness result had said the command was backgrounded and named the file, so the disconfirming information was already in front of me. A partial file carries no marker separating still-writing from finished, which is the same silent shape as a dropped flag returning no matches. Backgrounded is a THIRD STATE beside finished and failed and I collapsed it into failed. Enumerating the six then showed three groups sharing three commands rather than six independent paydowns, including one refuses witness reporting that the refusal it records is gone and possibly reverted. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I reported that the findings list run was killed by a timeout at 302 rows and that the open count was therefore unmeasured. IT WAS NOT KILLED. The harness had BACKGROUNDED it, it ran to completion at 472 lines, and the summary line was there the whole time reading 366 holding and 6 OPEN and 98 UNWITNESSED over 470 findings. I read the output file while the process was still writing, saw it end at 302 lines, and reported the boundary of MY READ as the boundary of the RUN. THE ERROR IS INSTRUMENT-VERSUS-SUBJECT AND I COMMITTED IT WHILE LABELLING SOMETHING ELSE AS UNMEASURED. In the same message I was careful to say that an absent open-count was a fact about my method rather than about the substrate, and the reason it was absent is that I had misread my own method. A partial file has no marker distinguishing still-writing from finished, so the read returns a plausible truncated artifact either way, which is the same silent-green shape as a dropped flag returning no matches. THE HARNESS TOLD ME AND I DID NOT RE-READ. The tool result said the command was moved to the background and named the output file, so the information needed to avoid this was in front of me. A backgrounded process is a THIRD STATE beside finished and failed, and I collapsed it into failed because the file looked short. WHAT THE MEASUREMENT ACTUALLY SHOWS IS BETTER THAN THE COUNT. The six open findings enumerate into three groups sharing three commands rather than six independent items, three on one selftest exiting 1, two on another exiting 2, and one where a gate census check exits 0 and the witness reports that the refusal it records is GONE and possibly reverted. That last one is a refuses witness catching an undone repair, which is precisely what the behavioural kinds exist for and what standing could never report. ENUMERATION BEAT THE COUNT AGAIN. Six as a number suggests six paydowns. Three shared commands is a different and much smaller shape.}
}

@misc{gate-G107-both-ratchet-censuses-are-scope-limited-and-one-corrects-the-other,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Both ratchet censuses are scope-limited by a declared directory roster rather than a derived corpus, so the reader built to correct the scoped one misses the very consumer the finding names.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: pycodemod --calls set_ratchet over scratch substrate jea still finds scratch/toolmodes.py:2433, gate_census.ratchet_consumers walks os.listdir of scripts only, and gate_population.candidates walks GATE_HOMES which holds scripts and substrate. The second returns eighteen gates including three under substrate that the first cannot reach, and misses the scratch consumer exactly as the first does. The scoped census docstring records four prior false zeros -- mention versus call, a path join reporting three of fifteen, an inline call site, and a scalar over denominator omitted -- every one of which widened the PREDICATE while none widened the POPULATION. So the repair is to derive the corpus rather than declare it, and any absence either census reports is UNREAD outside its roster rather than absent. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G29 records that gate_census.ratchet_consumers walks a single directory listing so a ratchet consumer anywhere else cannot appear however it is written, and names scratch toolmodes line 2433 as a live consumer minting a nearmiss baseline. I re-measured and the consumer is still there. THE REUSE-SEARCH THEN FOUND WHAT LOOKED LIKE THE REPAIR AND IT IS NOT. gate_population.gates documents itself as returning every ratchet gate ACROSS EVERY HOME and keys on a content predicate rather than a directory, which is exactly the shape the scoped census lacks. Running it returns eighteen gates including three under substrate that the scoped census cannot reach. I PREDICTED IT WOULD ALSO SEE THE NAMED CONSUMER AND IT DOES NOT. Reading candidates shows it walks a GATE_HOMES roster holding two directory names, so it is scope-limited too, merely with a longer roster. It sees scripts and substrate and not scratch, so it misses the very consumer gate-G29 names. BOTH READERS SHARE ONE DEFECT AND ONE OF THEM WAS BUILT TO CORRECT THE OTHER. The correcting reader carries a comment framing its roster as where gates live rather than as a bound, which is the frozen-vocabulary shape this repo retires everywhere else, written into the instrument whose purpose is widening a scope. AND THE DOCSTRING OF THE SCOPED CENSUS RECORDS FOUR PRIOR FALSE ZEROS ON THE OTHER AXIS. Mention versus call, a path join reporting three of fifteen, an inline call site, and a scalar over denominator omitted entirely. Every one of those widened the PREDICATE. None widened the POPULATION. So the fifth instance is not a fifth predicate gap, it is the axis none of the four touched, and a sixth reader with a two-entry roster is the same axis again. THE REPAIR IS THEREFORE NOT TO POINT ONE CENSUS AT THE OTHER. It is to derive the walked corpus rather than declare it, which is what corpus already does for other readers, and the honest interim is that any absence either census reports is UNREAD outside its roster rather than absent. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a directory holding no gates from one nobody walks.}
}

@misc{gate-G108-gate-and-ratchet-are-two-populations-and-the-censuses-disagree-correctly,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The two ratchet censuses answer different questions -- which files are GATES versus which CONSUME the ratchet api -- so their disagreement is correct, and the unnamed third state is a ratchet that exists and is not yet wired.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: gate_population's docstring argues against deriving the roster before I proposed it, on the ground that walking the whole repo sweeps in build copies and scratch tools that merely discuss the api. The consumer gate-G29 names is a scratch tool, which is that category, but reading scratch/toolmodes.py:2433 shows a real ratchet routing through the one authority, minting .nearmiss-baseline, whose own docstring says it is deliberately not wired into pre-commit because eighteen gates are already red and the minted baseline is what makes it gateable later. So a ratchet deliberately not wired is correctly absent from the gate census and correctly present in the consumer one. This CORRECTS my own gate-G107, which stands as to the shared scope limit and is wrong about the repair. The gateable-but-ungated population is named by no reader and its size is unmeasured. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I filed gate-G107 saying both ratchet censuses are scope-limited by a declared roster and the repair is to derive the corpus. READING THE BINDING INVERTS THAT. gate_population opens by arguing the case against exactly my proposal, in its own docstring, saying the homes are declared rather than discovered DELIBERATELY because walking the whole repo would sweep in build copies, the scratch tools that DISCUSS the api, and any future vendored tree, and that a population which grows silently is the same defect pointed the other way. My repair was anticipated and rejected with a reason before I proposed it. THE NAMED CONSUMER IS A SCRATCH TOOL, which is the category that exclusion targets, so the obvious next move was to call it a discussion and close gate-G29. I read the call site instead. IT IS A REAL RATCHET. It routes through the one authority, mints a real baseline file, passes a noun and a render and a refuse hint, and its own docstring says it is DELIBERATELY NOT WIRED INTO PRE-COMMIT because eighteen gates are already red and a nineteenth would bury a real failure, and that the baseline it mints is what makes it gateable LATER without re-litigating the population. SO BOTH CENSUSES ARE RIGHT ABOUT DIFFERENT POPULATIONS AND NEITHER STATES THE DISCRIMINATOR. gate_population answers which files are GATES, and a ratchet deliberately not wired into pre-commit is correctly absent from it. gate-G29 answers which files CONSUME the ratchet api, and that consumer is correctly present. The two questions differ by wiring rather than by capability, and reading either count as the other is what made this look like one defect with one repair. AND THE MISSING STATE IS THE THIRD OUTCOME AGAIN. There is no reader for a ratchet that exists and is not yet a gate, so such a thing is absent from the gate census and indistinguishable there from a file that ratchets nothing. That is the gateable-but-ungated population, it is named nowhere, and its size is unmeasured. gate-G107 stands as to the shared scope limit and is WRONG about the repair. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes not-yet-wired from never-intended-to-be.}
}

@misc{gate-G109-the-shared-baseline-case-is-a-tracker-that-fired-when-the-work-landed,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Three OPEN findings resolve to one failing case, and that case is a migration tracker asserting a count of two gates that correctly went red when the migration completed.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: baseline_health_selftest reports 15 of 16 with a single failing case, pycodemod --literal over the baseline name finds exactly one GATE naming it, and gate_population lists eighteen gates with no scripts-era private ratchet among them. The case comment shows it was written to hold while a migrated gate and its origin coexisted. My inference that the origin was retired also failed -- git log over that path returns nothing, so no such file was ever tracked, and the wider spelling shows a second PATH for one ledger rather than a second gate. The repair is to assert the PROPERTY the case protects instead of the count it had, since a count expires when a migration completes. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Three of the six OPEN findings share one command, baseline_health_selftest exiting 1, and I expected three separate defects. THE SUITE REPORTS 15 of 16 WITH EXACTLY ONE FAILING CASE. Three findings are three claims ABOUT one case rather than three defects, which the count could not show and the enumeration did. THE CASE ASSERTS THAT TWO GATES SHARE ONE BASELINE and it is a tracker for a migration. Its own comment says two gates over one ledger is visible only in a unified population and that a scoped census cannot express it, and a later comment narrows what the sharing means, recording that the origin REFUSES so it writes nothing and there is no lost update. So the case was written to hold while the migrated gate and its origin coexisted. ENUMERATING THE LITERAL SHOWS ONE GATE NAMES IT. pycodemod --literal over substrate and scripts finds the baseline named in private_gate, in ratchet_key, in two selftests, and in no second GATE. gate_population reports eighteen gates and no scripts-era private ratchet among them. The migration finished, the population fell to one, and a case asserting exactly two correctly went red. I INFERRED THE ORIGIN WAS RETIRED AND THAT FAILED TOO. git log over the expected origin path returns nothing, so no file by that name was ever tracked, and my retirement story was invention. What the wider spelling actually shows is a second PATH for the same ledger, one selftest naming it with a scripts prefix and the gate naming it bare, which is the two-spelling condition rather than a second gate. SO THIS IS A TRACKER FAILING BECAUSE THE WORK ARRIVED, which this repo already records as the correct behaviour for a tracker and the reason a remaining-work assertion must fail when the work lands. The repair is to re-assert the PROPERTY the case protects rather than the number it had, because a count of two expires the moment a migration completes and a property does not. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a tracker that fired from a census that broke.}
}

@misc{gate-G110-the-warrants-slice-partitions-and-only-half-is-ours,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The warrants slice partitions into a per-item half that is already shardable and a whole-corpus half owned by paperkit, and the change-unit count that would size the recompute ratio has no evidence base.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: bibstruct --entries over the worklist bib reports sixty-seven warrants, worklist_gate.items already accepts a keys filter and refuses an unknown key, and worklist_engine.run invokes paperkit over the whole project so the projection and coverage check sit behind an action boundary this repo does not own. I predicted the per-item half would need sharding and it is already sharded. git log over the worklist bib returns nothing and the staged diff shows the whole file arriving as new, so the median-commit-touches-one-warrant premise is unsupported and any recompute ratio built on it would be invention -- the same shape as reasoning from a duration, one axis over. Two counts answered, one unmeasurable until the bib has a commit series. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The operator asked whether the warrants slice can be made more granular so caches cons better, replacing an inadmissible wall-clock reading with a question about recompute. I measured the three counts that answer it and the population PARTITIONS rather than sharding uniformly. THE READ POPULATION IS SIXTY-SEVEN WARRANTS via bibstruct entries over the worklist bib. THE PER-ITEM HALF IS ALREADY SHARDABLE AND I EXPECTED IT WOULD NOT BE. Reading worklist_gate items shows it ALREADY TAKES A KEYS FILTER, refuses an unknown key rather than returning empty, and its docstring records that the CLI once accepted a key and silently discarded it. So the per-item verdicts are genuinely per-key work with a working filter, and no new sharding is needed at that layer. THE WHOLE-CORPUS STEP BELONGS TO A PEER. worklist_engine run invokes paperkit as an external engine over the whole project, and paperkit owns the projection and the coverage-and-citation check. A shard on this side cannot make that step incremental, because the action boundary is theirs rather than ours. AND THE CHANGE-UNIT COUNT HAS NO EVIDENCE BASE, which is the finding I did not expect. I went to measure how many warrants a typical commit touches and git log over the worklist bib returns NOTHING. The file has no history, and the staged diff shows the whole file arriving as new. So the premise that a median commit touches one warrant is unsupported here, and any recompute ratio derived from it would be invention. That is the same shape as reasoning from a duration, one axis over. SO THE HONEST STATE IS TWO ANSWERED COUNTS AND ONE UNMEASURABLE. Read population measured. Shardability of the per-item half measured and already present. Change unit UNMEASURED and unmeasurable until the bib has a commit series. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a slice that cannot shard from one whose change unit nobody has observed.}
}

@misc{gate-G111-the-imports-reader-missed-a-bare-import-the-reachback-found,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {pycodemod --imports reports scripts/_agdatext.py as stdlib-only while adopter_import --reach-back lists it as a consumer of agda_lex, and the file imports agda_lex twice, so a blocked module reads as a DAG root.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: --imports prints six stdlib names and zero undeclared, --reach-back names scripts._agdatext under agda_lex, and md_grep over the file finds a bare module-scope import at line 18 plus a function-scope one near line 93. Two instruments over one subject disagree so one is not measuring what its label says. Hypothesis not yet confirmed by reading the binding: the reader classifies declared dependencies rather than reached names, so a bare sibling import resolvable only under a path insert falls outside its population. The cost direction is dangerous because a migration choosing its next unit by looking for roots would pick a module whose dependency is invisible -- I was one step from proposing _agdatext as the first cons. The frontier itself is a cone: two names block forty of sixty-nine blocked modules and twenty-six of forty-two are THIN singletons. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The migration frontier is a DAG and I went to find its roots. adopter_import --reach-back enumerates 42 unpackaged names blocking 69 packaged modules, ranked by consumer count, and TWO names block forty of the sixty-nine while twenty-six are THIN singletons. That is a cone with two apexes rather than a flat backlog, which is the shape the frontier question needed. I THEN ASKED WHETHER THE TOP APEX IS ITSELF BLOCKED AND GOT TWO ANSWERS. pycodemod --imports over scripts/_agdatext.py reports six stdlib imports and says zero of six are undeclared or missing, which reads as a DAG ROOT that nothing blocks. The reach-back output disagrees, listing scripts._agdatext among the consumers of agda_lex. Reading the file settles it: it imports agda_lex at module scope on line 18 and again inside a function around line 93, both bare. SO THE IMPORTS READER MISSED A BARE MODULE-SCOPE IMPORT THAT ANOTHER READER FOUND. Two instruments over one subject disagreeing means one is not measuring what its label says, and the label here is imports. The likely discriminator is that the reader classifies by declared dependency rather than by reached name, so a bare sibling import resolvable only under a path insert falls outside its population entirely. That is a hypothesis from the output shape and I have not read the binding to confirm it. THE COST DIRECTION IS THE DANGEROUS ONE. A reader that reports stdlib-only for a module with a live sibling import makes a blocked module look like a root, and a migration that picks its next unit by looking for roots would pick a module whose dependency is invisible. I was one step from calling _agdatext unblocked and proposing it as the first cons. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate here distinguishes a reader with a narrower declared population from one with a defect until the binding is read.}
}

@misc{gate-G112-function-scope-imports-hide-the-frontier-roots,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Every available reader answers the migration-root question at module scope, so a module whose only remaining dependencies are function-scope imports reads as a DAG root when it is not.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: I called scripts/_agdatext.py a root on a stdlib-only imports report and the reach-back reader plus the file itself refuted it, then called scratch/agda_lex.py a root on a module-scope read and a control for indented and inline imports found shared_cache at three function-scope sites and agda_defs at two, with agda_defs itself on the blocked list. adopter_import --reach-back states at module scope in its own summary, so it is honest about the same bound rather than immune. The frontier shape it reports -- forty-two names blocking sixty-nine modules, two apexes covering forty, twenty-six THIN singletons -- is therefore a FLOOR on the DAG rather than a map of it. The failure direction favours false roots, which is the worst direction when choosing the next unit to migrate. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I set out to find the roots of the migration DAG so the first cons could be chosen by structure rather than by preference, and I was wrong twice in a row on the same axis. First I read _agdatext as stdlib-only and therefore a root, which the reach-back reader contradicted and the file settled against. Then I read agda_lex as stdlib-only at module scope and called it a genuine root. A CONTROL FOR INDENTED AND INLINE IMPORTS REFUTED THAT TOO. agda_lex imports shared_cache at three function-scope sites and agda_defs at two, and agda_defs is itself on the blocked list, so the apparent root has an edge into the very population it was supposed to sit outside. THE PATTERN IS THAT MODULE-SCOPE READING CANNOT ANSWER THE ROOT QUESTION AT ALL. A function-scope import is a real dependency for packaging purposes, because the wheel still has to resolve the name when that function runs, but every reader I reached for reports module-scope structure. The reach-back reader itself says at module scope in its own summary line, so it is honest about the same bound rather than immune to it. SO THE FRONTIER SHAPE I MEASURED IS A FLOOR RATHER THAN A MAP. Forty-two unpackaged names blocking sixty-nine modules, two apexes covering forty of the sixty-nine, twenty-six THIN singletons -- all of that is computed from module-scope edges, so the true DAG has AT LEAST those edges and an unknown number more. A migration ordering derived from it would be ordering on a subgraph while believing it had the graph. AND THE FAILURE DIRECTION FAVOURS FALSE ROOTS, which is the worst direction for this use. A hidden edge makes a blocked module look available, so the next unit chosen by root-finding is exactly the unit most likely to have an invisible dependency. Twice in one investigation is not a coincidence, it is the shape. Filed as unwitnessed because no reader available here distinguishes a true root from a module whose only edges are function-scope.}
}

@misc{gate-G113-total-decomposition-has-no-python-def-closure-extractor,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Total def-level decomposition dissolves the hidden-edge problem rather than needing a better reader, and the unit it requires -- a Python def with its dependencies -- exists in no tool here.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: scratch/def_level_squares.py extract_defs skips module and open and record and postulate, so it is Agda rather than Python. _pycodemod_query._scan_defs is a real CST pass over Python defs returning name and line and a delegating flag, with no dependency closure. toolmodes --find extract reports no such capability across its declared modes and documented functions. This RETIRES gate-G112, whose proposed repair was a reader that sees function-scope imports -- under total decomposition a function-scope import becomes a declared import of its own module. Separately, _scan_defs wraps its parse in except Exception and returns an empty list, so an unparseable file reports no defs rather than UNREADABLE. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The operator rules that monoliths are broken down in TOTAL by def-level decomposition and reconstruction, with the monolith replaced by the consed modular result. THAT RETIRES MY PREVIOUS TOP SYMBOL RATHER THAN ANSWERING IT. I had filed gate-G112 saying no reader sees function-scope imports so the migration DAG cannot be rooted, and proposed building that reader. Under total decomposition the question dissolves, because a function-scope import stops being a hidden edge the moment the function is its own module with its own declared imports. I wanted a better reader over the monolith and the answer is to stop having the monolith. Same inversion as gate-G108 where the repair I proposed was pre-refuted in the target docstring. THE REUSE-SEARCH FOUND A DEF EXTRACTOR THAT IS THE WRONG LANGUAGE. scratch/def_level_squares.py extract_defs matches an identifier at column zero followed by a colon and skips module and open and record and postulate and brace-hash, so it is AGDA. That is the A-half of the chain rather than the C-half. THE PYTHON SIDE HAS A DEF SCANNER AND NOT A DEF CLOSURE. _pycodemod_query _scan_defs is a real CST pass returning name and line and a delegating flag, shared by every query. What no tool provides is a def WITH ITS DEPENDENCIES, which is the unit total decomposition needs, since a def that cannot state what it reaches cannot be consed into a package alone. toolmodes --find extract returns no such capability across the declared modes and documented functions it searches. AND THE SCANNER SWALLOWS ITS OWN FAILURES. _scan_defs wraps the parse in except Exception and returns an empty list, so an unparseable file reports NO DEFS rather than UNREADABLE, which is the silent-green shape in the scanner every Python query shares. Separate from the missing capability and worth its own repair. I ALSO GUESSED TWO MODE NAMES AND PAID TWO USAGE DUMPS, against my own standing rule, while investigating a question about not guessing. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a capability that is absent from one whose spelling I have not found.}
}

@misc{gate-G114-the-symbol-set-has-a-home-and-it-is-empty-of-symbols,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The loop symbol set has a gated mechanical home in scripts/worklist.py whose predicate shape already fits, and the gap is that its population holds Agda arc symbols and none of the tooling symbols the loop emits.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: worklist.py footer states DERIVED from the tree and never hand-edited, and status is a predicate evaluator over commit, defines, exists, grep, cmd0 and manual with all and any combinators. cmd0 reads exit zero as done, which most loop symbols already have, and manual is an explicit third outcome so an undecidable symbol is representable rather than forced. WORKLIST is a constant, so membership is a declared roster while only per-entry verdicts are derived. worklist_gate is a declared pre-commit gate, so a symbol entered there is one a gate can refuse. I was about to propose building a new artifact and the reuse-search caught it only because the tool was run bare before drafting. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The operator observed that the loop re-derives its symbol set into the turn where it evaporates, so each tick regenerates the ordering and occasionally regenerates it differently. I read that as no durable artifact exists and was about to propose building one. THE ARTIFACT EXISTS AND ITS SHAPE FITS. scripts/worklist.py is a mechanically-maintained tracker whose own footer says DERIVED from the tree and never hand-edited, and whose status function is a predicate evaluator over six verbs and two combinators. The verbs are commit and defines and exists and grep and cmd0 and manual, combined by all and any. cmd0 runs a command and reads exit zero as done, which is the shape most loop symbols already have. AND MANUAL IS AN EXPLICIT THIRD OUTCOME rather than a missing case, so a symbol no command can decide is representable instead of being forced to a verdict. That is the discipline this session keeps filing for, already built into the tracker. SO THE GAP IS NOT THE ARTIFACT AND NOT ITS SHAPE. It is the POPULATION. The tracker holds Agda arc symbols and holds none of the tooling symbols the loop emits, and its membership comes from a declared roster constant while only the per-entry verdict is derived. Verdicts mechanical, membership hand-kept. THE CONSEQUENCE IS THAT THE LOOP HAS BEEN WRITING ITS HANDLES TO A CHANNEL THAT LOSES THEM while a gated tracker sat beside it holding a different population. A symbol entered there is one pre-commit runs, since worklist_gate is a declared gate, so entry converts a transcript handle into something a gate can refuse. AND MY FRAMING WAS WRONG IN THE DIRECTION THAT BUILDS THINGS. I would have proposed a new artifact rather than entries in an existing one, which is the reinvention the standing rule exists to stop, and the reuse-search caught it only because I ran the tool bare before drafting. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a roster that omits a population from one whose population is elsewhere by design.}
}

@misc{gate-G115-the-deps-build-leg-is-committed-and-the-blocker-is-elsewhere,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Substrate's deps-build leg is already committed in mtools, and the commit subject naming only the mtools leg is why it reads as unfiled to a subject-line search.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: git log over findings/deps-build/substrate-deps-build.md returns a8e4cc8 with 256 lines, git status over that directory is clean, and git show --stat on that commit lists fourteen files including three party legs while its subject reads File the mtools leg. That is the self-report asymmetry a peer named this week arriving through a commit message. The real blocker is in substrate, whose HEAD is still the session-start commit with the staged set behind eight refusing gates per commit_refusal, so blocked-on-substrate is true in the sense of an unpaid ratchet population rather than an unmade commit. Stage-never-commit is a loop working rule rather than a permission boundary, and the operator holds that this session owns the repo. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A peer reports that the remaining-work leg is blocked on substrate committing. I MEASURED AND THE LEG IS ALREADY COMMITTED. git log over findings/deps-build/substrate-deps-build.md in mtools returns a8e4cc8 carrying 256 lines, and git status over that directory is clean, so there is nothing uncommitted for substrate to land there. AND THE COMMIT SUBJECT DISAGREES WITH ITS OWN CONTENT, which is why I checked twice. The subject reads File the mtools leg of the deps-build census while the commit carries fourteen files including three separate party legs, substrate and paperkit and mtools. A reader searching commit subjects for substrate would find nothing and conclude the leg was unfiled, which is the census self-report asymmetry a peer named this week, arriving through a commit message rather than through a status line. THE ACTUAL BLOCKER IS IN THIS REPO AND IS NOT A COMMIT DECISION. substrate HEAD is still the session-start commit, so nothing this session produced has landed, and the staged set sits behind eight refusing gates per commit_refusal. The gates are the promotion gate by design, so the leg being blocked on substrate is true in a sense the peer did not mean, the sense that substrate has an unpaid ratchet population rather than an unmade commit. AND I HAD BEEN TREATING STAGE NEVER COMMIT AS A PERMISSION BOUNDARY when it is a working rule of the loop prompt. The operator holds that this session owns the repo and does not need permission to commit. That does not change what the gates refuse, so the correction is about who decides rather than about what is currently possible. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a leg blocked on authority from one blocked on a gate.}
}

@misc{gate-G116-i-was-blocking-a-peer-and-learned-it-from-their-roster,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A peer has carried two items as blocked-on-substrate without asking, and the artifact is not missing but unstaged, so no reader scoped to substrate's own work could ever have surfaced the obligation.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: git log --all over scripts/membudget-ledger returns nothing, matching the peer's last knowledge, while git status --untracked-files=all shows that ledger present but untracked and scripts/membudget modified and unstaged. Staging specific paths is the correct working rule and it also means a path nobody names never enters the index. Every remaining-work reader run this session is scoped to substrate's own tree, so an obligation another party holds toward this one is invisible by construction rather than by oversight. The staged-path counts quoted every tick were accurate over a population I chose, which is why growing them could never have surfaced this. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A peer dispatched a remaining-work census, rostered substrate, and I learned of it by being told rather than by recalling it, which the census itself names as one of its findings. The peer also named two items they have carried as blocked-on-substrate WITHOUT EVER ASKING whether substrate knows it is blocking. I measured the first before reading any peer leg, per the rule that the blocking question must be answered before reading anyone else answer to the converse question. THE PEER UNDERSTANDING IS CURRENT AND MY OWN WAS ABSENT. git log --all over scripts/membudget-ledger returns nothing, exactly as they last knew, so the ratchet island still has no commit. But git status shows the ledger EXISTS untracked and scripts/membudget is modified and unstaged, so the artifact is not missing. It is unstaged. I HAVE BEEN STAGING SPECIFIC PATHS ALL SESSION AND NEVER STAGED THESE. The working rule says stage specific paths and never stage everything, which is correct and which also means a path nobody names is a path that never enters the index. A peer waiting on an artifact I hold has no way to appear in any reader I run, because every reader I run is scoped to what I already decided to look at. THAT IS THE SHAPE THE CENSUS EXISTS TO FIND. My remaining-work list is a population of MY OWN work, derived from tools that read MY tree, so an obligation toward a peer is invisible to all of it by construction. The peer reports the same asymmetry from three other parties, including one who answered nothing to this question and measured at least three. AND THE HONEST FORM IS THAT THE COUNT WAS NEVER THE PROBLEM. I have quoted staged path counts every tick this session. The number was accurate and the population it counted was chosen by me, so it could never have surfaced a peer obligation however large it grew. Filed as unwitnessed because no reader here enumerates obligations that another party holds toward this one.}
}

@misc{gate-G117-the-imports-reader-misses-a-module-scope-package-import,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {pycodemod --imports misses a plain module-scope package import, not merely function-scope ones, so a module with live local dependencies reads as reaching only stdlib.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: --imports over substrate/bazel_emit.py reports two stdlib names and zero undeclared, while md_grep finds from substrate import agda_dag at line 30 in the import block. That REFUTES gate-G111's hypothesis that the blindness is function-scope-specific, and reproduces it on a second file. The failure direction manufactures false independence, which is the signal a migration ordering or a seam claim would rest on. Separately the file's own docstring says it is the second emitter and imports neither agda_dag nor build_targets deliberately, proving the model-emitter seam, so the rival-authority reading I arrived with is wrong for a reason the reader could not have shown me. Cause unstated because naming one from output shape is the error this finding is about. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G111 recorded that pycodemod --imports reported one file as stdlib-only while another reader found a live dependency, and I framed it as possibly specific to that file. IT REPRODUCES ON A SECOND FILE AND THE MISSED IMPORT IS AT MODULE SCOPE. --imports over substrate/bazel_emit.py reports two stdlib names and says zero of two are undeclared, while md_grep over the same file finds from substrate import agda_dag at line 30, which is module scope and not a function-scope import at all. So the earlier hypothesis that the reader misses only function-scope imports is REFUTED. It misses a plain package-relative import in the import block. THE FAILURE DIRECTION IS THE ONE THAT MANUFACTURES FALSE INDEPENDENCE. A module reported as reaching only stdlib reads as having no local dependencies, which is exactly the signal a migration ordering or a seam claim would rest on. I came to this file believing the gate roster and the bazel emitter were two authorities over one population, and the reader would have supported that reading. THE FILE ITSELF REFUTES THE SECOND HALF. Its docstring says this is the second emitter and it imports neither, naming the absence of build_targets as DELIBERATE and as the proof that the model-emitter seam is real. So there is no rival authority. There is one model with two emitters, which is the design, and I nearly filed the opposite from a reader I had already measured as blind. AND THE CORRECT MOVE WAS THE DECORRELATED INSTRUMENT rather than a better reading of the first one. Two readers over one subject disagreed, and the tie was broken by opening the file, which is the rule this session keeps paying for. Filed as unwitnessed because I have not read the reader binding to say WHICH import shapes it decodes, and naming a cause from output shape is the error this finding is about.}
}

@misc{gate-G118-the-imports-reader-is-a-dependency-census-and-gate-G117-is-retracted,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {pycodemod --imports is a dependency census over external packages that excludes first-party modules by design, so gate-G117's claim that it misses module-scope package imports is retracted.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: the binding at _pycodemod_census.imports declares its subject as what the corpus imports from outside with a four-way verdict over declaration status, excludes first-party names because a sibling tool is not a dependency, and records that deciding first-party by find_spec rather than by the tree once got 124 rows wrong. Positive control: substrate/bib_corpus.py imports substrate.bib_parse and --imports reports stdlib-only there too, so the behaviour is uniform and intended rather than a defect on two files. The error was reading the mode's LABEL as its SCOPE, which is the mis-named-population shape, committed against a tool whose docstring names its population in the first line. gate-G111 is downgraded rather than closed, since its two readers are now explained as having different subjects and the reach-back binding is unread. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I filed gate-G117 claiming pycodemod --imports misses module-scope package imports and therefore manufactures false independence. READING THE BINDING RETRACTS IT. The function is a DEPENDENCY census whose docstring states its subject as what the corpus imports from outside, with a four-way verdict over DECLARATION STATUS -- stdlib, declared, undeclared, missing. First-party modules are excluded DELIBERATELY and the docstring says why, that a sibling tool is not a dependency, and it records that deciding first-party by find_spec rather than by the tree once got 124 rows wrong. So the omission I measured is the tool answering its own question correctly. A POSITIVE CONTROL SETTLES IT AND I SHOULD HAVE RUN ONE FIRST. substrate/bib_corpus.py imports substrate.bib_parse, which I wrote this session, and --imports reports stdlib-only there too. Uniform intended behaviour rather than a defect on two files. Had I run that control before filing, gate-G117 would never have been written. THE ERROR IS READING A LABEL AS A SCOPE. The mode is called imports and I read it as every import, when it censuses EXTERNAL DEPENDENCIES. That is the mis-named-population shape this session keeps filing, committed by me against a tool whose docstring names its population in its first line. gate-G111 IS ALSO WEAKENED AND I AM NOT RETRACTING IT HERE. It reported a disagreement between --imports and adopter_import --reach-back over _agdatext, and the disagreement is now explained by the two readers having different subjects rather than by either being blind. Whether any residue remains needs the reach-back binding read, which I have not done, so it stays open and its claim is downgraded rather than closed. AND THE COST DIRECTION IS THE ONE I HAVE BEEN WARNING ABOUT. A false defect filed against a correct tool would have sent the next reader to repair something that works, and I filed it while explicitly declining to name a cause because naming one from output shape is the error. Declining the cause was right and filing the claim was not.}
}

@misc{gate-G119-declare-the-reads-is-built-and-declaring-them-was-tried-and-failed,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Read-footprint measurement is already built as a two-layer runtime spy, and the declared form I proposed is the one it retired for manufacturing false independence.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: wg_order's reads function measures an item witness footprint by running it under a spy over the introspected figure layer plus the open builtin plus an L2 wrapper for cached derivations that perform no read, typing each as op for a derived artifact with a producer or file for a source read. Its docstring records that the population is DISCOVERED not declared, that the declared allow-list version reported a LIE rather than a gap by making any witness reading state another way register as reading nothing, and that this printed a disjoint-footprint equivalence for two items another instrument measured reading the same file. It also records that a witness passes its target as an ARGUMENT to a shared helper, so a static call-graph sees one call site for eight items and the footprint must be a runtime fact. The first check target I probed reads 3598 modules, so a declared field would have been a frozen roster or a summary and both report reads-nothing outside themselves. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I carried declare-the-reads as a top symbol for several ticks, on the reasoning that a slice which declares its inputs is both shardable and countable, and that the repair is declaration rather than instrumentation. THE CAPABILITY EXISTS AND MY PROPOSED FORM WAS ALREADY TRIED HERE AND FAILED. wg_order defines a reads function returning the figures an item witness consumes, its measurement footprint, and its docstring states the rationale as mechanized adjudication rather than taste. IT MEASURES BY RUNNING THE WITNESS UNDER A SPY, in two layers, because one does not cover it. The figure layer is discovered by introspection and the open builtin is spied underneath to catch a witness that walks the tree by any other route, with a third declared half caught at an L2 wrapper because a cached derivation performs NO read and would otherwise report an empty footprint. Reads are typed op for a derived artifact with a producer and file for a source read, and the docstring records that collapsing those two made six unrelated failure classes report that finishing either moves what the other measures. AND THE DECLARED FORM I WAS PROPOSING IS THE ONE IT RETIRED. Its own words are that the population is DISCOVERED and not declared, and that the declared version reported a LIE rather than a gap, because a hardcoded allow-list made any witness reading state by another route register as reading nothing, so the tie reader printed that two items had disjoint footprints and were a real equivalence while another instrument measured both reading the same file. An empty footprint is indistinguishable from an unmeasured one, so the allow-list manufactured a confident verdict of independence out of its own blind spot. THAT IS THE EXACT FAILURE MY FIELD WOULD HAVE REINTRODUCED. A reads field on a target record is a hand-authored roster, and the first target I probed reads 3598 modules, so the field would have held either a frozen list or a summary, and both report reads-nothing for anything outside them. AND THE STATIC HALF IS ALSO ANSWERED. The docstring records that a witness calls a shared helper and passes the gate name as an ARGUMENT, so a static call-graph sees one call site for eight items, which is why the footprint has to be a runtime fact. My assumption that reads are declarable from source is refuted by a measurement already in the tree.}
}

@misc{gate-G120-i-announced-a-delivery-that-is-staged-not-landed,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {I reported the ratchet island as moved when it is staged and not committed, so a peer's accurate record was corrected on a false report -- the inverse of delivery-without-acknowledgement and the worse direction.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: git status reports scripts/membudget as M and scripts/membudget-ledger as A, both staged and neither in history, so mtools' record of blocked-on-substrate remains accurate. Staging was the correct discipline because eight ratchets refuse the commit, and the honest report was staged-and-gate-blocked rather than moved. A stale ledger gets corrected when someone measures, but a ledger corrected on a false report goes wrong on the strength of the correction and the peer has no reason to re-measure. Separately, mtools reported their census frozen at rev 7 while git log over the run file reports rev 12, in which five legs ran the blocked-for-others split and every one found the question defective -- a four-revision gap between a message copy and the file their own instruction says to read. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Summit dispatched a second census whose SK-5b asks what a party has DELIVERED that a peer still records as owed, supplied by linux-sources against itself after they found a peer had filed an answer sixteen days earlier and written to say so, unread. I checked my own exposure and found the MIRROR IMAGE. I told mtools that the ratchet island had moved, and git status reports scripts/membudget as modified-staged and scripts/membudget-ledger as added-staged, both in the index and neither in history. So their record of it as blocked-on-substrate is STILL ACCURATE and my report of movement was premature. THE TWO FAILURES ARE OPPOSITE AND THE SECOND IS WORSE. Theirs was delivery without acknowledgement, where the work existed and the ledger was stale. Mine is acknowledgement without delivery, where the ledger was correct and I told the peer to update it. A stale ledger gets corrected when someone measures. A ledger corrected on a false report gets WRONG on the strength of the correction, and the peer has no reason to re-measure something they were just told changed. AND I HAD THE MEASUREMENT AND DREW THE WRONG BOUNDARY FROM IT. I ran git add on both paths and verified with git diff --cached that both were staged, which is exactly what the working rule asks for and is not the same as landed. Staging is the correct discipline here because eight ratchets refuse the commit, so the honest report was that it is staged and blocked behind the gates rather than that it moved. AND THE PEER MESSAGE THAT PROMPTED THIS ALSO MOVED UNDER ME. mtools reported their census frozen at rev 7 and git log over the run file reports rev 12, in which five legs ran the blocked-for-others split on themselves and every one found the question defective. Their own instruction was to read the file rather than the message copy, and the gap between the two is four revisions. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes an announced delivery from a landed one without asking what the announcement claimed.}
}

@misc{gate-G121-my-own-leg-witness-used-the-wrong-instrument-and-has-since-gone-stale,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {My census leg cites git log --all as its witness for a never-committed path, but --all includes refs/stash, so the claim is true and its stated witness now returns four rows and invites a false refutation.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: git log --all over scripts/membudget-ledger returns four commits all reading index on main, which are stash commits from eleven stash entries, while git log --branches over the same path returns empty and a positive control on the sibling path scripts/membudget returns a real branch commit. So the reader can see the shape it calls absent and the underlying claim holds under the correct instrument. The failure direction manufactures a false positive, since a stash created before the check would report commits for a file that never landed. My original empty reading was true by luck of timing rather than by the instrument being right. This is the complement of the flattering-direction problem: the population was right and the instrument wrong, so faithful re-derivation would reproduce the wrong instrument every time. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): My census leg SB-4 cites git log --all over scripts/membudget-ledger returning empty as its witness that the ratchet island has never been committed. A peer re-ran it and got four commits, and I re-ran it and got the same four. All four are STASH commits reading index on main, because --all includes refs/stash and this tree holds eleven stash entries. THE NEGATIVE WAS CORRECT AND THE INSTRUMENT WAS NOT. git log --branches over the same path returns empty, and a positive control on the sibling path scripts/membudget returns a real branch commit, so the reader can see the shape it calls absent and the ledger genuinely has no branch history. The claim in my leg is true. Its stated witness is not the command that establishes it. AND THE FAILURE DIRECTION IS THE ONE THAT MANUFACTURES A FALSE POSITIVE. A stash created before the check would have made --all report commits for a file that never landed on a branch, and the reader would have concluded COMMITTED about work that does not exist in history. My original reading was true by luck of timing rather than by the instrument being right. THE LEG IS NOW WRONG IN A WAY IT WAS NOT WHEN FILED. SB-4 quotes a command whose output has changed since filing, so a reader re-running my stated witness today gets four rows and concludes the leg is refuted, when the underlying claim holds under the correct instrument. That is worse than a stale figure because it invites a false REFUTATION rather than a false confirmation. AND IT IS THE FLATTERING-DIRECTION PROBLEM WITH THE SIGN REVERSED. I told a peer that re-deriving every tick protects against staleness but not against a wrong population. This is the complement: the population was right and the instrument was wrong, and re-deriving it faithfully would have reproduced the wrong instrument every time. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a correct claim with a wrong witness from a wrong claim, without reading which question the command asks.}
}

@misc{gate-G122-the-second-spelling-rule-is-stated-four-times-and-gated-nowhere,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The cross-check-with-a-second-spelling rule appears at four sites and every one is a docstring, while the spelling ratchet that exists measures mutation flag pairs and has never minted a baseline.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: pycodemod --literal cross-check over scripts and substrate returns four sites all typed doc, and toolmodes --nearmiss reports no baseline exists because minting belongs to the commit path as the promotion gate, refusing the environment-variable bypass as the no-verify shape. I predicted nothing related existed and that failed -- near_misses returns tool to spelling-and-missed-pair-flag, so a spelling ratchet exists over MUTATION FLAG PAIRS while the stated rule is about CAPABILITY SPELLINGS. A reader searching for spelling finds the ratchet and could conclude the rule is handled, which is the near-miss shape arriving inside the search for the near-miss tool. Substrate therefore holds a ratchet that cannot mint while eight gates refuse the commit. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Summit SK-5 asks what a party has STOPPED counting and adds that a rule which NEVER STARTED reads the same from inside, supplied by linux-sources measuring it on themselves. I named a candidate here and ran the check rather than reporting the intuition. CLAUDE.md states the rule as ask for the capability and cross-check with a second spelling. pycodemod --literal over scripts and substrate finds cross-check at four sites and EVERY ONE IS A DOCSTRING. Four prose statements, zero implementations, no gate and no witness. I PREDICTED NOTHING RELATED EXISTED AND THAT FAILED. toolmodes --nearmiss is declared a paydown-only ratchet over dry-spelling and its near_misses function returns a mapping from tool to spelling and the pair flag it misses, so a spelling ratchet DOES exist and is the same one whose call site I read two ticks ago minting a nearmiss baseline. THE TWO ARE DIFFERENT SUBJECTS AND THAT IS THE FINDING. The built ratchet measures MUTATION FLAG PAIRS, whether a tool declaring apply also declares dry-run. The stated rule is about CAPABILITY SPELLINGS, whether a census keyed on one name missed the same capability under another. A reader searching for spelling finds the ratchet and could conclude the rule is handled, which is the near-miss shape arriving in the search for the near-miss tool. AND THE RATCHET HAS NEVER RUN. Its own output says no baseline exists and that minting belongs to the commit path because that is the promotion gate, with an explicit refusal of the environment-variable bypass as the no-verify shape. So substrate holds a ratchet that cannot mint while eight gates refuse the commit, which is SB-3 one level in. THE COST IS THE ONE LINUX-SOURCES MEASURED. Every census I have run this session used single-spelling queries, and the rule saying to cross-check them is prose. I have hit that defect at least twice in measured findings, once on a calls query missing an underscore-prefixed sibling and once on a literal query over a mode label rather than its scope. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a rule nobody implemented from one whose implementation I have not found.}
}

@misc{gate-G123-the-run-file-i-was-dispatched-to-has-no-history,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Summit's census run file is untracked, so its three amendments reach surveyors only as messages and no party can cite, fetch or diff the revision a leg was filed against.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: git log over proceedings/census-known-work.md in summit returns nothing and git status reports it untracked. REACH control read rather than merely run -- the same command over a sibling proceedings ruling returns a real commit with a subject, so the reader sees that directory's history. PREDICATE control -- git status over proceedings reports exactly two untracked files and both are census artifacts, so the reader recognises the claim's class. The run file's own instruction is to read it rather than a message copy, and its amendments arrive only as messages, which is the snapshot-with-no-drift-check defect arriving inside the artifact that warns about it. The contrast is measured rather than hypothetical: the other census governing substrate is committed and was stamped at rev 15 this tick. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Summit dispatched me to a census run file with the instruction to read it there rather than from the message because a copy is a snapshot and the file moves. They have since told me three of its bindings were amended, twice for absence claims and once for producers. I WENT TO STAMP THE REVISION I FILED AGAINST AND THERE IS NONE. git log over proceedings/census-known-work.md in summit returns nothing and git status reports it as untracked. REACH CONTROL, AND I READ ITS OUTPUT RATHER THAN MERELY RUNNING IT. The same command over a sibling proceedings ruling returns a real commit with a subject line, so the reader sees that directory history and the run file absence is a true absence rather than reader blindness. PREDICATE CONTROL, WHICH IS THE AMENDMENT THEY JUST SENT. git status over proceedings reports exactly two untracked files and BOTH ARE CENSUS ARTIFACTS, the known-work run file and a build-hermeticity leg. So the reader recognises the class the claim is about and does not merely see the corpus. THE CONSEQUENCE IS THAT NO PARTY CAN CITE A REVISION OF THE THING GOVERNING THE RUN. There is no way to fetch what rev 4 said, diff rev 4 against rev 5, or verify that what I read matches what a peer cited. Every amendment reaches surveyors as a MESSAGE, which is the snapshot-with-no-drift-check that mtools and I both filed against this session, arriving in the file whose own instruction is to prefer the file over the message. AND THE CONTRAST IS SHARP RATHER THAN HYPOTHETICAL. The other census governing me is committed and I stamped it at rev 15 this tick, so I could name what I filed against and a reader could check. For this one my leg can only say the file as read at a wall time, which is the provenance form the fleet has spent the day retiring. Filed as unwitnessed because whether the file is meant to be tracked is the convener decision and not mine to assert.}
}

@misc{gate-G124-a-refuses-witness-cannot-distinguish-installed-from-effective,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Every refuses witness asserts that a guard is INSTALLED and none asserts that it is EFFECTIVE, because the kind takes its command from the filer and so cannot express a firing nobody staged.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: findings --kinds refuses enumerates eighteen keys, and reading gate-G104 back shows its command is a misspelled clustered flag against CLAUDE.md that I constructed for the finding. The claim is true and the command is right for it, so this is a property of the KIND rather than a defect in any filing. The split the kind cannot represent is untested with no exercise, fixture-passed where the filer built the failing case, and production-fired where the guard caught something nobody staged. The risk direction is that a repair which has never had the opportunity to fail is indistinguishable from one that cannot fail, which is the standing vacuity arriving inside a kind the same roster calls falsifiable. Found by mtools sweeping their own controls and naming the distinction in their row 3. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mtools swept their own section 5 table and found two of three controls validating a different reader than the claim depends on, and their row 3 named a distinction I have been collapsing all session. One fixture pass, zero production firings, recorded as UNTESTED rather than as working. I said I would sweep my own refuses witnesses for that shape and this is the sweep. THE POPULATION IS EIGHTEEN AND I ENUMERATED IT RATHER THAN COUNTING IT. findings --kinds refuses lists every key. Three are mine from this session. I read the G104 witness back and its command is a misspelled clustered flag against CLAUDE.md, which is an invocation I CONSTRUCTED for the finding. It proves the guard is INSTALLED. It has never refused a mis-invocation I did not author. THE DISTINCTION IS A PROPERTY OF THE KIND RATHER THAN OF MY THREE. A refuses witness holds while a named command exits non-zero, and the command is supplied by the filer. So every refuses witness in the ledger asserts INSTALLATION and none asserts EFFICACY, because the kind has no way to express a firing the filer did not stage. The three-way split the kind cannot represent is untested with no exercise, fixture-passed where the filer built the failing case, and production-fired where the guard caught something nobody staged. AND THE RISK DIRECTION IS THE STANDING PROBLEM ARRIVING INSIDE A BEHAVIOURAL KIND. A repair that has never had the opportunity to fail is indistinguishable from one that cannot fail, and the ledger reports both as holding. That is exactly the vacuity the kind roster warns about for standing, reached through a kind the same roster calls falsifiable. AND IT IS NOT A DEFECT IN THE FILER. My G104 command is the right command for the claim it makes, and the claim is true. What is missing is a way to say that the claim is about installation, so a reader cannot mistake it for a claim about the guard having worked in the wild. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a fixture pass from a production firing without a record of who supplied the input.}
}

@misc{gate-G125-my-published-predicate-control-validated-a-different-reader,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {My published predicate control runs git status while the claim's reader is git log, so it validates a different instrument and the correct control is a committed census artifact read by git log itself.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: SB-L publishes a PREDICATE control running git status over proceedings, reporting two untracked census artifacts, while the claim that summit's run file has no commit history is read by git log. The reach control is correct -- git log over a sibling ruling returns a real commit. The correct predicate control is git log --branches over the substrate deps-build leg in mtools, which returns a8e4cc8, proving git log recognises the shape the claim says is absent. My second control was a corroborating measurement from another instrument rather than a control on the claim's reader. The failure is silent by construction: both controls passed and the claim is true, so only reading which reader each control exercises catches it. Found by applying mtools' sharpened rule to my own leg. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mtools swept their own controls, found two of three validating a reader the claim does not use, and sharpened the rule. A positive control proves the reader can see the shape THE READER searches for, which is the shape the CLAIM needs only when those coincide. I applied it to the leg I filed an hour earlier and the defect is there. THE CLAIM IS THAT SUMMIT RUN FILE HAS NO COMMIT HISTORY AND ITS READER IS GIT LOG. My published reach control runs git log over a sibling ruling and returns a real commit, which validates the claim own instrument on its own corpus. Correct. My published PREDICATE control runs GIT STATUS over the proceedings directory and reports two untracked census artifacts. That is a DIFFERENT READER. It corroborates the class and validates nothing about git log. THE CORRECT PREDICATE CONTROL IS A CENSUS ARTIFACT THAT IS COMMITTED, read by the same reader. git log --branches over the substrate deps-build leg in mtools returns a8e4cc8, so git log recognises exactly the shape the claim says is absent. That is the control I should have published and I have now run it. AND THE TWO CONTROLS ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, which is why labelling matters more than having two. Reach asks whether the instrument sees the corpus at all. Predicate asks whether it recognises the claim subject. My second control was a third thing, a corroborating measurement from another instrument, which is useful and is not a control on the claim reader. AND THE FAILURE IS SILENT BY CONSTRUCTION. Both my controls passed, the claim is true, and no verification of the conclusion could have caught the mislabel. Only reading which reader each control exercises does, which is the same discipline that caught the wrong-instrument witness in my other leg. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a control on the claim reader from a corroborating measurement without reading both commands.}
}

@misc{gate-G126-the-smallest-refusing-gate-is-blocked-by-the-largest-monolith,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The smallest refusing gate holds one key whose three-line repair is already written in a comment twenty lines away, and the per-file gate refuses the edit at 566 findings, so the eight refusing gates are not a uniform backlog.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=gate. The witness: check_mode_contracts --list reports one key over eight adopted rosters on a zero-tolerance baseline, toolmodes --find reports --sizes dispatched and in no usage line with corpus_size_report as its live implementation, and the dispatch at schema.py carries a three-line comment stating exactly what the mode does. The file convention is a flag-and-gloss roster where --sizes belongs beside --growth. pycheck refused the edit citing 566 findings and forbidding paydown to land the change. So payability is a separate and unmeasured question from refusal: I enumerated one of eight and found it walled behind an unrelated monolith, and the other seven are unmeasured on that axis. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I have quoted eight refusing gates every tick without once enumerating what they hold, so I popped the paydown and started with the smallest. check_mode_contracts --list reports ONE key over eight adopted rosters, schema.py --sizes undeclared, on a zero-tolerance baseline, so that single row IS the gate. THE REPAIR IS THREE LINES AND ITS TEXT ALREADY EXISTS IN THE FILE. toolmodes --find reports --sizes as dispatched and in no usage line, with a live implementation named corpus_size_report documented at the function. Reading the dispatch shows a three-line comment above the branch explaining exactly what the mode does and why it exists. The documentation is present and in the wrong place, since the gate reads usage strings and this is a code comment. THE FILE CONVENTION IS A ROSTER OF FLAG AND GLOSS TUPLES and --sizes belongs beside --growth, both being size distributions. I wrote the entry with the gloss lifted from the dispatch comment rather than invented. AND THE PER-FILE GATE REFUSED IT AT 566 FINDINGS. So the smallest refusing gate in the tree, one key, a repair whose words are already written twenty lines away, is unreachable because its host is a monolith. The refusal names the shape and forbids paying it down to land the change. THAT INVERTS WHAT PAY-DOWN-THE-EIGHT MEANS. I had it as eight independent debts to work through. At least one of them is not payable at all until an unrelated monolith is drained, so the gate roster is not a worklist of eight items, it is a set of claims whose payability is a separate and unmeasured question. AND THE OTHER SEVEN ARE UNMEASURED ON THIS AXIS. I have enumerated one of eight and found it blocked. Whether the rest are payable is not something the refusal counts can answer, and quoting eight as a backlog treats them as uniform when the first one sampled is not. Filed as unwitnessed because no predicate distinguishes a payable gate from one whose repair is walled behind a different file.}
}

@misc{gate-G127-the-monolith-is-a-data-literal-not-a-def-tangle,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {findings.py is not a def-level monolith but a small program welded to a 7265-line data literal, so decomposing it is an authoring act on the data rather than a def-graph cut},
  check    = {refuses:python3,scratch/pycodemod.py,--split,.claude/agents/findings.py,--max-defs,12},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. MEASURED — module_outline reads 20 defs and 6 constants over 8420 lines, with the defs occupying about 1100 of them, while lines 453-7717 are ONE statement (the WITNESSES dict). pycodemod --split REFUSES and names the governing rule: relocation cannot shrink a body, so a part holding one statement must be decomposed as an authoring act. No SCC and no def tangle exist here. Every repairable ruff finding sampled sits in the machinery at lines 93-450, not in the data. A def-level DAG extractor would have been built against a nearly discrete def graph. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The drain plan called findings.py a monolith to decompose def-first and cons back leaf-first. MEASURED, and the shape is the opposite. module_outline reads 20 defs and 6 constants over 8420 lines; the defs occupy roughly 1100 of them and lines 453-7717 are ONE statement, the WITNESSES dict literal. pycodemod --split REFUSES with the exact diagnosis: 'no packing fits 1200 lines / findings_02.py 7270 lines = 5 header + 7265 body / WITNESSES' and states the rule that decides the strategy — relocation cannot shrink a body, so a part holding ONE statement must be decomposed as an AUTHORING act, not a move. There is no def-level tangle here and no SCC to break. The per-file gate is judging a DATA LITERAL, and every repairable finding sampled (PTH100/PTH120/ANN001/ANN202/UP031/S603/PLW1510/PLC0415) sits in the machinery at lines 93-450, not in the data. A def-level DAG extractor would have been built against a file whose def graph is nearly discrete.}
}

@misc{gate-G128-the-intern-has-no-emit-arm,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the code intern is one-directional — lowering exists with correct free-bound resolution but nothing renders interned ids back to source, so lint compliance cannot yet be made a property of an emitter},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. MEASURED — jea_pyalg.lower_source and lower_source_cst lower a module into a content-addressed store, the cst arm resolving free-vs-bound via libcst ExpressionContextProvider. jea_pydecode._walk interns ast nodes and children. Two spellings searched (unparse, emit source) return no inverse, and pycodemod --split moves statements BYTE-IDENTICALLY because it has no emitter. The gap is narrower than an interner — a NORMALIZING emitter with one compliant surface form per construct, under which an unannotated def or a percent-format is unrepresentable rather than a finding. UNWITNESSED because the discriminating quantity is a census value that exits 0 either way. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The proposal is to intern all the code and serialize it back out in a form guaranteed lint-compliant, making a lint CLASS unrepresentable rather than repairing instances. MEASURED — the intern half exists and is good. jea_pyalg.lower_source and lower_source_cst lower a module into a content-addressed store, the cst arm resolving free-vs-bound via libcst ExpressionContextProvider so distinct free globals do not collapse. jea_pydecode._walk interns ast nodes and children. The INVERSE does not exist. Two spellings searched (unparse, emit source) return no function rendering interned ids back to source, and pycodemod --split moves statements BYTE-IDENTICALLY for exactly that reason — it slices text with libcst because it has no emitter. The missing piece is narrower than an interner: a NORMALIZING emitter with one compliant surface form per construct, under which an unannotated def or a percent-format is unrepresentable rather than a finding. Same shape as bib_report.Census, which cannot be constructed without a denominator so a bare zero is unrepresentable. UNWITNESSED because the discriminating quantity is a CENSUS VALUE — toolmodes --find exits 0 whether it finds zero rows or many, so no exit code distinguishes an absent emitter from a present one. The polarity probe refused a refuses: filing on exactly this ground.}
}

@misc{gate-G129-two-instruments-two-counts-for-one-blocked-file,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the file blocking the two smallest refusing gates is a star-import re-export shim, not an undrained monolith, and the two gates hold one key between them rather than two debts},
  check    = {selftest:python3,scripts/pycheck_show.py,--count,jea/metalanguage/schema.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. gate-G126 recorded 566 findings for schema.py. RE-MEASURED — pycheck_show --count reads 2558 in 35 rules, of which 2273 are misc, a MYPY code rather than ruff, while pycodemod --types reads misc as 1 file. Two instruments over one subject with different denominators (instances versus files), the gate-G26 shape. READ THE BINDING and the bulk dissolves — schema.py was already drained on 2026-08-11, the declaration moved to schema_decl.py, and this file re-exports VIA STAR-IMPORT per its own docstring. F405 at 44 plus name-defined and used-before-def and the 2273 misc are the checker failing to resolve names arriving through import star. The gate refuses, so the blocker is real, but the file is misdescribed. AND the two zero-tolerance gates it blocks, mode contracts at 1 of 1 and divergence ratchet at 15 of 15, both hold the SAME key schema.py --sizes — one debt counted twice in the roster of eight. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G126 recorded schema.py as carrying 566 findings and therefore blocking the smallest refusing gate. RE-MEASURED and the prediction failed. pycheck_show --count reads 2558 findings in 35 rules, of which 2273 are misc — a MYPY code, not ruff. pycodemod --types over the same file reads misc as 1 file. Two instruments, two denominators (instances versus files) over one subject, which is the gate-G26 shape: neither is wrong and quoting either without naming its question is. READ THE BINDING and the bulk dissolves further. schema.py was ALREADY drained on 2026-08-11 — the declaration moved to schema_decl.py and this file re-exports it VIA A STAR-IMPORT, stated in its own docstring. F405 at 44, name-defined, used-before-def and the 2273 misc are the checker failing to resolve names arriving through import star, not 2558 authored defects. So the blocker gate-G126 named is real in that the gate refuses, and MISDESCRIBED in that the file is not an undrained monolith — it is a re-export shim whose findings are largely an artifact of the shim mechanism. The two zero-tolerance gates it blocks (mode contracts 1 of 1, divergence ratchet 15 of 15) both hold the SAME key, schema.py --sizes, so they are one debt counted twice in the roster of eight.}
}

