  claim    = {the gate's own 566-finding count for schema.py was the reliable figure and the 2558 that contradicted it was unresolved-name noise from a checker that could not see through a star-import},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. gate-G129 read pycheck_show --count at 2558 against gate-G126's 566 and I treated 566 as suspect. ATTEMPTING THE EDIT SETTLED IT THE OTHER WAY — the per-file gate refuses stating 566 FINDINGS, its own checker over the file. The 2558 counted names the checker could not resolve through the star-import, and pycheck_show REFUSES to emit a count for findings.py on exactly that ground (a checker COULD NOT RUN is UNKNOWN, not a count), so the guard exists and did not fire here. SURVIVING from gate-G129 — schema.py is a re-export shim drained 2026-08-11, and the two zero-tolerance gates hold ONE key between them. RETRACTED — the inference that the blocker was misdescribed because its count was inflated. This is the doubting-a-correct-reader shape the working rules name from gate-G45. The refusal names the only admissible route, extraction into a clean module, and states that one extraction STRANDS when the sole consumer is inside the file — which is the case for a MODES roster read by this file's own dispatch. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G129 read pycheck_show --count at 2558 findings for schema.py against gate-G126's 566, and I treated 566 as the suspect figure and 2558 as evidence the file was misdescribed. ATTEMPTING THE EDIT SETTLED IT AND I HAD IT BACKWARDS. The per-file gate refuses with its own count: 566 FINDINGS, stated in the refusal text. 566 is the gate's own checker over the file. The 2558 is pycheck_show --count running a checker that could not resolve names arriving through the star-import, reporting unresolved-name noise as findings — and that same tool REFUSES to emit a count for findings.py on exactly this ground, saying a checker COULD NOT RUN is UNKNOWN not a count, so the guard exists and did not fire here. What SURVIVES from gate-G129 is the structural half: schema.py is a re-export shim drained on 2026-08-11, and the two zero-tolerance gates hold ONE key between them. What is RETRACTED is the inference that the blocker was misdescribed because its count was inflated. The gate's figure was right, my doubt of it was the error, and this is the DOUBTING-A-CORRECT-READER shape the working rules already name from gate-G45. The refusal also names the only admissible route — extract the concern into a new clean module rather than paying down the monolith — and states that one extraction STRANDS when the sole consumer is inside the file, which is the case for a MODES roster read by this file's own dispatch.}
}

@misc{gate-G131-the-write-target-defaults-silently-and-the-refusal-names-the-wrong-subject,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {bibstruct refuses a read mode given the write target but not a write mode given nothing, so an absent --bib silently defaults and the refusal reports the default corpus size as if it were the named file},
  check    = {refuses:python3,scratch/bibstruct.py,--set,ask-paperkit-publish-wheel,note,probe},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session reported-by=summit. REPRODUCED and the reproduction carries the discriminator summit could not see. The reader answers 24 of 24 over summit's floor from substrate's cwd. The writer refuses the same key with 'have 67' where summit got 'have 0' — same command, different cwd, different denominator — so the number is the size of whatever corpus the writer defaulted to. READ THE BINDING — bib_argv._target returns None with NO refusal when --bib is absent, and parse refuses only a stray positional ending in .bib, so a write invoked with neither flag nor path parses cleanly with target=None and a default applies downstream. The tool HAS an explicit refusal for a read mode given --bib, carrying a comment that a peer misread false dangling edges from that exact class, and NO refusal for a write mode given nothing. That asymmetry is the defect. The misleading message is the consequence — 'have N' is honest about the loaded corpus and silent about which one it loaded. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): summit reported that bibstruct's readers take a path operand while its writers take none, so a write cannot reach a borrower's corpus, and that the refusal 'no entry KEY (have 0)' reads as a fact about the corpus when it is a fact about which corpus the writer chose. REPRODUCED FROM SUBSTRATE and the reproduction carries the discriminator summit could not see. The reader answers 24 of 24 over summit's floor from substrate's cwd. The writer refuses the same key with 'have 67' where summit got 'have 0' — SAME COMMAND, DIFFERENT CWD, DIFFERENT DENOMINATOR. So the number in the message is the size of whatever corpus the writer defaulted to, and it is nonzero here because substrate's default bib set is nonzero. READ THE BINDING and the mechanism is not a resolution that fails to reach. bib_argv._target returns None with NO REFUSAL when --bib is absent, and parse refuses only a stray positional ending in .bib, so a write mode invoked with neither flag nor path parses cleanly with target=None and a default is applied downstream. The tool has an explicit refusal for a read mode given --bib, with a comment recording that a peer misread false dangling edges from exactly this class, and NO refusal for a write mode given nothing. The asymmetry is the defect. The misleading message is a consequence, not the cause — 'have N' is honest about the loaded corpus and silent about which one that is, and the caller has no way to distinguish an empty file from a wrong default.}
}

