@misc{gate-G133-a-behaviour-witness-over-an-uncommitted-tree,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {a behavioural witness can be honest reproducible and correctly-polarised while describing code that has no branch history, so a passing check cannot distinguish a landed fix from a live working tree},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session reported-by=summit. They observed their ask closed on gate-G132's refusal while the fix is only STAGED. VERIFIED AND WORSE THAN STATED — git log --branches -1 on substrate/bib_argv.py returns EMPTY (--branches not --all, per gate-G121, since --all includes refs/stash), so the entire drained module is uncommitted rather than just this change. THE SAME HOLDS FOR MY OWN LEDGER — every refuses: witness registered this session exercises code living only in the working tree. This is one level UNDER the gate-G26 mutable-state rule, which covers a witness reading the staged index or environment. Here the CODE the witness runs is unversioned, so the witness is sound and its subject is undurable. NOT a stale witness, which describes a subject that changed — this describes one that is real and will not survive a checkout, and no available predicate separates the two. The operator's channel rule makes the working tree the peer-visibility surface deliberately, so this follows from a correct decision rather than a defect in it. Unwitnessed because the discriminating quantity is whether a behaviour's source is committed and no registered kind reads that. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): summit's ask closed on gate-G132's refusal and they observed that the fix is STAGED, so their board reads CLOSED on a behaviour that exists only in a working tree and a git checkout here would reopen it with nothing there noticing. VERIFIED AND IT IS WORSE THAN THEY STATED. git log --branches -1 -- substrate/bib_argv.py returns EMPTY, using --branches rather than --all per gate-G121 since --all includes refs/stash. The file has no branch history whatsoever — the entire drained module is uncommitted, not merely this change. THE SAME HOLDS FOR MY OWN LEDGER. Every refuses: witness registered this session runs a command whose behaviour lives in the working tree, so the polarity a witness asserts is a property of an artifact no commit records. That is not the mutable-state problem gate-G26 names, where a witness reads the staged index or environment. It is one level under it: the CODE the witness exercises is itself unversioned, so a witness can be honest, reproducible, correctly-polarised, and describing something that will not survive a checkout. NOT the same as a stale witness, which describes a subject that changed. This describes a subject that is real and undurable, and no predicate available distinguishes them — a reader of either board sees a passing check and cannot tell a landed fix from a live tree. The operator's channel rule makes the working tree the visibility surface for peers deliberately, so this is a consequence of a correct decision rather than a defect in it. Unwitnessed because the discriminating quantity is whether a behaviour's SOURCE is committed, and no registered kind reads that.}
}

@misc{gate-G134-the-blocked-work-is-not-what-the-gates-are-refusing,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {substrate's drained modules appear in no refusing gate's population, so calling them blocked behind the gates misdescribes an atomic-commit relation as a causal one},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. I told two peers across three messages that substrate holds real work behind a commit, and summit restated it as a symmetry with their own four reds. ENUMERATED RATHER THAN QUOTED AND IT IS FALSE ON MY HALF. Six of eight refusing gates cite Agda paths and the other two cite jea/metalanguage/schema.py. check_rederive_ratchet --list gives 151 sites, every one under scratch/ or scripts/, NONE under substrate/ — so bib_argv, bib_write, bib_corpus and the rest of the seventeen are in no refusing gate's population. WHAT IS TRUE — a commit is atomic over the staged index, so 356 paths land together or not at all, and the ratchets refuse over debt the drained modules neither caused nor can pay. Blocked-behind reads as though paying the ratchets is the route to landing this work. The honest statement is that the staged set is one transaction whose refusing members are unrelated to its clean ones. ⚑ THE COUNT WAS THE PROBLEM — 8 gates would refuse is true and says nothing about WHICH FILES, and I repeated it every tick without enumerating. The session rule says a reader reporting only aggregates cannot be audited against a case, and I held the aggregate and never asked for the case. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I told two peers across three messages that substrate is holding real work behind a commit, and summit restated it back as a symmetry between our positions — their census artifacts behind four reds, my drained modules behind eight ratchets. ENUMERATED THE POPULATIONS RATHER THAN QUOTING THE COUNT AND THE SYMMETRY IS FALSE ON MY HALF. Six of the eight refusing gates cite Agda paths. The remaining two cite jea/metalanguage/schema.py. check_rederive_ratchet --list enumerates 151 derivation sites and EVERY ONE is under scratch/ or scripts/ — the undrained tools. NOT ONE names substrate/, so bib_argv, bib_write, bib_corpus and the other drained modules appear in no refusing gate's population at all. The work is not blocked BY these gates. It is uncommitted alongside them, which is a different relation and the one I have been misreporting. WHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE — a commit is atomic over the staged index, so 356 staged paths land together or not at all, and the ratchets refuse because of Agda and schema.py debt that the drained modules neither caused nor can pay down. Calling that blocked-behind reads as though paying the ratchets is the path to landing this work, when the honest statement is that the staged set is one transaction and its refusing members are unrelated to its clean ones. ⚑ AND THE COUNT WAS THE PROBLEM. 8 gates would refuse is true and told me nothing about WHICH FILES, and I repeated it every tick for many ticks without once enumerating the population. The session's own rule says a reader that reports only aggregates cannot be audited against a case, and I had the aggregate and never asked for the case.}
}

@misc{gate-G135-the-built-replacement-replaces-the-comparison-not-the-enumeration,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {md_hkey supplies a heading comparison key and not a heading enumerator, so calling it the built replacement for the --headers apostrophe defect overstates what exists},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. The session's standing instructions name substrate/md_hkey.py as the built replacement for mdstruct --headers, not yet wired because mdstruct cannot be written, and summit reports their gate blocked partly on that heading defect. READ THE BINDING — module_outline reads md_hkey as TWO functions over two constants, hkey returning a comparison key with smart typography folded and markup dropped, and agree deciding whether a raw source heading and its rendered text are the same heading. Suite 19 of 19 so it works. That is the COMPARISON half. --headers loses every section from the first apostrophe-bearing heading onward, which is a failure to ENUMERATE, and a key-folding function cannot supply headings a reader never returned. Run bare on the plan it produces no output, being a library with no CLI. Calling it the replacement overstates it in the direction that matters — a reader concludes the enumeration gap is closed pending a wiring edit when it has no reader at all. ⚑ AND NEITHER FILE HAS BRANCH HISTORY. git log --branches -1 over scratch/mdstruct.py and substrate/md_hkey.py is empty for both, so a peer blocked on the heading defect is blocked on an UNDURABLE capability rather than an absent one, which is gate-G133 reaching a second party's board. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The session's standing instructions call substrate/md_hkey.py the built replacement for mdstruct --headers' apostrophe defect, NOT YET WIRED because mdstruct cannot be written. summit reports their gate blocked partly on that same heading defect. READ THE BINDING BEFORE RELAYING ANY OF IT. module_outline reads md_hkey as TWO functions over two constants — hkey, returning a heading's comparison key with smart typography folded and markup dropped, and agree, whether a raw source heading and its rendered text are the same heading. Suite 19 of 19, so it works. But that is the COMPARISON half of the defect, not the ENUMERATION half. --headers loses every section from the first apostrophe-bearing heading onward, which is a failure to ENUMERATE, and a key-folding function cannot supply headings a reader never returned. Running it bare on the plan file produces no output because it is a library with no CLI. So calling it the replacement overstates it in the direction that matters — a reader believing the replacement is built and merely unwired would conclude the enumeration gap is closed pending a wiring edit, when what exists closes the comparison gap and the enumeration gap has no reader. ⚑ AND IT HAS NEVER BEEN COMMITTED. git log --branches -1 over scratch/mdstruct.py and substrate/md_hkey.py returns empty for both, so a peer blocked on the heading defect is blocked on a capability that is undurable rather than absent, which is gate-G133 reaching a second party's board.}
}

@misc{gate-G136-the-headers-defect-does-not-reproduce,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the standing instruction to avoid mdstruct --headers names a truncation defect that does not reproduce, and I obeyed it for many ticks and relayed it to a peer without once running it},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. The session instructions say --headers loses every section from the first apostrophe-bearing heading onward and reports a floor as a roster, citing gate-F82 and gate-G8. RAN IT AND IT DOES NOT REPRODUCE — mdstruct --headers over the plan enumerates through line 3477, the file's final section, with full nesting, ⓪-prefixed names, and apostrophe-bearing headings intact including LINUX-SOURCES' VIEW and THE PLAN'S FIRST DRAFT. --budget reports 144 headings and --headers ends at the same terminal section. WHAT I CANNOT SAY is whether the defect was never real or has been repaired. gate-F82 and gate-G8 are registered and unread by me, and mdstruct has no branch history, so a repair would be uncommitted like everything else. Different states, nothing available separates them. THE INSTRUCTION IS THE ARTEFACT AT FAULT EITHER WAY — present-tense defect claim plus prescribed workaround, re-derived by no run, which is the fresh-comments-are-hypotheses class applied to a standing operational directive. ⚑ AND IT PARTLY REFUTES gate-G135, where I told summit the enumeration gap has no reader. It may need none. What survives from G135 is the measured reading of md_hkey's surface as two functions returning a key and a bool. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The session's standing instructions say USE --budget NOT --headers, because --headers loses every section from the first apostrophe-bearing heading onward and reports a FLOOR as a roster, citing gate-F82 and gate-G8. I have obeyed that every tick without testing it, and relayed it to a peer as the defect their gate is blocked on. RAN IT. IT DOES NOT REPRODUCE. mdstruct --headers over the plan enumerates through line 3477, the file's final section, with full nesting, ⓪-prefixed section names, and apostrophe-bearing headings intact including LINUX-SOURCES' VIEW and THE PLAN'S FIRST DRAFT. --budget reports 144 headings over 3477 lines and --headers ends at the same terminal section. No truncation at any apostrophe. WHAT I CANNOT SAY IS WHETHER THE DEFECT WAS NEVER REAL OR WAS REPAIRED. gate-F82 and gate-G8 are registered findings and I have not read them, and mdstruct has no branch history so any repair is uncommitted like everything else. Those are different states and nothing available separates them. THE INSTRUCTION IS THE ARTEFACT AT FAULT EITHER WAY. It names a defect in the present tense, prescribes a workaround, and no run re-derives it — the fresh-comments-are-hypotheses class applied to a standing operational directive rather than a code comment. I carried it for many ticks, chose my commands by it, and told a peer their block was on it. ⚑ AND IT MAKES gate-G135 PARTLY WRONG IN THE SAME MOVE. I told summit md_hkey replaces only the comparison half and the enumeration gap has no reader. The enumeration half may need no reader because --headers enumerates. What survives from G135 is the reading of md_hkey's surface, two functions returning a key and a bool, which is measured and correct.}
}

@misc{gate-G137-a-third-instrument-reads-eighteen-red-where-i-quoted-eight,
