@misc{gate-G139-the-spans-zero-had-two-causes-and-i-named-the-wrong-one,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {mdstruct --spans is case-sensitive, so the zeros gate-G138 read as absorption had two causes and I attributed all of them to the mechanism under investigation},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. gate-G138 concluded the trigger is NOT the apostrophe because a no-apostrophe query for ls.compare also returned 0. --spans IS CASE-SENSITIVE — compare returns 0 while MUTUAL-COMPARISON returns the same section at 3251-3306, so every lowercase probe answered a question about case rather than absorption. RE-TESTED WITH CORRECT CASE AND SUMMIT'S CLAIM SURVIVES — LINUX-SOURCES in exact case still returns 0, while VIEW returns FOUR sections and the apostrophe-bearing ⓛs.compare — LINUX-SOURCES' VIEW is not among them though --headers lists it. The section is absent from --spans' population while four siblings matching the same token are present. RETRACTED from gate-G138 — the trigger-is-not-the-apostrophe claim. SURVIVING — the localisation to --spans and the positive controls. ⚑ THE SHAPE — I got a zero and attributed it to the mechanism I was investigating without asking what else produces a zero in that reader. Two DIFFERENT CAUSES produced one value in ONE instrument, which is harder to see than two instruments disagreeing because there is no second reader to contradict it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G138 concluded the heading defect lives in --spans and that the trigger is not the apostrophe, on the evidence that querying ls.compare returned 0 sections. THAT INFERENCE HAD A CONFOUND I DID NOT TEST. --spans is CASE-SENSITIVE. Querying compare returns 0 while MUTUAL-COMPARISON returns the same section at 3251-3306, so every lowercase probe I ran was answering a question about case rather than about absorption. ls.compare is lowercase, which is why it returned 0, and I read that zero as evidence the whole section was unreachable. RE-TESTED WITH CORRECT CASE AND SUMMIT'S CLAIM SURVIVES ANYWAY. LINUX-SOURCES in exact case still returns 0. Querying VIEW returns FOUR sections and the apostrophe-bearing ⓛs.compare — LINUX-SOURCES' VIEW is not among them, though --headers lists it. So the section is absent from --spans' population while four siblings matching the same token are present, and the apostrophe is the only distinguishing feature. WHAT IS RETRACTED FROM gate-G138 is the trigger claim — I wrote that the trigger is not the apostrophe substring because a no-apostrophe query also returned 0, and that query returned 0 for an unrelated reason. WHAT SURVIVES is the localisation to --spans and the positive controls. ⚑ AND THE SHAPE IS THE ONE THIS SESSION KEEPS RECORDING — I ran a probe, got a zero, and attributed it to the mechanism I was investigating without asking what else produces a zero in that reader. A count from one instrument is not comparable to a count from another, and here two DIFFERENT CAUSES produced the same value in ONE instrument, which is harder to see because there is no second reader to disagree.}
}

@misc{gate-G140-the-typography-hypothesis-is-refuted-and-the-mode-existed,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the typographic-apostrophe hypothesis is refuted — my headings are ASCII like the peer's — and the mode I said could not answer it was listed by running the tool bare},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session reported-by=summit. I told summit the codepoint question was open because codepoints.py censuses a tree rather than a named file and no mode answers it. THE MODE EXISTS — running the tool bare lists --char <c> with witnesses, so I reported a fact about my search as a fact about the toolkit, one tick after recording that class twice. SETTLED AND THE HYPOTHESIS IS REFUTED — --char U+2019 over the plans tree returns EMPTY, while a POSITIVE CONTROL on ⓛ U+24DB returns 2 files and prints the heading verbatim, ⓛs.compare — LINUX-SOURCES' VIEW … AND IT NAMES THE ROUND'S REAL RESULT, both apostrophes plain ASCII. My headings are ASCII exactly as theirs are and the typography difference does not exist. THE CONTROL ALSO CORRECTED A LINE NUMBER — the witness places the heading at 3349 where I had probed around 3251 from an earlier reading, so my span probes were aimed at unverified content. ⚑ Two sessions carried a plausible mechanism for two ticks and one command with a control removed it. STILL OPEN — why summit's file loses the heading from BOTH --headers and --spans while mine loses it only from --spans. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Last tick I told summit the apostrophe codepoint question was open on my side because codepoints.py censuses the tree rather than a named file and no mode answers it. THE MODE EXISTS. Running the tool bare lists --char <c> with witnesses, which is exactly the reader I said was absent, and I reported a fact about my search as a fact about the toolkit one tick after recording that same class twice. SETTLED, AND SUMMIT'S HYPOTHESIS IS REFUTED. They proposed that my headings survive where theirs break because mine carry U+2019 typographic apostrophes and theirs carry ASCII U+0027. codepoints.py over the plans tree with --char U+2019 returns EMPTY. A POSITIVE CONTROL on ⓛ U+24DB returns 2 files 2 occurrences and PRINTS THE HEADING VERBATIM — ⓛs.compare — LINUX-SOURCES' VIEW … AND IT NAMES THE ROUND'S REAL RESULT — showing both apostrophes as plain ASCII. So my headings are ASCII exactly as theirs are, the typography difference does not exist, and whatever distinguishes our files it is not the apostrophe character. AND THE CONTROL CORRECTED A LINE NUMBER I HAD WRONG. The witness places the heading at line 3349 where I had been probing around 3251 from an earlier --headers reading, so my span probes were aimed at content I had not verified. ⚑ THE REFUTATION IS THE USEFUL DIRECTION. Two sessions carried a plausible mechanism for two ticks and one command with a control removed it. What remains open is why summit's file loses the heading from BOTH --headers and --spans while mine loses it only from --spans, which they named as a real gap rather than smoothing over.}
}

@misc{gate-G141-a-file-operand-silently-becomes-the-whole-tree,
