  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session reported-by=summit. They reported the operand inert under --where all. VERIFIED AND WIDER — passing CLAUDE.md with --char scans 14402 files and returns hits from .claude/agents/ nowhere near the named file. THE CAUSE IS ONE PREDICATE at line 277, next(a for a in args if not a.startswith('-') and os.path.isdir(a), '.'). A file fails isdir, is skipped, root falls back to dot. Inert for any FILE in EVERY mode, not only --where all. THE FAILURE DIRECTION IS THE DANGEROUS ONE — a narrowing that does not narrow returns MORE rows, so it reads as a rich answer rather than an error. summit read a whole-tree population as a fact about one file and I could not have caught it from their message because the count was plausible. AND MY OWN gate-G140 WAS SCOPED BY LUCK — I passed a DIRECTORY so isdir passed. Had I passed the plan file the same command would have censused the tree and I would have read it identically. gate-G140 stands on its control firing and printing the heading verbatim, but its scoping was accidental. Unwitnessed because the tool exits 0 whether it scans one file or the tree — and the polarity probe's refusal quoted applied_grammar.py as the last output of a CLAUDE.md query, the defect demonstrating itself inside the guard. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): summit reported codepoints.py's file operand inert under --where all, offering it as a report rather than an ask. VERIFIED AND IT IS A DEFECT, WIDER THAN THEY MEASURED. Passing CLAUDE.md with --char scans 14402 files and returns hits from .claude/agents/ nowhere near the named file. READ THE BINDING AND THE CAUSE IS ONE PREDICATE — line 277 resolves root as next(a for a in args if not a.startswith('-') and os.path.isdir(a), '.'). A FILE operand fails isdir, is skipped, and root falls back silently to dot. So the operand is inert for any FILE in EVERY mode, not only under --where all, and summit's reading was narrower than the defect. THE FAILURE DIRECTION IS THE DANGEROUS ONE — a narrowing that does not narrow returns MORE rows rather than fewer, so it never looks like an error, it looks like a rich answer. summit read a whole-tree population as a fact about one file and reported that reading to me, and I could not have caught it from their message because the count was plausible. AND MY OWN gate-G140 MEASUREMENT WAS SCOPED BY LUCK — I passed ~/.claude/plans, which IS a directory, so isdir passed and the scoping was real. Had I passed the plan FILE the same command would have censused the whole tree and I would have read the result identically. gate-G140 stands because the control fired and printed the heading verbatim, but the scoping I claimed was accidental rather than intended. UNWITNESSED because the discriminating quantity is a census value and the tool exits 0 whether it scans one file or the tree, which the polarity probe refused a refuses: filing on — and its refusal quoted applied_grammar.py as the last output of a CLAUDE.md query, which is the defect demonstrating itself inside the guard.}
}

