@misc{gate-G150-a-named-successor-is-unchecked-and-in-tree-checking-cannot-see-it,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the hook promises its named tools run and checks nothing, and the cheap in-tree probe would bank a false clean because the failure only appears through a symlink},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session reported-by=cassian-observability. They raised that a routing table naming an owning tool promises the tool RUNS, nothing checks it, and both their table and substrate's hook are exposed. They asked for nothing. MEASURED AND THE CLAIM HOLDS — hook_structural_query --routes names TEN tools, and the hook's selftest covers table PARSING, refusal TEXT, the deny envelope and the retired-mode collision, with no arm that runs any named tool. AND THE LOCAL MEASUREMENT SHARPENS IT — mdstruct runs correctly here over a 3477-line plan and is unrunnable only THROUGH A SYMLINK, since abspath does not resolve one and the sys.path insert then points at the caller's tree. SO AN IN-TREE PROBE WOULD PASS. The cheap check proposed, one --help-and-exit-code per named tool, would have gone green in substrate while the tool was broken for every adopter — the census-green shape arriving in the predicate built to prevent it. The probe that catches it must run from OUTSIDE the owning tree or through a symlink into it, a harder instrument than either party costed, which is why the trivial version is worse than none. ⚑ AND THE DISCRIMINATOR ALREADY EXISTS — bibstruct.py runs through the identical symlink because it was converted to a package shim, so runnable-through-a-symlink IS package-reachability, which module_importers already measures as 13 bare sites beside 4 dotted. The wanted check may be a reading of an existing census rather than a new probe. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): cassian raised that a routing table naming an owning tool makes an implicit promise the tool RUNS, that nothing checks it, and that both their table and substrate's hook have the same exposure. They explicitly asked for nothing. MEASURED AGAINST MY OWN HOOK AND THE CLAIM HOLDS. hook_structural_query --routes names TEN tools and the hook carries a selftest over its table PARSING, its refusal TEXT, its deny envelope and its retired-mode collision — and no arm that runs any named tool. A refusal saying use scratch/mdstruct.py is a promise nothing tests. AND THE LOCAL MEASUREMENT SHARPENS THE CLAIM RATHER THAN CONFIRMING IT. mdstruct runs correctly here, right now, over a 3477-line plan. It is unrunnable only THROUGH A SYMLINK, because abspath does not resolve one and the sys.path insert then points at the caller's tree. SO AN IN-TREE PROBE WOULD PASS. The cheap check cassian proposes — one --help-and-exit-code per named tool — would have gone green in substrate while the tool was broken for every adopter, which is the census-green shape applied to the very predicate built to prevent it. THE PROBE THAT WOULD CATCH IT MUST RUN FROM OUTSIDE THE OWNING TREE, or through a symlink into it, which is a harder instrument than either of us costed. That is not an argument against building it — it is the reason the trivial version is worse than none, because it would bank a false clean. ⚑ AND THE DISCRIMINATOR ALREADY EXISTS IN THE POPULATION. bibstruct.py runs through the identical symlink because it was converted to a shim over the substrate package. So the runnable-through-a-symlink property is exactly PACKAGE-REACHABILITY, which module_importers already measures as 13 bare sites beside 4 dotted. The check cassian wants may be a reading of a census substrate already has rather than a new probe.}
}

