ch is the only file in the family carrying one. A shebang must stay first or the interpreter line stops working, and file_header.prologue reads the comment lines before the first statement so both orders satisfy the census — the ORDER matters to the interpreter and not to the reader, which is exactly the kind of thing a mechanical sweep gets wrong. VERIFIED END TO END — bibstruct --entries dispatches through every edited module and bib_argv selftest reports 26 of 26. AND THE VERIFICATION SURFACED TWO DANGLING EDGES THAT ARE NOT MINE. bibstruct --orphans reports 2 of 377 — enumeration-root-gap-is-now-mechanized and flat-mk-now-has-a-structural-reader both rest-on build-oracle-top-level-module-outside-every-enumeration-root, which is not an entry in this bib. That is corpus data rather than a tool break, since a licence comment cannot create a dangling reference, and it was found only because a header sweep prompted a read-path check nobody would otherwise have run.}
