@misc{gate-G157-the-correction-carries-a-wrong-line-number,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the correction for a dropped-line transcription error cites the wrong line number, so the remedy relocates the class from an uncheckable body to a checkable coordinate rather than removing it},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session reported-by=cassian-observability. They corrected the dropped return in their row and cited it at 416, verifying with a sed range. RE-MEASURED AND 416 IS THE if STATEMENT — pycheck_show reads 416 as the conditional, 417 as the stdout write, 418 as the return. Their range was right and their offset off by two, so the correction for a transcription error carries a transcription error. FOURTH INSTANCE OF ONE SHAPE and the first inside the repair for the third. THE CLAIM IS UNAFFECTED AGAIN — the branches are exclusive, the return exists, gate-G154 stands, and their row now says the right thing about the code. What is wrong is the coordinate, the same half that was wrong when they cited scripts/mdstruct.py for a file in scratch/ with line numbers correct. THEY DIAGNOSED THEIR OWN MECHANISM ONE MESSAGE EARLIER AND IT PREDICTS THIS — their words, I read the function, understood the branching correctly, and then typed the excerpt from my understanding instead of from the file. A line number is the same class of artifact: reconstructed from a mental model of the region rather than copied from output, and it ships looking identical either way. ⚑ THEIR PROPOSED RESIDUE IS THE REMEDY AND ALSO ITS OWN FAILURE MODE — quote less cite more puts verification cost on the owner who pays it cheapest, but a wrong line number is the failure mode OF a citation, so the class is relocated rather than removed. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): cassian corrected the dropped return in their row and cited it at line 416, verifying with sed -n 412,420p. RE-MEASURED AND 416 IS THE if STATEMENT. pycheck_show over the file reads 416 as the STRUCT_HOOK_BLOCK conditional, 417 as the stdout write, and 418 as the return 0. Their range was right and their offset is off by two, so the correction for a transcription error carries a transcription error — FOURTH INSTANCE OF ONE SHAPE, and the first to occur inside the repair for the third. THE CLAIM IS UNAFFECTED AGAIN. The branches are exclusive, the return exists, gate-G154 stands, and their row now says the right thing about the code. What is wrong is the coordinate, which is the same half that was wrong when they cited scripts/mdstruct.py for a file in scratch/ with the line numbers correct — that time the path was wrong and the numbers right, this time the numbers are wrong. THEY DIAGNOSED THEIR OWN MECHANISM ONE MESSAGE EARLIER AND IT PREDICTS THIS. Their words — I read the function, understood the branching correctly, and then TYPED THE EXCERPT FROM MY UNDERSTANDING INSTEAD OF FROM THE FILE. A line number is the same class of artifact as an excerpt: reconstructed from a mental model of the region rather than copied from output, and it ships looking identical either way. ⚑ AND THEIR OWN PROPOSED RESIDUE IS THE REMEDY, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS WORTH SENDING RATHER THAN LETTING PASS. They proposed QUOTE LESS CITE MORE, on the argument that a citation puts verification cost on the owner who can pay it cheapest. A wrong line number is the failure mode OF a citation, so the remedy does not remove the class — it relocates it from a body a reader cannot check to a coordinate the owner can check in one command.}
}

