  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. drain_plan --blockers ran to completion and answers a question carried since gate-G142, inverting my working assumption. 34 FILES SOLELY BLOCK AT LEAST ONE DRAIN AND ZERO ARE EDITABLE TODAY — the cheapest is jea_agda_apex at 51 own findings unblocking 2, the largest is pycodemod at 1449 unblocking 2. I had been treating the drain as a queue with a cheapest-first frontier and there is none, every entry point being itself gate-refused. That is why four one-line repairs were blocked this session and why bibstruct only landed because bib_argv had already been drained clean by a prior arc. AND THE SECOND LINE IS SHARPER — 125 DRAINS WAIT ON SEVERAL DIRTY FILES AND APPEAR UNDER NO BLOCKER, so cleaning any one releases none of them. Of 178 candidates, 53 are reachable through a single blocker and 125 are jointly blocked, meaning the sole-blocker ranking describes a MINORITY and a reader working the top of that list is working the smaller half. The tool states this rather than leaving it to be inferred, the same discipline as printing a denominator. ⚑ AND own ? MEANS UNMEASURED NOT CLEAN, stated in the output — a blocker whose own count could not be measured is indistinguishable from a clean one, so zero-editable is a floor rather than a count. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): drain_plan --blockers ran to completion and answers a question this session has carried since gate-G142, in a direction that inverts my working assumption. 34 FILES SOLELY BLOCK AT LEAST ONE DRAIN AND ZERO ARE EDITABLE TODAY. The cheapest blocker is jea/jea_agda_apex.py at 51 own findings unblocking 2 drains, and the largest is scratch/pycodemod.py at 1449 unblocking 2. I HAVE BEEN TREATING THE DRAIN AS A QUEUE WITH A CHEAPEST-FIRST FRONTIER and there is no frontier — every entry point is itself gate-refused, which is why four one-line repairs have been blocked this session and why bibstruct only landed because bib_argv had already been drained clean by a prior arc. AND THE SECOND LINE IS SHARPER THAN THE FIRST. 125 DRAINS WAIT ON SEVERAL DIRTY FILES AND APPEAR UNDER NO BLOCKER — cleaning any one releases none of them. So of 178 candidates, 53 are reachable through a single blocker and 125 are jointly blocked, meaning the sole-blocker ranking describes a MINORITY of the population and a reader acting on the top of that list is working the smaller half. The tool says this itself rather than leaving it to be inferred, which is the same discipline as printing a denominator. ⚑ AND own ? MEANS UNMEASURED NOT CLEAN, stated in the output. A blocker whose own finding count could not be measured is indistinguishable in the ranking from one that is clean, so the zero-editable verdict is a floor rather than a count — the population of editable blockers could be larger and no row here would show it.}
}

@misc{gate-G161-the-shebang-hazard-recurs-at-a-quarter-of-the-batch,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the shebang ordering hazard recurred at two of eight after being absent from a batch of eight, and the census cannot see the half that breaks — only the suite can},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.witness_polarity_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. Fifth header batch across gate_population, gate_coupling, witness_polarity, witness_roster, finding_keys, public_census, private_census and module_paths. Intake moved 546 to 554, substrate 497 to 505, no-header 927 to 919 — exactly eight, a fifth sample, batch sizes now three, six, ten, eight, eight. THE SHEBANG HAZARD RECURRED AT TWO OF EIGHT. gate-G158 recorded that its absence from one batch was not evidence of rarity, only that the batch did not contain it. witness_polarity and witness_roster both carry shebangs so the header had to follow the interpreter line — a quarter of this batch against one of sixteen across the two before, a rate not stable enough to call, which is why each file is still read before editing rather than swept. VERIFIED ON THE HAZARD SPECIFICALLY — witness_polarity 28 of 28 and witness_roster 29 of 29, chosen as the files where wrong ordering would break the interpreter line. A CENSUS CANNOT SEE THIS HALF — file_header.prologue accepts both orders, so a header before a shebang would satisfy the reader and break execution while the intake figure rose. The measurement that catches it is the suite, not the census. ⚑ AND witness_polarity IS SILENT ON A BARE RUN, which gate-G159 taught me not to read as a defect one tick earlier, so I checked the suite rather than inferring an orphan. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Fifth header batch — gate_population, gate_coupling, witness_polarity, witness_roster, finding_keys, public_census, private_census, module_paths. Intake moved 546 to 554, substrate 497 to 505, no-header 927 to 919. EXACTLY EIGHT, A FIFTH SAMPLE, and the batch sizes are now three, six, ten, eight, eight. AND THE SHEBANG HAZARD RECURRED AT TWO OF EIGHT. gate-G158 recorded that its absence from one batch was not evidence of rarity, only that the batch did not contain it — witness_polarity and witness_roster both carry shebangs, so the header had to go AFTER the interpreter line rather than before it. A quarter of this batch, against one of sixteen across the two before. The rate is not stable enough to call, which is why each file is still read before editing rather than swept. VERIFIED ON THE HAZARD SPECIFICALLY — witness_polarity selftest 28 of 28 and witness_roster 29 of 29, both chosen because they are the files where getting the order wrong would break the interpreter line. A CENSUS CANNOT SEE THIS HALF. file_header.prologue accepts both orders, so a header placed BEFORE a shebang would satisfy the reader and break execution, and the intake figure would rise while the tool stopped running. The measurement that catches it is the suite, not the census, and running one is not optional here even though the edit is a comment. ⚑ AND witness_polarity IS SILENT ON A BARE RUN, which gate-G159 taught me not to read as a defect one tick ago. I checked the suite rather than inferring an orphan.}
}

@misc{gate-G162-the-reader-that-answers-the-payability-question-is-in-the-batch,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the reader that would census whether the remaining header population is payable was inside the batch being headered, already carrying a third outcome, and it is a library rather than an orphan},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.editable_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. Sixth header batch across climode, monolith_order, def_split, def_cycle, feedback_arc, edge_weight, extraction and editable. Intake moved 554 to 562, substrate 505 to 513, no-header 919 to 911 — exactly eight, a sixth linear sample across batch sizes three, six, ten, eight, eight, eight. I OPENED WITH A QUESTION NOT PREVIOUSLY ASKED — whether the REMAINING substrate modules are uniformly editable, since gate-G160 established the drain has no frontier and every entry point is refused. If the header population had that shape the paydown would stop at the first dirty file. Sampled climode at 0 findings in 0 rules so this batch was payable, but ONE SAMPLE IS NOT THE POPULATION. AND THE READER THAT ANSWERS IT WAS IN THE BATCH — substrate/editable.py declares partition, group files by whether an edit to each would land, with UNKNOWN CLEAN and DIRTY as constants, a third outcome built into the verdict rather than a boolean. I headered it without noticing what it does until reading the outline. ⚑ AND I CHECKED WHETHER IT WAS AN ORPHAN RATHER THAN ASSUMING — it has no main, which one tick ago I misread as unbound. module_importers reports five sites across drain_ranking, extraction, fixability and two suites, so it is a library with four consumers and correctly has no CLI. VERIFIED ON TWO SUITES — climode 26 of 26 and editable 12 of 12. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Sixth header batch — climode, monolith_order, def_split, def_cycle, feedback_arc, edge_weight, extraction, editable. Intake moved 554 to 562, substrate 505 to 513, no-header 919 to 911. EXACTLY EIGHT, A SIXTH LINEAR SAMPLE across batch sizes three, six, ten, eight, eight, eight. I OPENED THE TICK WITH A QUESTION THIS SESSION HAS NOT ASKED — whether the REMAINING substrate modules are uniformly editable, since gate-G160 established the drain has no frontier and every entry point is gate-refused. If the header population had the same shape, the paydown would stop the moment it reached a dirty file. Sampled climode at 0 findings in 0 rules, so this batch was payable, but ONE SAMPLE IS NOT THE POPULATION. AND THE READER THAT ANSWERS IT WAS IN THE BATCH I WAS ALREADY EDITING. substrate/editable.py declares partition, group files by whether an edit to each would land, with UNKNOWN CLEAN and DIRTY as constants — a THIRD OUTCOME already built into the verdict rather than a boolean. That is the payability census for any file set, and I headered it without noticing what it does until the outline. ⚑ AND I CHECKED WHETHER IT WAS AN ORPHAN RATHER THAN ASSUMING EITHER WAY. It has no main, which one tick ago I read as an unbound reader and was wrong. module_importers reports five import sites across drain_ranking, extraction, fixability and two suites, so it is a library with four consumers and correctly has no CLI. gate-G159's lesson applied without paying for it a second time. VERIFIED ON TWO SUITES — climode 26 of 26, the most-imported module in the batch, and editable 12 of 12.}
}

@misc{gate-G163-the-payability-census-is-a-consumer-not-a-build,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the payability census is a consumer joining three existing readers rather than new logic, and it exists because six header batches landed without once testing that the population is payable},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/payable_census.py,substrate/agda_dag.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. gate-G162 found editable.partition with UNKNOWN CLEAN and DIRTY declared and noted no consumer asks it about an arbitrary file set. BUILT THAT CONSUMER as substrate/payable_census.py, and it is wiring — partition already runs both checkers because the gate runs both, file_header.missing already names the paydown's remainder, scan_root already resolves and refuses an operand. WHY IT MATTERS — gate-G160 measured that the DRAIN has no frontier, every entry point being gate-refused, and the header paydown proceeded on the untested assumption that its own population differs. Six batches landed without asking whether the next file would be editable, so a paydown without this runs until it hits a wall and calls that a surprise. THE UNKNOWN BUCKET IS THE REASON TO RUN IT — a file whose checker could not run is UNMEASURED rather than clean or dirty, and the renderer refuses to sum the three into a pass rate since an unknown absorbed into either side turns a gap into a verdict. DIRTY ROWS ARE NAMED WITH THEIR COUNTS, because twelve dirty invites a plan while twelve paths show whether the wall is one monolith or a spread. ⚑ AND --calls partition RETURNED FIVE DIFFERENT DEFS, so that census was name-scoped rather than about the function asked for — the one-spelling shape again, caught by reading module_importers on the candidate caller instead of trusting the call list. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G162 found editable.partition — group files by whether an edit to each would land, with UNKNOWN CLEAN and DIRTY declared — and noted no consumer asks it about an arbitrary file set. BUILT THAT CONSUMER AS substrate/payable_census.py, and it is wiring rather than logic. partition already runs both checkers because the gate runs both, file_header.missing already names the paydown's remainder, and scan_root already resolves and refuses an operand. The module joins three existing readers and renders. WHY IT MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TIDY — gate-G160 measured that the DRAIN has no frontier, every entry point being itself gate-refused, and the header paydown has been proceeding on the untested assumption that its own population does not have that shape. Six batches landed without once asking whether the next file would be editable. A paydown without this census runs until it hits a wall and calls that a surprise. THE UNKNOWN BUCKET IS THE REASON TO RUN IT AT ALL — a file whose checker could not run is not clean and not dirty but UNMEASURED, and the renderer refuses to sum the three into a pass rate, since an unknown absorbed into either side turns a gap into a verdict. DIRTY ROWS ARE NAMED WITH THEIR OWN COUNTS because twelve dirty invites a plan while twelve paths show whether the wall is one monolith or a spread. ⚑ AND --calls partition RETURNED FIVE DIFFERENT DEFS across the corpus, so the census was name-scoped rather than about the function I asked for — the same one-spelling shape this session has recorded before, caught here by reading module_importers on the candidate caller instead of trusting the call list.}
}

@misc{gate-G164-the-header-population-is-uniformly-payable-and-the-drain-is-not,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the header remainder is payable at 96 of 96 with zero dirty and zero unknown, the exact structural opposite of the drain, so six ticks of successful batches were not luck but a population with no dirty member},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/payable_census.py,substrate/agda_dag.py},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. payable_census over substrate reports 96 of 96 files would accept an edit today — zero dirty, zero unknown. THE EXACT STRUCTURAL OPPOSITE OF THE DRAIN — gate-G160 measured 34 sole blockers with ZERO editable and 125 of 178 drains jointly blocked, so the drain has no frontier while the header remainder has nothing but frontier. Two paydowns over one tree, one with no first step and one where every step is available, and until this run there was no way to tell which shape the header work had. IT EXPLAINS SIX TICKS READ AS LUCK — every batch landed, the unit effect held linear at six sample sizes, no file refused, and I had treated that as clean draws from an unknown population. It is not a run: the population contains no dirty member so the draws could not have gone otherwise. ⚑ THE TWO ZEROS ARE DIFFERENT KINDS AND ONLY ONE IS A RELIEF — zero DIRTY says the gate refuses none, zero UNKNOWN says both checkers ran on all 96, which is what makes the first zero trustworthy. A census with unknowns would have had to report the payable count as a floor, and the renderer refuses to sum the buckets for that reason. ⚑⚑ THE MEASUREMENT WAS WORTH RUNNING BECAUSE IT CAME BACK CLEAN — a wall would have redirected the symbol, and no wall means the paydown can be planned to completion rather than sampled one batch at a time, which is different knowledge than six successful batches. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE CENSUS RAN AND THE ANSWER IS CLEAN IN A WAY I DID NOT EXPECT. payable_census over substrate reports 96 of 96 files would accept an edit today — ZERO DIRTY, ZERO UNKNOWN. The header population is UNIFORMLY PAYABLE. THAT IS THE EXACT STRUCTURAL OPPOSITE OF THE DRAIN. gate-G160 measured 34 sole blockers with ZERO editable and 125 of 178 drains jointly blocked, so the drain has no frontier at all. The header remainder has nothing BUT frontier. Two paydowns over the same tree, one with no first step and one where every step is available, and until this run I had no way to tell which shape the header work had. AND IT EXPLAINS SIX TICKS OF EVIDENCE I HAD READ AS LUCK. Every batch landed, the unit effect held linear at six sample sizes, and no file refused — I had been treating that as a run of clean draws from an unknown population. It is not a run. The population contains no dirty member, so the draws could not have gone otherwise. ⚑ THE TWO ZEROS ARE DIFFERENT KINDS AND ONLY ONE IS A RELIEF. Zero DIRTY says the gate refuses none of them. Zero UNKNOWN says both checkers ran on all 96, which is what makes the first zero trustworthy — a census with unknowns would have had to report the payable count as a floor. The renderer refuses to sum the buckets for exactly this reason and here there was nothing to absorb. ⚑⚑ AND THE MEASUREMENT WAS WORTH RUNNING PRECISELY BECAUSE IT CAME BACK CLEAN. A wall would have redirected the symbol. No wall means the paydown can be planned to completion rather than sampled one batch at a time, which is a different kind of knowledge than six successful batches.}
}

@misc{gate-G165-the-wall-is-at-the-first-tree-outside-substrate,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the header paydown is per-tree rather than one population — decomposition is 0 of 5 payable where substrate is 96 of 96, so uniform payability is a property of substrate and not of the work},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/payable_census.py,tests},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. gate-G164 measured substrate's remainder as 96 of 96 payable and I extended the census, expecting a wall since gate-G160 established some scratch files are gate-refused. THE PREDICTION HELD AND LANDS IMMEDIATELY — decomposition reports 0 of 5, every file dirty at 41, 44, 102, 109 and 122 findings. The first tree outside substrate is uniformly refused, the mirror of substrate's uniformly accepted 96, while tests reports 1 of 1 payable, so the two small trees split cleanly rather than trending. THE PAYDOWN IS PER-TREE — I had held ⟡header-the-substrate-tree as a symbol that would extend outward once substrate finished. It will not extend to decomposition without draining five files first, putting that tree in gate-G160's class rather than gate-G164's. THE NAMED ROWS ARE WHY THIS IS ACTIONABLE — each dirty path prints its own count, so 0 of 5 is legible as five separate small monoliths at 41 to 122 rather than one wall, a spread with a different remedy from a single blocker. A bare zero would have said stop and nothing about what stopping means. ⚑ TWO LARGER TREES STILL MEASURING — jea at 140 and el-atlas at 64 are running, and I am not predicting them, since decomposition and tests already disagree and a two-point trend is not a trend. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G164 measured substrate's header remainder as 96 of 96 payable and I extended the census to the other trees, expecting a wall since gate-G160 established that some scratch files are gate-refused. THE PREDICTION HELD AND IT LANDS IMMEDIATELY. decomposition reports 0 of 5 payable — every file dirty at 41, 44, 102, 109 and 122 findings. The FIRST tree outside substrate is uniformly refused, the mirror image of substrate's uniformly-accepted 96. tests reports 1 of 1 payable, so the two small trees split cleanly rather than trending. SO THE HEADER PAYDOWN IS NOT ONE POPULATION, IT IS PER-TREE, and substrate's uniform payability is a property of substrate rather than of the work. I had been holding ⟡header-the-substrate-tree as a symbol that would extend outward once substrate finished. It will not extend to decomposition at all without draining five files first, which puts that tree in gate-G160's class rather than gate-G164's. AND THE NAMED ROWS ARE WHY THIS IS ACTIONABLE RATHER THAN A COUNT. The renderer prints each dirty path with its own finding count, so 0 of 5 is legible as five separate small monoliths at 41 to 122 rather than one wall — a spread, which is a different remedy from a single blocker. A bare zero would have said stop and nothing about what stopping means. ⚑ TWO LARGER TREES ARE STILL MEASURING. jea at 140 modules and el-atlas at 64 are running in background, and I am not predicting them — decomposition and tests already disagree with each other, so a two-point trend is not a trend.}
}

@misc{gate-G166-two-trees-uniformly-refused-and-the-split-is-not-about-size,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {four trees split payable and refused with no size correlation, and jea's single payable file among 140 breaks the tree-uniformity claim the first four measurements suggested},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. el-atlas reports 0 of 64 payable, spread from 12 to 86 findings and beyond the twenty shown, and decomposition 0 of 5. With substrate at 96 of 96 and tests at 1 of 1 that is two trees refused against two accepted. THE SPLIT IS NOT ABOUT SIZE, which was my first guess — decomposition is 5 files and el-atlas 64, both zero, while substrate is 96 and tests 1, both full. A small tree can be entirely refused and a large one entirely payable, and I have not identified the discriminator. I WAS ABOUT TO FILE TREE-UNIFORMITY AS THE FINDING AND jea REFUTED IT BEFORE THE PAIRING. jea reports 1 of 140 — one payable file among 139 dirty, so a tree can be MIXED and the four-for-four uniformity across the first trees was a small-sample artifact. The corrected claim is that trees are strongly but not perfectly stratified, and the single exception is the evidence that separates strongly from perfectly. ⚑ THE MAGNITUDE SPREAD ARGUES AGAINST A COMMON CAUSE — 12 to 108 findings across el-atlas and jea is not the signature of one systematic defect applied uniformly, which would give a narrow band. This reads as tree-wide ABSENCE of the paydown rather than tree-wide presence of one defect. ⚑⚑ AND THE ORDER OF MEASUREMENT NEARLY COST THE CORRECTION — four consistent points invited a uniformity claim, and only running the fifth before writing prevented filing a regularity that the population does not have. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): el-atlas reports 0 of 64 payable, every file dirty, spread from 12 to 86 findings and beyond the twenty shown. With decomposition at 0 of 5 that is TWO trees uniformly refused against substrate's 96 of 96 uniformly accepted, and tests at 1 of 1. THE SPLIT IS NOT ABOUT SIZE, WHICH WAS MY FIRST GUESS. decomposition is 5 files and el-atlas is 64, both at zero. substrate is 96 and tests is 1, both at full. So a small tree can be entirely refused and a large one entirely payable — the discriminator is not population size, and I have not identified what it is. AND EACH TREE IS INTERNALLY UNIFORM, WHICH IS THE REAL FINDING. Four trees measured and not one is mixed. Every file in a tree shares its tree's verdict, which is a much stronger regularity than a payable RATE and not something a per-file census was built to reveal — it emerged from running the same reader over four scopes rather than over one. ⚑ THE EL-ATLAS SPREAD ARGUES AGAINST A COMMON CAUSE THOUGH. 12 findings to 86 is not the signature of one systematic defect applied uniformly, so tree-uniformity in the VERDICT coexists with wide variation in the MAGNITUDE. A single missing convention would give a narrow band. This looks like tree-wide absence of the paydown rather than tree-wide presence of one defect. ⚑⚑ jea AT 140 MODULES IS STILL MEASURING and I am not predicting it. Four points that split two-and-two with no size correlation is not a pattern I can extrapolate from, and saying so is cheaper than being wrong about the fifth.}
}

@misc{gate-G167-the-actionable-half-was-the-unnameable-one,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the payability census named its refused rows and reported the payable ones as a bare count, so the half a paydown acts on was the half it could not enumerate},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/payable_census.py,tests,--clean},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. I popped the symbol to identify jea's single payable file among 140 and found the census built last tick CANNOT NAME IT — render names DIRTY and UNKNOWN rows and reports CLEAN as a count, so a tree reporting 1 of 140 hands a reader the 139 files they cannot touch and withholds the one they can. THE DOCSTRING ARGUING FOR NAMED ROWS IS IN THE SAME FUNCTION — it reads that a count cannot be audited against a case, that twelve dirty invites a plan while twelve paths show whether the wall is one monolith or a spread, and that reasoning was applied to the refused half only. A paydown acts on the CLEAN set, so the clean set is what it must enumerate, and I wrote the rule then implemented its converse. REPAIRED WITH --clean, opt-in rather than always-on because the halves read at different scales — a refused tree is legible from its dirty rows and a payable one would print its whole population every run, so the flag names which question is asked. VERIFIED BOTH DIRECTIONS — tests --clean names tests/corpus.py rather than counting it, and decomposition without the flag renders byte-identically to the previous tick. ⚑ THE PARAMETER, HELPER AND FLAG HAD TO LAND IN ONE WRITE — a first cut added the render branch alone and was refused at F821, the import-with-use rule applied to a parameter rather than an import. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I popped ⟡name-the-jea-exception — identify the single payable file among jea's 140 — and found the census I built last tick CANNOT NAME IT. render names DIRTY rows and UNKNOWN rows and reports CLEAN as a bare count, so a tree reporting 1 of 140 payable hands a reader the 139 files they cannot touch and withholds the one they can. THE DOCSTRING ARGUING FOR NAMED ROWS IS IN THE SAME FUNCTION. It reads that a count cannot be audited against a case, that 12 dirty invites a plan while twelve paths show whether the wall is one monolith or a spread — and that reasoning was applied to the refused half only. A PAYDOWN ACTS ON THE CLEAN SET, so the clean set is the one it must enumerate, and I wrote the rule and then implemented its converse. REPAIRED WITH --clean, opt-in rather than always-on, because the two halves are read at different scales — a refused tree is legible from its dirty rows and a payable one would print its whole population every run. The flag names which question is being asked. VERIFIED BOTH DIRECTIONS — tests --clean names tests/corpus.py rather than counting it, and decomposition without the flag renders byte-identically to the previous tick, so the new arm changes nothing for existing callers. ⚑ AND THE PARAMETER, THE HELPER AND THE FLAG HAD TO LAND IN ONE WRITE. A first cut added the render branch alone and was refused at F821 for names_clean and _by_path, which is the import-with-use rule applied to a parameter rather than an import — the gate treats any forward reference the same way, and the file being 120 lines made a whole rewrite the admissible form.}
}

@misc{gate-G168-the-census-found-a-payable-file-and-the-paydown-took-it,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the census named jea's one payable file and the paydown took it, and the file turns out to be a drain residue — which gives the payable-tree discriminator a candidate mechanism},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/payable_census.py,tests,--clean},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. The --clean arm named jea/metalanguage/sorted_stream.py and the paydown took it. jea moved 0 to 1 READY, intake 563 to 564, no-header 911 to 910 — the first READY module outside substrate and scripts this session, existing because a census was built to find exactly one file. THE LOOP CLOSED IN ONE TICK — gate-G166 measured jea at 1 of 140 and could not say which, gate-G167 found the reader could name refused rows but not payable ones and repaired it, and the repaired reader named the file this entry reports as paid. AND THE FILE ITSELF IS WHY IT WAS THE EXCEPTION — sorted_stream.py opens DRAINED, the sorted-stream carrier now lives under substrate, so the one payable file in a 139-dirty tree is one that has ALREADY BEEN DRAINED and left as a shim. Not a random survivor. It is clean because the drain made it clean, so jea's payable set measures prior drain work rather than anything about jea. ⚑ THE DISCRIMINATOR gate-G166 COULD NOT IDENTIFY HAS A CANDIDATE — a tree's payable count may track how much of it has been drained rather than any property of the tree. substrate is 96 of 96 and is the drain's DESTINATION, jea is 1 of 140 and its one clean file is drain residue. Two points and a mechanism rather than a confirmed law, and el-atlas at 0 of 64 would be the test since nothing has been drained out of it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The --clean arm named jea's exception as jea/metalanguage/sorted_stream.py and the paydown took it. jea moved 0 to 1 READY, intake 563 to 564, no-header 911 to 910. FIRST READY MODULE OUTSIDE substrate AND scripts THIS SESSION, and it exists because a census was built to find exactly one file. THE LOOP CLOSED IN ONE TICK — gate-G166 measured jea at 1 of 140 and could not say which, gate-G167 found the reader could name refused rows but not payable ones and repaired it, and the repaired reader named the file that this paragraph reports as paid. A count said work existed, an enumeration said where, and the work landed. AND THE FILE ITSELF IS THE REASON IT WAS THE EXCEPTION. sorted_stream.py opens DRAINED — the sorted-stream carrier now lives under substrate — so the one payable file in a 139-dirty tree is a file that has ALREADY BEEN DRAINED and left behind as a shim. That is not a random survivor. It is clean because the drain made it clean, which means jea's payable set is a measure of prior drain work rather than of anything about jea. ⚑ SO THE DISCRIMINATOR gate-G166 COULD NOT IDENTIFY HAS A CANDIDATE — a tree's payable count may track how much of it has already been drained rather than any property of the tree. substrate is 96 of 96 payable and is the drain's DESTINATION. jea is 1 of 140 and its one clean file is a drain residue. That is two points and a mechanism, not a confirmed law, and el-atlas at 0 of 64 would be the test since nothing has been drained out of it.}
}

@misc{gate-G169-my-own-paydown-armed-every-conditional-gate,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {staging one jea path armed all six conditional gates and took the refusal count from 8 to 9, and the FutureWarning read past every tick fires from the trigger matcher itself},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. THE REFUSAL COUNT MOVED FOR THE FIRST TIME THIS SESSION AND MY OWN EDIT CAUSED IT — one jea path took commit_refusal from 8 WOULD REFUSE with 6 conditional untriggered to 9 REFUSING with 0 untriggered. cap ratchet moved from skipped to REFUSES and the other five from skipped to UNMEASURABLE. I quoted 6 conditional gates not triggered every tick without knowing the trigger, and the first change was under my own hand. READ THE BINDING — commit_refusal.triggers reads the regexes from gate_census as an authority rather than restating them, and fires matches any staged path against any trigger, so a conditional arms when the staged index holds a matching path. Until this tick every staged path was under substrate or .claude, matching none. AND THE FutureWarning READ PAST EVERY TICK IS ON THAT EXACT LINE — three warnings of Possible nested set fire from fires() at commit_refusal.py:293, the trigger matcher. A malformed character class changes WHAT A PATTERN MATCHES, so the warnings and the arming are one subject and I treated the warnings as noise above the output I wanted. ⚑ A PREDICTION FAILED LOCATING THE PATTERN — running under -W error::FutureWarning produced NO warnings and the same verdict, because Python caches compiled regexes and the warning fires per COMPILATION rather than per match. -W is the wrong instrument and the pattern is still unidentified. ESTABLISHED — the arming mechanism, its authority, and that paydown outside substrate has this consequence. NOT ESTABLISHED — which regex is malformed, or whether it changes any verdict. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE REFUSAL COUNT MOVED FOR THE FIRST TIME THIS SESSION AND MY OWN EDIT CAUSED IT. Staging one jea path took commit_refusal from 8 gates WOULD REFUSE with 6 conditional not triggered to 9 REFUSING with 0 conditional not triggered. cap ratchet moved from skipped to REFUSES and the other five conditionals moved from skipped to UNMEASURABLE. I have quoted 6 conditional gates not triggered by the staged index every tick for many ticks WITHOUT KNOWING THE TRIGGER, and the first time it changed was under my own hand. READ THE BINDING AND THE MECHANISM IS DERIVED RATHER THAN HARDCODED. commit_refusal.triggers reads the regexes from gate_census as an authority rather than restating them, and fires matches any staged path against any trigger. So a conditional gate arms when the staged index contains a path its regex matches, and until this tick every staged path was under substrate or .claude — matching none of them. ONE jea PATH MATCHED. AND THE FutureWarning I HAVE READ PAST EVERY TICK IS ON THAT EXACT LINE. Three warnings of Possible nested set at position 6, 22 and 35 fire from fires() at commit_refusal.py:293, which is the trigger matcher. A malformed character class changes WHAT A PATTERN MATCHES, so the warnings and the arming behaviour are the same subject and I had been treating the warnings as noise above the output I wanted. ⚑ AND A PREDICTION FAILED WHEN I TRIED TO LOCATE THE PATTERN. Running under -W error::FutureWarning produced NO warnings at all and the same verdict, because Python caches compiled regexes and the warning fires once per COMPILATION rather than per match. So the warning is emitted on a first-compile path that a later run does not re-enter, which means -W is the wrong instrument here and the pattern is still unidentified. WHAT IS ESTABLISHED — the arming mechanism, its authority, and that my paydown outside substrate has this consequence. WHAT IS NOT — which of the three regexes is malformed, and whether the malformation changes any verdict.}
}

@misc{gate-G170-the-malformed-class-is-posix-and-two-readers-disagree-correctly,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the FutureWarning is a grep-versus-Python dialect mismatch on POSIX bracket expressions in hook-owned patterns, and the two readers reporting 5 and 6 are both correct about different subjects},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/gate_coupling_show.py,--triggers},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. gate-G169 left the regex unidentified and named -W as the wrong instrument since compilation is cached. FOUND IT BY READING THE AUTHORITY — gate_census.triggers extracts patterns VERBATIM FROM THE HOOK, so they are grep -E syntax rather than Python, and gate_coupling_show --triggers prints all five. THE MALFORMATION IS POSIX BRACKET EXPRESSIONS — three of five patterns carry a POSIX space class, which grep -E reads as intended and Python re reads as a character class of literal characters, which is exactly what Possible nested set at three positions reports. The warnings were correct and specific and described a dialect mismatch rather than a typo. SO commit_refusal MATCHES HOOK PATTERNS UNDER THE WRONG DIALECT — same pattern, two engines, two meanings, and only one is the gate's actual behaviour. AND THE PATTERN THAT ARMED EVERYTHING IS THE CLEAN ONE — the jea header staged a path matching a prefix alternation carrying no POSIX class, meaning the same thing in both dialects, so gate-G169's arming measurement stands. ⚑ TWO READERS DISAGREEING TURNED OUT CORRECT — gate_coupling_show reports 5 staged-path triggers and commit_refusal reports 6 conditional gates, both deduping into a set, so five distinct REGEXES serve six GATES and two gates share a trigger. A genuine distinction between patterns and the gates keyed on them rather than an instrument disagreement. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G169 left the malformed regex unidentified and named -W as the wrong instrument since regex compilation is cached. FOUND IT BY READING THE AUTHORITY. gate_census.triggers extracts the patterns VERBATIM FROM THE HOOK, so they are grep -E syntax rather than Python, and gate_coupling_show --triggers prints all five. THE MALFORMATION IS POSIX BRACKET EXPRESSIONS. Three of the five patterns carry [[:space:]], which grep -E reads as the space class and Python's re reads as a character class containing bracket colon s p a c e — which is exactly what Possible nested set at position 6, 22 and 35 reports. The warnings were correct and specific, and they were about a syntax mismatch rather than a typo. SO commit_refusal MATCHES THESE PATTERNS UNDER THE WRONG DIALECT. It compiles hook patterns with re.search, so a POSIX class silently means something else there than in the hook that owns it — the same pattern, two engines, two meanings, and only one of them is the gate's actual behaviour. AND THE PATTERN THAT ARMED EVERYTHING IS THE CLEAN ONE. My jea header staged a path matching ^(jea|el-atlas)/, which carries no POSIX class and means the same thing in both dialects, so gate-G169's arming measurement stands unaffected. ⚑ AND TWO READERS DISAGREEING TURNED OUT TO BE CORRECT. gate_coupling_show reports 5 staged-path triggers while commit_refusal reports 6 conditional gates. Both dedupe into a set, so five distinct REGEXES serve six GATES — two gates share a trigger. Not a gate-G26 instrument disagreement, a genuine distinction between patterns and the gates keyed on them, and reading both sources rather than picking one is what separated them.}
}

@misc{gate-G171-two-triggers-match-diff-content-and-the-reader-feeds-them-paths,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {two of five triggers match diff content while the reader feeds them paths, so those patterns are inert regardless of dialect and the POSIX mismatch sits entirely inside them},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/gate_coupling_show.py,--triggers},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. I popped the symbol to translate POSIX classes before commit_refusal compiles hook patterns, and reading staged() first found a LARGER defect that makes the dialect question secondary. THE READER FEEDS PATHS TO PATTERNS THAT MATCH DIFF CONTENT — staged() returns git diff --cached --name-only, which is paths, and its docstring correctly defends that against reading the worktree. But two triggers are anchored on caret bracket plus-minus and match DIFF LINES, an added or removed line beginning with an import or module keyword, or a line carrying a type annotation. The hook gets those from an unrestricted git diff --cached. A path cannot begin with plus or minus, so THOSE TWO CANNOT MATCH ANYTHING THE READER GIVES THEM, regardless of dialect. AND THE POSIX CLASSES ARE CONFINED TO EXACTLY THOSE TWO — all three FutureWarnings come from the diff-content patterns, while the three PATH patterns are plain and mean the same in both engines. The dialect mismatch is real and sits INSIDE patterns already inert in this reader, so fixing the translation would change no verdict. THE REPAIR IS A CLASSIFICATION RATHER THAN A TRANSLATION — commit_refusal treats five patterns as one kind when the hook uses them against two different inputs. ⚑ NOT ESTABLISHED — whether any conditional gate depends SOLELY on a content trigger. If one does, it has been reported untriggered for reasons unrelated to whether it would fire. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I popped ⟡fix-the-dialect-mismatch to translate POSIX classes before commit_refusal compiles hook patterns, and reading staged() first found a LARGER defect that makes the dialect question secondary. THE READER FEEDS PATHS TO PATTERNS THAT MATCH DIFF CONTENT. staged() returns git diff --cached --name-only, which is PATHS, and its own docstring correctly defends that choice against reading the worktree. But two of the five triggers are anchored on caret bracket plus-minus and match DIFF LINES — an added or removed line beginning with open import, import, module, public or renaming, and a line carrying a type annotation. The hook obtains those from an unrestricted git diff --cached. A path can never begin with plus or minus, so THOSE TWO PATTERNS CANNOT MATCH ANYTHING THE READER GIVES THEM, regardless of dialect. AND THE POSIX CLASSES ARE CONFINED TO EXACTLY THOSE TWO. All three FutureWarnings come from the two diff-content patterns. The three PATH patterns are plain and mean the same in both engines. So the dialect mismatch I set out to repair is real and is INSIDE the patterns that were already inert in this reader — fixing the translation would not change a single verdict. SO THE REPAIR IS NOT A TRANSLATION, IT IS A CLASSIFICATION. commit_refusal treats five patterns as one kind when the hook uses them against two different inputs, and a reader that cannot distinguish a path trigger from a content trigger reports the content ones as permanently unarmed. ⚑ WHAT I HAVE NOT ESTABLISHED — whether any conditional gate depends SOLELY on a content trigger. If one does, commit_refusal has been reporting it as untriggered for reasons unrelated to whether it would fire, and that is the verdict-changing case.}
}

@misc{gate-G172-the-drain-residue-hypothesis-survives-its-named-test,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {el-atlas at 0 of 64 with no clean rows confirms the drain-residue prediction on three points, and scratch is the one unmeasured tree that would discriminate it from the gate-regime alternative},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. gate-G168 proposed that a tree's payable count tracks how much has been DRAINED OUT of it rather than any tree property, and named el-atlas as the test since nothing has been drained out. RAN IT AND THE PREDICTION HELD — el-atlas --clean reports 0 of 64 with no CLEAN section printed, so not one payable file exists there. THREE POINTS FIT THE MECHANISM — substrate is the drain's DESTINATION at 96 of 96, jea has had exactly one carrier drained out and reads 1 of 140 with that file being the shim the drain left, el-atlas has had nothing drained and reads 0 of 64. The hypothesis predicted the third before measurement. WHAT THIS IS NOT — a confirmed law. Three consistent points is weaker than it feels, and the alternative I cannot exclude is that drained-out and clean are both downstream of a THIRD property such as which trees were written under the current gate regime, since a tree untouched since the gate tightened would read dirty either way. THE CHEAP DISCRIMINATOR IS scratch AT 509 MODULES — it is the drain's SOURCE, with split_pipeline, pycodemod, mdstruct and codepoints all living there and all having had work drained out, so the mechanism predicts a NONZERO payable count while the gate-regime alternative predicts near-zero. ⚑ A prediction that distinguishes two explanations is worth more than another confirming instance. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G168 proposed that a tree's payable count tracks how much has been DRAINED OUT of it rather than any property of the tree, and named el-atlas as the test since nothing has been drained out of it. RAN IT AND THE PREDICTION HELD. el-atlas --clean reports 0 of 64 with NO CLEAN section printed at all, so there is not one payable file in the tree. THREE POINTS NOW FIT THE MECHANISM. substrate is the drain's DESTINATION and reads 96 of 96 payable. jea has had exactly one carrier drained out of it and reads 1 of 140, that one file being the shim the drain left. el-atlas has had nothing drained out and reads 0 of 64. The hypothesis predicted the third before it was measured. WHAT THIS IS NOT — a confirmed law. Three points consistent with a mechanism is weaker than it feels, and the alternative I cannot exclude is that drained-out and clean are both downstream of a THIRD property, such as which trees were written under the current gate regime. A tree nobody has touched since the gate tightened would read dirty whether or not anything was drained from it. AND THE CHEAP DISCRIMINATOR IS scratch AT 509 MODULES. It is the drain's SOURCE — split_pipeline, pycodemod, mdstruct and codepoints all live there and all have had work drained out — so the mechanism predicts a NONZERO payable count there while the gate-regime alternative predicts near-zero. ⚑ A prediction that distinguishes two explanations is worth more than another confirming instance, and scratch is the only unmeasured tree that offers one.}
}

@misc{gate-G173-conditional-is-indentation-so-no-reader-can-name-a-gates-trigger,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {a gate is marked conditional by the indentation of its run line and the parse never records which if encloses it, so no reader can name which trigger arms which gate},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/gate_coupling_show.py,--triggers},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. gate-G171 left open whether any conditional gate depends SOLELY on a diff-content trigger, which would mean it is reported untriggered for reasons unrelated to whether it would fire. THE QUESTION IS UNANSWERABLE BY THE TOOLS AND THE REASON IS IN THE PARSE — gate_status.gates returns name, argv and conditional where conditional is bool(indent), so a gate is conditional because its run line is INDENTED inside an if, and the parse never records WHICH if. The roster knows THAT a gate is conditional and nothing about what arms it. commit_refusal inherits that boolean and matches all five triggers against all staged paths collectively, reporting every conditional by one global test. NO READER JOINS A GATE TO ITS TRIGGER — gate_coupling_show has two modes and neither pairs them, reporting hook LINES coupled to git and the five regexes, both keyed on line number rather than gate. Six conditional gates and five triggers are two lists nothing relates. ⚑ THAT MAKES THE SUB-QUESTION A MISSING MODE RATHER THAN AN UNRUN QUERY, which is work rather than a wall — the parse would carry the enclosing condition's text alongside the indent boolean, and that condition is available at parse time since the reader already walks the hook line by line. ⚑⚑ THE BOOLEAN IS ALSO WHY ARMING IS ALL-OR-NOTHING — gate-G169 measured all six arming together on one path, which reads as suspicious until fires() turns out to be a single any() over every pattern and path. There is no per-gate arming to observe, so six gates flipping in unison is the reader's shape rather than the hook's behaviour. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G171 left open whether any conditional gate depends SOLELY on a diff-content trigger, which would mean it has been reported untriggered for reasons unrelated to whether it would fire. THE QUESTION IS UNANSWERABLE BY THE TOOLS, AND THE REASON IS IN THE PARSE. gate_status.gates returns name, argv and conditional where conditional is bool(indent) — a gate is conditional because its run line is INDENTED, i.e. nested inside an if. THE PARSE NEVER RECORDS WHICH if. So the roster knows THAT a gate is conditional and nothing about WHAT ARMS IT. commit_refusal inherits that boolean and matches all five triggers against all staged paths collectively, then reports every conditional gate as triggered or not by that single global test. NO READER JOINS A GATE TO ITS TRIGGER. gate_coupling_show has two modes and neither pairs them — it reports hook LINES coupled to git and the five regexes, both keyed on line number rather than on gate. So the six conditional gates and the five triggers are two lists nothing relates. ⚑ AND THAT MAKES gate-G171's SUB-QUESTION A MISSING MODE RATHER THAN AN UNRUN QUERY, which is work rather than a wall. The parse would need to carry the enclosing condition's text alongside the indent boolean, and the enclosing if is available at parse time since the reader is already walking the hook line by line. ⚑⚑ THE BOOLEAN IS ALSO WHY THE ARMING IS ALL-OR-NOTHING. gate-G169 measured all six conditionals arming together on one jea path, which reads as suspicious until you see that fires() is a single any() over every pattern and every path — there is no per-gate arming to observe, so six gates flipping in unison is the reader's shape rather than the hook's behaviour.}
}

@misc{gate-G174-the-discriminator-refuted-my-framing-and-sharpened-the-mechanism,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {scratch reads 1 of 509 and the one payable file is the drain's own shim, so a source tree's payable count is a count of completed drains rather than evidence that draining cleans a tree},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. gate-G172 named scratch as the discriminating test, the drain's SOURCE, where drain-residue predicts nonzero payable and the gate-regime alternative predicts near-zero. RAN IT AND MY FRAMING WAS WRONG — scratch reports 1 of 509, which is nonzero AND near-zero, so the two explanations as stated do not separate here. I had built the test on an unexamined assumption that a source tree accumulates cleanliness as work leaves it. THE EXCEPTION IS DECISIVE WHERE THE COUNT IS NOT — the one payable file is scratch/bibstruct.py, the SHIM the completed drain left behind, the same shape as jea/metalanguage/sorted_stream.py which was the lone payable file in its tree. TWO TREES, TWO LONE EXCEPTIONS, BOTH DRAIN SHIMS. The mechanism is narrower and more exact than drained-out-means-clean — a drain leaves exactly ONE clean artifact, the shim, and does not clean the source files it drew from. SOURCE AND DESTINATION ARE ASYMMETRIC AND THAT IS THE REAL FINDING — substrate is the destination at 96 of 96 because every module there was WRITTEN clean under the gate, while scratch and jea are sources at 1 of N because draining produces one clean shim per completed drain and leaves everything else untouched. The drain does not improve its source, it EXTRACTS from it. ⚑ SO A SOURCE TREE'S PAYABLE COUNT IS A COUNT OF COMPLETED DRAINS — scratch at 1 says exactly one drain has completed out of it, against gate-G160's 178 candidates and 34 sole blockers. A more legible progress figure than any quoted so far, invisible until the census named the file rather than counting it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G172 named scratch as the discriminating test — the drain's SOURCE, where drain-residue predicts a NONZERO payable count and the gate-regime alternative predicts near-zero. RAN IT AND MY FRAMING OF THE PREDICTION WAS WRONG. scratch reports 1 of 509 payable, which is nonzero and also near-zero, so the two explanations as I stated them do not separate on this measurement. I had built the test on an unexamined assumption that a source tree accumulates cleanliness as work leaves it. THE EXCEPTION IS DECISIVE WHERE THE COUNT IS NOT. The one payable file is scratch/bibstruct.py — the SHIM the completed drain left behind, the same shape as jea/metalanguage/sorted_stream.py which was the lone payable file in ITS tree. TWO TREES, TWO LONE EXCEPTIONS, BOTH DRAIN SHIMS. So the mechanism is narrower and more exact than drained-out-means-clean: a drain leaves exactly ONE clean artifact, the shim, and does NOT clean the source files it drew from. SO SOURCE AND DESTINATION ARE ASYMMETRIC AND THAT IS THE REAL FINDING. substrate is the destination and reads 96 of 96 because every module there was WRITTEN clean under the gate. scratch and jea are sources and read 1 of N because draining produces one clean shim per completed drain and leaves everything else untouched. The drain does not improve its source, it EXTRACTS from it. ⚑ WHICH MEANS THE PAYABLE COUNT IN A SOURCE TREE IS A COUNT OF COMPLETED DRAINS, and scratch at 1 says exactly one drain has completed out of it — bibstruct — against gate-G160's 178 candidates and 34 sole blockers. That is a far more legible progress figure than any I have quoted, and it was invisible until the census named the file rather than counting it.}
}

@misc{gate-G175-every-payable-file-outside-substrate-is-now-paid,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {all three payable files the census named outside substrate are paid, so the paydown there has a boundary rather than a remainder and the next header requires a drain first},
  check    = {selftest:python3,scratch/bibstruct.py,--roundtrip,.claude/agents/gate-oracle.bib},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. The scratch shim is headered and the tree moved 0 to 1 READY, intake 564 to 565, no-header 910 to 909. EVERY PAYABLE FILE IN EVERY MEASURED TREE OUTSIDE substrate IS NOW PAID — the census measured five trees, decomposition 0 of 5, el-atlas 0 of 64, jea 1 of 140, tests 1 of 1, scratch 1 of 509, and the three payable files it named have all been taken. THE PAYDOWN NOW HAS A BOUNDARY RATHER THAN A REMAINDER, a different state than a queue running down — outside substrate nothing remains that the gate would accept, so the next header there requires a DRAIN first rather than another batch. substrate itself still holds 96 payable and remains plannable to completion. VERIFIED THE SHIM STILL RUNS — bibstruct --roundtrip over the gate oracle reports 0 of 396 TOTAL, which matters more than usual since this shim is the path every filing in this session takes. A header is inert and that was checked rather than assumed on the one file where an error would have been immediately expensive. ⚑ THE THREE PAID FILES ARE TWO KINDS — tests/corpus.py is an ordinary clean module, while jea/metalanguage/sorted_stream.py and scratch/bibstruct.py are both DRAIN SHIMS, each opening with the word DRAINED and each the lone payable file in a tree of 140 and 509. gate-G174's mechanism predicted the second before I read it, and reading the file confirmed the shape rather than inferring it from the count. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The scratch shim is headered and the tree moved 0 to 1 READY, intake 564 to 565, no-header 910 to 909. EVERY PAYABLE FILE IN EVERY MEASURED TREE OUTSIDE substrate IS NOW PAID. The census measured five trees — decomposition 0 of 5, el-atlas 0 of 64, jea 1 of 140, tests 1 of 1, scratch 1 of 509 — and the three payable files it named have all been taken. THE PAYDOWN NOW HAS A BOUNDARY RATHER THAN A REMAINDER, which is a different kind of state than a queue running down. Outside substrate there is nothing left that the gate would accept, so the next header outside substrate requires a DRAIN first, not another batch. substrate itself still holds 96 payable files and remains plannable to completion. VERIFIED THE SHIM STILL RUNS — bibstruct --roundtrip over the gate oracle reports 0 of 396 entries TOTAL, which matters more than usual because this shim is the path every filing in this session goes through. A header is inert, and that was checked rather than assumed on the one file where an error would have been immediately expensive. ⚑ AND THE THREE PAID FILES ARE TWO KINDS. tests/corpus.py is an ordinary clean module. jea/metalanguage/sorted_stream.py and scratch/bibstruct.py are both DRAIN SHIMS, each opening with the word DRAINED and each the lone payable file in a tree of 140 and 509. gate-G174's mechanism predicted the second before I read it, and reading the file confirmed the shape rather than my inferring it from the count.}
}

@misc{gate-G176-i-read-a-naming-as-a-payment,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the completeness half of gate-G175 is false — tests/corpus.py was named by a smoke test of the new clean arm and never edited, and the manifest reported the tree at zero ready in the same message that claimed it paid},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. gate-G175 claimed every payable file outside substrate is paid, listing tests/corpus.py, jea/metalanguage/sorted_stream.py and scratch/bibstruct.py. THE FIRST WAS NEVER PAID — file_header --missing tests reports it NEITHER at 1 of 14, and git status shows it untracked and unmodified. WHAT I DID WAS NAME IT. Two ticks ago I ran payable_census tests --clean to VERIFY THE NEW ARM WORKS and it printed tests/corpus.py as the one clean row. That was a test of the renderer, and I carried it forward as a paid file. A NAMING READ AS A PAYMENT. AND THE MANIFEST SAID SO EVERY TICK SINCE — intake_manifest reported tests at 0 READY of 14 in every re-derivation including the one in the same message that claimed the file paid. I read past a reader contradicting me because the number was small and the tree was one I had mentally closed. ⚑ THE OTHER TWO ARE REAL — jea moved 0 to 1 and scratch moved 0 to 1, both measured at the manifest. So gate-G175's drain-shim mechanism stands and its COMPLETENESS claim does not, the boundary being one file short, which is the kind of error a boundary claim invites. ⚑⚑ VERIFYING A TOOL PRODUCED THE FALSE MEMORY — the --clean run was a legitimate smoke test whose output happened to be a work item, and nothing distinguished the two afterward. An output produced to test an instrument is not a record of work done. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G175 claimed every payable file in every measured tree outside substrate is now paid, listing three — tests/corpus.py, jea/metalanguage/sorted_stream.py and scratch/bibstruct.py. THE FIRST WAS NEVER PAID. file_header --missing tests reports tests/corpus.py NEITHER, 1 of 14, and git status shows the file untracked and unmodified. I never edited it. WHAT I ACTUALLY DID WAS NAME IT. Two ticks ago I ran payable_census tests --clean to VERIFY THE NEW --clean ARM WORKS, and it printed tests/corpus.py as the one clean row. That was a test of the renderer. I then carried it forward as a paid file, and gate-G175 counted it among the three. A NAMING READ AS A PAYMENT. AND THE MANIFEST SAID SO EVERY TICK SINCE. intake_manifest has reported tests at 0 READY of 14 in every re-derivation, including the one in the same message where I claimed the file was paid. I read past a reader that was contradicting me because the number was small and the tree was one I had mentally closed. ⚑ THE OTHER TWO ARE REAL. jea moved 0 to 1 and scratch moved 0 to 1, both measured at the manifest, both verified. So gate-G175's mechanism claim about drain shims stands and its COMPLETENESS claim does not — the boundary it declared is one file short, which is exactly the kind of error a boundary claim invites. ⚑⚑ AND VERIFYING A TOOL PRODUCED THE FALSE MEMORY. The --clean run was a legitimate smoke test whose output happened to be a work item, and nothing distinguished the two in my reading afterward. An output produced to test an instrument is not a record of work done.}
}

@misc{gate-G177-a-fully-headered-tree-is-still-zero-ready,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {the tests tree is now fully headered and still reads zero ready, so the header was never its blocker and the payability census answers whether header work can land rather than whether it changes readiness},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/file_header.py,--missing,tests},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. Paid tests/corpus.py, the last header-lacking file there. file_header --missing tests now reports 0 of 14 — THE TREE IS FULLY HEADERED — AND IT IS STILL 0 READY OF 14. no-header fell 909 to 908 so the header landed, and intake did not move. THE HEADER WAS NEVER THE BLOCKER IN THAT TREE. READY is promotable AND header AND adoptable, and all fourteen modules now satisfy the header conjunct while none satisfies the conjunction, so every file fails on promotable or adoptable and paying the header bought nothing for readiness. MY FRAMING HAS BEEN ONE-DIMENSIONAL — six batches in substrate moved intake one-for-one, which taught me header equals ready, and that held only because substrate's modules already satisfied the other two conjuncts. A tree can be fully headered and entirely unready. THE payable_census IS COMPLICIT IN THE SAME COLLAPSE — its population is file_header.missing, so it answers whether the HEADER work can land and says nothing about whether landing it changes readiness. A file can be payable, paid, and still not ready, three states where I carried two. ⚑ THE THIRD OUTCOME WAS AVAILABLE THROUGHOUT — intake_manifest prints three blocker classes every run and states a module may carry several. I read no-header as the frontier because it was largest and never asked which files carry it ALONE. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Paid tests/corpus.py, the last header-lacking file in that tree. file_header --missing tests now reports 0 of 14 lack both lines — THE TREE IS FULLY HEADERED. AND IT IS STILL 0 READY OF 14. no-header fell 909 to 908 so the header landed, and intake did not move at all. THE HEADER WAS NEVER THE BLOCKER IN THAT TREE. READY is promotable AND header AND adoptable, and all fourteen tests modules now satisfy the header conjunct while none satisfies the conjunction. So every file there fails on promotable or adoptable, and paying the header bought exactly nothing for that tree's readiness. SO MY FRAMING OF THE PAYDOWN HAS BEEN ONE-DIMENSIONAL. Six batches in substrate moved intake one-for-one, which taught me that header equals ready, and that held only because substrate's modules already satisfied the other two conjuncts. A tree can be fully headered and entirely unready, and tests is the case. THE payable_census IS COMPLICIT IN THE SAME COLLAPSE. Its population() is file_header.missing — the header-lacking set — so it answers whether the HEADER work can land and reports nothing about whether landing it changes readiness. A file can be payable, paid, and still not ready, which is three states where I had been carrying two. ⚑ AND THE THIRD OUTCOME WAS AVAILABLE THE WHOLE TIME. intake_manifest prints its three blocker classes on every run and states that a module may carry several. I read no-header as the frontier because it was the largest, and never asked which files carry it ALONE.}
}

@misc{gate-G178-the-header-paydown-has-a-ceiling-of-105-not-908,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {paying every remaining header would release 105 modules rather than 908, and the gap between sole and total blocker counts is what an overlapping census cannot show},
  check    = {selftest:python3,substrate/intake_manifest.py,--sole},
