ss reads as the frontier. Added --sole to the module that owns the question, a reader plus a dispatch on a clean file. MEASURED — no-header 105 SOLE of 908 TOTAL, not-promotable 16 of 819, reaches-unpackaged 2 of 69. SO PAYING EVERY REMAINING HEADER WOULD RELEASE 105 MODULES, NOT 908. The paydown I have run for many ticks has a ceiling of an eighth of the figure I was reading, and substrate holds 96 payable of those 105 — so the substrate work is nearly the WHOLE of the effective header paydown rather than the beginning of it. AND THE GAP BETWEEN THE COLUMNS IS THE FINDING RATHER THAN EITHER NUMBER. not-promotable at 16 sole of 819 total is the extreme case — 803 modules carry it alongside something else, so paying promotability alone would release almost nothing. A blocker with a large total and a small sole count is one whose paydown is mostly blocked elsewhere, and no ordering by total could have shown that. ⚑ THE MODULE HAS NO SELFTEST, which I found by trying to run one. The default rendering is verified byte-identical to this tick's Phase 1 run, so the regression risk is covered by comparison rather than by a suite, and the absent suite is recorded as a gap rather than papered over.}
