@misc{gate-G179-the-sole-blocked-set-is-mostly-the-suites-of-modules-i-already-paid,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {most of the 105 sole-blocked modules are the selftest files of modules already headered, so six batches each left a second sole-blocked file behind and a count could never show it},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.bib_argv_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate-session. gate-G178 counted the sole-blocked set at 105 and could not name it — sole_blocked returns the modules and its only caller reduced them to a count, which I wrote ONE TICK AFTER filing a finding about a census that named its refused half and counted its payable one. Added --names and the composition is unpredicted. THE MAJORITY OF THE 105 ARE _selftest FILES — bib_argv_selftest, bib_parse_selftest, bib_write_selftest and twenty more, the SUITES of modules headered across six batches. I paid each module and left its suite, and each suite is separately sole-blocked, so half the effective paydown is work my own earlier batches created by touching one file of each pair. PAID FOUR FROM THE NAMED SET AND BOTH READERS MOVED BY EXACTLY FOUR — intake 565 to 569, sole-blocked 105 to 101, no-header 908 to 904. First time this session the two figures agree on a batch's effect, because the batch was drawn from a NAMED sole-blocked set rather than the header-lacking set. Six earlier batches moved intake one-for-one by luck of substrate's composition and the tests batch moved it by zero. ⚑ THE PAIRING IS THE MECHANISM AND IS INVISIBLE TO A COUNT — a module and its suite are two files each carrying no-header alone, so paying the module releases one and leaves a second sole-blocked file. by_blocker cannot show that and --names does, the enumerate-before-acting rule paying off on a population already acted on six times. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G178 counted the sole-blocked set at 105 and could not name it — sole_blocked returns the modules and its only caller reduced them to a count, which I wrote ONE TICK AFTER filing a finding about a census that named its refused half and counted its payable one. Added --names, and the composition is a finding I could not have predicted. THE MAJORITY OF THE 105 ARE _selftest.py FILES. substrate/bib_argv_selftest, bib_parse_selftest, bib_write_selftest and twenty more — the SUITES of the modules I headered across six batches. I paid each module and left its suite, and each suite is separately sole-blocked, so half the effective paydown is work my own earlier batches created by touching only one file of each pair. PAID FOUR FROM THE NAMED SET AND BOTH READERS MOVED BY EXACTLY FOUR. Intake 565 to 569, sole-blocked 105 to 101, no-header 908 to 904. That is the first time this session the two figures have agreed on a batch's effect, and the reason is that the batch was drawn from a NAMED sole-blocked set rather than from the header-lacking set. Six earlier batches moved intake one-for-one by luck of substrate's composition and the tests batch moved it by zero. ⚑ THE PAIRING IS THE MECHANISM AND IT IS INVISIBLE TO A COUNT. A module and its suite are two files, each carrying no-header alone, so paying the module alone releases one and leaves a second sole-blocked file behind. by_blocker cannot show that and --names does, which is the enumerate-before-acting rule paying off on a population I had already acted on six times.}
