}

@misc{gate-G248,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G242},
  claim    = {module_importers answers identically for a module that does not exist and one with genuinely no importers — 0 sites in 0 of 1552 files — so a large denominator reads as thorough coverage while the reader has no arm for an operand it does not know.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum, found by sweeping for a tell paperkit named and summit-74 relayed. The witness: a fabricated module name returns the same numerator and the same 1552-file denominator a real absence gives, and a real module with one importer returns 1 of 1552, so nothing in the output separates absent-operand from absent-result. I acted on this last tick — reading 0 of 1551 for table_fit and concluding orphaned, which was correct but indistinguishable from a typo. Same class as gate-G242 in a different tool, where an absent bib read as an empty one until the reader was made to refuse. And my standing control discipline cannot reach it: a zero needs a positive control says nothing when the zero is real and the DENOMINATOR is the misleading half, so the discipline checks the wrong end of the ratio. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Swept for paperkit's extreme-value tell and found an instance in my own reader, of the INVERSE shape. module_importers on a module that does not exist returns 0 import sites in 0 of 1552 files — byte-identical to what a real module with no importers would give, with a LARGE denominator doing the reassuring. Their tell is two arms agreeing on an extreme value. Mine is one arm whose denominator is extreme and whose numerator is zero for two different reasons, and the denominator is what makes it read as thorough. ⚑ I ACTED ON THIS LAST TICK. I read 0 of 1551 for table_fit and concluded orphaned module — correct, but I could not have distinguished it from a typo'd module name, and the reader offers no arm that would. ⚑⚑ SAME CLASS AS gate-G242 IN A DIFFERENT TOOL: bibstruct --entries reported an absent bib as empty until it was made to refuse. A reader that cannot say I-DO-NOT-KNOW-THAT-OPERAND reports its own limit as a fact about the subject. ⚑⚑⚑ AND MY STANDING CONTROL DISCIPLINE CANNOT SEE IT — a zero needs a positive control says nothing when the zero is real and the DENOMINATOR is the misleading half. The discipline checks the wrong end of the ratio.}
}

@misc{gate-G249,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G245},
  claim    = {I fixed one instance of a crash without asking how many copies existed — a sibling file carried the identical body, and the outside-path rule it needed already had two homes written before either crash was reported.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum, second instance reported by summit-74 and reproduced here. The witness: module_importers.population crashes from a borrowing checkout exactly as attr_reads.population did one tick earlier, and pycodemod --calls population reads eighteen definitions of that name across substrate, so the one I repaired was one of eighteen and I checked none. name_collision._relative and case_count._label both already implement the fallback, and the former's docstring states the remedy exactly — a reader handed an outside path wants a row not a crash — consulted by neither repair. Importing it was refused at SLF001, correctly, since reaching a peer module's private is coupling rather than reuse, so the honest repair is one owner for the rule and that is wider than either fix. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): I repaired one instance of a defect and never asked how many copies existed — summit-74 found the second in a sibling file. module_importers.population carries the identical relative_to body I fixed in attr_reads.population one tick earlier, and crashes the same way from a borrowing checkout. pycodemod --calls population reads EIGHTEEN definitions of that function name across substrate, so the population I acted on was one of eighteen and I checked none of them. ⚑ AND THE RULE ALREADY HAD TWO HOMES BEFORE EITHER REPAIR. name_collision._relative and case_count._label both implement the outside-path fallback, and the former's docstring states the remedy exactly — a reader handed an outside path wants a row not a crash — written before either crash was reported and consulted by neither fix. My attr_reads._label was a third spelling and this is a fourth. ⚑⚑ IMPORTING THE EXISTING ONE WAS REFUSED CORRECTLY at SLF001: reaching a peer module's private is coupling rather than reuse, so the honest repair is ONE OWNER for the rule and that is wider than either fix. Recorded rather than approximated by a fifth copy.}
}

@misc{gate-G250,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G249},
  claim    = {The borrowed-root crash class spans EIGHT function names and thirteen live sites, not the one name the first two reports carried — a class keyed on the name a defect was first seen under is a fact about the first report.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. The witness: borrowed_root_census reads 13 LIVE and 48 latent root-measuring sites over 1554 files scanned, and only two of the thirteen live ones are called population — the rest are log_index and tree_of and py_files and census and scoped and type_errors and md_files and relative and shadow_of. Confirmed by a third crash in file_mode.py, a file neither I nor the peer had opened, raising the identical ValueError from a borrowing checkout. The live-versus-latent split is load-bearing: a live site defaults its root so a caller in another tree reaches it with a foreign one, while a latent site has the identical body and is protected only by callers passing a root explicitly, so one number would overstate today's breakage or hide the copies one edit away from it. The type gate refused two cuts of the CLI for the same leak, an __import__ shortcut and an unannotated sort key, each admitting Any at the top of a pipeline. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Enumerated the borrowed-root crash class and the population is EIGHT function names, not one. I had been reasoning about eighteen copies of population because that is the name the two reported crashes carried. borrowed_root_census reads 13 LIVE and 48 latent root-measuring sites over 1554 files scanned, and only two of the thirteen live ones are called population — the others are log_index, tree_of, py_files, census, scoped, type_errors, md_files, relative and shadow_of. A class keyed on the name a defect was FIRST SEEN under is a fact about the first report. ⚑ CONFIRMED BY A THIRD CRASH IN A FILE NEITHER I NOR THE PEER HAD OPENED: file_mode.py raises the identical ValueError from a borrowing checkout, so the census finds real breakage rather than lookalikes. ⚑⚑ THE SPLIT IS LIVE VERSUS LATENT AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING. A live site DEFAULTS its root so a caller in another tree reaches it with a foreign one, a latent site has the identical body and is protected only by every caller passing a root explicitly — drain_queue.population is latent and does not crash. Reporting one number would overstate today's breakage or hide the copies one edit away from it. ⚑⚑⚑ THE TYPE GATE REFUSED TWO CUTS OF THE CLI, both for the same leak: an __import__ shortcut and then an unannotated sort key, each admitting Any at the top of a pipeline. A census whose own types are unchecked is a poor instrument for a defect that lives in a composition.}
}

@misc{gate-G251,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G250},
  claim    = {The borrowed-root census carries at least one false positive — corpus.py_files measures against the resolved root of its own walk and cannot receive a foreign path — so 13 LIVE is a floor with an unknown error rate rather than a roster to repair against.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum, found by opening a listed row rather than trusting the count. The witness: py_files computes relative_to against base, which resolve_root produced for the very walk yielding the path, so the two roots are the same by construction. The census matches a SHAPE — a relative_to call plus a defaulted root parameter — where the defect is a COMPOSITION of a root from one source with paths from another, and a proxy admits rows the real predicate would not. Three sites are confirmed by running them, attr_reads and module_importers reported by summit-74 and file_mode found by the census, and the remaining ten are candidates. The suite landed first at 16 of 16 with a sensitivity arm and did not catch this, because every fixture states the composition the census assumes, so it verifies the classifier against its own premise and cannot test whether the premise selects the right population. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The borrowed-root census has at least one FALSE POSITIVE and I found it by opening a row rather than trusting the count. corpus.py_files is listed LIVE, and it cannot crash: its relative_to measures against base, the resolved root of the very walk that produced the path, so it never receives a foreign one. The census matches the SHAPE — a relative_to plus a defaulted root parameter — where the defect is a COMPOSITION, a root from one source and paths from another. Shape is a proxy for composition and a proxy admits rows the real predicate would not. ⚑ SO 13 LIVE IS A FLOOR WITH AN UNKNOWN FALSE-POSITIVE RATE, not a verified roster, and I am not repairing thirteen sites on a number whose first sampled row was wrong. Three are confirmed by running them — attr_reads and module_importers reported by summit-74, file_mode found by the census — and the other ten are candidates. ⚑⚑ THE SUITE LANDED FIRST AND DID NOT CATCH THIS, which is the honest limit: 16 of 16 including a sensitivity arm proving the default decides live-versus-latent. Every fixture states the composition the census assumes, so the suite verifies the classifier against its own premise and cannot test whether the premise selects the right population. A passing suite says the check works, never that the check asks the right question.}
}

@misc{gate-G252-borrowed-root-census-half-false-positive,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A shape-proxy census of borrowed-root crash sites reported 13 LIVE and execution from a borrowing checkout confirmed 6 and refuted 5 — the proxy is a floor with a measured false-positive rate near one half, so repairing on its count would edit files that are not broken.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate. The witness: env -C ~/github/summit python3 substrate/<mod>.py run once per candidate. CRASH file_header:157 drain_leverage:127 module_size:141 pid_ownership:356 attr_reads module_importers. NO CRASH mypy_census prose_numbers agda_log_fresh collision_scope walk_scope. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The borrowed_root census reported 13 LIVE sites; running each from a borrowing checkout confirmed 4 new crashes plus 2 already repaired and REFUTED 5 outright. The shape proxy defaults-a-root AND calls relative-to is necessary and not sufficient, because a site can default its root and never measure a walked path against it. No predicate distinguishes the two without executing the module, which is why this is unwitnessed rather than a mode check.}
}

@misc{gate-G253-root-is-an-input-not-a-derivation,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G252-borrowed-root-census-half-false-positive},
  claim    = {A repo root derived from a source file's own location is an ownership claim the runtime never made, and every path defect in this arc descends from it — so the root becomes a required --root input at the CLI boundary and the package's own data is asked of importlib.resources instead.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.tree_root_selftest},
  note     = {kind=selftest by=substrate. The witness: python3 -m substrate.tree_root_selftest 23 of 23, including a differential that labels 400 live corpus paths identically to corpus.ROOT and asserts the derived and given roots agree. Operator ruling: required --root no fallback, and build the replacement whole then swap. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): corpus.ROOT = Path(__file__).absolute().parent.parent asserts the repo root is two directories above one source file — a filesystem-layout claim the Python runtime never made, true only while substrate/ sits directly under the root and the package is reached as source. It is read at 121 sites in 66 files and resolve_root's two-tree CWD-then-tool search exists only to patch it. tree_root splits the collapsed question: package_files asks importlib.resources for substrate's own data, from_argv takes the caller's tree as a required --root and REFUSES when absent. The relative_to crash class dissolves because the root that labels is the root that was walked.}
}

@misc{gate-G254-budget-second-positional-is-bytes-not-rows,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A census mode that reports a value and exits 0 at every value cannot be witnessed by an exit code, so a guessed modifier binds as its operand and the tool answers a question nobody asked while looking correct.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=substrate. The witness: mdstruct --budget FILE --top 12 printed all 144 sections and an OVER by 239258 bytes footer, because --budget takes an optional BYTE BUDGET as its second positional. The polarity probe refused a refuses: filing and named this the gate-F38 census case. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mdstruct --budget takes an optional BYTE BUDGET as its second positional, not a row cap. Passing --top 12 to bound the listing bound 12 as the budget instead, printing all 144 sections plus an OVER by 239258 bytes footer — a plausible output answering a question nobody asked. No exit code can witness this: --budget is a census that reports a VALUE and exits 0 at any budget, which the polarity probe named as the gate-F38 case when it refused a refuses: filing. The discriminating quantity is whether the second positional was consumed as a budget, which no registered kind reads.}
}

@misc{gate-G255-four-borrowed-root-crashes-repaired-and-a-fifth-spelling-found,
