  claim    = {Drafting a suite against a drained reader tests the origin's prose as well as its code, so a docstring claim nobody could run is falsified at the moment it becomes a case.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.md_families_selftest},
  note     = {kind=selftest by=substrate. The witness: md_families_selftest 24 of 24 after two first-run failures. The origin claims T13 and R7b share a shape and the measurement is A9 versus A9a since R7b carries a trailing lowercase run. Separately the strip set omitted brackets while the position pattern admits them, so a bracketed label clustered as its own family — a real defect the origin shares, now fixed in the replacement. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): substrate/md_families.py replaces mdstruct.label_families taking TEXTS — 24 of 24. Two arms failed on the first run and they were DIFFERENT KINDS. The origin's docstring states that T13 and R7b share a shape and they do NOT: measured A9 versus A9a, because R7b carries a trailing lowercase run that collapsing preserves. My case asserted the docstring and failed, so writing the suite falsified a claim the prose had carried unchallenged — and the property collapsing actually buys is arity-independence WITHIN a run, which the corrected arms now state. The second failure was a REAL DEFECT the origin shares: the position pattern admits a bracketed token and the strip set omitted brackets, so [T13] became the token [T13 and clustered into a family of its own — a reader bracketing its labels would see every one separated from the same labels written bare. Both were invisible while the reader took paths and could only be run against a corpus.}
}

@misc{gate-G280-two-drift-measures-disagree-and-the-drain-nearly-collapsed-them,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G279-a-drained-suite-tested-a-docstring-claim-and-found-it-false},
  claim    = {Two functions answering what looks like one question can measure differently enough that reuse destroys information, so the reuse-search must end in reading both bindings rather than in matching their names.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.md_roundtrip_selftest},
  note     = {kind=selftest by=substrate. The witness: md_roundtrip_selftest 19 of 19 with an arm asserting the two measures disagree on an insertion. md_fixpoint.changed_lines zips positionally so an insert shifts every following line, while difflib aligns so an insert costs one row — right for convergence and right for a human report respectively. A pure rewrite makes them agree, so only the insertion arm separates them. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): substrate/md_roundtrip.py replaces mdstruct.roundtrip and _diff together, taking TEXTS — 19 of 19. The reuse-search nearly cost a real distinction. md_fixpoint.changed_lines already counts line deltas and reusing it looked obvious, but reading both bindings showed they measure differently: changed_lines zips POSITIONALLY so an inserted line makes every following line count as changed, while difflib ALIGNS so an insertion costs one row. Each is right for its question — positional for a CONVERGENCE sequence where the shape is stable between passes, aligned for a REPORT a human reads — and collapsing them would have reported a single insertion as hundreds of changes with no way to tell an insertion from a rewrite. Both are carried and named, and an arm asserts they DISAGREE on an insertion, which is the only case that separates them since a pure rewrite makes them agree. Reuse is not always the right move and reading both bindings is how the difference surfaced.}
}

@misc{gate-G281-the-audit-that-refuses-to-be-a-generator,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G280-two-drift-measures-disagree-and-the-drain-nearly-collapsed-them},
  claim    = {A tool that generated a lint config from its own findings would encode a document's defects as permanent policy, so the honest form reports what an exemption COSTS and leaves the decision to a person.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.md_lint_audit_selftest},
  note     = {kind=selftest by=substrate. The witness: md_lint_audit_selftest 20 of 20 with the paydown boundary asserted from both sides and a threshold sensitivity arm. The measured incident is a first config disabling four rules of which one exemption was real, two firing zero times and one wanting a width the documents already had. Five verdicts because on-or-off is what let the decoration survive, and live rows for unmentioned firing rules are what stop a clean bill over a corpus with debt. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): substrate/md_lint_audit.py replaces mdstruct.lint_config_audit taking the CONFIG and the COUNTS rather than a path — 20 of 20. The origin records the measurement it exists for: this project's first markdownlint config disabled FOUR rules and only ONE exemption was real, two firing zero times over the documents they govern and one wanting a width the documents already had. THE DESIGN CLAIM WORTH KEEPING IS THAT IT REFUSES TO GENERATE. A tool writing the config from the findings would emit a disable for two missing blank lines, encoding a document's defects as permanent policy — so paydown is a verdict rather than an action, and for a small population the fix is the document. Five verdicts because on-or-off is what let the decoration survive, and the live rows for firing rules the config never mentions are what stop the audit reading as a clean bill for a corpus with real debt. The paydown boundary is asserted from both sides plus a threshold sensitivity arm, since a one-sided comparison drifts toward justifying exemptions.}
}

@misc{gate-G282-the-last-reader-was-blocked-by-an-unpackaged-dependency-not-by-itself,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G281-the-audit-that-refuses-to-be-a-generator},
  rests-on = {gate-G278-half-drained-reads-as-drained-when-the-roster-counts-modules},
  claim    = {Draining every reader of a module does not make it swappable when its dependencies resolve only through a path insert, so the count of drained readers can reach the end while the blocker was never a reader at all.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.file_read_selftest},
  note     = {kind=selftest by=substrate. The witness: swap_readiness scratch/mdstruct.py reports vfs climode edit_snapshot and ratchet UNPACKAGED and refuses the repoint on 12 blockers, while toolmodes --find presence shows nothing packaged provides the three-valued read. file_read_selftest 24 of 24 lifting the READ half only, with the broken arm driving a real directory since IsADirectoryError is an OSError and that is the collapse being undone. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): _text is the last mdstruct reader and it could not be drained as itself. It is already lifted onto scratch/vfs.py's three-valued Presence read, and swap_readiness scratch/mdstruct.py classifies vfs as UNPACKAGED resolving only through a sys.path insert into scratch — so a substrate module importing it would be unpackaged too, which is what the drain exists to end. toolmodes --find presence confirms nothing packaged provides that read. Operator ruled drain vfs first because it is the real blocker: swap_readiness lists vfs climode edit_snapshot and ratchet as unpackaged, so mdstruct cannot be repointed however many readers are drained. substrate/file_read.py lifts the READ half only — 24 of 24 — because a drain unit is a responsibility rather than a file, and vfs also owns a write seam with dry-run enforcement, a git revision source and a glob listing. The collapse it undoes: IsADirectoryError IS an OSError, so a caller guarding except OSError counts a directory-as-document as an absence, and the broken arm drives a REAL directory rather than a fabricated error.}
}

@misc{gate-G283-the-dry-run-was-ambient-state-and-mypy-deleted-a-guard-it-proved-dead,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G282-the-last-reader-was-blocked-by-an-unpackaged-dependency-not-by-itself},
  claim    = {A runtime guard the type system already enforces is not defence in depth but a line no case can reach, and a mode held in a module global is the same ambient-state defect as a root derived from a file's own location.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.file_write_selftest},
  note     = {kind=selftest by=substrate. The witness: file_write_selftest 18 of 18 with the atomicity arm driven by a real unwritable directory since the temp file is created beside the target. The origin honoured a module-level DRY_RUN so a write depended on state no signature mentioned, and mypy reported the origin's TypeError branch unreachable under a checked str-or-bytes signature. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): substrate/file_write.py lifts vfs's write seam — 18 of 18. TWO FINDINGS FROM THE LIFT. The origin honoured a module-level DRY_RUN global, so whether a write happened depended on ambient state no signature mentioned — the same shape as a root derived from a source file's own location, which this arc's operator ruling already rejected. The mode is now a parameter, so a reader of the call site can see which way it runs without knowing what another module set. And mypy PROVED THE TYPE GUARD UNREACHABLE: the origin raised TypeError for anything neither str nor bytes, and under a checked signature that branch is dead code, so keeping it would ship a line no case could reach. A runtime check the type system already enforces is not defence in depth, it is an untestable line. The atomicity arm drives a REAL unwritable directory rather than a doctored payload, because the temp file is created BESIDE the target and that is exactly the step atomicity is about.}
}

@misc{gate-G284-a-refusal-arm-needs-its-portable-twin-or-refusing-everything-passes,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G283-the-dry-run-was-ambient-state-and-mypy-deleted-a-guard-it-proved-dead},
  rests-on = {gate-G277-a-preservation-check-needs-the-false-positive-arm-more-than-the-damage-arm},
  claim    = {A suite of refusal arms alone is satisfied by a check that refuses everything, so each defect case must be paired with a twin differing only in the feature under test.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.glob_portable_selftest},
  note     = {kind=selftest by=substrate. The witness: glob_portable_selftest 19 of 19 with a portable twin beside each of the three matcher disagreements, and a listing arm where strict=False finds the shallow file and drops the buried one — the loss demonstrated rather than asserted. The Rev source is not lifted so the unit is the pure pattern check plus the disk listing. The recorded incident is a six-module drop where a smaller result read as a smaller corpus. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): substrate/glob_portable.py lifts vfs's pattern-portability refusal and the working-tree listing — 19 of 19. The Rev source is NOT lifted, so listdir's revision branch stays behind and the unit is the pure pattern check plus the disk listing, which is the half that is testable without a corpus. The recorded incident is a six-module drop: a pattern selected one depth on disk and every depth at a revision, and the smaller result read as a smaller corpus rather than as a bug. TWO THINGS THE SUITE HAD TO DO. Each of the three disagreements needs a REFUSAL arm and a PORTABLE TWIN, because a check that refused every pattern would satisfy all three defect cases and be worthless — the twins differ in exactly the feature under test, and the single-segment star is the subtle one since it has no directory context to cross into. And the lax listing arm makes the loss VISIBLE rather than asserted: with strict off it finds the shallow file and drops the buried one, which is the disagreement the refusal warns about, demonstrated.}
}

@misc{gate-G285-suite-arm-pinned-a-mutable-debt-state,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A suite arm that pins a NAMED file's per-file-gate verdict cannot hold a stable polarity, because the debt it asserts is mutable state someone else may pay down},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. Two swap_readiness_selftest arms asserted scripts/check_rederive_ratchet.py is DIRTY and states its debt. Both went red when that file was repaired independently of this change, which touched only the imports axis. Measured CLEAN and SWAP READY. Repaired by asserting the PROPERTY over whichever file carries debt today, with a SKIPPED arm when none does. Suite 28 of 30 to 30 of 30. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A swap_readiness_selftest arm asserted check_rederive_ratchet.py is DIRTY and inverted when that file was repaired; a case pinning a per-file gate verdict for a NAMED file cannot hold a stable polarity because the debt is mutable, so the arm must assert the PROPERTY (a dirty file reads DIRTY and states how much) over whichever file carries debt today}
}

@misc{gate-G286-a-result-that-drops-its-source,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A read Result that does not carry its source makes ABSENT ambiguous between absent-from-the-working-tree and absent-from-that-commit, and pairing the source beside the Result loses it whenever a function returns the Result onward},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. Operator ruled ONE Result vocabulary over a separate RevResult type or a caller-held pairing. Added source to substrate/file_read.Result defaulting to None meaning the working tree, so every existing construction site stayed valid and the suite held at 24 of 24 before new arms. Widened error from OSError to Exception because a bad revision raises ValueError and typing it narrowly would force a laundering. Suite 24 to 33 of 33. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A three-valued read Result without a source field makes ABSENT ambiguous between not-in-the-working-tree and not-in-that-commit, and pairing the source beside the Result instead of on it loses the pairing the moment any function RETURNS the Result, which is the ordinary case rather than the exotic one}
}

@misc{gate-G287-a-differential-keyed-on-exit-code-scored-untraced,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A differential keyed on returncode scores every arm the origin reports through a non-zero exit as UNTRACED, and because UNTRACED passes as an honest third outcome the skipped arm is indistinguishable from agreement in the suite total},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. Building substrate/rev_read the differential against scratch/vfs.py read 30 of 30 while its BROKEN arm never compared — vfs exits 2 for a bad revision and prints the verdict to stderr, so the returncode guard returned None. Repaired by reading the verdict word off both streams and by asserting the number of arms that actually compared against the case table, derived not typed. The origin had agreed throughout. Suite 30 to 31 of 31. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A differential that decides whether the origin ran by testing returncode != 0 scores every arm the origin reports through a NON-ZERO exit as UNTRACED, so the arm passes as a third outcome and is indistinguishable from agreement in the total — the exit-code-collapse class committed inside a check written to verify a reader whose subject is exit-code collapse}
}

@misc{gate-G288-three-consumers-on-retired-cassian-ports,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A client that degrades rather than fails on a dead endpoint makes a retired port silent, so substrate carried three sites from one cassian cutover with no crash and no log, and the per-file gate refuses every edit to two of the files holding them},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. Reported by cassian-observability and re-measured here rather than taken on report. Verified with substrate/cache_endpoint --candidates that 127.0.0.1:30637 answers RESP PONG while 6379 refuses, and with tsdbprobe --reachable --base that 30829 is live while 8428 refuses. The three sites are scratch/shared_cache.py ENDPOINT, scripts/membudget_otlp.py OTLP_ENDPOINT, and scratch/tsdbprobe.py DEFAULT_BASE — the third found here and absent from the peer patch, which also leaves QUERY_BASE on the retired port one line below the line it fixes. shared_cache carries 645 findings so the gate refuses the edit, which is why the repair is a new packaged module rather than an in-place change. Suite 21 of 21. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A cache client and a metrics client both DEGRADE rather than fail on a dead endpoint, so a retired port produces no crash and no log and is indistinguishable from a cold cache or an empty store, and substrate carries three such sites from one cassian cutover while the per-file gate refuses every edit to two of the files holding them}
}

@misc{gate-G289-a-site-at-a-time-discovery-has-no-termination,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Finding retired-endpoint sites one incident at a time has no termination condition, so the population must be derived from the owner's migration declaration rather than accumulated from the accidents that surfaced each site},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. Three sites arrived by three unrelated accidents — a peer noticing one, grepping that one's consumers, and my own positive control failing on a port I was using AS the control. The fourth, scratch/traceprobe.py DEFAULT_BASE at 10428, was found only by sweeping against cassian's own store-migration registry. Re-measured here rather than taken on report — traceprobe --reachable --base 30428 reports REACHABLE with 3 services indexed and 10428 reports errno 111. The new port answering is a control drawn from outside the store under test, which is what my earlier 8428 control was not. Prose sites are reported by the peer as 18 including a guide in scratch/inbox and remain UNCOUNTED here because mdstruct --spans matches headings and these live in code blocks. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Three retired-endpoint sites were found by three unrelated accidents — a peer noticing one, grepping that one's consumers, and my own positive control failing — which is a discovery process with no termination condition, and the fourth was found only by sweeping against the migration registry that DECLARES what was retired, so the population must be derived from the owner's declaration rather than accumulated from incidents}
}

@misc{gate-G290-the-derived-census-found-a-fifth-site,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Deriving the retired-endpoint population from the owner's migration declaration surfaced a fifth code site on its first run, and that site is in the packaged tree rather than behind the per-file gate wall, so incident-driven search had missed the one site that was actually repairable},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. Operator ruled build the population reader before repairing any site. substrate/endpoint_census --retired over scratch scripts substrate jea catalog reports 5 python constants and 10 prose lines, the two never summed because an AST reading and a code-fence match are different instruments. The fifth is substrate/ratchet_log.py line 41 on retired vlogs 9428. ⚑ AND THE CENSUS CANNOT SEE THE SITE THAT STARTED THE ARC — shared_cache spells its endpoint as a tuple and this reader matches a port inside a string, so the bound is asserted by a suite arm rather than left to be rediscovered by whoever next trusts a zero. Suite 16 of 16. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Deriving the retired-endpoint population from the migration declaration immediately surfaced a fifth code site nobody had reported — substrate/ratchet_log.py naming retired vlogs 9428 — and it is in the packaged tree rather than behind the per-file gate wall, so the incident-driven search had missed the one site that was actually repairable}
}

@misc{gate-G291-a-promql-probe-calls-a-logs-engine-unreachable,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A PromQL probe reports a VictoriaLogs endpoint UNREACHABLE on an HTTP 400 unsupported-path, which is a live server refusing a query it does not speak, so the verdict is a fact about the instrument and reading it as a dead endpoint blocks a correct repoint},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. Operator ruled build the ingest probe before repointing. Built substrate/logs_probe with read-your-write because ratchet_log's own docstring records that VictoriaLogs answers HTTP 200 while ingesting zero rows — a transport 200 verifies nothing. Measured 30928 accepts the write and 9428 refuses the connection, which are genuinely different outcomes where tsdbprobe called both unreachable. ⚑ THE READ-BACK IS UNCONFIRMED and that is reported as the third outcome rather than as failure — it does not bear on the repoint because ratchet_log only writes. Repointed the default, suite 14 of 14, and the packaged tree now censuses 0 of 706. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): tsdbprobe reports UNREACHABLE for a VictoriaLogs endpoint on an HTTP 400 unsupported-path, which is a live server refusing a query it does not speak, so the verdict is a fact about the instrument rather than the store and reading it as a dead endpoint would have blocked a correct repoint}
}

@misc{gate-G292-a-wallclock-deadline-cannot-separate-slow-from-absent,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A deadline that expires with nothing returned cannot distinguish an absent row from a query too expensive to complete, so the discriminator must be whether any read ANSWERED — a boolean read-back erases exactly that},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. My logs_probe reported UNCONFIRMED against 30928 and I predicted my own LogsQL spelling was at fault. cassian measured the spelling correct and found the real cause by tripping over it harder — their unbounded probe HUNG past 120s where mine timed out at 20s, and a hang is evidence a timeout destroys. An unbounded LogsQL filter scans the whole retention. Adding a _time:5m prefix moved the same probe from UNCONFIRMED to CONFIRMED against the same endpoint. Split Ingest into four outcomes and read_back into an answered/found pair, because SLOW and UNCONFIRMED are different OBSERVATIONS rather than different confidences. ⚑ AND A SUITE ARM CAUGHT THE PROBE SENDING tool%2Creason where the emitter sends tool,reason — a probe that does not send what the emitter sends verifies a path nothing else takes. Suite 18 of 18. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A read-your-write probe whose deadline expires with nothing returned cannot distinguish the row being absent from the query being too expensive to complete, so an unbounded LogsQL filter scanning the whole retention produced UNCONFIRMED against an endpoint that ingests perfectly — the discriminator is whether any read ANSWERED, which a boolean read-back erases}
}

@misc{gate-G293-one-retired-port-can-have-two-live-successors,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A migration registry whose domain is the WRITE path declares one successor per retired address, so a site holding a READ base cannot follow the declared row — a census can locate sites but the site's own direction chooses its successor},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. Operator ruled read the owner's registry with a local fallback and state which answered. Doing so surfaced a disagreement that looks like an error and is not — the registry says vm 8428 to 30828 while my transcribed table said 30829. Measured both rather than picking: tsdbprobe --reachable --base 30828 returns HTTP 400 unsupported path for api-v1-query while 30829 answers, so 30828 is ingest and 30829 is query. Two live services, one retired port. ⚑ AND THE TRANSCRIBED TABLE COULD NOT SEE ITSELF — its ports are integer literals in tuples and the reader matches ports in strings, so --retired over its own file reported ZERO. The instrument was blind to exactly the defect it hunts. Declaration now carries a provenance field and the report leads with it. Suite 16 to 21 of 21. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A migration registry declares one replacement per retired address because its domain is the WRITE path, so a site holding a READ base cannot follow the declared row — measured on vm where 30828 is the ingest surface answering HTTP 400 for api-v1-query and 30829 serves the query API, both live and neither wrong, which means a census can locate sites but cannot choose their successor}
}

@misc{gate-G294-a-derived-reader-that-ignores-its-declaration-schema,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Reading a declaration does not make a result derived unless the reader honours that declaration's schema, and a reader that silently drops rows is worse than the transcription it replaced because a derived reading is questioned less},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. cassian declared a second successor for 8428 — vmagent 30828 for writes and vm-read 30829 for queries, different Deployments — and my reader built a dict keyed on the retired port, so the later row overwrote the earlier. Every 8428 site then showed ONE successor with no sign a second existed. The registry's own header says old_port is NOT A KEY and I had read that file. ⚑ THE LIVE INSTANCE IS TWO ADJACENT LINES WANTING OPPOSITE PORTS — membudget_otlp 47 is an OTLP writer and 48 is a query base, so a single-successor census would have recommended a wrong repoint for exactly one of them, onto a live server that refuses the request rather than a dead port that fails at connect. Site now carries all successors and states that direction decides. Suite 21 to 25 of 25. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Reading a peer's registry does not make a result derived unless the reader honours that registry's schema — mine keyed a dict on the retired port while the registry's own header says old_port is NOT A KEY, so a second declared successor silently overwrote the first and every affected site showed one option with no sign a second existed, which is worse than the transcription it replaced because a derived reading is questioned less}
}

@misc{gate-G295-a-premise-encoded-as-a-data-structure-cannot-announce-its-violation,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A load-bearing premise expressed as a dict comprehension rather than an assertion fails silently when falsified, because a dict keyed on a no-longer-unique key does not complain — it keeps one row},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. cassian hit my gate-G294 defect in a generator that emits LIVE systemd drop-ins — nine writing collectors re-pointed to a read-only surface, three failed, and the wrong value was generated, hook-committed and symlinked into the live tree, so it arrived wearing every mark of a derivation. ⚑ THEIR SECOND DEFECT WAS THE DEEPER ONE AND I HAD A VARIANT — their reader sliced three columns and dropped kind, the only column distinguishing two rows sharing a retired port. Mine READ kind but folded it into a display string, so it was legible to a human and invisible to a program and a direction-aware consumer would have had to re-parse prose. Made kind a typed field, added successors_for which returns EMPTY for an undeclared direction rather than falling back, and put the kind in the report line after a first cut dropped it there too. Suite 25 to 30 of 30. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A load-bearing premise expressed as a dict comprehension rather than an assertion fails silently when falsified — a dict keyed on a no-longer-unique key does not complain, it keeps one row — and a reader that drops the disambiguating column cannot detect the ambiguity it hands downstream, which is the same defect one layer up}
}

@misc{gate-G296-the-sensitivity-arm-belongs-where-the-defect-was-reachable,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A sensitivity arm aimed at a layer where the defect was never reachable proves nothing about the code that failed, so the forced state must sit where both incidents actually occurred},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. Operator ruled add a suite arm forcing a repeated old_port, scoped to this repo's consumer. Building it, the obvious placement was the PARSE — but read_registry appends to a list and cannot collapse a repeat, while both outages came from an INDEX built over rows that parsed correctly. So the constructed defect is the dict comprehension itself and the arms assert it loses a row and keeps read where a writer needs store. ⚑ AND THE PEER CORRECTED A COUNT I WOULD HAVE CARRIED — they said two consumers and four unaudited readers, measured the four, and found none keys on old_port. One consumer each. Naming a suspect population is not measuring it. Suite 30 to 36 of 36. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A sensitivity arm aimed at the wrong layer tests a state nobody was ever in — the registry PARSE appends to a list and cannot collapse, while both repos' outages came from an INDEX built over correctly-parsed rows, so forcing the defect at the parse would have proved nothing about the code that actually failed}
}

@misc{gate-G297-anchor-matching-compares-rendered-against-raw,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Matching a rendered heading against a raw source line fails for any codepoint pandoc rewrites, because stripping markup cannot un-rewrite a character — and the heading does not merely go missing, every section after it collapses into its predecessor's span},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. Reported by linux-sources-94 and reproduced here against their probe rather than taken on report. mdstruct --spans on a heading containing an apostrophe returns 0 sections while --budget lists that same heading, and a plain heading in the same file resolves — the control is what makes the zero readable. An inline-markup heading also resolves correctly bounded, which is the peer's distinction: anchor_key strips delimiters and nothing reaches a rewritten codepoint. ⚑ THE COLLAPSE IS VISIBLE IN THE CONTROL — the plain section reports lines 3 to 10 while the next heading sits at line 7, so it has swallowed the unfindable one. ⚑⚑ AND md_spans' OWN DOCSTRING NAMES THIS CLASS, recording the same defect fixed for inline markup and stopping one step short of the codepoint case. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): md_spans._anchors compares anchor_key of pandoc's RENDERED heading against anchor_key of the RAW source line, and anchor_key strips markup but cannot un-rewrite a codepoint, so pandoc's smart extension turning an apostrophe into a typographic one makes the heading unfindable and every section after it collapses into its predecessor's span}
}

@misc{gate-G298-the-replacement-cli-is-not-argv-compatible,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The replacement reader spells its modes as subcommands where the origin spells them as flags, so a consumer cannot migrate by repointing a path — every call site and every skill file changes shape},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=unwitnessed by=momentum. Operator directed substrate to purge its own mdstruct and use mtools'. Declared mikemol-mdstruct as a PEP 508 git reference with subdirectory=mdstruct, reading the URL from git remote and the branch from git branch rather than guessing. ⚑ THE FIRST RESOLVE FAILED ON A DECLARATION CONFLICT, NOT A RUNTIME ONE — mikemol-mdstruct requires Python 3.13 and substrate declared 3.12 while the active interpreter was already 3.13, so the old floor asserted support for a configuration nothing in this tree ever ran. Operator ruled raise substrate. ⚑⚑ THE DIFFERENTIAL IS DECISIVE — on the peer's probe mtools returns 6 sections with the apostrophe heading at 7 to 10 and the section before it correctly bounded at 3 to 6, where substrate returns 0 sections for that heading and lets its predecessor span 3 to 10. ⚑ AND THE INVOCATION WAS GUESSED TWICE before reading the manifest: it is a console script, not a -m target, and its modes are subcommands. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): mikemol-mdstruct spells its modes as SUBCOMMANDS where scratch/mdstruct.py spells them as FLAGS, so the roughly ten trees whose struct-tools skill names substrate's path cannot migrate by repointing a path — every call site and every skill file changes shape, which makes the purge a consumer migration rather than a dependency swap}
}

@misc{gate-G299-the-fix-was-already-written-and-wired-to-nothing,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The repair for a live defect existed tested in the same tree as the defect and was reachable by nothing, because a built-then-orphaned module is indistinguishable from an absent one to every consumer},
