  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — intake_manifest --sole put not-promotable at 735 SOLE after the header sweep released it from 0, and promote_frontier reports 799 promotable with 731 DIRTY and ZERO blocked. Nothing waits on a callee, so each member is dirty in itself and the population spans scratch/eliza el-atlas/tools and jea/evolution. Named and not started this tick. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the intake manifest's not-promotable blocker is the per-file gate's own backlog — promote_frontier reports 731 DIRTY and ZERO blocked, so no module waits on a callee and the 735 sole-blocked is a paydown arc rather than one repair}
}

@misc{gate-G318-the-private-tail-read-as-no-suite,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A coverage census keyed on one spelling of a definition name reports modules with passing cases as having no suite at all, and the error direction manufactures work by proposing suites that already exist},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — TAIL_SUITE matched only def selftest so nine modules defining _selftest at top level fell into DISPATCH reaching nothing. iface_cost answers 5/5 and iface_readers 8/8 when run. Widening to _?selftest moved the report from 10 dispatch-empty and 1 split-owed to 1 and 10. The 44-case suite passed identically before and after, so a new arm was added and verified RED by reverting the pattern. A THIRD spelling survives — type_scope names its arms _cases and has 14 passing — recorded rather than admitted by widening again. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): suite_gap keyed its tail predicate on def selftest so the private _selftest spelling could not match and nine modules with passing arms read as owed a NEW SUITE — the fix moved them to owed a SPLIT and no arm in the 44-case suite distinguished either state}
}

@misc{gate-G319-key-the-census-on-the-shape-not-the-name,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {When a census keyed on a name needs widening twice it is measuring the wrong thing — the repair is to key on the structural fact the name was standing in for, which no further spelling can escape},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — suite_gap matched def selftest then def _?selftest and type_scope still read as owed a NEW SUITE because it names its arms _cases and answers --selftest with 14 passing. Reading the AST for a --selftest guard that calls a top-level def in the same file moved it to owed-a-SPLIT and left the dispatch-reaching-nothing category EMPTY. The arm was written first and seen RED. The delegating population stayed at 5 and an arm asserts a dispatch reaching nothing still reads false, so the empty category is reachable rather than broken. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): two regex widenings could not close the tail census because the fact is structural — a --selftest guard calling a top-level def in the same file — and reading that from the AST emptied the dispatch-reaching-nothing category that two name spellings had populated falsely}
}

@misc{gate-G320-the-trim-writes-one-side,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A census row naming a tool as the remedy is read as an instruction, so a tool that performs half the named operation destroys what it was invoked to relocate},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — suite_gap prints owed a SPLIT (module_trim) and module_trim --apply wrote the trimmed substrate/iface_bottom.py while creating no sibling suite. The module answered --selftest with 38/38 before and exited silently after. Trim.removed carries the extracted text and no write path places it, so the caller is meant to. Recovered from the index because the file was staged and not yet at HEAD. Three CLEAN four UNSAFE and three FINDINGS across the eleven, so the population is not uniform either. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): module_trim --apply writes the trimmed module and never the extracted text, so following the census row owed a SPLIT module_trim deletes the cases — iface_bottom went from 38/38 to a silent exit and was recovered only because the pre-trim version sat in the index}
}

@misc{gate-G321-import-star-drops-the-private-helpers,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Stating a bound in a writer's docstring does not honour it — a split that re-homes cases behind a star import produces a suite that imports cleanly and fails at its first arm on every private helper that stayed behind},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — a sibling writer was built whose own docstring named the unexportable-private-name hazard, then splitting substrate/iface_bottom wrote both halves and the suite raised NameError name _named is not defined. Origin restored from the index at 38/38 and the broken sibling deleted. The cluster trim is CLEAN for all six case defs so cohesion is not the obstacle — the residue is that a qualified back-import plus a rebinding per private name is a transform the writer does not do. Left unbuilt rather than shipped as a template that emits broken suites. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): a split sibling re-importing its origin with import star produces a suite that imports cleanly and dies at the first arm — iface_bottom's cases reach _named which leads with an underscore so the star cannot carry it, and the bound was written in the writer's own docstring before the run that hit it}
}

@misc{gate-G322-what-appears-is-not-what-must-be-imported,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A reader answering what names appear cannot answer what names must be imported, and substituting one for the other emits keywords and locals into an import list — an unparseable file that no downstream checker gets to see},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — module_trim's sibling writer built its back-import from names_used alone and emitted False None True bool int len __doc__ and the cases own locals check got fn, giving SyntaxError at the import list. The docstring had claimed the staged checker would filter non-importable names, which is false for a syntax error. Repaired by intersecting with the origin's top-level bindings read through a second reader that covers assignments as well as defs, since the tables iface_bottom needs are plain assignments. Result — both halves CLEAN, sibling runs 38/38 matching the origin's pre-split count, and suite_gap moved 331 to 332 with splits owed 11 to 10. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): a back-import built from every bare Name the cases read emitted False None bool len and the cases own locals into an import list — an unparseable file caught before any checker ran, so the docstring claim that the checker would filter them was false — and the repair is to intersect with the origin's top-level bindings which admits no builtin or local by construction}
}

@misc{gate-G323-the-sorter-owns-the-import-order,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A codemod that orders its own emitted imports reimplements the sorter that runs in the very next step, and passes by accident until a name set exposes the collation difference},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — module_trim's sibling writer used plain sorted() and ruff refused stub_declines_selftest with I001 because isort collates _BLANK_STOP before PARSED while lexicographic order reverses them. iface_bottom and iface_infer had passed, so the template was right by accident twice. Repaired by handing the written sibling to record_migrate._autofix, whose own docstring names this class — an isort reimplementation growing inside a codemod while the real sorter ran in the next step. Both halves CLEAN afterwards and the suite runs 21/21. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the sibling writer emitted its back-import in plain sorted order and ruff refused with I001 because its collation puts _BLANK_STOP before PARSED — two earlier splits passed only because their name sets never exposed the difference, and record_migrate._autofix's docstring already named this exact bug class}
}

@misc{gate-G324-the-branch-is-the-unit-not-the-block,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A refusal keyed on a whole block hides that its branches have opposite fates, and the same content in two locations then reads as two different defects},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — module_trim reported UNSAFE for iface_readers whose __main__ guard holds a --selftest branch beside a surviving main call, and FINDINGS for sandbox_connect whose identical branch sits inside main. Both say the command line outlives the cases. Asking per branch rather than per block moved iface_readers from UNSAFE to CLEAN with its cases running 8/8 and its CLI still answering --eligible. A first cut recursed on orelse and matched nothing because the tail branch is a nested statement in the guard body with a sibling after it, not an elif — read from the tree after the probe failed. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): treating a dispatch guard as atomic refused six splits because one branch is tail and its sibling survives — and the same shape appears in main's body, so the tool gave UNSAFE for one location and FINDINGS for the other while the branch content was the only fact}
}

@misc{gate-G325-a-reexported-import-is-not-a-binding,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A name a module imported is bound there at runtime and is not part of its interface, so a generated back-import that reaches it through that module is refused by the checker even though the code runs},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — module_trim's back-import named iface_infer and sys through substrate.iface_readers. The split suite RAN at 8/8 and the gate refused it with mypy saying the module does not explicitly export those attributes, plus F811 for sys being imported twice. Two binding kinds were collapsed — a def or assignment belongs to the origin and an imported name belongs to ITS source — so the writer must route each to its own home. Left unbuilt this tick rather than guessed at a fourth time. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the back-import routed an imported name through the origin and mypy refused it — module does not explicitly export attribute iface_infer — so a name the cases reach must be imported from its own source rather than from the module that also imported it}
}

@misc{gate-G326-route-each-name-to-its-own-home,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A generated import list must route each name to the module that owns it, because a name the source merely imported is bound there at runtime and is not part of its interface},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — module_trim now emits reached_names from the origin and re-emits the origin's OWN import line for anything it only imported, rather than routing everything through the origin. Also stopped hardcoding import sys because most origins import it too and both together gave F811. Result — iface_readers iface_construct stub_top_type and iface_dataflow all split with both halves CLEAN and suites at 8/8 21/21 14/14 and 31/31. suite_gap moved 334 to 338 with splits owed 8 to 4. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): a generated back-import must split two populations — names the origin DEFINES come from the origin and names it merely IMPORTED come from their own source re-emitted verbatim — and getting that right unblocked four splits that the single-set reading had refused}
}

@misc{gate-G327-conserved-is-not-valid,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A conservation guard establishes that no line was lost or invented and says nothing about whether the result parses, so a generated half can satisfy it and still be unloadable},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — module_trim lifted a --selftest branch out of main for sandbox_connect and iface_cost, carrying return _selftest() to module level where CPython refuses it. Both halves were written and both suites died with return outside function. The guard was satisfied because those lines landed in the extracted text. Repaired by compiling the generated sibling before either half lands. A first cut of THAT check used cst.parse_module and ACCEPTED the broken file — libcst is more permissive than the runtime — so it uses the builtin compile instead. The check runs in dry-run as well, since a preview reporting CLEAN for a split the apply refuses is a preview of a different operation. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the conservation guard passed and two broken suites still shipped because a branch lifted out of main carries return _selftest which is a SyntaxError at module level — the lines WERE conserved into the extracted text and conserved is not the same as valid, and the sibling was written without ever being parsed}
}

@misc{gate-G328-the-template-assumed-one-spelling,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A generated file that hardcodes a name its caller already supplies works everywhere the two spellings agree and fails silently at the gate everywhere they do not},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — module_trim's sibling template emitted sys.exit(_selftest()) as a constant. For wg_row whose entry is spelled selftest the cases still RAN at 5/5 because python resolved the module-level name at import, and the gate refused the file with F821 Undefined name _selftest. The name was in trim.names throughout. Repaired by deriving the dispatch name from what was trimmed, and the split then landed CLEAN on both halves. Four earlier splits passed only because their origins happened to use the underscore. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the emitted dispatch hardcoded _selftest so wg_row's sibling ran 5/5 while the gate refused it with F821 — the origin spells its entry selftest without the underscore and the name was already in trim.names for the template to read}
}

@misc{gate-G329-a-reachable-sys-exit-steals-the-exit-decision,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A codemod may not convert a returning branch into sys.exit, because SystemExit on an importable path takes the exit decision away from whatever imported the module},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Operator ruling when offered that transform as the mechanical way to re-home a --selftest branch out of main — we do not want sys.exit, it breaks anything that imports the code and expects to own exit conditions. The distinction is REACHABILITY not the call — sys.exit inside if __name__ == __main__ never runs on import and is the house pattern, as suite_run does. So iface_cost and sandbox_connect stay refused rather than repaired, and the refusal is correct rather than a gap. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the operator refused rewriting a returning branch to sys.exit because raising SystemExit on an importable path takes the exit decision from the importer — sys.exit is safe ONLY inside a __main__ guard which never runs on import, so the two blocked splits stay refused rather than mechanically repaired}
}

@misc{gate-G330-the-template-appended-a-guard-the-tail-already-carried,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A generator that appends a fixed epilogue duplicates whatever the extracted content already ended with, and the duplicate is unreachable so neither the runtime nor the gate reports it},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — when nothing survives a module's __main__ block it travels WITH the cases, so four of eight generated siblings ended with two byte-identical guards. iface_bottom iface_infer stub_declines and wg_row all carried the pair. Every suite passed and every file gated CLEAN because the second block is unreachable after the first exits. Found by censusing sys.exit calls across the generated siblings rather than by any check. Repaired by emitting the tail only when the extracted text carries no guard of its own, and the four existing siblings de-duplicated by hand at 38/38 15/15 21/21 and 5/5. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): four generated siblings ended with two byte-identical __main__ blocks because the origin's guard travelled with the cases and the template appended its own — unreachable and gate-clean so nothing noticed across four splits, found only by censusing sys.exit in the generated files}
}

@misc{gate-G331-a-nested-import-is-invisible-to-a-body-walk,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A reader that iterates a module's top-level statements cannot see a conditional import, so the name it binds lands in no population at all and a generated consumer reports it undefined},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — sandbox_connect binds DictRow inside if TYPE_CHECKING, and module_trim's borrowed_imports and imported_bindings both walked tree.body only. The split suite RAN at 7/7 and the gate refused the annotation with F821 Undefined name DictRow. Both readers now descend the whole tree and must agree, since a name visible to one and not the other falls out of both import populations. RESIDUE — re-emitting the nested statement verbatim flattens it to module level and ruff then asks for it back under TYPE_CHECKING with TC002, so sandbox_connect is still unsplit and the reader fix is general rather than sufficient. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): borrowed_imports and imported_bindings both iterated tree.body so a TYPE_CHECKING import bound one level down was in neither population — sandbox_connect's DictRow read as undefined in the split sibling while the suite ran 7/7}
}

@misc{gate-G332-the-guard-travels-with-the-import,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Re-emitting a conditional import without its condition changes what the statement means, so the context must travel with the line rather than be reconstructed downstream},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — sandbox_connect binds DictRow under if TYPE_CHECKING because the name is used only in an annotation and the generator emits from __future__ import annotations, so it is never evaluated. Flattened to module level ruff refused it with TC002 Move third-party import into a type-checking block. Repaired by returning the nested line already wrapped in its guard, and by emitting from typing import TYPE_CHECKING whenever a guarded line is present since the cases never reference that name themselves. Both remaining splits then landed — sandbox_connect 7/7 and iface_cost 5/5 with all four files CLEAN. suite_gap moved 339 to 341 with splits owed 3 to 1. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): a re-emitted TYPE_CHECKING import must keep its guard because the guard is what made it correct — flattened it becomes a real import of an annotation-only name and ruff refuses with TC002, and the emitted guard also needs TYPE_CHECKING imported since the cases never name it}
}

@misc{gate-G333-a-rewritten-import-vanishes-and-appears,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {An exemption written for a deletion does not cover a rewrite, because a narrowed line is simultaneously a line that vanished and a line that appeared},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — module_trim's conservation guard allowed an import to vanish and never to appear, reasoning about ruff removing a now-unused import. type_scope uses suite_emit only in its cases dispatch, so after the trim ruff narrowed from substrate import corpus mypy_census suite_emit to drop the last name, and the guard reported the shortened line as appearing from nowhere. Extended the exemption to both directions for imports only — a genuinely invented BODY line is still reported, which is the property the seven silent-deletion bugs were caught by. The refusal then moved from UNSAFE to FINDINGS, surfacing a second and unrelated blocker the conservation refusal had been masking. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the conservation guard exempted imports only in the losing direction because it was written against ruff DELETING one — a NARROWED import both vanishes and appears, so trimming type_scope read as invented content when ruff dropped the now-unused suite_emit from a shared import line}
}

@misc{gate-G334-the-staging-name-loses-the-per-file-ignore,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A prospective check staged under a different filename is checked under different rules than the file it stands for, whenever any rule is keyed on the path},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — record_migrate writes its candidate to .record-migrate-staging.py inside the project, and its own docstring reasons that staying in the tree keeps ruff and mypy finding the same configuration. That covers config DISCOVERY and not per-file-ignores, which key on the path itself. pyproject line 617 exempts S603 for substrate/type_scope.py, pycheck_show reports that file CLEAN on disk, and the staged trim reports S603 — a finding the real file cannot have. The blocker was recorded four ticks ago as pre-existing and unrelated which was wrong and carried unchecked. Not repaired — record_migrate has other callers and the fix is a change to shared machinery. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): record_migrate checks a prospective file as .record-migrate-staging.py so a per-file-ignore keyed on the real path does not apply — type_scope is CLEAN on disk and its staged trim reports S603 which pyproject exempts for that exact path, and the pipeline therefore misreports every file carrying such an ignore}
}

@misc{gate-G335-record-migrate-has-one-consumer,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Calling a module shared machinery is a claim about its consumers that a reader can answer, and asserting it instead sizes a change by reputation rather than by reach},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — I declined to restage record_migrate this tick partly on the grounds that it has other callers. module_importers reports THREE import sites — module_trim plus module_trim_selftest and its own suite — so the operator-ruled change reaches exactly one consumer. The reader states its own bound of 12 dynamic-import shapes it cannot resolve, so three is a floor rather than a total. The caution was not wrong to raise and was wrong to assert without the one call that answers it. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): record_migrate was called shared machinery with other callers as a reason not to change it — module_importers reports THREE import sites of which one is module_trim and two are suites, so the restaging change has a single consumer and the caution was asserted rather than measured}
}

@misc{gate-G336-the-dot-and-the-module-name-conflict,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Relocating a staging artifact to inherit a path-keyed rule fails when the rule keys on an EXACT path, and the hiding convention that keeps the artifact out of a corpus walk is itself what the linter rejects inside a package},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Operator ruled stage beside the original to recover the per-file-ignore lost by a fixed repo-root staging name (gate-G334). Built and measured — S603 still reported for type_scope because pyproject keys that exemption on the exact string substrate/type_scope.py and no stand-in equals it, and the staged name drew a NEW N999 Invalid module name. The leading dot is required so corpus.roots drops the file and is the character ruff rejects, so the two constraints cannot both hold in a package directory. Reverted. The operator then ruled the stand-in filename instead — ruff has --stdin-filename which resolves per-file-ignores against a named path while reading other bytes, and mypy has no equivalent so a path-keyed mypy rule would still be lost. That is the next item and it changes pycheck_show which the pre-commit gate also uses. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): staging beside the original cannot satisfy both constraints — the leading dot keeps corpus.roots from collecting the file and is itself what ruff rejects as N999 Invalid module name inside a package directory — and no stand-in filename matches an exact per-file-ignore entry anyway so the restage does not reach the case that motivated it}
}

@misc{gate-G337-mypy-has-shadow-file,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {An absence recorded against one tool's SPELLING is not an absence of the capability, and shipping the bound would have left half a repair reading as whole},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. gate-G336 recorded that ruff has --stdin-filename and mypy has no equivalent, so a path-keyed mypy rule would stay lost after the repair. Running mypy --help refuted it — --shadow-file SOURCE_FILE SHADOW_FILE reads and type checks SHADOW's contents when it encounters SOURCE, which is exactly the identity-versus-bytes split the repair needs and is BETTER shaped than ruff's since the real path stays the argument. The claim was written from what I knew of the flag names rather than from the help text, which is the ask-the-tool rule applied to a third-party tool. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-G336 recorded that mypy has no equivalent of ruff's --stdin-filename which is true of the spelling and false of the capability — mypy --shadow-file SOURCE SHADOW checks SOURCE's identity while reading SHADOW's bytes, found by running mypy --help rather than by reasoning}
}

@misc{gate-G338-the-trim-tool-cannot-add-a-parameter,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The mechanical answer to the non-contiguous-edit wall only covers REMOVALS, so an additive change to a large file still has no path but a hand retype the gate makes dangerous},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Teaching pycheck_show.run_checks an optional stands_for needs three regions — a new argv helper, the signature, and the run loop — and the per-file gate refused every partial edit in turn, each refusal reverting the previous. module_trim's own docstring names this wall and dissolves it through record_migrate's stage-check-write pipeline, but its transform REMOVES named definitions and cannot add a parameter. The file is 380 lines of which I had read 121, so a whole-file Write would retype 260 unread lines into the checker the pre-commit gate uses — the silent-deletion risk the conservation guard exists for, with no guard available. Stopped rather than retyped. The gap is a transform that ADDS under the same pipeline. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): adding a parameter to run_checks needs three non-contiguous regions and the per-file gate refuses each partial edit, so it needs one whole-file Write — and module_trim which exists to abolish that retype only REMOVES definitions, so a 380-line file the pre-commit gate depends on has no mechanical path for an additive change}
}

@misc{gate-G339-the-additive-transform-closes-the-wall,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A pipeline that stages and checks a whole prospective file dissolves the non-contiguous-edit wall for any transform expressible over it, so the missing capability was a transform rather than a mechanism},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. module_trim removes named definitions and gate-G338 recorded that an ADDITIVE change had no mechanical path. substrate/module_replace supplies it — swap one named top-level definition for caller-supplied text, reusing record_migrate's staging fix check and three-state verdict rather than opening a second write path. The conservation guard could not be reused because a replacement legitimately adds and drops lines inside the span, so the half that still discriminates is asserted instead — every line OUTSIDE the replaced definition must be untouched in both directions. Measured — it landed the three-region stands_for change to scripts/pycheck_show.run_checks that four Edit attempts had each reverted, refusing first on a real tuple-typing error in my payload and writing nothing. Then the Protocol and _check in record_migrate, both CLEAN. The trim of substrate/type_scope.py went FINDINGS to CLEAN with the false S603 gone, the split landed at 14/14 with both halves CLEAN, and suite_gap moved 341 of 376 to 342 of 377 with the owed-a-SPLIT category now EMPTY. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): module_replace supplies the additive half module_trim lacked — replace one named top-level definition with caller-supplied text through the same check-then-write pipeline — and it landed a three-region change to the pre-commit checker that four Edit attempts could not, then closed the split arc by removing the false S603}
}

@misc{gate-G340-the-outside-span-guard-catches-a-renaming-payload,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A guard keyed on the replaced NAME rather than the replaced SPAN reports a payload defining a different name as invented content, which is the protection a caller most needs from a transform that takes a text file},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured by accident while building the suite — pointing module_replace at outside_span with a payload that defines _check reported eleven lines appearing outside the span and wrote nothing. Reproduced against substrate/type_scope.py replacing main with the same payload, identical refusal, so the behaviour is general rather than a property of one file. That is the highest-value failure mode for this tool since the payload is a separate file a caller can easily mismatch with the name. The suite itself is RED on two arms whose fixtures assume _body_outside strips by SPAN when it strips by NAME — a defect in the arms rather than the module, left unstaged rather than patched unverified. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): module_replace's guard was verified by an accident — feeding a payload that defines a DIFFERENT name reported eleven invented lines and wrote nothing, reproduced on a second unrelated file, so the protection is real and general rather than asserted}
}

@misc{gate-G341-suite-gap-counts-existence-not-passing,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A coverage census keyed on a sibling file existing cannot distinguish a passing suite from a failing one, so its count is a floor on coverage and says nothing about outcome},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — substrate/module_replace_selftest.py was written last tick failing two arms and deliberately left unstaged, and suite_gap_report moved from 342 to 343 of 377 with it counted. The reader answers does a sibling exist which is the right question for what it was built for and the wrong one for any claim about verification. run_selftests is the reader that knows outcome. Recorded rather than repaired — joining the two is a design question about what the census is FOR, not a defect in either. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): suite_gap keys coverage on a sibling file existing so a RED suite reads as coverage — module_replace_selftest was untracked and failing two arms and the census moved 342 to 343 of 377 anyway, which is the census-green shape inside the coverage reader itself}
}

@misc{gate-G342-a-two-sided-edit-cannot-pin-one-side,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A fixture that changes a line exercises both halves of a two-direction guard at once, so the arm passes whichever half is broken and certifies a direction it never reached},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — module_replace_selftest's mirror arm rewrote return 1 to return 99 to pin the GAINED direction of the outside-span guard. Inverting that comparison in the module left the suite at 22 of 22, because a rewritten line is simultaneously a loss and a gain and the LOST loop reported it. Repaired to a pure addition which removes nothing, and the same inversion then failed the arm by name. A second fixture defect in the same suite had the mirror shape — a dropped-neighbour arm spliced by str.replace on an exact blank-run spelling libcst does not guarantee, so the replace found nothing, the text was unchanged, and the guard correctly reported nothing while the arm read as a miss. Both arms now assert the fixture BIT before asserting the verdict. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the rewritten-neighbour arm changed a line which is a loss AND a gain so the lost loop caught it and the gained direction stayed unexercised — verified by inverting the gained comparison and watching the suite stay green, then repaired to a PURE ADDITION which goes red on the same plant}
}

@misc{gate-G343-the-named-batch-path-emits-no-bound,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A second rendering path that formats its own summary inherits none of the bounds the shared renderer carries, so the caveat is present or absent depending on which mode the reader invoked},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — suite_gap_report.main has two branches. The whole-package branch calls suite_gap.render and now prints both footers. The named-batch branch writes its own ratio line and returns, so asking about specific modules emits no bound at all — verified by invoking it with one name and reading the single line back. Found while chasing why an arm testing the new bound stayed red, which it did for an unrelated reason. Not repaired this tick — the fix is to route both branches through one renderer, which is a change to what render takes rather than a line added to a branch. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): suite_gap_report's named-batch branch builds its own ratio line and never calls render so it emits NEITHER footer — the not-a-defect-list bound nor the coverage-is-not-passing one — and a reader asking about specific modules gets the count with no caveat at all}
}

@misc{gate-G344-i-reasoned-four-times-before-reading-once,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A case-sensitive assertion written from the intent of a line rather than from its emitted bytes fails in a way that invites every explanation except the true one},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — an arm asserted the substring not PASSING while the render emits NOT PASSING ONE inside an emphasis block written in capitals. The report showed the new line correctly and the suite stayed red, which I read in turn as an import staleness a module-versus-path difference a shadowing installed copy and a fixture divergence. One pycodemod --literal over the module returned zero sites and settled it. The rule this violates is the tree's own — read the binding rather than infer the shape — and the tell was that the text was VISIBLE in the report output I had already printed. Repaired by asserting the emitted spelling and the arm then verified load-bearing by planting a reworded bound. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): an arm asserting not PASSING stayed red against a render emitting NOT PASSING ONE in the emphasis block's capitals — four probes went into staleness path-shadowing and fixture theories before one pycodemod --literal over the module showed the substring was simply absent}
}

@misc{gate-G345-module-replace-swaps-one-def-and-cannot-add-a-sibling,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A transform whose unit is one named definition cannot introduce a second one, because its own guard reads the new sibling as content invented outside the replaced span},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — extracting the shared footer into a new bounds function beside render, passed to module_replace as one payload naming render, was REFUSED with eleven lines appearing outside render. The guard is right — by its definition a statement outside the named span is invented — so this is a boundary rather than a defect. The working order is to land the sibling by ordinary Edit first and then rewire the existing definition through module_replace, which is what happened here. Worth stating because the tool's purpose is the non-contiguous additive change and this is an additive change it does not cover. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): extracting a shared helper beside an existing definition is refused by module_replace's own outside-span guard because the new sibling lands outside the named span and is invented content by definition — correct behaviour and a real boundary, so the helper must land by ordinary Edit first and the rewire follow}
}

@misc{gate-G346-an-arm-cannot-precede-the-function-it-names,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Write-the-failing-arm-first is unavailable under a per-file type gate whenever the arm names a symbol that does not exist yet, so for an extraction the order inverts and the red state must be produced by planting instead},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured — an arm calling suite_gap.bounds was refused with Module has no attribute bounds before the function existed. The discipline it serves is unchanged and the mechanism differs — land the extraction, add the arm, then PLANT the failure the arm claims to catch and watch it fire. Done here by giving render its own drifted copy of the footer, which failed both the sharing arm by name and the covered-side arm. A reworded bound did NOT fail anything, correctly, because both readers move together when the source is shared — that is the property under test rather than a gap. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): writing the failing arm first is unavailable when the arm names a function that does not exist yet — the per-file gate refuses the suite for an undefined attribute — so for an extraction the module lands first and the arm is verified load-bearing by planting a drifted copy afterwards}
}

@misc{gate-G347-an-absent-name-reads-as-covered,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A batch reader that requires a path to exist before testing it reports a name it cannot find as satisfying the test, so a typo inflates the covered side rather than surfacing},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. Measured while writing the report's suite — invoking the named-batch path with no_such_module_at_all.py prints one of one having a suite. uncovered_among filters on the path existing AND lacking a suite, so an absent name fails the first half and never reaches the second. The direction is the flattering one and the reader cannot tell a typo from a module it was not asked about. Cased rather than repaired — whether an unknown name should refuse or be reported as a third state is a question about what the batch mode promises its caller, and the ratio is honest about its own denominator either way. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): uncovered_among requires the path to exist before asking whether it has a suite so a typo or a deleted module reads as COVERED rather than uncovered — the flattering direction — and the reader cannot distinguish a typo from a real module in the caller's name list}
}

@misc{gate-G348-the-prefix-hazard-in-the-packaged-test,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A membership test over dotted names that matches a string prefix rather than the root segment swallows a differently-named sibling distribution and reports its imports as already satisfied},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. adopter_import._resolvable decides whether a module-scope import is reachable for an adopter, and intake_manifest reports the resulting blocker for the whole corpus — so a wrong verdict moves the READY count everywhere while the blocker's own SOLE count stays small. Verified by planting startswith in place of the dot split — substrate_extra then read as packaged and the new arm failed by name while the other thirteen held. Restored with no residue and the manifest unchanged. The three acceptance paths are pinned separately because the dotted-root one looks like a special case of the exact-match one and is the easiest to lose. The find_spec path is asserted only against the stdlib and an unfindable name, since it reads what is INSTALLED and a third-party name could not hold a stable polarity. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): adopter_import._resolvable splits a dotted name on the first dot and asks whether the ROOT is packaged — replacing that with startswith makes substrate_extra read as packaged, which reports a genuine reach into an unpackaged tree as satisfied and empties a blocker in the flattering direction}
}

@misc{gate-G349-the-paydown-is-three-codes-outside-the-package,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A backlog counted in files reads as many arcs and counted in error codes reads as few, so sizing by the wrong unit decides the wrong shape of work},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. intake_manifest reports not-promotable at SOLE 735 and promote_frontier at 731 DIRTY with ZERO blocked, which reads as 731 independent repairs. type_census_summary sizes the same population by CODE — misc at 79.9 percent with no-untyped-call and no-untyped-def bringing three codes to 96.7 percent of 44030 messages over 25 codes and 288 files. By promoted root jea holds 53.3 percent scripts 34.0 el-atlas 12.7 and substrate 0.1, so the backlog sits almost entirely outside the package that ships. misc under disallow_any_expr is the expression-has-type-Any family, which is one class rather than a long tail. Sized rather than started — the shape is a small number of mechanical classes in trees the wheel does not carry, and which of those the operator wants paid is a scoping decision this reader cannot make. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): type_census_summary sizes the not-promotable arc — misc at 79.9 percent plus no-untyped-call and no-untyped-def take 96.7 percent of 44030 messages across 25 codes, and by tree jea holds 53.3 percent scripts 34.0 and el-atlas 12.7 while substrate holds 0.1 so the backlog is almost entirely outside the package that ships}
}

@misc{gate-G350-annotating-defs-does-not-promote-a-file,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Under an all-or-nothing per-file gate a paydown organised by error CODE retires messages without promoting a single file, because promotion needs every code on that file cleared at once},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. The operator ruled attack no-untyped-def across all trees on the strength of its being 5.2 percent of messages and the cause of most no-untyped-call downstream. Measured against a member — scripts/reuse_catalog.py carries 605 findings in 51 rules, of which the annotation family ANN001 ANN201 ANN202 ANN204 is about 60. Retiring all of them leaves roughly 545 findings in 48 rules and the file is still refused and still not promotable. The distribution is flat rather than concentrated — the heaviest file is 2.9 percent of messages and 268 files hold 63.3 percent — so there is no small set of files a code-wise sweep finishes either. A code-wise paydown moves a NUMBER and a file-wise one moves the PROMOTABLE count, and only the second is what not-promotable measures. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): the per-file gate is all-or-nothing so paying one code class leaves every touched file still refused — scripts/reuse_catalog.py carries 605 findings across 51 rules and retiring its ANN001 ANN201 and ANN202 sites would leave about 545 in 48 rules, promoting nothing}
}

@misc{gate-G351-the-absent-third-state-needs-its-premise-pinned,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Where a sibling has a refusal branch and this one deliberately does not, the case to write is the PREMISE that makes the absence safe rather than a copy of the sibling's arm},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=substrate. private_gate loads scanners out of scratch and must distinguish no-violations from scanner-missing, so it carries an Unmeasurable branch. public_gate walks the tree itself and its docstring argues an empty census IS an empty tree, so there is no third state — a case demanding a refusal there would assert a branch the design says must not exist. That safety rests entirely on the census delegating to the tree-walking reader, so the arm asserts the delegation. Verified by planting a census that drops one key and watching ONLY that arm fail, and separately by planting a key_move that no longer collapses paths and watching only the relocation arm fail. 12 of 12, both plants left no residue, and the live gate still refuses on the tree's standing state — 97 paydowns held by 36 additions, which is pre-existing Agda debt. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): public_gate deliberately has no Unmeasurable branch because it walks the tree so an empty census IS an empty tree — that safety rests entirely on the census delegating to the tree-walking reader, so the suite pins the DELEGATION rather than copying the sibling's refusal arm}
}

@misc{gate-G352,
