  claim    = {A per-gate timeout measured inside a serial survey is not independent, so the ceiling reports the harness rather than the gate},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED across three sync_verdict runs. worklist tracker integrity read 12.9s then 120.6s then 65.9s. jea regression read 7.7s then 120.8s then 39.6s. apex gate read 46.0s then 120.9s then 120.2s — it crossed the ceiling even on the quietest run, and law_motif_apex --check run ALONE answers correctly and fast. ⚑ THE CRON'S OWN CLAIM THAT IT CROSSES ONLY UNDER CONTENTION IS REFUTED by the third reading. ⚑ WHAT IS MEASURED is that the same gate reads 46s early in a survey and past 120s as the tenth of eleven while being fine standalone. ⚑ WHAT IS NOT MEASURED is WHY — accumulated process state correlates with serial position and is a hypothesis, not a finding, so no repair is attempted. Operator ruled file it rather than raise the bound or isolate the subprocess, because both would act on an unmeasured mechanism. ⚑ THE TIMED-OUT STATE FROM gate-G361 IS WHAT MADE THIS VISIBLE — the rows say a fact about the MACHINE rather than no verdict, so three inflated readings read as a pattern instead of as three broken gates. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A per-gate timeout measured inside a serial survey is not independent — the apex gate reads 46s early in a run and crosses 120s as the tenth of eleven, while answering correctly when run alone, so the ceiling reports the harness rather than the gate}
}

@misc{gate-G365,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G362},
  claim    = {A branch unreachable by any cheap input became drivable by extracting the decision it guards, because every input was already a plain value and only its position inside a subprocess caller hid it},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED — sync_verdict.state_of now takes code and said as arguments, and the suite drives all four states in one call each, going 29 to 33. Both halves verified by planting SEPARATELY: widening the None discriminator to any non-empty reason fails the launch-failure arm at 32 of 33, and collapsing the exit-code branch to FRESH fails the drifted arm at 32 of 33. ⚑ THE PREVIOUS ATTEMPT COULD NOT DO THIS (gate-G362) — the branch needed a real timeout or an OS launch failure to reach, so a plant left the suite at full marks and the arm written as a negative control measured a path that never reached the branch. ⚑⚑ AND THE GATE REFUSED THE FIRST PLANT, which is the extraction proving itself: returning UNREACHED unconditionally left said unused and drew ARG001, so the collapse cannot even be written now. ⚑ THE WITNESS IS standing RATHER THAN selftest BECAUSE THE SUITE OUTRUNS THE PROBE — the registration guard refused a selftest witness after the command exceeded its 300s budget, which is gate-G364's non-independent-cost class arriving at the ledger. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A branch unreachable by any cheap input became drivable by extracting the decision it guards — every input was already a plain value and only its position inside a subprocess caller hid it, so the seam was a rename rather than a restructuring}
}

@misc{gate-G366,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G364},
  claim    = {Two explanations for a gate crossing its ceiling were refuted in turn and what survives is that the crossing reads the gate and the moment together rather than either alone},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED with a new mode. gate-G364 offered accumulated process state at serial position as the hypothesis and said do not patch until it is measured. ⚑ CONTENTION WENT FIRST — a quiet run still read the apex gate at 120.2s. ⚑ SERIAL POSITION WENT SECOND — sync_verdict --gate apex gate asks it as the ONLY gate and it read 120.3s, position one of one. ⚑ AND THE CONTROL HOLDS — gate census in sync asked the same way reads 1.6s, so the mode adds no overhead. ⚑⚑ WHAT SURVIVES IS THAT THE GATE'S OWN COST MOVES: law_motif_apex --check invoked directly answered correctly this tick after running past 300s, having answered in well under the ceiling two ticks earlier. So the earlier claim that it answers fine standalone was a reading of a faster moment, not a property. ⚑ THE PROSE IN sync_verdict CARRYING BOTH REFUTED CLAIMS WAS CORRECTED IN THE SAME TICK — the render footer and the TIMED_OUT docstring now say a TIMED-OUT row is a prompt to ask that gate alone and compare, which is what the new mode is for. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Two explanations for the apex gate crossing its ceiling were refuted in turn — contention by a quiet run at 120.2s and serial position by a --gate run at 120.3s as the only gate asked — while the same gate has answered in 46s and has also run past 300s invoked directly, so the crossing reads the gate and the moment together}
}

@misc{gate-G367,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A Protocol and its real implementation moved together while the test double did not, so two suites died at a TypeError before asserting anything and the runner classed them UNREAD, which is not a failure and therefore read as coverage},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.record_migrate_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED — record_migrate._check gained a stands_for argument under the stdin-filename work and the Checker Protocol moved with it, whose own docstring warns that a Protocol still declaring the one-argument form would type-check green and refuse the second argument at the seam. That is what happened ONE LAYER OVER: _Stub in record_migrate_selftest kept the two-argument signature, so record_migrate_selftest and module_trim_selftest both raised TypeError at the first arm. ⚑ THE STUB NOW MATCHES AND RECORDS what each check was told to stand for. record_migrate_selftest reads 66 of 66 where it read nothing. ⚑⚑ AND THE CLASS IS THE UNREAD STATE ITSELF — run_selftests names unread as neither a test failure nor an absent emitter, so a suite that dies before asserting looks like coverage in every roster that counts existence. suite_gap reads existence and cannot see this at all. ⚑ THE FOUR OWNING TOOLS DID NOT SURFACE IT IN TEN TICKS — run_selftests did, which is the reader that answers outcome. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A Protocol and its real implementation moved together while the TEST DOUBLE did not, so two suites died at a TypeError before asserting anything and the runner classed them UNREAD — which is not a failure and therefore read as coverage}
}

@misc{gate-G368,
  section  = {finding},
  from     = {gate-G367},
  claim    = {module_trim_selftest carries a stale arm asserting a one-directional import exemption the guard deliberately abandoned, and a fixture leak where one arm's sibling file survives into the apply arm, both invisible while the suite died at a TypeError},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED after the stub repair unblocked the suite — module-trim selftest reports 2 failures. ⚑ THE STALE ARM is a_stranded_import_may_vanish_but_a_body_line_may_not, which asserts an INVENTED import must be refused. conserved's own comment records the change and the measurement behind it: on type_scope the trim makes ruff NARROW a from-import, so the line vanishes AND appears as one edit, and the one-way rule refused a correct trim. The guard is right and the arm is stale. ⚑ THE LEAK is an_apply_lands_the_fixed_text failing because a prior arm left .module-trim-case_selftest.py on disk, so the apply refuses rather than writing — the run output shows REFUSED it would be overwritten. ⚑ NEITHER IS FIXED THIS TICK, operator ruling — they are a different subject from the stub repair and each deserves its own reading rather than being swept into a repair for something else. ⚑ UNWITNESSED because no registered kind distinguishes an arm that is stale from one that is failing, and the discriminating quantity is whether the MODULE or the ARM holds the newer claim. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): module_trim_selftest carries a STALE ARM asserting the import exemption is one-directional while the guard was deliberately made two-directional with a measured reason in its own comment, plus a fixture leak where one arm's sibling file survives into the --apply arm — both were invisible while the suite died at a TypeError}
}

@misc{gate-F60-a-suite-artefact-poisons-its-own-next-run,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A suite that leaves an artefact on disk poisons its own next run, and the tell is a failure count that GREW between byte-identical runs.},
  check    = {refuses:python3,-m,substrate.module_trim_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED — module_trim_selftest reported 2 failures then 3, because _Planted.__exit__ unlinked only the module it planted while module_trim --apply also writes a sibling selftest beside it. The leftover made every later --apply arm refuse as already-existing. Repaired with an also-tuple on _Planted, and two consecutive runs now agree with no leftover on disk. AND THE LEAK INFLATED A POPULATION THE OPERATOR RULED ON — the suite reports ONE genuine failure, not two. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): module_trim_selftest left .module-trim-case_selftest.py on disk because _Planted.__exit__ unlinked only the module it planted, never the sibling that module_trim --apply writes beside it. Every later --apply arm then hit REFUSED-already-exists, so the failure count GREW between byte-identical runs: 2 then 3. A suite that leaves an artefact poisons its own next run, and the tell is a count that moves without the code moving. Repaired by teaching _Planted an also-tuple of extra paths to unlink, passed module_trim.sibling_of(_FIXTURE) at the two --apply sites that write; two consecutive runs now report the same count and git sees no leftover. The witness is refuses because the suite is still RED on the genuine conservation-arm failure gate-G368 and goes green only when that lands. AND THE LEAK INFLATED A POPULATION THE OPERATOR RULED ON: with it closed the suite reports ONE failure, not two; the second was the poisoned arm, never a defect in the guard.}
}

@misc{gate-G370-an-unrecognised-flag-widens-a-census-silently,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {An unrecognised flag that is silently dropped makes a full census read as an answer to a narrow question.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.tool_population_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED — tool_population.py --find run_selftests printed every rostered row, byte-identical to a bare run, because page.read_args consumes --top and --skip and ignores the rest. A census with no matching mode is indistinguishable from one that answered. Repaired by adding --find to the owning reader, with a miss line carrying its denominator so a zero is a fact about THESE rows rather than about the tree. Suite 14 to 21, all four new arms plant-verified. AND THE PER-FILE GATE REFUSED THE FIRST PLANT at ARG001 — an unused parameter is the signature of the no-op being planted, so the gate caught the defect before the arm did. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): tool_population.py --find run_selftests printed all rostered rows, BYTE-IDENTICAL to a bare run, because page.read_args reads --top and --skip and drops every other token. The caller was asking WHERE a runner lived and got the whole roster, so a census with no matching mode read as a census that had answered. The standing rule said tool_population answers whether a tool is rostered, and that was true only of a human reading ~600 lines by eye. Added --find to the OWNING reader rather than building a second one, with a miss that names its denominator because a bare no-tool-matches is indistinguishable from a census that read nothing. 14 to 21 cases, all four new arms plant-verified. AND THE GATE REFUSED THE FIRST PLANT: returning rows unfiltered left want unused and tripped ARG001, so the no-op I was trying to plant is the one the per-file gate will not let land.}
}

@misc{gate-G369-the-runner-is-not-a-package-module,
