@misc{gate-G371-the-unfit-roster-was-enumerated-from-memory,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A blocker list carried across ticks without re-derivation named three members of a population that has four.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED from scripts/run_selftests.py — the unfit population is FOUR suites, not the three the cron carried. The missing one is catalog/library/figures.py. All four die at the same TOP lease 192MB while global free read 6.4 to 7.6 GB, so the kill is the per-scope MemoryMax rather than global pressure. AND THE FOURTH IS INVISIBLE TO THE TOOL CENSUS — tool_population --find figures misses over 755 rostered tools because catalog/library is not in TOOL_DIRS, so any reader keyed on that population under-reports the blocker by one. Enumerate-before-acting-on-a-count, firing on the cron's own list. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The momentum cron carried Ⓑ⟡unfit-suites as THREE suites — sync_verdict.py, sync_verdict_selftest.py, module_importers.py — and named them as the population. MEASURED from scripts/run_selftests.py: there are FOUR, and the fourth is catalog/library/figures.py, which no earlier tick mentioned. All four are lease-killed at the same TOP lease 192MB while global free read 6.4 to 7.6 GB, so the kill is the per-scope MemoryMax and not global pressure. AND THE FOURTH IS OUTSIDE THE TOOL ROSTER: tool_population --find figures reports no match over 755 rostered tools, because catalog/library is not in TOOL_DIRS — so a census keyed on the tool population cannot see it at all. This is enumerate-the-population-before-acting-on-its-count firing on the cron's own list, and the list had been carried forward across ticks without re-derivation.}
}

@misc{gate-G372-unfit-is-three-different-things,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {unfit is a symptom class rather than a cause — four lease-killed suites had three unrelated reasons.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED under a 2048MB headroom lease via membudget peaks, on the operator's ruling to measure before deciding. FOUR SUITES, THREE CAUSES. sync_verdict 421MB and sync_verdict_selftest 422MB against a 192MB default, the suite passing 37/37 unkilled — both suggest 512MB, and this is the only genuine footprint case. module_importers_selftest peaks at 18MB and passes 34/34, a tenth of the default, so the roster row is not the suite: the bare module refuses with a usage line and the --unimported corpus fold peaks at 559MB over 378 seconds, suggesting 1024MB. Two programs behind one label, differing by a factor of thirty, and the cheap one carried the blame. catalog/library/figures.py is not memory-bound at all and dies at AttributeError worklist_gate has no attribute scratch_size, classed unfit only because the kill preceded the real failure. A blanket lease raise would have turned one real failure into a silent pass and left the 18MB case unexplained. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The operator ruled MEASURE THE FOUR PEAKS FIRST on the unfit-suites blocker, and measuring partitions the population into three unrelated causes rather than one lease question. MEASURED under a 2048MB headroom lease with membudget peaks. ONE is genuinely large — sync_verdict_selftest peaks at 422MB against the 192MB default and passes 37/37 when not killed, suggested lease 512MB. ONE IS NOT LARGE AT ALL — module_importers_selftest peaks at 18MB and passes 34/34, a tenth of the default, so the roster row for substrate/module_importers.py is not the suite: the bare module refuses with a usage line and the corpus-wide --unimported fold is the expensive target, a different program than the label names. ONE IS NOT A MEMORY PROBLEM — catalog/library/figures.py dies at AttributeError module worklist_gate has no attribute scratch_size, and was classed unfit only because the lease kill fired before the real failure surfaced. So unfit is a SYMPTOM CLASS, not a cause, and a blanket lease raise would have converted one real failure into a silent pass while leaving the 18MB case unexplained.}
}

@misc{gate-G373-figures-calls-a-provider-that-no-longer-defines-it,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A row whose provider was deleted does not degrade — it raises AttributeError and takes the whole suite with it, and a lease kill can hide that for as long as the budget is tight.},
  check    = {refuses:python3,catalog/library/figures.py,--selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED — catalog/library/figures.py:1402-1406 calls worklist_gate.scratch_size() and reads worklist_gate.SCRATCH. module_outline shows worklist_gate defining seven functions and one constant ROOT, and attr_reads finds figures.py:1406 as the only reader of SCRATCH across 1617 files. run_selftests classed the file UNFIT, a lease finding and not a test failure, because the 192MB kill preceded the crash — at 2048MB it fails instantly and visibly. The dependency is also a cycle, since worklist_gate.figures() imports this module back, and worklist_gate is hard-quarantined for a recursion defect. The operator ruled DROP THE ROW and the edit is REFUSED at 1103 pycheck findings under an all-or-nothing gate, so even deleting a broken block cannot land. Correct repair, blocked on draining the file. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): catalog/library/figures.py:1402-1406 calls worklist_gate.scratch_size() and reads worklist_gate.SCRATCH to report a scratch-bytes-held row. NEITHER EXISTS — module_outline reports worklist_gate with seven definitions and one constant ROOT, and attr_reads worklist_gate.SCRATCH finds figures.py:1406 as the ONLY reader across 1617 files. So the row does not degrade, it raises AttributeError and takes the whole selftest with it. AND THE LEASE WAS HIDING IT: run_selftests classed the file UNFIT, which is a lease finding and explicitly not a test failure, because the 192MB kill fired before the crash surfaced. Given 2048MB it fails instantly. The dependency is also a CYCLE — worklist_gate.figures() imports this module back — and worklist_gate is hard-quarantined at the top of its main for a recursion defect. THE OPERATOR RULED DROP THE ROW, and the edit is REFUSED: the file carries 1103 pycheck findings and the per-file gate is all-or-nothing, so even deleting a broken block cannot land. The repair is correct and blocked on draining the file.}
}

@misc{gate-G374-the-selftest-lease-was-a-constant-beside-a-live-ledger,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A fleet-wide lease constant sat beside a ledger of per-label peaks that nothing consumed, and the comment justifying it described a retry ladder that cannot fire on that path.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.suite_command_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED — suite_command.DEFAULT_SUITE_MB leased 192MB for all ~234 suites. Its docstring claimed the retry ladder doubles an over-lease and records the peak, but a literal census puts MEMBUDGET_RETRY_OOM in only compile_recipe, its suite, and gate_status, and suite_command set no environment, so an over-lease was killed rather than escalated. membudget records a peak only on rc 0, so the heaviest suites erased themselves from the ledger and the self-correction depended on the run that never completed. label_lease.size and label_ledger.ledger_path already existed and module_importers reported label_lease with ONE importer, its own suite — built and orphaned. Wired suite_mb to size per label, SELFTEST_MB still winning outright. Suite 11 to 15, sizing and ceiling arms plant-verified. On the live ledger a 90MB-peak label now sizes 128 where it leased 192, so the median case pays down while the tail stops dying. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): suite_command.DEFAULT_SUITE_MB was one 192MB constant for every suite, and its own docstring justified that with two claims that are both false. It said 192 is the shim's own cold-module default and that if a suite exceeds it the retry ladder doubles it and the peak is recorded so the budget self-corrects. MEASURED — a literal census of MEMBUDGET_RETRY_OOM finds it in exactly three files, compile_recipe and its suite and gate_status, and suite_command set no environment at all, so a suite over its lease was KILLED rather than escalated. And membudget records a peak only on rc 0, so the heaviest suites erased themselves from the ledger and the correction depended on the run that never completed. Meanwhile substrate/label_lease.size already did the arithmetic and substrate/label_ledger.ledger_path already named the file, and module_importers label_lease reported ONE importer, its own suite. Wired suite_mb to size per label from recorded peaks, keeping SELFTEST_MB as an outright override because an operator naming a size did so for a reason history cannot see. Suite 11 to 15, sizing arms plant-verified. VERIFIED ON THE LIVE LEDGER — a label with history sizes 128 against a 90MB peak where it used to lease 192, so the common case pays DOWN.}
}

