  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.glyph_flagged_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED — glyph_flagged_selftest ran the real checker over scripts/worklist.py to derive its escape set from ruff's output. pycheck_show now reports that file CLEAN, so the report was empty, the flags-characters arm found nothing, and the iota positive control that exists to catch an empty-set reader could not fire either. Both failed together, correctly. The operator ruled a synthetic test, and the distinction that keeps the module's thesis intact is that the SUBJECT is planted while the REPORT stays real — ruff reads a fixture this file writes and emits its own RUF001 and RUF002 rows in its own wording, so the parse is verified against the emitter while the arm can no longer be cleared by tidying an unrelated file. Verified by inverting the plant, a subject carrying no ambiguous glyph reproduces the original 16/18 exactly. The fixture lives in a TemporaryDirectory so nothing survives the run. 16/18 to 18/18. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): glyph_flagged_selftest read 16/18 because its live subject was a BORROWED tree file. The arm ran the real checker over scripts/worklist.py to derive the escape set from ruff's own output, and pycheck_show now reports that file CLEAN — so the report was empty, the flags-characters arm found nothing, and the iota positive control that exists to catch an empty-set reader could not fire either. Both failed together, correctly. The suite had been measuring the tree's tidiness rather than its own reader, and tree hygiene was being reported as a regression. THE OPERATOR RULED A SYNTHETIC TEST, and the distinction that makes it sound is that the SUBJECT is planted while the REPORT stays real — ruff reads a fixture this file writes and emits its own RUF001 and RUF002 rows in its own wording, so the parse is still verified against the emitter exactly as the module's thesis demands, while the arm can no longer be cleared by someone tidying an unrelated file. Verified by inverting the plant: a subject with no ambiguous glyph reproduces the original 16/18 exactly, which proves the checker is reading the planted file. The fixture lives in a TemporaryDirectory so nothing survives the run. 16/18 to 18/18.}
}

@misc{gate-G378-two-arms-pinned-a-migration-that-rewired-itself-away,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Two arms asserted lines from a branch the tree no longer reaches, because the migration they measured rewired its own importers away.},
  check    = {refuses:python3,scratch/split_pipeline.py,--selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED — split_pipeline reads 53/55 on two arms probing ready_verdict for Substrate.Foundation.Nat, one expecting an UNMAPPED line and one a NOT-compile-checked cap line. Both sit behind the same branch, which ready_verdict returns early from when the rewire reaches zero importers. rewire_to_leaves reports 27 names over 13 leaves to zero importers for that target with no UNMAPPED sites, and 31 names over 25 leaves to zero for Algebra.F2. The function's docstring records Nat measuring three importers with one UNMAPPED name on 2026-08-17, so ban-decompose rewired them away and the arms pinned the old measurement. The third arm on the same verdict passes because it asserts the NO-OP branch both targets now take. The operator ruled PLANT A FIXTURE TARGET per gate-G377, and it cannot land — the file carries heavy pre-existing debt across nine mypy codes plus a large ruff volume under an all-or-nothing gate. Third blocked repair this arc after gate-G373 and gate-G376. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): split_pipeline reads 53/55 on two arms probing ready_verdict for Substrate.Foundation.Nat — one expecting an UNMAPPED line, one expecting a NOT-compile-checked cap line. BOTH SIT BEHIND THE SAME BRANCH: ready_verdict returns early when the rewire reaches zero importers, so neither line can be produced. MEASURED — rewire_to_leaves Substrate.Foundation.Nat --manifest --dry-run reports 27 names / 13 leaves to WOULD rewire 0 importer(s), with no UNMAPPED sites at all, and Substrate.Algebra.F2 reports 31 names / 25 leaves to 0 importers likewise. The function's own docstring records Nat measuring 3 importers with one UNMAPPED name on 2026-08-17, so the ban-decompose migration rewired those importers away and the arms pinned the old measurement. The third arm on the same verdict passes because it asserts the NO-OP branch both targets now take. THE OPERATOR RULED PLANT A FIXTURE TARGET per the gate-G377 pattern, and it CANNOT LAND: scratch/split_pipeline.py carries heavy pre-existing pycheck debt across nine mypy codes plus a large ruff volume under an all-or-nothing gate. Third file this arc where a correct repair is blocked on draining a monolith, after gate-G373 and gate-G376.}
}

@misc{gate-G379-the-ready-mode-is-named-by-its-own-tool-and-dispatched-by-none,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A tool names a flag in its own output that its dispatch no longer recognises, so a reader following the tool's advice gets a refusal.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED — split_pipeline --ready refuses with an explicit does-not-exist message while ready_verdict remains a live in-module function the selftest calls directly. toolmodes --find ready reports both halves in its NAMED-but-not-declared section: the flag is named in the tool's own output and declared by no tool, and separately dispatched by that file while appearing in no usage line. The capability is not missing, only its spelling is unreachable. This is the dead-capability-from-the-reader's-side shape the file's own Ⓜ1 docstring records about itself, where seventeen of seventeen modes were undocumented and that cost a duplicate distance() implementation written and then deleted. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): split_pipeline --ready refuses with an explicit does-not-exist message while ready_verdict remains a live in-module function that the selftest calls directly. toolmodes --find ready reports the split cleanly in its NAMED-but-not-declared section: --ready is named in scratch/split_pipeline.py's own output and declared by NO tool, and separately dispatched by that file while appearing in no usage line. Both readings are true of different layers — the tool PRINTS the flag in a remedy string and the dispatch has been retired, so a reader following the tool's own advice gets a refusal. This is the dead-capability-from-the-reader's-side shape the file's own Ⓜ1 docstring records about itself, where all seventeen of its modes were once undocumented and that cost a duplicate distance() implementation. The capability is not missing — ready_verdict answers, and gate-G378 exercised it by import — only its spelling is unreachable.}
}

@misc{gate-G380-a-generated-cache-and-a-licence-sweep-are-in-a-permanent-standoff,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A generator that omits the licence header its tree adds leaves a drift gate permanently STALE while every unit it compares agrees.},
  check    = {refuses:python3,jea/metalanguage/query_defs.py,--check},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED — query_defs --check reports query_sql_postgres.py STALE with zero builders differing. The verdict is a whole-file text compare while the breakdown compares builders one by one, so the drift lives in text that is not a builder. The committed cache carries a two-line SPDX and copyright header that compile_text does not emit, and a literal census finds no SPDX string in query_defs.py at all. Running --compile would strip the licence header, which is why regenerating never closed it. The operator ruled compile_text should emit the header and the authority already exists — substrate/header_write.header_lines builds the two lines from file_header's prefixes, warning that a second spelling passes the writer's cases and fails the census. Blocked: query_defs.py is refused on heavy pre-existing debt and header_write is built-then-orphaned with only its own suite importing it. Fourth blocked repair in four ticks after gate-G373, gate-G376 and gate-G378. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): query_defs --check prints a self-contradicting verdict: query_sql_postgres.py STALE with 0 builder(s) differ. MEASURED — the verdict is a whole-file text compare, fresh == on_disk in do_check, while the breakdown loops INTERN_BUILDERS and compares each against the committed SQL dict. Zero builders differ, so the drift is in text that is not a builder. Reading the committed cache shows a two-line SPDX and copyright header at the top, and compile_text emits only three GENERATED-by comment lines with no licence header — a literal census finds no SPDX string anywhere in query_defs.py. So the two files differ on those two lines permanently while every builder agrees, and running --compile would STRIP the licence header, which is why this was never closed by regenerating. THE OPERATOR RULED compile_text SHOULD EMIT THE HEADER, and the authority already exists: substrate/header_write.header_lines returns the two lines built from file_header's own prefixes, with a docstring warning that a second spelling would pass the writer's cases and fail the census. IT CANNOT LAND: query_defs.py is refused by the per-file gate on heavy pre-existing debt, and header_write is itself built-then-orphaned with only its own suite importing it, so wiring them is a new cross-tree dependency into a refused file. Fourth blocked repair in four ticks after gate-G373, gate-G376 and gate-G378.}
}

@misc{gate-G381-a-route-that-omits-its-working-directory-is-a-claim-about-the-reader,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A route that resolves from one working directory and not another is a claim about the reader's position reported as a claim about the artefact.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. A peer reported both pycodemod_retired successor paths as one component short, reproduced from ~/github where the first component is itself the repo directory. MEASURED from substrate's repo root — python3 substrate/module_importers.py resolves and runs over 1617 files with its twelve-shape bound printed. Neither spelling is wrong, they differ only in working directory. ⚑ CORRECTED BY THE PEER: I cited CLAUDE.md for the run-from-the-repo-root convention and md_grep reports no line in it matches. The convention is real and lives in tool docstrings — a literal census finds 7 sites in 7 files including split_pipeline, rewire_to_leaves, check_indented_imports, procstruct, insert_kind and tree_root, which owns the question programmatically. The conclusion held and the warrant under it did not, which is worth recording because a citation that does not check out weakens a verdict that does. The proposed four-literal repair would have inverted the paths and reddened pycodemod_retired's own selftest, which the peer independently confirmed by re-running the sweep. Their route-taking found one site because route-taking finds the site you stand in front of. The class: a route that resolves from one CWD and not another is a claim about the reader's position reported as a claim about the artefact, and a refusal that names a route should name the directory that route assumes. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A peer reported both pycodemod_retired successor paths as one component short, reproduced from ~/github where substrate/substrate/module_importers.py reaches the file and substrate/module_importers.py does not. MEASURED from substrate's own repo root: python3 substrate/module_importers.py resolves and runs, reporting 0 import sites over 1617 files with its twelve-shape bound printed. Neither spelling is wrong — they differ only in working directory, and the repo's tools pin which one by documenting Run from the REPO ROOT, so a successor path in substrate's docstring is repo-relative by convention. The proposed four-literal repair would have INVERTED it, breaking the route for every reader following the stated convention to fix it for one standing a directory up. ⚑ AND THE PEER'S OWN CAVEAT FOUND MORE THAN THEIR LETTER DID: they wrote that their report was bounded by the artefact they happened to open and that a literal sweep would say more. It does — the spelling reaches 7 sites in 5 files including the retired module's suite, hook_structural_query _cases_retired, worklist_engine and worklist_paths, so a four-literal edit would have left five sites disagreeing with two. THE CLASS IS NOT ABOUT PATHS: a route that resolves from one CWD and not another is a claim about the READER'S POSITION reported as a claim about the ARTEFACT, the same shape as an empty census read as an absence. A refusal that names a route should name the directory the route assumes.}
}

@misc{gate-G382-the-refusal-now-names-the-directory-its-route-assumes,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A refusal that names a route must name the directory that route assumes, and the arm asserting it cannot be the arm that resolves the route.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.pycodemod_retired_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. pycodemod_retired.refusal now prints the directory its redirect is relative to, with a note that the route is repo-relative and should be spelled absolute from elsewhere. This is the class fix for gate-G381, where a peer took the redirect from ~/github and reported both successors as one component short when neither spelling was wrong. A block with a route beats a block without one, and a block with a route that fails from where the reader stands is worse than both. ⚑⚑ THE NEW ARM IS SEPARATE FROM every_successor_exists BY NECESSITY: that arm resolves each successor against ROOT, so it and the route vary together and it stays green for a reader standing anywhere, structurally unable to see this defect. The new arm asserts the refusal says which position. Plant-verified by naming the repo root in prose rather than as a copyable path, the tempting wrong fix, and both cases fired. Suite 22 to 24, both halves gate clean and staged — the first editable repair in five ticks after four blocked on monolith debt. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): pycodemod_retired.refusal now prints the directory its redirect is relative to — from /home/mikemol/github/substrate with a note that the route is repo-relative and should be spelled absolute from elsewhere. This is the class fix for gate-G381, where a peer took the redirect from ~/github and reported both successors as one component short when neither spelling was wrong. A block WITH a route beats a block without one, and a block with a route that fails from where the reader stands is worse than both because they get a failure to debug instead of a decision to make. ⚑⚑ THE NEW ARM IS SEPARATE FROM every_successor_exists BY NECESSITY, NOT PREFERENCE: that arm resolves each successor against ROOT, so the arm and the route vary together and it stays green for a reader standing anywhere — it structurally cannot see this defect, which is the instrument-and-subject-vary-together shape the peer named. The new arm asserts the refusal SAYS which position. Plant-verified by naming the repo root in PROSE rather than as a copyable path, the tempting wrong fix, and both cases fired. Suite 22 to 24, both halves gate CLEAN and staged — the first editable repair in five ticks after four blocked on monolith debt.}
}

@misc{gate-G383-the-suite-for-the-module-that-refuses-to-derive-a-root-derived-one,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The suite for the module that refuses to derive a repo root derived one itself, so its green reading could not distinguish a holding contract from a lucky working directory.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.tree_root_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. tree_root.from_argv requires --root with no fallback, by operator ruling, because a CWD default answers about whichever tree the shell was in and a __file__ default about the tool's own. Its own suite held a relative ROSTER and passed dot as the flag's operand. MEASURED — 23/23 from the repo root, and from scripts/ it dies at RootError before a single assertion. The same suite, two verdicts, decided by the invocation. Anchored on package_files().parent, which the import system answers rather than a path walk, so it holds under a checkout, a wheel, a zip import and a symlinked tool. The NOT-the-repo-root arm also read tr.of(dot) and now reads the anchor, since it passed from anywhere the shell was not the package dir — true for the wrong reason. 23 to 24, reading 24/24 from both positions, both plants verified. Found by applying a peer's arm-covariance class to my own tree after I described keeping an arm separate because it and its route varied together. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): tree_root exists so that no reader derives a repo root — from_argv REQUIRES --root with no fallback, on operator ruling, because a CWD default silently answers about whichever tree the shell was in and a __file__ default about the tool's own. ITS OWN SUITE VIOLATED THAT. ROSTER was the relative pair dot and substrate, and the sensitivity arm passed dot as the flag's operand, so every case handed tree_root a path resolved against wherever the reader stood. MEASURED: 23/23 from the repo root, and from scripts/ it dies at RootError substrate is not a directory before reaching a single assertion — the same suite, two verdicts, decided by the invocation rather than the code. So the suite's position was an undeclared input to the module whose thesis is that position must be declared, and a green reading could not distinguish the contract holds from I ran it where the relative paths happen to resolve. ⚑ ANCHORED ON package_files().parent, which the IMPORT SYSTEM answers rather than a path walk, so it is correct under a checkout, a wheel, a zip import and a symlinked tool alike. The _package_cases NOT-the-repo-root arm also read tr.of(dot) and now reads the anchor, since that arm passed from anywhere the shell was not the package dir — true for the wrong reason. 23 to 24, and it now reads 24/24 from BOTH positions. Both plants verified: restoring the relative roster reproduces the RootError from scripts/, and a relative anchor fires the new arm. FOUND BY A PEER'S CLASS applied to my own tree — linux-sources named arm-covariance after I described keeping an arm separate because it and its route varied together.}
}

@misc{gate-G384-a-refuted-entry-outlived-the-function-it-refuted,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A REFUTED entry keyed on a function name outlives the function, and the suite reads its absence through the same accessor it reads a wrong value through.},
  check    = {refuses:python3,jea/metalanguage/sql_lift.py,--selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. MEASURED with two decorrelated instruments — pycodemod --sqlname canon_syms reports 0 definitions and 5 references, all inside sql_lift.py itself, and --calls over scripts jea substrate scratch reports 0 defs 0 calls 0 refs. The function is gone, so _classify is never called and R has no such key. Sixth stale tracker in six failing suites. NOT store-dependent despite postgres being down: _classify is a pure AST walk and _reads_stored resolves against schema_decl.MD.tables, the declaration rather than a connection. AND THE CITED WITNESS IS NOT AT THE CITED LINES — three comments point at sppf_db.py near 1041 to 1049 for a probe discriminating canon from positional ordering, and those lines carry a materialisation-volume finding instead. A fourth restatement cites a different line again, so four copies of one pointer disagree three ways. Blocked by pre-existing debt under the all-or-nothing gate. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): sql_lift reads 30/33 and all three failures are canon_syms returning None where a verdict is expected. MEASURED with two decorrelated instruments: pycodemod --sqlname canon_syms reports 0 definitions and 5 references, every one inside sql_lift.py itself — the REFUTED dict key and the four arms — and --calls canon_syms over scripts jea substrate scratch reports 0 defs 0 calls 0 refs. The function is GONE from the corpus, so _classify is never called on it and R has no such key, which is why the arms read absence through the same .get() they would read a wrong value through. Sixth stale tracker in six failing suites. ⚑⚑ NOT STORE-DEPENDENT despite postgres being down: _classify is a pure AST walk and _reads_stored resolves against schema_decl.MD.tables, the DECLARATION rather than a connection, so an unreachable store cannot remove a candidate. These are assertions, not environment. ⚑ AND THE CITED WITNESS IS NOT AT THE CITED LINES — three comments point at sppf_db.py around 1041 to 1049 for a probe discriminating canon c,d,e from positional e,d,c, and reading scripts/sppf_db.py:1035-1052 finds a materialisation-volume finding instead: 943,927 observations, 70 distinct heights, largest level 529,003 rows. A fourth restatement cites a different line again. Four copies of one pointer, three disagreeing — the cite-a-SYMBOL-never-a-line defect. BLOCKED: the file carries heavy pre-existing debt under the all-or-nothing gate.}
}

@misc{gate-G385-a-refutation-and-the-rule-it-indicts-retire-independently,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A refutation and the rule it indicts retire independently, so deleting a stale counterexample silently re-asserts a rule nobody re-tested.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. sql_lift.REFUTED carries canon_syms so the tool reports its own known-wrong verdict, and the dict's docstring makes it a ratchet — deleting an entry is a claim the classifier now gets it right, which someone must justify. The subject function is gone, measured by two decorrelated instruments. The INSTANCE verdict retires with its subject, having nothing left to be true of. The RULE-LEVEL indictment does not: if classifying a variable-arity flatten-and-sort as residue is still wrong as a rule, deleting the entry to green the suite converts we-no-longer-have-a-counterexample into this-rule-is-correct. That is absence read as does-not-exist inside the instrument built to stop it, and the file's own prose warns that reporting only the rule is how the counterexample stayed invisible while sitting in the repo. The honest state is that the tracker is stale and the rule's status is UNKNOWN — a repair retires the entry and re-tests the rule as two acts. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): sql_lift.REFUTED carries canon_syms so the tool reports its own known-wrong verdict rather than a reader having to already know, and the dict's docstring makes it a ratchet: deleting an entry is a claim that the classifier now gets it right, which is a change someone must justify. The subject function is GONE from the corpus, measured by two decorrelated instruments. THE TWO HALVES HAVE DIFFERENT FATES AND COLLAPSING THEM IS THE HAZARD. The INSTANCE verdict retires with its subject — a refutation of a verdict about a deleted function has nothing to be true of. The RULE-LEVEL indictment does not: if sorted_flatten classifying a variable-arity flatten-and-sort as residue is still wrong AS A RULE, then deleting the entry to make the suite green silently re-asserts a rule nobody re-tested, converting WE NO LONGER HAVE A COUNTEREXAMPLE into THIS RULE IS CORRECT. That is absence read as does-not-exist, inside the instrument built to stop exactly that, and the file's own prose warns that reporting only the rule is how the counterexample stayed invisible while sitting in the repo. SO THE HONEST STATE IS: the tracker is stale, the rule's status is UNKNOWN, and a repair must retire the entry and re-test the rule as two acts rather than one.}
}

@misc{gate-G386-the-per-file-gate-reads-green-when-half-its-checker-is-absent,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A gate whose checker is an undeclared dependency reads green the moment the environment moves, and it reads green in the direction that admits debt.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. The tree moved to a new host and the venv survived as a dead shell with a broken symlink to an absent mise interpreter. Recreated with uv on a pinned 3.14 per operator ruling, synced 112 packages, installed the package editable. The promotability census then read 69 where eighteen ticks had read 735 — not the tree improving. pycheck_show printed No module named ruff. The per-file gate runs ruff plus mypy, pyproject declared only mypy, and ruff had been a global binary on the old host that nothing recorded. With half the checker absent the gate promoted 666 modules it had refused the day before. uv add --dev ruff restored it and the census read 735 of 800 again, byte-identical on a different interpreter — the figure is the tree's, the 69 was the environment's. Three readings of one census in one turn: 1617 with no package, 69 with no ruff, 735 with both. The project-tooling skill is also stale in calling substrate legacy requirements.txt when it has pyproject and a lock. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The tree moved to a new host and the venv survived as a dead shell — pyvenv.cfg naming mise CPython 3.13.11 with .venv/bin/python a broken symlink and mise not installed. Recreated per operator ruling with uv on an explicitly pinned 3.14, uv sync installed 112 packages from pyproject and the lock, and uv pip install -e . made the package importable. THEN THE PROMOTABILITY CENSUS READ 69 WHERE EIGHTEEN TICKS HAD READ 735, and scratch went 509 of 509 ready. That was not the tree improving. pycheck_show on a known-blocked file printed No module named ruff — the per-file gate runs ruff plus mypy, pyproject declared only mypy, and on the old host ruff was a global binary nothing recorded. With half the checker absent the gate counted only mypy findings and promoted 666 modules it had refused the day before. uv add --dev ruff restored it and the census read 735 of 800 again, byte-identical to every prior tick on a different interpreter version — the strongest possible evidence the figure is a property of the tree and the 69 was a property of the environment. Three readings of one census in one turn, and only one true: 1617 with no package, 69 with no ruff, 735 with both. A GATE WHOSE CHECKER IS AN UNDECLARED DEPENDENCY READS GREEN THE MOMENT THE ENVIRONMENT MOVES, and it reads green in the direction that admits debt. Also measured: the project-tooling skill calls substrate legacy requirements.txt and it has pyproject plus uv.lock — the skill is stale on that point.}
}

@misc{gate-G387-the-interpreter-change-moved-nothing-and-pandoc-moved-nine,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A 3.13 to 3.14 interpreter change moved no suite outcome, and every delta between hosts was one absent binary the project does not declare.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. First roster reading on the new host. Old host 38 of 470, new host 47 of 470. The entire delta of nine is pandoc — seven md_* suites read VACUOUS with SKIP pandoc is not on this machine, two more read UNREAD dying at FileNotFoundError pandoc. Refused, silent and unfit are row-for-row identical. Cases fell 7 to 6 because the three suites repaired this arc hold on 3.14 and left the list. Two new rows: hook_fire_probe 23/27 with shellcheck SILENT for a clean command makes shellcheck the third undeclared checker after ruff and pandoc, and module_trim_selftest read UNREAD on a missing staging file that run directly is exactly the one gate-G368 failure — a collision with record_migrate_selftest sharing one staging path under the parallel pool. The md_* suites skip loudly rather than passing, which is the honest failure, but a dependency the tools cannot run without belongs in pyproject the same as ruff. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): First run_selftests reading on the new host, CPython 3.14 against the old host's 3.13. Old host 38 of 470 non-OK — 7 cases 1 ok 13 refused 11 silent 4 unfit 1 unread 1 vacuous. New host 47 of 470 — 6 cases 1 ok 13 refused 11 silent 4 unfit 4 unread 8 vacuous. THE ENTIRE DELTA OF NINE IS PANDOC: seven md_* suites read VACUOUS with SKIP pandoc is not on this machine, and two more read UNREAD dying at FileNotFoundError pandoc. Refused, silent and unfit are row-for-row identical, and cases fell 7 to 6 because the three suites repaired this arc — baseline_health, glyph_flagged, tree_root — hold on 3.14 and have left the list. NOTHING MOVED ON THE INTERPRETER CHANGE. Two new rows worth their own reading: hook_fire_probe 23/27 with shellcheck SILENT for a clean command, which makes shellcheck the THIRD undeclared checker after ruff and pandoc, and module_trim_selftest UNREAD on a missing .record-migrate-staging.py — run directly it reads exactly one failure, the gate-G368 arm, so the roster row was a COLLISION between it and record_migrate_selftest sharing one staging path under the parallel pool, the gate-F60 fixture-shares-a-path class with a second party. Also the selftest-contract census fell 386 to 383, the three repaired suites having moved to typed records. The md_* suites SKIP loudly rather than passing, which is the honest failure — but a dependency the tools cannot run without belongs in pyproject the same as ruff.}
}

@misc{gate-G388-the-tools-a-tree-shells-out-to-are-part-of-its-install,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The external tools a tree shells out to are part of its install, and a census of them must ask each tool the way its calling site does — a module of the interpreter, a binary of the PATH — or it reads a false PRESENT.},
  check    = {selftest:python3,-m,substrate.install_freshness_selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=momentum. install_freshness --binaries censuses the required external tools and refuses on absence, naming what each starves, by operator ruling over a mise.toml and over prose. The roster is declared rather than walked because pycheck_show builds argv from _VENV_PY and -m so a static reader finds no head, and hook_shellcheck resolves through shutil.which then a mise shim. Two kinds never conflated: shutil.which finding a system ruff says nothing about python -m ruff in this venv. The conflation arm plants python3, on PATH and not importable, and a resolver falling through to PATH fires it — verified by planting exactly that. The live census corrected my record: pandoc is present at /usr/bin/pandoc now, only shellcheck is absent, and hook_shellcheck already announces ARMED BUT INERT for it. Of the three found by accident only ruff was a silent false green. New suite 12/12, suite gaps 347 to 348 — the first owning-tool movement in nineteen ticks that was work rather than environment. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): install_freshness --binaries now censuses the external tools the tree shells out to and REFUSES when one is absent, naming what it starves. Operator ruled this shape over a mise.toml and over a documented install step. THE ROSTER IS DECLARED, NOT WALKED, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED: pycheck_show builds its argv from _VENV_PY and -m, so a static reader finds subprocess.run(argv) and no head to name, and hook_shellcheck resolves through shutil.which then a mise-shim path. The exec sites are real and the binaries they need are not recoverable from them by a reader. TWO KINDS, NEVER CONFLATED: a MODULE tool is asked of THIS interpreter via find_spec and a BINARY of the PATH then the mise shim dir, because shutil.which finding a system ruff says nothing about whether python -m ruff works in this venv, which is the invocation the gate makes. The conflation arm plants python3 — on PATH, not importable — and a resolver that fell through to PATH for a module lookup fires it. Verified by planting exactly that. THE LIVE CENSUS CORRECTED MY OWN RECORD: pandoc is at /usr/bin/pandoc now, present since the roster read it absent — only shellcheck is genuinely missing on this host, and hook_shellcheck already announces ARMED BUT INERT for that. So of the three found by accident, ruff was the only silent false green, pandoc skips loudly per suite, and shellcheck fails open by its own rule. New suite 12/12, suite gaps 347 to 348 — the first owning-tool movement in nineteen ticks that was work rather than environment.}
}

@misc{gate-G389-the-census-found-agda-absent-and-a-roster-entry-that-was-a-script,
  section  = {finding},
